window.QUESTION_BANK = [{"module": 1, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A person purchases a condominium, lives in one room, and rents out the remaining rooms for income. Which statement best describes the property?</p>", "options": [["A", "It cannot be an investment because the owner lives there."], ["B", "It is necessarily a security rather than property."], ["C", "It can serve both personal consumption and investment purposes."], ["D", "It qualifies as an investment only if its market price increases."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An investment is defined by the expectation of income and/or appreciation. The condominium can supply housing services and rental income simultaneously. Appreciation is not required for an asset to qualify as an investment.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not assume that consumption and investment purposes are mutually exclusive.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 2, "question": "<p>An investor directly purchases a newly issued 10-year corporate bond. How should the investment be classified?</p>", "options": [["A", "Indirect, equity and short-term"], ["B", "Direct, debt and long-term"], ["C", "Direct, derivative and short-term"], ["D", "Indirect, debt and short-term"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor directly holds the security, acts as a creditor rather than an owner, and holds an instrument with a maturity longer than one year.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Investment classifications operate across several dimensions simultaneously.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 3, "question": "<p>An investor buys shares in a professionally managed mutual fund that owns hundreds of stocks. What is the most accurate description?</p>", "options": [["A", "An indirect investment that provides equity exposure"], ["B", "A direct ownership claim against every underlying company"], ["C", "A risk-free investment because it owns many securities"], ["D", "A fixed-income instrument because it distributes returns"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor owns an interest in the pooled fund rather than directly holding each underlying security. Diversification can reduce certain risks, but does not guarantee safety.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Owning units in a fund does not mean directly holding its underlying stocks.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Investment X has an expected return of $15\\%$, while Investment Y has an expected return of $7\\%$. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "X is superior because $15\\%$ is greater than $7\\%$."], ["B", "Y is superior because low-return investments always have low risk."], ["C", "Neither can be judged without understanding risk and investor objectives."], ["D", "Both investments must have identical risk because returns are uncertain."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Expected return alone does not establish suitability. The investor must compare compensation with the uncertainty accepted and their investment goals, time horizon and liquidity needs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Expected return is not guaranteed return, and higher return is not automatically better.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 5, "question": "<p>An investor purchases an option whose value changes with the price of a company's shares. Why is the option classified as a derivative?</p>", "options": [["A", "It guarantees a fixed return."], ["B", "It grants ongoing ownership of the company."], ["C", "It requires the investor to lend money to the issuer."], ["D", "Its value is derived from an underlying asset."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The defining feature of a derivative is that its value is linked to an underlying asset. Debt and equity represent different economic claims.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An option does not automatically make its holder an ordinary shareholder.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 6, "question": "<p>According to the chapter's general classification, which group is typically a net supplier of funds?</p>", "options": [["A", "Individuals"], ["B", "Businesses expanding production"], ["C", "Governments financing projects"], ["D", "Corporations issuing securities to raise capital"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter generally classifies individuals as net suppliers of funds, while businesses and governments are typically net demanders. Individuals can still borrow for housing or cars.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Net supplier describes the group's typical aggregate role, not every individual transaction.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 7, "question": "<p>A pension fund manages the retirement savings of employees and buys securities on their behalf. It is best described as:</p>", "options": [["A", "An individual investor because the beneficiaries are people"], ["B", "An institutional investor because it manages funds for others"], ["C", "A direct investment because it invests in equities"], ["D", "A government investor regardless of who manages the fund"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Institutional investors manage money for other parties and commonly invest at scale. The identity of ultimate beneficiaries does not convert a pension fund into an individual investor.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The distinction is primarily the investor's role and structure, not the personal identity of beneficiaries.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 8, "question": "<p>A bank accepts deposits from households and channels funds to a manufacturing company through loans. What economic function is illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Conversion of all debt into equity"], ["B", "Elimination of all borrower default risk"], ["C", "Financial intermediation between suppliers and demanders of funds"], ["D", "A transaction that makes households net demanders of funds"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Financial intermediaries channel savings from those with available capital toward borrowers requiring funds. Intermediation does not eliminate credit risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Follow the direction of capital through the financial system.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 9, "question": "<p>Which feature most clearly distinguishes an exchange-traded fund (ETF) from a conventional mutual fund in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "An ETF must hold only bonds."], ["B", "An ETF cannot provide diversification."], ["C", "A conventional mutual fund must always be actively managed."], ["D", "ETF shares trade on exchanges during market trading hours."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Both vehicles can pool investors' money and provide diversified exposure. The textbook emphasizes that ETFs can be bought and sold on exchanges while trading is open.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> ETFs are not defined by always being safer, riskier or passively managed.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Which description best matches hedge funds in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Government-guaranteed funds with no minimum subscription"], ["B", "Pooled investment vehicles that typically have higher minimums and lighter regulation than mutual funds"], ["C", "Funds that may invest only in government securities"], ["D", "Funds whose return must equal an index"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes hedge funds as pooling investors' resources, typically with larger minimum investments and less regulation than mutual funds, in its textbook context.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A hedge fund is not automatically a low-risk hedge against losses.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 11, "question": "<p>A person has $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ in savings, unstable income, no emergency reserve and no adequate protection against major financial shocks. What should they prioritize first?</p>", "options": [["A", "Meeting investment prerequisites"], ["B", "Purchasing aggressive growth securities"], ["C", "Increasing leverage to build wealth faster"], ["D", "Selecting the investment with the highest historical return"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Step 1 of the seven-step investment process requires meeting basic needs, emergency cash requirements and protection against common financial risks before aggressive investing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The prerequisites precede portfolio construction for an economic reason, not bureaucratic formality.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 12, "question": "<p>An investor has identified retirement as a goal. They now create a written plan specifying a 25-year horizon, contribution targets and risk tolerance. Which investment step is primarily involved?</p>", "options": [["A", "Step 1: Meeting prerequisites"], ["B", "Step 2: Establishing goals"], ["C", "Step 3: Adopting an investment plan"], ["D", "Step 7: Managing the portfolio"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Establishing retirement as an objective is Step 2. Translating that objective into a written strategy with target dates and risk tolerance is Step 3.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish identifying an objective from designing a plan to achieve it.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 13, "question": "<p>A portfolio was designed for a 20-year retirement horizon. Five years later, the investor must access half the capital within $12$ months. What is the most appropriate action?</p>", "options": [["A", "Keep the portfolio unchanged because the original plan was rational."], ["B", "Automatically sell every investment at once."], ["C", "Increase risk to make up for the shortened horizon."], ["D", "Reassess investment suitability and adjust the portfolio for the changed liquidity need."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor's circumstances have changed materially. Portfolio management requires comparing current conditions with expectations and taking suitable corrective action rather than mechanically maintaining an outdated allocation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A good investment plan is monitored and updated when relevant constraints change.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 14, "question": "<p>An investor purchases $20$ securities from different companies. They claim the portfolio is therefore guaranteed not to suffer major losses. Which response is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because owning more than ten securities eliminates all risk."], ["B", "Incorrect, because diversification can reduce risk but does not guarantee against losses."], ["C", "Correct, provided the securities all belong to the same industry."], ["D", "Incorrect, because diversification cannot affect investment risk in any way."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter encourages diversified portfolios to manage risk, but it does not promise that diversification eliminates every risk or prevents broad-market losses.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Diversification is risk management, not a financial guarantee.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Before choosing a particular company's shares, an investor compares the expected returns and risks of several investment categories. Which step is most directly represented?</p>", "options": [["A", "Step 4: Evaluating investments"], ["B", "Step 5: Selecting suitable investments"], ["C", "Step 6: Constructing a diversified portfolio"], ["D", "Step 7: Managing the portfolio"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Step 4 involves evaluating potential return and risk. Step 5 moves toward researching and selecting specific investments, while Step 6 combines them into a portfolio.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Evaluation, product selection and portfolio construction are connected but distinct steps.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 16, "question": "<p>Assume Investment A earns $9\\%$ before tax and its entire return is taxed at $40\\%$. Investment B earns $6\\%$ and is entirely tax-exempt. With otherwise comparable characteristics, which provides the higher after-tax return?</p>", "options": [["A", "A, because $9\\%$ exceeds $6\\%$"], ["B", "Both provide $6\\%$"], ["C", "They cannot be compared even with the tax assumptions given"], ["D", "B, because its after-tax return exceeds A's $5.4\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A returns $9\\% \\times (1 - 0.40) = 5.4\\%$ after tax, whereas B retains $6\\%$. This illustrates the chapter's principle that investors should evaluate what they keep. The tax treatments here are hypothetical.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Always apply the assumed taxes before comparing net returns.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Under the chapter's U.S. textbook classification of individual income, rental income from an investment property is generally an example of:</p>", "options": [["A", "Active income"], ["B", "Portfolio dividend income"], ["C", "Passive income"], ["D", "Interest income on debt"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides distinguish active income (wages and similar employment-related income), portfolio income (investment interest, dividends and capital gains), and passive income (including rents and royalties).</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the income classification given in the slides, rather than assuming all investment income belongs to the portfolio-income category.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 18, "question": "<p>A retiree relies on invested wealth to supplement living expenses and has limited capacity to recover from large losses. Which portfolio objective best fits the chapter's lifecycle framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "Maximum speculation through derivatives"], ["B", "Preservation of capital and reliable investment income"], ["C", "Maximizing short-term share-price appreciation regardless of volatility"], ["D", "Concentrating all assets in one aggressive growth stock"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The retirement stage emphasizes capital preservation and income. The recommendation reflects the investor's cash-flow needs and reduced flexibility to recover from adverse outcomes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Match the asset characteristics to the investor's circumstances, not just the advertised return.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A 23-year-old student needs their savings to pay tuition fees in eight months. A friend recommends an aggressive equity strategy solely because the student is young. What is the strongest objection?</p>", "options": [["A", "The short investment horizon and need for capital preservation override an age-only recommendation."], ["B", "Every young person should invest entirely in bonds."], ["C", "Equities are never appropriate for investors below $30$."], ["D", "Risk tolerance and liquidity requirements cannot affect investment decisions."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Although younger investors may generally have longer investing horizons, this specific money is needed soon. A short-term objective demands different asset characteristics from long-term retirement investing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Age is a useful lifecycle indicator, not an overriding decision rule.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 20, "question": "<p>What is the central purpose of the tax-advantaged retirement plans discussed in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "To guarantee high investment returns"], ["B", "To eliminate all investment risk"], ["C", "To ensure every participant can withdraw funds without restrictions"], ["D", "To provide tax advantages, including tax deferral for qualifying arrangements"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter discusses U.S. tax-advantaged retirement arrangements such as employer plans and IRAs. Their function is related to tax treatment and retirement saving, not investment guarantees.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Tax treatment, risk and investment return are different concepts. Not every plan has identical tax mechanics.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 21, "question": "<p>An investor needs $\\text{RM}50{,}000$ for a house deposit in nine months and cannot tolerate a large temporary loss. Which approach best matches the objective?</p>", "options": [["A", "Concentrate the funds in a speculative growth stock."], ["B", "Use a liquid, relatively low-risk short-term instrument."], ["C", "Purchase highly volatile long-term securities and rely on appreciation."], ["D", "Use margin borrowing to increase the deposit amount."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The short horizon and need for reliable access make liquidity and capital stability especially important. High volatility can threaten the planned purchase even when a security is easy to sell.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Operational liquidity and stability of market value are not identical.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 22, "question": "<p>A property is worth approximately $\\text{RM}1$ million under normal conditions, but the owner can sell immediately only by accepting $\\text{RM}750{,}000$. Which characteristic is being compromised?</p>", "options": [["A", "Diversification"], ["B", "Expected dividend yield"], ["C", "Liquidity"], ["D", "Direct ownership"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Liquidity involves converting an investment into cash quickly with little or no loss in value. A large forced-sale discount shows that the property is not highly liquid under those circumstances.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An asset can be valuable without being easy to liquidate at a fair price.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A short-term deposit earns $3\\%$ annually, while inflation is $5\\%$. Which statement best describes the result?</p>", "options": [["A", "The deposit is guaranteed to produce a $5\\%$ real return."], ["B", "It has become a derivative security."], ["C", "Inflation is irrelevant because the deposit remains liquid."], ["D", "Its nominal value may rise while purchasing power declines."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The account may increase in ringgit while inflation erodes the purchasing power of those ringgit. This is one of the disadvantages of low-return short-term investments highlighted in the chapter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Nominal principal protection is not the same as preservation of purchasing power.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 24, "question": "<p>Market interest rates rise substantially. All else equal, what would normally happen to the market price of an existing fixed-rate bond?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its market price would tend to decline."], ["B", "Its coupon rate would automatically increase to match market rates."], ["C", "Its price must rise because investors now prefer fixed-income securities."], ["D", "Its principal would automatically be converted into equity."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Newly issued securities can offer more attractive yields when rates rise. Existing bonds with fixed cash flows generally become less valuable at their previous prices, producing an inverse price-rate relationship.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish the fixed contractual coupon from the bond's changing market price.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Which instrument is most accurately described in the chapter as a short-term debt security issued by the U.S. Treasury, typically sold at a discount?</p>", "options": [["A", "Corporate common stock"], ["B", "Treasury bill"], ["C", "Long-term corporate bond"], ["D", "Equity mutual fund"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The short-term investment comparison identifies Treasury bills as U.S. Treasury instruments with maturities of one year or less and normally sold at a discount.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse Treasury bills with long-term government debt instruments.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 26, "question": "<p>A large, creditworthy corporation wants to obtain financing for approximately $90$ days by issuing an unsecured short-term note. Which instrument best fits the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Preferred stock"], ["B", "Commercial paper"], ["C", "Common stock"], ["D", "A long-term convertible bond"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Commercial paper is described as an unsecured short-term note issued by a company with high credit standing. Preferred and common stock are equity-type claims, not short-term unsecured notes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Identify the issuer, contractual nature and maturity of the financing.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 27, "question": "<p>An investor keeps a portion of their portfolio in short-term instruments while waiting for a more attractive long-term opportunity. Which interpretation is most consistent with the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "The investor has abandoned investing because short-term holdings are not investments."], ["B", "The investor must be making a mistake because all capital should always be fully invested in equities."], ["C", "The holdings are unsuitable unless their yield exceeds all stock returns."], ["D", "The investor is using short-term instruments as a temporary outlet that maintains flexibility."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter specifically recognizes short-term investments as temporary places for funds while investors wait for attractive longer-term opportunities. Liquidity itself can have economic value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A lower expected return may be a rational price for maintaining flexibility.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 28, "question": "<p>GDP and industrial production are expanding, and unemployment is falling. What is the most appropriate conclusion about stock prices under the chapter's business-cycle discussion?</p>", "options": [["A", "The environment is generally associated with stronger markets, but does not guarantee gains for every stock."], ["B", "Every stock must rise because the economy is expanding."], ["C", "Stock prices are mathematically independent of business conditions."], ["D", "The economy's expansion guarantees that bonds and shares earn equal returns."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes that share prices tend to rise during expansions and weaken during declines. This is a broad tendency, not a deterministic relationship for individual securities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish an economic association from a guaranteed investment outcome.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A manufacturing company wants assistance raising capital by issuing securities to investors. Which finance career is most directly associated with that work?</p>", "options": [["A", "Personal insurance claims management"], ["B", "Commercial bank deposit administration"], ["C", "Investment banking"], ["D", "Household retirement budgeting"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies investment banking as a field that helps organizations raise capital. Other finance professions may be involved in planning, insurance or routine banking activities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Investment banking and investment management are not the same career function.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": 30, "question": "<p>An investor has two goals: $\\text{RM}50{,}000$ for a house in one year and retirement savings for $30$ years. What is the strongest overall investment strategy?</p>", "options": [["A", "Separate the goals into suitable allocations: liquid, low-risk assets for the house and longer-horizon investments for retirement."], ["B", "Combine everything into one aggressive growth portfolio because retirement is the longer goal."], ["C", "Invest everything in cash forever because the house purchase is near."], ["D", "Choose the asset with the highest historical return and apply it to both goals."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The two goals have different time horizons, liquidity requirements and capacities for loss. A goal-based strategy can use short-term instruments for the house deposit while taking suitable longer-term growth exposure for retirement.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> One investor can have multiple goals requiring different investment strategies.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A corporation issues a new 10-year bond to raise money for factory expansion. An investor purchases the bond directly from the new offering. Which classification is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Primary money market"], ["B", "Secondary capital market"], ["C", "Primary capital market"], ["D", "Secondary money market"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The bond is newly issued, so the transaction is in the primary market. Its 10-year maturity makes it a capital-market security. Primary/secondary and money/capital are separate classification dimensions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Always classify the issuance status and maturity separately.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 2, "question": "<p>Investor A sells $1{,}000$ existing shares of ABC Berhad to Investor B through a stock exchange. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "ABC Berhad receives the sales proceeds as new capital."], ["B", "Investor A receives the proceeds; the transaction occurs in the secondary market."], ["C", "The transaction must be classified as a primary-market rights offering."], ["D", "Investor B becomes a lender rather than a shareholder."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In a normal secondary-market resale, the seller receives the proceeds. The company does not directly raise new capital from that resale, even though secondary-market trading supports liquidity and price discovery.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Follow the money: who actually receives the transaction proceeds?</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 3, "question": "<p>A government issues a new Treasury bill maturing in $90$ days. An investor buys it at issuance. Which market classification is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Primary money market"], ["B", "Secondary capital market"], ["C", "Primary capital market"], ["D", "Secondary money market"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A newly issued instrument belongs to the primary market, and a 90-day maturity places the Treasury bill in the money market.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A government security is not automatically a long-term capital-market instrument.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 4, "question": "<p>A student claims secondary markets are economically unimportant because companies receive no new funds when previously issued shares are resold. What is the strongest response?</p>", "options": [["A", "The student is correct because resale trading serves no economic function."], ["B", "Secondary markets create new company shares every time an investor buys."], ["C", "Secondary markets guarantee the price of newly issued shares."], ["D", "Secondary markets improve liquidity and price discovery, making initial investment more attractive."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides identify liquidity and continuous pricing as central secondary-market functions. Investors may be more willing to buy newly issued securities when they expect to have a practical way to sell later.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A market can add economic value without directly transferring fresh capital to the issuer.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 5, "question": "<p>What distinguishes an initial public offering (IPO) from an ordinary secondary-market purchase of shares?</p>", "options": [["A", "An IPO is the first public sale of a company's stock."], ["B", "An IPO occurs only when existing investors trade among themselves."], ["C", "An IPO always sells corporate bonds rather than shares."], ["D", "An IPO guarantees positive capital gains to subscribing investors."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines an IPO as the first public sale of company stock. A secondary-market purchase involves securities that have already been issued.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Going public is not the same as routine trading after the company is public.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 6, "question": "<p>A listed corporation gives its existing shareholders the opportunity to subscribe for additional newly issued shares in proportion to their existing holdings. Which primary-market method best describes this?</p>", "options": [["A", "Private placement"], ["B", "Secondary-market resale"], ["C", "Rights offering"], ["D", "Short sale"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies rights offerings as a primary-market distribution alternative. They provide existing shareholders with subscription rights to purchase newly issued shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Issuing additional shares to existing owners is not simply a secondary-market transaction.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 7, "question": "<p>A company sells a new securities issue privately to a small group of selected institutional investors rather than broadly offering it to the public. Which method best describes the transaction?</p>", "options": [["A", "Rights offering"], ["B", "Private placement"], ["C", "Fourth-market resale"], ["D", "Ordinary secondary-market exchange trade"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter lists private placement as a primary-market issuance method. Here the new issue is offered to a selected group instead of being broadly distributed through a public offering.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Private placement concerns how newly issued securities are distributed; it is not synonymous with secondary trading.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 8, "question": "<p>During an IPO waiting period, prospective investors receive a preliminary offering document containing information about the company and proposed issue. What is this document called?</p>", "options": [["A", "Tombstone"], ["B", "Final audit report"], ["C", "Selling-group agreement"], ["D", "Red herring"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides define a red herring as the preliminary prospectus available during the waiting period. The final prospectus is the broader formal disclosure document describing the issue and issuer.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse the preliminary prospectus with the roadshow, tombstone or final prospectus.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 9, "question": "<p>An investment bank purchases a securities issue from the company at an agreed price and assumes the risk of reselling it to investors. What is the bank primarily doing?</p>", "options": [["A", "Acting as a guaranteed investment-return provider"], ["B", "Operating as a short seller of the issuing company's shares"], ["C", "Underwriting the offering and accepting distribution risk"], ["D", "Eliminating all risks associated with the issuing company"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Under the chapter's described underwriting arrangement, the investment bank purchases the issue and bears the financial risk of reselling it. This does not eliminate the issuer's business risk or guarantee returns to public investors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish underwriting distribution risk from the investment risk borne by shareholders.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 10, "question": "<p>A large IPO involves several investment banks sharing underwriting risk, while other brokerage firms help sell the shares to investors. Which distinction is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The underwriting syndicate shares financial risk; the selling group helps distribute securities."], ["B", "The selling group guarantees all future investor returns."], ["C", "The underwriting syndicate exists solely to regulate the company's accounting."], ["D", "The selling group and underwriting syndicate have no different responsibilities."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter distinguishes the underwriting syndicate, which shares underwriting risk, from the selling group, which assists in marketing and distributing the securities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk sharing and distribution assistance are related functions, but not the same responsibility.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 11, "question": "<p>In Transaction A, an intermediary matches a buyer with a seller. In Transaction B, an intermediary purchases securities from a seller and later resells them from its own inventory. What are the roles?</p>", "options": [["A", "A is dealer; B is broker"], ["B", "Both are brokers"], ["C", "Both are dealers"], ["D", "A is broker; B is dealer"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A broker primarily facilitates trades between investors. A dealer trades as principal and acts as a counterparty. The dealer may bear inventory and market-price risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The central distinction is whether the intermediary merely matches the trade or becomes a trading counterparty.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 12, "question": "<p>A dealer quotes a bid of $\\text{RM}50.80$ and an ask of $\\text{RM}51.20$. You want to immediately sell $100$ shares to the dealer. What is the gross amount received?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}5{,}120$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}5{,}080$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}5{,}100$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}40$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The bid is the price the dealer offers to pay when buying. Selling $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50.80$ gives $100 \\times \\text{RM}50.80 = \\text{RM}5{,}080$, before transaction costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Bid and ask quotations are defined from the market maker's perspective.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 13, "question": "<p>A dealer quotes $\\text{RM}40.00$ bid and $\\text{RM}40.40$ ask. Ignoring other costs and price changes, an investor buys $100$ shares from the dealer and immediately sells them back. What is the investor's loss?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}0$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}20$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}40$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}80$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor buys at the ask for $\\text{RM}4{,}040$ and sells at the bid for $\\text{RM}4{,}000$. The $\\text{RM}40$ difference reflects the bid-ask spread for $100$ shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Even without a change in quoted market prices, crossing the spread creates a transaction cost.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 14, "question": "<p>Two pension funds execute a large securities transaction directly with each other through an electronic communications network, bypassing a market maker. Which market structure best matches the textbook?</p>", "options": [["A", "Fourth market"], ["B", "Third market"], ["C", "Primary market"], ["D", "Traditional dealer market"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The fourth market involves institutional investors transacting directly with one another, often using ECNs to bypass market makers. The third market involves institutional trading through non-exchange-member market makers.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Third market uses a market maker; fourth market bypasses one.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Under the chapter's historical description, an investor is considering a small, thinly traded company quoted on the OTC Pink Sheets. Which concern is most consistent with the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "The company must satisfy the strictest exchange-listing rules."], ["B", "The security is guaranteed to be as liquid as a large NYSE stock."], ["C", "The security cannot experience a capital loss."], ["D", "Liquidity may be extremely low and the investment may be highly risky."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes Pink Sheets securities as small, frequently illiquid and potentially highly speculative. Its regulatory descriptions are historical and should not be treated as a current universal legal rule.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Trading access does not imply liquidity, listing quality or investor protection.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 16, "question": "<p>Which combination most closely describes a bull market in the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Falling prices, pessimism and economic slowdown"], ["B", "Rising prices, optimism and favorable market conditions"], ["C", "Flat prices, zero volatility and guaranteed dividends"], ["D", "Rising bond coupons and falling economic activity"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The textbook associates bull markets with rising security prices, optimism and generally favorable market conditions, while bear markets involve falling prices and pessimism.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Market sentiment and broad direction are not guarantees about every security.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Why can an internationally diversified portfolio have lower overall risk than a portfolio invested entirely in one domestic market?</p>", "options": [["A", "International securities always have higher returns."], ["B", "Foreign currencies eliminate financial risk."], ["C", "Different markets may move imperfectly together, reducing portfolio concentration."], ["D", "Foreign governments guarantee investors against losses."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter explains that international markets do not necessarily move in the same direction or at the same time. Different economic cycles can provide diversification benefits, although new risks are introduced.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Diversification benefit depends on return relationships, not merely owning foreign securities.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 18, "question": "<p>A Malaysian investor buys a U.S. stock. Its USD value rises $10\\%$, but the USD depreciates $12\\%$ against MYR during the holding period. Ignoring distributions and costs, what is the MYR return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$+22\\%$"], ["B", "$-2\\%$"], ["C", "$+3.2\\%$"], ["D", "$-3.2\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The correct currency-adjusted return is $(1.10 \\times 0.88) - 1 = -3.2\\%$. A gain in the asset's foreign currency can be more than offset by depreciation of that currency against the investor's home currency.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not simply add a stock return and an exchange-rate movement when calculating the exact combined return.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A U.S. investor wants exposure to a foreign company's shares through a U.S.-traded depositary instrument. Which instrument best matches the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "American Depositary Receipt (ADR)"], ["B", "U.S. Treasury bill"], ["C", "Corporate commercial paper"], ["D", "A domestic government savings account"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies ADRs as dollar-denominated receipts representing shares of foreign companies held by a depositary bank, providing a route to foreign-equity exposure.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Trading or being denominated in USD does not eliminate exposure to the underlying foreign investment.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A foreign government unexpectedly changes securities taxation and capital-transfer restrictions, impairing overseas investors' ability to recover proceeds. Which risk category is most directly illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Only ordinary stock-price volatility"], ["B", "Government-policy and political/regulatory risk"], ["C", "Only broker inventory risk"], ["D", "No investment risk because the assets remain foreign"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies government-policy risk from foreign laws, regulation and political conditions. Currency risk can also affect international investing, but the scenario principally concerns legal and policy changes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Differentiate changes in exchange rates from changes in government rules.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 21, "question": "<p>A company preparing a U.S. public securities issue is subject to a law emphasizing disclosure about the issuer and offering. Which textbook law is the best match?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002"], ["B", "Maloney Act of 1938"], ["C", "Securities Act of 1933"], ["D", "Insider Trading and Fraud Act of 1988"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter associates the Securities Act of 1933 with securities-offering disclosure. Sarbanes-Oxley emphasizes accounting and audit controls, while the other laws address different regulatory issues.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The 1933 Act focuses on disclosure; the 1934 Act established the SEC in the chapter.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes the Securities Act of 1934 from the Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002 in the slides?</p>", "options": [["A", "The 1934 Act established the SEC; Sarbanes-Oxley strengthened accounting and audit requirements."], ["B", "Both laws were introduced specifically to govern short-selling margins."], ["C", "The 1934 Act created all mutual funds; Sarbanes-Oxley established the Nasdaq market."], ["D", "Sarbanes-Oxley eliminated the need for financial reporting."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>According to the chapter, the 1934 Act established the SEC as the federal regulator, while Sarbanes-Oxley tightened accounting and audit guidelines to reduce corporate fraud.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Learn each regulation by the market failure or institutional function it addresses.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A corporate executive trades shares using material nonpublic information about an impending earnings announcement. Which statement best matches the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "The activity is automatically permitted because the executive owns the shares."], ["B", "It is a form of ordinary diversification."], ["C", "The trade is legitimate as long as a broker places it."], ["D", "It raises insider-trading concerns because nonpublic company information is being used in securities transactions."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides identify insider trading as using nonpublic company information for profitable securities transactions. Ownership of the security or use of a broker does not itself justify use of privileged information.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The problem concerns information advantages, not the mechanics of execution.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 24, "question": "<p>According to the regular U.S. trading session stated in the chapter, which time range applies to the major U.S. exchanges and Nasdaq?</p>", "options": [["A", "8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., Eastern"], ["B", "9:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m., Eastern"], ["C", "4:00 p.m. to 6:30 p.m., Eastern"], ["D", "9:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m., Malaysian time"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides specify regular U.S. trading hours of 9:30 a.m.-4:00 p.m. Eastern. They discuss extended-hour trading separately. The exact extended sessions in the older slides should not be assumed current.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish regular trading sessions from extended-hours sessions and keep the timezone explicit.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An investor purchases $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$ each and later sells them at $\\text{RM}60$. The investor receives no dividends. Ignoring costs, which description is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A short sale producing $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ profit"], ["B", "A margin trade with necessarily unlimited losses"], ["C", "A long purchase producing $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ capital gain"], ["D", "A long purchase producing $\\text{RM}500$ capital gain"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The investor buys first and later sells at a higher price, making this a long purchase. The capital gain is $100 \\times (\\text{RM}60 - \\text{RM}50) = \\text{RM}1{,}000$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Identify the order of buying and selling before analyzing the profit.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 26, "question": "<p>An investor buys $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ of securities subject to a hypothetical $50\\%$ initial margin requirement. How much personal equity and borrowed money are needed?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}5{,}000$ equity and $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ borrowing"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}10{,}000$ equity and no borrowing"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}2{,}500$ equity and $\\text{RM}7{,}500$ borrowing"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}7{,}500$ equity and $\\text{RM}2{,}500$ borrowing"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A $50\\%$ initial margin means the investor contributes half the initial purchase value. Thus equity is $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ and the broker loan is $\\text{RM}5{,}000$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Initial margin is the proportion funded by the investor, not the amount borrowed.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 27, "question": "<p>An investor controls $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ of stock using $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ equity and $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ borrowed money. The stock subsequently increases $20\\%$, while the loan balance stays constant. What is the gain on the investor's initial equity, ignoring costs?</p>", "options": [["A", "$+20\\%$"], ["B", "$+40\\%$"], ["C", "$+10\\%$"], ["D", "$+100\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The position gains $\\text{RM}2{,}000$ in value. Relative to the investor's $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ original equity, the gain is $2{,}000 / 5{,}000 = 40\\%$. If the stock had fallen $20\\%$, the equity loss would similarly be $40\\%$ before costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Leverage magnifies percentage outcomes measured against equity rather than total securities value.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A margin investor contributed $\\text{RM}2{,}500$ equity to purchase RM5,000 of shares. They later sold the shares for $\\text{RM}7{,}500$, received $\\text{RM}100$ dividends and paid $\\text{RM}125$ margin interest. What was the return on initial invested equity, ignoring other costs?</p>", "options": [["A", "$95\\%$"], ["B", "$100\\%$"], ["C", "$120\\%$"], ["D", "$99\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Use the chapter's margin-return formula: $[\\text{RM}100 - \\text{RM}125 + \\text{RM}7{,}500 - \\text{RM}5{,}000] / \\text{RM}2{,}500 = \\text{RM}2{,}475 / \\text{RM}2{,}500 = 99\\%$. Financing costs reduce the return, even when the asset's price appreciation is substantial.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include both investment income and borrowing interest in the numerator, and use initial investor equity as denominator.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A margin account holds securities worth $\\text{RM}6{,}500$ and has a debit balance of $\\text{RM}1{,}200$. What is its current margin ratio?</p>", "options": [["A", "$18.5\\%$"], ["B", "$54.2\\%$"], ["C", "$81.5\\%$"], ["D", "$120.0\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Margin} = (V - D) / V = (6{,}500 - 1{,}200) / 6{,}500 = 5{,}300 / 6{,}500 \\approx 81.5\\%$. This measures investor equity as a proportion of current securities value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not calculate borrowed funds divided by value and call that the investor's margin.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 30, "question": "<p>A short seller borrows and sells $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$ each. Later they repurchase the shares for $\\text{RM}30$ and return them to the lender. Ignoring all costs, what is the profit?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}2{,}000$ profit"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}2{,}000$ loss"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}3{,}000$ profit"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}5{,}000$ profit"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Initial sale proceeds are $\\text{RM}5{,}000$. Repurchasing $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}30$ costs $\\text{RM}3{,}000$. $\\text{Profit} = \\text{RM}5{,}000 - \\text{RM}3{,}000 = \\text{RM}2{,}000$. A short seller profits when the share price falls.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Short selling reverses the usual trade sequence: borrow shares, sell, repurchase, then return the shares.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": 31, "question": "<p>A short seller sells $100$ borrowed shares at $\\text{RM}50$ and deposits $\\text{RM}2{,}500$ additional margin. The account holds $\\text{RM}7{,}500$. The shares rise to $\\text{RM}70$, and the broker requires a $30\\%$ maintenance margin. Which conclusion is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The margin is $50\\%$, so no margin call is possible."], ["B", "The investor has $\\text{RM}2{,}500$ equity because initial margin never changes."], ["C", "The investor profits because the share price has increased."], ["D", "The margin falls to about $7.1\\%$, so a margin call is triggered under the stated requirement."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The liability to repurchase the shares is now $\\text{RM}7{,}000$. $\\text{Short-account equity} = \\text{RM}7{,}500 - \\text{RM}7{,}000 = \\text{RM}500$. $\\text{Margin} = \\text{RM}500 / \\text{RM}7{,}000 \\approx 7.14\\%$, below the $30\\%$ requirement. Rising share prices hurt the short seller because the repurchase obligation becomes more expensive.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> For a short sale, the broker-held funds are offset by the changing cost of buying back the borrowed shares.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 1, "question": "<p>An investor argues that online trading platforms necessarily improve investment performance because they provide faster execution, more information and lower commissions. Which response is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because greater access to information guarantees profitable decisions."], ["B", "Incorrect, because online investing always produces lower returns than traditional investing."], ["C", "Online tools improve accessibility, but investment outcomes still depend on decision quality, costs and risk management."], ["D", "Correct, provided the investor trades frequently enough."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter recognizes the advantages of convenient research and execution but warns about unreliable advice, excessive trading, transaction costs, lack of a live broker as a safety net and margin risks. Faster trading is not the same as better investing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish technological efficiency from the quality of an investment decision.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 2, "question": "<p>A company's annual report shows that revenue increased by $12\\%$ and net income declined by $4\\%$ last year. How should this information primarily be classified?</p>", "options": [["A", "Analytical, because it predicts future stock prices"], ["B", "Descriptive, because it reports historical company performance"], ["C", "Speculative, because accounting statements cannot contain facts"], ["D", "Advisory, because every annual report contains a recommendation to purchase shares"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Descriptive information presents factual data concerning historical behavior. Revenue and earnings figures in a company report are historical observations, rather than forecasts or recommendations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Observed financial performance is not automatically a forecast of future performance.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 3, "question": "<p>An analyst observes that Company X's revenue rose $15\\%$ last year and then predicts that its shares will appreciate $25\\%$ next year. Which interpretation is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both statements are purely descriptive."], ["B", "The revenue figure is analytical and the price prediction is descriptive."], ["C", "Both statements are guaranteed facts because an analyst made them."], ["D", "The revenue figure is descriptive, while the stock-price forecast is analytical."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The historical revenue figure reports what happened. The share-price forecast uses assumptions and judgment to estimate what might happen. The chapter distinguishes descriptive data from analytical projections and recommendations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Professional forecasts remain forecasts, even if they are based on genuine financial data.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 4, "question": "<p>An investor wants to inspect a U.S. public company's annual financial disclosures rather than rely solely on social media summaries. Which source from the chapter is most directly relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "The company's Form 10-K filing"], ["B", "An anonymous investment discussion thread"], ["C", "A promotional newsletter with no disclosed sources"], ["D", "An unrelated market index price chart"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies Form 10-K as an annual filing submitted to the SEC by public companies in its U.S. context. Company filings offer direct information for verification and analysis.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Primary-source disclosure is more suitable for verifying reported financial results than unsupported commentary.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 5, "question": "<p>A publicly traded company discloses material corporate information to selected professional analysts before making it available to ordinary investors. Which regulatory principle discussed in Chapter 3 is most directly relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "The principle that every investor must earn the same return"], ["B", "The rule that all investments require identical brokerage commissions"], ["C", "Regulation FD's requirement concerning simultaneous public and professional disclosure"], ["D", "The rule that all analysts must own shares in companies they cover"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter discusses Regulation FD as requiring important company information to be disclosed simultaneously to investment professionals and the public. Its purpose relates to unequal access to material company information.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The central issue is selective disclosure, not whether investors eventually earn identical returns.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 6, "question": "<p>An online promoter buys a small company's shares, publishes exaggerated claims to attract investors, and sells their holdings after the share price rises. What scheme best describes this?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ordinary portfolio rebalancing"], ["B", "Pump-and-dump"], ["C", "An investment club's research process"], ["D", "A legitimate capital raising exercise by the issuer"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A pump-and-dump scheme involves promoting or hyping a security to inflate market demand and price, then selling into the artificially created demand. The promoter's incentives may conflict with those of later investors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Ask who benefits when investors act on the information being promoted.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 7, "question": "<p>An unlicensed promoter offers an investment promising $25\\%$ monthly returns with no possibility of loss. The investment structure is unclear. What is the most appropriate response using the chapter's investor-protection questions?</p>", "options": [["A", "Invest quickly because the potential return compensates for missing documentation."], ["B", "Ignore licensing because promised returns are more important."], ["C", "Assume the investment is legitimate if many people recommend it."], ["D", "Verify the seller, registration, risk-reward characteristics and investment mechanism before committing capital."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter recommends checking whether sellers are licensed, investments are registered, risks are understood and investor recourse exists. Unusually high guaranteed returns and opacity are reasons for greater scrutiny.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> High promised return is not proof of legitimacy or low risk.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Your equity portfolio earned $8\\%$ last year, while its appropriately matched market benchmark earned $20\\%$. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "The portfolio earned a positive absolute return but underperformed its benchmark by $12$ percentage points."], ["B", "The portfolio outperformed because any positive return is automatically superior."], ["C", "The portfolio must have lower risk because its return was lower."], ["D", "The market benchmark is irrelevant whenever a portfolio is profitable."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An $8\\%$ return is positive in absolute terms but $12$ percentage points below the relevant benchmark's $20\\%$ return. The comparison indicates relative underperformance, although a complete evaluation should also consider risk and the portfolio's objectives.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Separate absolute performance from relative performance.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 9, "question": "<p>According to the chapter, what most clearly distinguishes a stock market index from a simple market average?</p>", "options": [["A", "An index includes only bonds, whereas an average includes equities."], ["B", "An index measures market behavior relative to a previously established base value."], ["C", "An index always guarantees a return matching inflation."], ["D", "Averages are always more diversified than indexes."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter distinguishes averages based on arithmetic price behavior from indexes reflecting values relative to a base period. Both help summarize market performance, but they use different measurement constructions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An index is a measurement of a specified securities basket, not a promise of investment performance.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 10, "question": "<p>An investor wants a broader indicator of major U.S. equities instead of one limited to $30$ selected large companies. Which index is more suitable under the chapter's comparison?</p>", "options": [["A", "Dow Jones Transportation Average"], ["B", "Dow Jones Utilities Average"], ["C", "S&amp;P 500 Composite Index"], ["D", "Dow Jones Industrial Average"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes the S&amp;P 500 as covering 500 companies across major sectors and as more representative of the broad stock market than the 30-stock DJIA.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The most familiar market average is not necessarily the broadest benchmark.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 11, "question": "<p>An investor wants an index in which smaller constituent companies are not overshadowed solely because other companies have larger market values. Which method best addresses that concern?</p>", "options": [["A", "Use a portfolio weighted entirely toward the largest company."], ["B", "Exclude every company with a market value below the average."], ["C", "Use an index consisting of just one large stock."], ["D", "Use equal weighting, as described for the Value Line Composite Index."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter highlights the Value Line Composite's equal-weighting approach as a method of reducing the domination of an index by large market-value companies. The weighting method affects how index returns are measured.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Two indexes holding similar securities can behave differently if their weighting rules differ.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 12, "question": "<p>An investor is studying U.S. small-company stocks and wants the most directly relevant benchmark among the indices discussed in the slides. Which one should they consider?</p>", "options": [["A", "Russell 2000 Index"], ["B", "Russell 1000 Index"], ["C", "Dow Jones Utilities Average"], ["D", "Dow Jones Transportation Average"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies the Russell 2000 as covering smaller companies and the Russell 1000 as covering larger companies. The benchmark should represent the segment being analyzed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Always match the benchmark's investment universe to the portfolio being evaluated.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 13, "question": "<p>A bond pays a fixed coupon, but an investor purchases it below its face value and intends to hold it to maturity. Why might its yield differ from its coupon rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Coupon payments automatically change whenever the market price changes."], ["B", "The bond becomes ordinary equity when purchased below face value."], ["C", "Yield incorporates the return relative to the price paid, including the effect of maturity value."], ["D", "Yield must always equal the bond's original stated coupon rate."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's bond-yield discussion emphasizes total return associated with buying a bond at its current price and holding it to maturity. The price paid matters because the investor can receive a gain or loss relative to the bond's maturity repayment.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Coupon rate and yield are not interchangeable, especially when the bond trades away from face value.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 14, "question": "<p>An investor's shares are registered in the brokerage firm's name but held for the investor, who remains the beneficial owner. What arrangement is described?</p>", "options": [["A", "Short selling"], ["B", "Street-name registration"], ["C", "A custodial account that transfers ownership permanently"], ["D", "A corporate rights offering"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes street name as securities being registered in the brokerage firm's name while the client retains beneficial ownership. It is a custody and administration arrangement, not a sale of ownership to the broker.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Legal registration of securities and beneficial economic ownership need not be in the same name.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 15, "question": "<p>An experienced investor conducts independent research, makes their own investment decisions and requires only cost-efficient trade execution. Which broker type is most aligned with that need?</p>", "options": [["A", "A full-service broker charging more for extensive advisory services"], ["B", "A professional advisor with discretionary control regardless of fees"], ["C", "An investment club instead of a brokerage account"], ["D", "A basic discount broker focused on transaction execution"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes basic discount brokers as concentrating on electronic execution with little or no research or investment advice. For an investor who does not require extra advisory services, avoiding unnecessary fees is economically sensible.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Paying more for services is rational only when those services add sufficient value.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 16, "question": "<p>A commission-based broker repeatedly encourages a client to buy and sell securities even though the trades serve no clear investment purpose. What is the main concern?</p>", "options": [["A", "Churning to generate commissions"], ["B", "Improved diversification through every additional trade"], ["C", "Regulation FD disclosure violations by the issuing company"], ["D", "A guaranteed increase in after-tax returns"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter warns that churning involves excessive trading of client accounts to increase commissions. More trading can benefit an intermediary while hurting the client through costs and poor decisions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Consider whether the intermediary's compensation incentives align with the client's objectives.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 17, "question": "<p>An investor wishes to borrow part of the funds needed to purchase shares, using securities as collateral. Which brokerage account type is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A cash account, which automatically provides unsecured loans"], ["B", "A custodial account for a minor"], ["C", "A margin account with borrowing privileges"], ["D", "A wrap account, which guarantees leveraged gains"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies margin accounts as brokerage accounts that provide borrowing privileges. A cash account does not serve the same financing role; margin borrowing also introduces financing costs and magnified losses.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An account's purpose is determined by the rights and obligations it creates, not merely by which securities it can hold.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 18, "question": "<p>An investor prefers to delegate investment decisions to a professional manager and pay a flat annual account fee. Which account type in the chapter best fits this arrangement?</p>", "options": [["A", "Cash account"], ["B", "Wrap account"], ["C", "Odd-lot account"], ["D", "Self-directed margin account"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines a wrap account as shifting investment decisions to a professional money manager in return for an annual account fee. This differs from an execution-only arrangement.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Delegating investment decisions is different from merely receiving research or execution services.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A stock is trading actively. An investor must sell their position as quickly as reasonably possible and is willing to accept the best available execution price. Which order best matches the objective?</p>", "options": [["A", "A buy limit order"], ["B", "A good-'til-canceled buy order"], ["C", "A sell limit far above the current market"], ["D", "A market sell order"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A market order prioritizes immediate execution at the available market price. Unlike a limit order, it does not guarantee an exact selling price and can be affected by liquidity or price changes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Execution priority and price protection are different objectives.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A stock is quoted around $\\text{RM}50$. An investor wants to purchase it only if the execution price is $\\text{RM}47$ or lower. What is the most suitable instruction?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy limit at $\\text{RM}47$"], ["B", "Buy market order"], ["C", "Sell stop at $\\text{RM}47$"], ["D", "Buy stop at $\\text{RM}53$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A buy limit order specifies the maximum acceptable price. The trade can execute at $\\text{RM}47$ or below if a suitable seller is available, but execution is not guaranteed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A buy limit sets a ceiling on the price paid, not a guaranteed future purchase.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 21, "question": "<p>An investor owns shares trading near $\\text{RM}60$ and will sell only for $\\text{RM}65$ or more. Which instruction is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Market sell immediately"], ["B", "Buy limit at $\\text{RM}65$"], ["C", "Sell limit at $\\text{RM}65$"], ["D", "Sell stop at $\\text{RM}65$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A sell limit establishes a minimum acceptable selling price. An order at $\\text{RM}65$ can fill at $\\text{RM}65$ or higher, subject to market conditions, but may remain unfilled.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Buy limits establish maximum purchase prices; sell limits establish minimum selling prices.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 22, "question": "<p>An investor submits a fill-or-kill order to purchase $500$ shares at a specified limit price. Only $300$ shares can be executed immediately under the order's conditions. What should happen?</p>", "options": [["A", "The broker must purchase the other $200$ shares at any price."], ["B", "The order is canceled rather than partially filled."], ["C", "The remaining $200$ shares must stay open for six months."], ["D", "The order automatically becomes a stop-loss order."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes fill-or-kill orders as requiring immediate execution or cancellation. The stated quantity cannot be completely filled, so the order is canceled rather than partially executed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse fill-or-kill with day orders or GTC instructions.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A stock trades at $\\text{RM}20$. The investor places a sell stop at $\\text{RM}17$. Overnight, adverse news causes the first available trading price to fall to $\\text{RM}14$, triggering the order. What is the most accurate conclusion?</p>", "options": [["A", "The broker must sell at $\\text{RM}17$ regardless of market conditions."], ["B", "The stop order cannot execute if the price falls below $\\text{RM}17$."], ["C", "The stop becomes a limit order at $\\text{RM}17$."], ["D", "The stop can become a market order and execute near $\\text{RM}14$, rather than at the stop price."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter states that a stop order becomes a market order when activated. $\\text{RM}17$ is the triggering level, not a guaranteed execution price. A gap down can produce substantial slippage.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Stop price is a trigger, not an insurance contract against losses beyond that level.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 24, "question": "<p>An investor places a sell stop-limit order with a stop trigger of $\\text{RM}18$ and a minimum selling limit of $\\text{RM}17.50$. The stock gaps down to $\\text{RM}15$ without available bids at $\\text{RM}17.50$ or higher. What is the main risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "The order activates but may remain unfilled because the price limit cannot be satisfied."], ["B", "The stock must be sold at exactly $\\text{RM}18$."], ["C", "The broker must guarantee execution at $\\text{RM}17.50$."], ["D", "The instruction automatically converts into a market buy order."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A stop-limit adds execution-price protection but sacrifices certainty of execution. Once activated, the sell limit will not accept prices below $\\text{RM}17.50$, so the investor can remain exposed if the market trades lower.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Stop-market prioritizes exit; stop-limit prioritizes price protection but can fail to exit.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An investor has sold a stock short at $\\text{RM}50$ and wants an order designed to initiate repurchasing if the share price rises to $\\text{RM}60$. Which order best fits the chapter's discussion?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sell limit at $\\text{RM}60$"], ["B", "Sell stop at $\\text{RM}40$"], ["C", "Buy stop at $\\text{RM}60$"], ["D", "Buy limit at $\\text{RM}40$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes that stop orders can also be used to buy securities to limit risk on short sales. Since rising prices hurt the short seller, a buy stop above the current price can trigger an attempt to close the short.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A long position is commonly protected with a sell stop; a short position can be protected with a buy stop.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 26, "question": "<p>An investor buys $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}10$ each and later sells them at $\\text{RM}10.30$. Brokerage commission is $\\text{RM}15$ on purchase and $\\text{RM}15$ on sale. Ignoring taxes and other charges, what is the net trading profit?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}30$ profit"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}15$ profit"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}30$ loss"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}0$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Gross capital gain is $100 \\times (\\text{RM}10.30 - \\text{RM}10.00) = \\text{RM}30$. The two brokerage commissions total $\\text{RM}30$. Net profit is therefore $\\text{RM}0$, illustrating how trading costs can consume apparent gains.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A positive gross trading gain does not necessarily produce a positive net return.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 27, "question": "<p>An investor suffers a substantial stock-market loss but the broker remains financially sound and the securities are properly accounted for. The investor demands SIPC reimbursement simply because the investment lost value. Which statement best follows the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "SIPC guarantees investors against ordinary market declines."], ["B", "SIPC protection against certain broker failures is not insurance against poor market performance."], ["C", "SIPC covers every loss caused by an inaccurate share-price forecast."], ["D", "Broker registration converts all investment losses into insured claims."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes SIPC as protecting against certain broker financial failures, not guaranteeing investors against bad advice, churning or ordinary investment losses. The historical slide's coverage figures should not be treated as automatically current.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Protection against an intermediary's failure is not protection against the securities' market risk.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A client and broker are resolving a dispute. Process A involves an informal, voluntary attempt to reach agreement. Process B involves presenting arguments to a panel whose decision is binding if the parties have agreed to binding arbitration. Which classification is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A = arbitration; B = mediation"], ["B", "Both processes are ordinary securities trades"], ["C", "A = mediation; B = arbitration"], ["D", "Neither process concerns investor protection"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes mediation as informal and voluntary, with no binding outcome if agreement fails. Arbitration is a more formal panel-based dispute process, with binding consequences where applicable.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Mediation facilitates agreement; arbitration can result in a binding adjudicated decision.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 29, "question": "<p>An investment advisor is properly registered and holds several professional qualifications. An investor therefore assumes the advisor's recommendations must generate positive returns. Which response is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Registration and qualifications are relevant, but neither guarantees investment competence or profitable outcomes."], ["B", "Registration guarantees that all recommended securities will rise."], ["C", "An advisor's professional designation eliminates market risk."], ["D", "The investor should ignore the advisor's track record and incentives."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter explicitly states that regulatory registration does not guarantee competence. Investors should also consider experience, reputation, track record and whether the advisor understands their circumstances.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Regulatory compliance, professional competence and investment performance are separate concepts.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": 30, "question": "<p>A group of investors forms a legal partnership, pools capital, shares research and votes on investment recommendations. Which practice would most improve the quality of its decisions?</p>", "options": [["A", "Purchase every security recommended by a majority without reviewing evidence."], ["B", "Concentrate all money in securities promoted by the most confident member."], ["C", "Increase trade frequency whenever members disagree."], ["D", "Require members to support recommendations with verifiable evidence, risk analysis and clear investment objectives."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter describes investment clubs as partnerships pooling knowledge and funds to analyze investment opportunities. A disciplined, evidence-based process helps the club make better use of shared research instead of relying only on group confidence.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A collective investment decision is not automatically a well-informed one.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 1, "question": "<p>An investor can receive $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ today or $\\text{RM}1{,}080$ one year from now. Assuming an available risk-free annual return of $8\\%$ and no other differences, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ today is worth more."], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}080$ next year is worth more."], ["C", "The two alternatives are financially equivalent."], ["D", "The alternatives cannot be compared using interest rates."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ today can grow to $\\text{RM}1{,}000 \\times 1.08 = \\text{RM}1{,}080$ after one year. They are equivalent at an $8\\%$ discount rate under the question's assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare cash flows at the same point in time rather than their nominal amounts.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 2, "question": "<p>$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ is deposited at $10\\%$ simple interest per year for three years. What is its future value?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}300$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}331$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}1{,}210$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}1{,}100$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Simple interest is earned only on the original principal. $\\text{Annual interest} = \\text{RM}1{,}000 \\times 10\\% = \\text{RM}100$. After three years, the balance is $\\text{RM}1{,}000 + 3(\\text{RM}100) = \\text{RM}1{,}300$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not compound interest when the question explicitly specifies simple interest.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 3, "question": "<p>The same $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ is invested at $10\\%$ compounded annually for three years. What is the future value?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}300$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}331$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}1{,}330$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}1{,}210$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$FV = \\text{RM}1{,}000(1.10)^{3} = \\text{RM}1{,}331$. Unlike simple interest, previously accumulated interest earns additional interest.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> $\\text{RM}1{,}300$ is the simple-interest answer, not the compound-interest answer.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 4, "question": "<p>An investor requires $10\\%$ annually. How much should they deposit today to receive $\\text{RM}1{,}210$ exactly two years from now?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}100$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}210$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}1{,}331$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$PV = \\text{RM}1{,}210 / (1.10)^{2} = \\text{RM}1{,}000$. Present value is obtained by reversing the compounding process.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Future money must be discounted, not multiplied by the compound growth factor.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 5, "question": "<p>$\\text{RM}3{,}000$ is invested for four years at $7\\%$ compounded annually. What will it be worth at the end?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}3{,}840.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}3{,}210.00$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}3{,}932.39$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}4{,}207.00$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$FV = \\text{RM}3{,}000(1.07)^{4} = \\text{RM}3{,}932.39$. The exponent represents four compounding periods.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> $\\text{RM}3{,}840$ is the simple-interest result; the question specifies compounding.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Two analysts value the same fixed future cash flow, but Analyst A uses a $5\\%$ discount rate and Analyst B uses $12\\%$. Which conclusion is correct, all else equal?</p>", "options": [["A", "Analyst B obtains a higher present value."], ["B", "Both must obtain identical present values."], ["C", "The discount rate cannot affect present value."], ["D", "Analyst A obtains a higher present value."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$PV = FV / (1 + r)^{n}$. A higher discount rate increases the denominator and therefore reduces the present value of a positive future cash flow.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compounding and discounting respond in opposite directions to increasing rates.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 7, "question": "<p>Which cash-flow pattern qualifies as an ordinary annuity?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ at the end of every year for five years"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ immediately followed by $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ annually starting next year"], ["C", "Unequal payments at the end of every year"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ at irregular dates"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An ordinary annuity consists of equal cash flows occurring at equal intervals, with each payment at the end of the period. A payment immediately at time zero does not follow the ordinary-annuity timing convention.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Check both equality of cash flows and when each payment occurs.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 8, "question": "<p>An investor deposits $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ at the end of each year for three years into an account earning $10\\%$ annually. What is the future value at the end of Year $3$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}3{,}000$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}3{,}310$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}3{,}641$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}3{,}300$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$FV = \\text{RM}1{,}000[(1.10^{3} - 1) / 0.10] = \\text{RM}3{,}310$. Alternatively, $\\text{RM}1{,}000(1.10)^{2} + \\text{RM}1{,}000(1.10) + \\text{RM}1{,}000 = \\text{RM}3{,}310$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The deposit at the end of Year $3$ has zero additional years to compound.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 9, "question": "<p>An investment pays $\\text{RM}110$ at the end of Year $1$ and $\\text{RM}110$ at the end of Year $2$. What is the present value at a $10\\%$ annual discount rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}200.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}181.82$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}190.91$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}220.00$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$PV = \\text{RM}110 / 1.10 + \\text{RM}110 / (1.10)^{2} = \\text{RM}100 + \\text{RM}90.91 = \\text{RM}190.91$. The two payments have different discounting horizons.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Equal cash flows do not have equal present values when received at different dates.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 10, "question": "<p>An investment pays $\\text{RM}100$ in Year $1$, $\\text{RM}200$ in Year $2$ and $\\text{RM}300$ in Year $3$. Using a $10\\%$ annual discount rate, what is its present value?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}600.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}545.45$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}500.00$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}481.59$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$PV = 100 / 1.10 + 200 / (1.10)^{2} + 300 / (1.10)^{3} = \\text{RM}481.59$. Unequal payments are discounted individually before summing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not use the ordinary-annuity formula because these cash flows are unequal.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 11, "question": "<p>You must choose between $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ immediately and $\\text{RM}1{,}200$ in three years. Assuming an $8\\%$ annual required return and otherwise identical risk, which is financially preferable?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}200$ in three years, because its nominal amount is higher."], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ immediately, because $\\text{RM}1{,}200$ in three years has a present value of about $\\text{RM}952.60$."], ["C", "Both are equivalent at an $8\\%$ rate."], ["D", "The answer depends only on the investor's age."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV of $\\text{RM}1{,}200 = \\text{RM}1{,}200 / (1.08)^{3} \\approx \\text{RM}952.60$, which is less than $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ now. Therefore the immediate payment is more valuable at the stated required return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A larger future payment can have a smaller present value.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 12, "question": "<p>Investor A invests $\\text{RM}8{,}000$ immediately. Investor B invests $\\text{RM}2{,}000$ at each year-end for four years. Both earn the same positive compound return. At the end of Year $4$, who has more wealth?</p>", "options": [["A", "Investor A"], ["B", "Investor B"], ["C", "Both must have identical balances because total deposits are $\\text{RM}8{,}000$."], ["D", "Neither can earn interest under these deposit arrangements."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Investor A's entire $\\text{RM}8{,}000$ earns interest for four years. Investor B's four payments have three, two, one and zero years to compound respectively. Earlier cash flows gain more compounding time.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Equal total contributions do not imply equal future values.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 13, "question": "<p>$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ is invested at a nominal annual rate of $8\\%$, compounded semiannually, for two years. What is the future value?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}160.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}166.40$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}1{,}169.86$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}1{,}188.10$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The periodic rate is $8\\% / 2 = 4\\%$ and there are $2 \\times 2 = 4$ periods. $FV = \\text{RM}1{,}000(1.04)^{4} = \\text{RM}1{,}169.86$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Adjust both the interest rate per period and the number of periods.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 14, "question": "<p>A savings product quotes $12\\%$ nominal annual interest compounded monthly. What is the approximate effective annual rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "$12.00\\%$"], ["B", "$12.36\\%$"], ["C", "$13.00\\%$"], ["D", "$12.68\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$EAR = (1 + 0.12 / 12)^{12} - 1 = (1.01)^{12} - 1 \\approx 12.68\\%$. Monthly compounding creates interest on interest within the year.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not compare quoted nominal rates without accounting for compounding frequency.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 15, "question": "<p>An investment compounds monthly for five years at a nominal annual interest rate of $6\\%$. Which N and periodic interest-rate inputs are appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "$N = 5$; $\\text{I} = 6\\%$"], ["B", "$N = 60$; $\\text{I} = 0.5\\%$"], ["C", "$N = 60$; $\\text{I} = 6\\%$"], ["D", "$N = 12$; $\\text{I} = 0.5\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>There are $12$ periods per year for five years, so $N = 60$. The monthly periodic rate is $6\\% / 12 = 0.5\\%$. This is the setup when using a calculator's periodic-rate convention.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Never mix an annual rate with a monthly number of periods.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 16, "question": "<p>An investment pays $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ immediately at time zero, then $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ at the end of Years $1$ and $2$. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is a mixed stream because no two payments are equal."], ["B", "It is a standard ordinary annuity starting at Year $1$."], ["C", "It is an annuity-due pattern rather than an ordinary annuity."], ["D", "It has no time-value-of-money effect because all amounts are equal."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Payments at time zero and at the beginning of subsequent periods form an annuity-due timing pattern. The appendix explicitly defines ordinary annuities as payments at period-end; applying that formula without timing adjustment would misvalue the stream.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The appendix focuses on ordinary annuities. Annuity-due terminology is an additional classification for this timing pattern.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 17, "question": "<p>A financial calculator or spreadsheet treats an initial investment as a cash outflow and a future payout as a cash inflow. Which sign convention is consistent with that perspective?</p>", "options": [["A", "Initial investment negative; future payout positive"], ["B", "Both amounts negative"], ["C", "Initial investment positive; future payout negative, regardless of perspective"], ["D", "Both amounts must be positive"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>From the investor's perspective, committing capital is a negative cash flow and receiving proceeds is positive. Opposite signs permit financial functions to represent transfers through time consistently.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A negative PV is not necessarily a bad investment; it often indicates an initial cash outflow.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 18, "question": "<p>An investment requires $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ today and pays $\\text{RM}3{,}000$ at the end of each of the next two years. The required return is $10\\%$. Which decision follows present-value analysis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Reject, because the investment has two payments rather than one."], ["B", "Accept, because the PV of benefits is about $\\text{RM}5{,}206.61$, exceeding the cost."], ["C", "Reject, because the first $\\text{RM}3{,}000$ is less than $\\text{RM}5{,}000$."], ["D", "Accept only if its nominal benefits exceed $\\text{RM}7{,}000$."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{PV of benefits} = 3{,}000 / 1.10 + 3{,}000 / (1.10)^{2} = \\text{RM}5{,}206.61$. $NPV = \\text{RM}5{,}206.61 - \\text{RM}5{,}000 = +\\text{RM}206.61$. Under the assumed discount rate and cash flows, benefits exceed costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The decision rule is based on discounted benefits relative to present costs, not arbitrary nominal thresholds.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 19, "question": "<p>The appendix's mixed-stream example pays $30$, $40$, $50$, $60$ and $70$ at successive year-ends. Discounted at $8\\%$, what is its approximate total present value?</p>", "options": [["A", "$250.00$"], ["B", "$220.00$"], ["C", "$210.38$"], ["D", "$193.51$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Table 4A.5 reports individual discounted values of approximately $27.78$, $34.29$, $39.69$, $44.10$ and $47.64$, adding to approximately $193.51$ using the table's underlying values.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Summing the undiscounted payments produces $250$, not the present value.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 20, "question": "<p>Two otherwise identical investments quote the same positive nominal annual interest rate, but one compounds annually and the other monthly. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The monthly-compounded investment has a higher effective annual rate, with diminishing incremental gains as compounding becomes increasingly frequent."], ["B", "Both must have the same effective annual rate."], ["C", "Annual compounding necessarily produces higher final wealth."], ["D", "Increasing compounding frequency makes interest simple instead of compound."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>More frequent compounding lets accumulated interest earn interest sooner, raising the effective rate for a fixed positive nominal rate. The appendix's compounding table shows that the incremental benefit becomes smaller at higher frequencies.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The quoted rate and effective return are not identical when compounding frequencies differ.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 21, "question": "<p>A share is purchased for $\\text{RM}100$, pays $\\text{RM}4$ dividends during the year and ends at $\\text{RM}108$. What is the holding-period return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$8\\%$"], ["B", "$4\\%$"], ["C", "$12\\%$"], ["D", "$108\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$HPR = [\\text{RM}4 + (\\text{RM}108 - \\text{RM}100)] / \\text{RM}100 = \\text{RM}12 / \\text{RM}100 = 12\\%$. It includes both investment income and capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A share's price return alone is not necessarily its total return.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Investment A begins at $\\text{RM}1{,}000$, pays $\\text{RM}80$ income and ends at $\\text{RM}1{,}100$. Investment B begins at $\\text{RM}1{,}000$, pays $\\text{RM}120$ income and ends at $\\text{RM}960$. Which investment has the greater total return?</p>", "options": [["A", "B, because $\\text{RM}120$ income exceeds $\\text{RM}80$"], ["B", "Both have identical total return because starting values are equal"], ["C", "Neither can be evaluated because their income differs"], ["D", "A, because its $\\text{RM}180$ total return exceeds B's $\\text{RM}80$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A earns $\\text{RM}80 + \\text{RM}100 = \\text{RM}180$ (income plus capital gain). B earns $\\text{RM}120 - \\text{RM}40 = \\text{RM}80$ (income minus capital loss). This mirrors the chapter's comparison of income and capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher cash income does not guarantee higher total return.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 23, "question": "<p>An investment earns a $9\\%$ nominal annual return while inflation is $5\\%$. Using the chapter's approximate real-return formula, what is the real return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$14\\%$"], ["B", "$4\\%$"], ["C", "$9\\%$"], ["D", "$3.81\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's approximation is nominal return minus inflation, giving $9\\% - 5\\% = 4\\%$. The exact inflation-adjusted return would differ slightly, but the question explicitly requests the slide's approximation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse the approximate real-return formula with the exact Fisher adjustment.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 24, "question": "<p>Suppose the real rate is $2\\%$, expected inflation premium is $3\\%$, and the investment-specific risk premium is $4\\%$. What required return follows the chapter's additive model?</p>", "options": [["A", "$9\\%$"], ["B", "$7\\%$"], ["C", "$5\\%$"], ["D", "$4\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$R_{\\text{required}} = R_{\\text{real}} + IP + RP = 2\\% + 3\\% + 4\\% = 9\\%$. The first two components sum to the risk-free rate of $5\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not omit the inflation component or confuse the risk premium with total required return.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An analyst forecasts a $9\\%$ expected return for an investment. Given its risk, the investor requires $12\\%$. Which conclusion is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The investment must be accepted because expected return is positive."], ["B", "The investor must ignore the required return."], ["C", "Expected compensation is inadequate relative to the required hurdle."], ["D", "The asset is certain to lose $3\\%$."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Expected return is a forecast, whereas required return is the minimum compensation demanded for risk. An expected return of $9\\%$ does not meet a $12\\%$ hurdle. This does not mean the realized return must be negative.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A shortfall against the hurdle is not a guaranteed cash loss.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 26, "question": "<p>Investment A gains $40\\%$ over five years. Investment B gains $30\\%$ over one year. Neither pays interim income. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A necessarily performed better because $40\\%$ exceeds $30\\%$."], ["B", "A's annual compound return is about $6.96\\%$, while B earned $30\\%$ in one year."], ["C", "The investments' annual rates cannot be calculated."], ["D", "Both investments have identical annualized performance."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A's CAGR is $(1.40)^{1 / 5} - 1 \\approx 6.96\\%$. B's one-year rate is $30\\%$. Holding-period returns over different lengths should not be compared without considering time.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A larger cumulative return over a much longer period may correspond to a lower annual compound rate.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 27, "question": "<p>An investment costs $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ and is expected to pay $\\text{RM}1{,}400$ exactly five years later, with no interim cash flows. What is its compound annual yield?</p>", "options": [["A", "$8.00\\%$"], ["B", "$40.00\\%$"], ["C", "$5.00\\%$"], ["D", "$6.96\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$IRR = (1{,}400 / 1{,}000)^{1 / 5} - 1 \\approx 6.96\\%$. The $40\\%$ is the total cumulative appreciation, not the annual yield.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not divide cumulative return by five and assume that is the compound annual rate.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A company's annual dividend rose from $\\text{RM}0.92$ to $\\text{RM}1.85$ over nine years. What approximate compound annual growth rate does this represent?</p>", "options": [["A", "$8.07\\%$"], ["B", "$11.23\\%$"], ["C", "$101.09\\%$"], ["D", "$5.56\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$g = (1.85 / 0.92)^{1 / 9} - 1 \\approx 8.07\\%$. This is the same dividend-growth illustration developed in the chapter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Total percentage growth and compound annual growth are different measurements.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 29, "question": "<p>An investment costs $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ now and produces $\\text{RM}600$ at the end of Year $1$ and $\\text{RM}600$ at the end of Year $2$. What is the approximate IRR?</p>", "options": [["A", "$10.00\\%$"], ["B", "$20.00\\%$"], ["C", "$13.07\\%$"], ["D", "$6.00\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Solve $1{,}000 = 600 / (1 + r) + 600 / (1 + r)^{2}$. The discount rate that equates these cash flows to the investment cost is approximately $13.07\\%$. Simply dividing the $\\text{RM}200$ nominal surplus by the investment amount ignores the timing of both receipts.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> For a stream of cash flows, IRR requires solving a present-value equation rather than using a simple cumulative return.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 30, "question": "<p>The chapter assumes interim investment income is reinvested at the original investment's IRR when interpreting a fully compounded return. If actual reinvestment opportunities earn materially less, what is the main implication?</p>", "options": [["A", "The investment's original coupon automatically increases."], ["B", "The realized terminal wealth from reinvesting income may be lower than that assumption implies."], ["C", "The initial purchase price must fall to zero."], ["D", "The investment necessarily becomes risk-free."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides emphasize interest on interest and the reinvestment rate. Earning less on interim distributions than the assumed compounding rate reduces the wealth accumulated from those distributions. This concerns the chapter's fully compounded wealth interpretation; IRR itself remains a rate solving the original cash-flow equation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A rate calculated from cash flows is not automatically the rate available when reinvesting those cash flows.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 31, "question": "<p>An investor buys shares at $\\text{RM}50$ and still owns them when their market price reaches $\\text{RM}75$. No shares have been sold. How should the $\\text{RM}25$ per-share appreciation be classified?</p>", "options": [["A", "Realized interest income"], ["B", "A guaranteed dividend"], ["C", "A realized capital gain from completed sale"], ["D", "An unrealized or paper capital gain"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A paper or unrealized gain reflects appreciation in an asset still held. A realized capital gain generally arises when the position is sold above its cost, ignoring other tax-specific rules.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Unrealized does not mean the change in market value is economically imaginary.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 32, "question": "<p>An investor demands a higher expected return whenever they are asked to accept greater risk. Which risk attitude best describes this investor?</p>", "options": [["A", "Risk-averse"], ["B", "Risk-indifferent"], ["C", "Risk-seeking"], ["D", "Risk-free"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines a risk-averse investor as one who requires greater return to accept greater risk. Risk indifference means required return does not change with risk; risk seeking involves accepting lower return in exchange for more risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk aversion does not mean refusing all risky investments.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 33, "question": "<p>Two otherwise comparable manufacturers face identical product demand. One has financed most operations with debt and faces large compulsory interest payments. Which risk is especially elevated for the heavily indebted firm?</p>", "options": [["A", "Currency risk only"], ["B", "Liquidity risk only"], ["C", "Financial risk arising from capital structure"], ["D", "No additional risk because operating demand is identical"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter associates financial risk with the firm's financing mix, especially debt obligations. Both companies may face similar operating or business risk, but the leveraged company has additional financial commitments.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Business risk concerns operating performance; financial risk concerns the financing structure and repayment obligations.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 34, "question": "<p>An investor receives a fixed annual bond coupon. Inflation unexpectedly rises substantially while the nominal coupon remains unchanged. What risk has most directly materialized?</p>", "options": [["A", "Only business risk"], ["B", "Purchasing-power risk"], ["C", "Only exchange-rate risk"], ["D", "Underwriting risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Unexpected inflation reduces the real value of fixed nominal cash flows. The chapter identifies fixed-income securities and bank deposits as exposed to purchasing-power risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A constant cash payment may buy less over time.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 35, "question": "<p>A fixed-rate bond's market value falls when market interest rates rise. Which interpretation best describes this phenomenon?</p>", "options": [["A", "An increase in the bond's fixed coupon"], ["B", "A direct reduction in the bond's face amount"], ["C", "A guarantee of issuer default"], ["D", "Interest-rate risk affecting the market value of existing fixed cash flows"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>As market rates increase, existing fixed coupons become less attractive relative to newly issued debt, so the market price of the old bond generally declines. The chapter also includes unfavorable reinvestment conditions in its discussion of interest-rate risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Separate fixed promised coupons from changing market prices.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 36, "question": "<p>A property valued at $\\text{RM}900{,}000$ under ordinary market conditions can only be sold immediately by accepting $\\text{RM}650{,}000$. Which risk is most directly illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Liquidity risk"], ["B", "Purchasing-power risk"], ["C", "Interest-rate risk alone"], ["D", "Corporate debt risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Liquidity risk is the possibility that an asset cannot be converted to cash conveniently at a reasonable price. A substantial urgent-sale discount indicates limited market liquidity.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An asset may be valuable but difficult to sell without sacrificing price.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 37, "question": "<p>A severe hurricane causes a major insurance company to report unexpected losses, immediately reducing its share price. Which risk type best describes the direct company-specific shock?</p>", "options": [["A", "Interest-rate risk"], ["B", "Event risk"], ["C", "Ordinary currency conversion risk"], ["D", "Tax risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies unexpected events, including major hurricanes affecting insurance companies, as examples of event risk because they cause significant and unusually immediate changes in investment value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A sudden event affecting a particular issuer is not automatically the same as a broad market downturn.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 38, "question": "<p>A broad stock market decline occurs following a major deterioration in economic conditions, affecting many companies simultaneously. Which category is most directly represented?</p>", "options": [["A", "Company-specific product liability risk"], ["B", "Only individual management risk"], ["C", "Market risk"], ["D", "Ordinary liquidity preference"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Market risk arises from broader market factors independent of any single investment, such as economy-wide downturns or political upheaval. The same event can influence many security returns.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Differentiate broad economic exposure from a shock unique to one company.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 39, "question": "<p>A Malaysian investor holds foreign securities. Their MYR value declines due to exchange-rate movement, and a new unfavorable tax rule lowers after-tax investment returns. Which two risk categories are illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Business risk and financial risk"], ["B", "Event risk and liquidity risk exclusively"], ["C", "Interest-rate risk and underwriting risk"], ["D", "Currency exchange risk and tax risk"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Exchange-rate changes influence the home-currency value of foreign holdings, while unfavorable tax-law changes can reduce after-tax returns and security valuations. The chapter identifies these as separate sources of risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The same investment can be affected by several risk sources at once.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": 40, "question": "<p>Investment A has an average return of $10\\%$ and standard deviation of $20\\%$. Investment B has an average return of $20\\%$ and standard deviation of $30\\%$. Assuming positive average returns, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A has lower absolute volatility, but B has lower relative dispersion measured by CV."], ["B", "B has lower absolute volatility and higher CV."], ["C", "Both investments have the same coefficient of variation."], ["D", "A has lower coefficient of variation because its standard deviation is smaller."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A has $\\sigma = 20\\%$ and $CV = 20 / 10 = 2.0$. B has $\\sigma = 30\\%$ and $CV = 30 / 20 = 1.5$. A has lower total standard deviation, while B has less standard deviation per unit of average return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Standard deviation measures absolute dispersion; coefficient of variation evaluates relative dispersion.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 1, "question": "<p>An investor's primary objective is to receive regular dividend and interest payments rather than maximize long-term share-price appreciation. Which portfolio type best matches the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Growth-oriented portfolio"], ["B", "Income-oriented portfolio"], ["C", "Speculative portfolio concentrated in derivatives"], ["D", "Portfolio selected solely by historical beta"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter distinguishes income-oriented portfolios, which emphasize regular interest and dividend income, from growth-oriented portfolios, which emphasize long-term capital appreciation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio objectives describe the investor's intended benefits, not simply the number of securities owned.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 2, "question": "<p>Portfolio A has an expected return of $8\\%$ and standard deviation of $12\\%$. Portfolio B has an expected return of $10\\%$ and the same standard deviation. Under the chapter's mean-variance framework, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A is superior because it has lower return."], ["B", "Both are equally efficient because their risks are identical."], ["C", "B dominates A because it offers more expected return for the same measured risk."], ["D", "A must be preferred by every risk-averse investor."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An efficient portfolio offers the highest expected return for a given level of risk. Since B provides more expected return with identical standard deviation, A is dominated on these measures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Risk aversion does not justify selecting a lower-return portfolio when another portfolio has exactly the same measured risk.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 3, "question": "<p>An investor allocates $50\\%$ to Stock A with an expected return of $12\\%$, $30\\%$ to Stock B at $8\\%$, and $20\\%$ to bonds at $5\\%$. What is the portfolio's expected return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$9.4\\%$"], ["B", "$8.33\\%$"], ["C", "$12.0\\%$"], ["D", "$8.0\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$E(R_{p}) = 0.50(12\\%) + 0.30(8\\%) + 0.20(5\\%) = 6\\% + 2.4\\% + 1\\% = 9.4\\%$. Portfolio expected return is a weighted average of constituent expected returns.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Apply portfolio weights instead of taking the simple arithmetic mean of individual expected returns.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Stock A has an expected return of $12\\%$, while Stock B has an expected return of $8\\%$. An investor wants a fully invested two-stock portfolio with an expected return of $11\\%$. What proportion must be allocated to A?</p>", "options": [["A", "$25\\%$"], ["B", "$50\\%$"], ["C", "$60\\%$"], ["D", "$75\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Let w be Stock A's weight. Then $11\\% = 12\\%w + 8\\%(1 - w)$, so $3\\% = 4\\%w$ and $w = 0.75$. The portfolio therefore requires $75\\%$ in A and $25\\%$ in B.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio weights must add to $100\\%$; solve for the unknown weight rather than averaging the returns.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 5, "question": "<p>Why is portfolio standard deviation generally not equal to the weighted average of individual stock standard deviations?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because portfolio weights cannot be expressed as percentages."], ["B", "Because portfolio risk also depends on how asset returns move together."], ["C", "Because all portfolios have a standard deviation of zero."], ["D", "Because diversification necessarily raises expected returns."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter explicitly states that portfolio standard deviation depends on weights, individual standard deviations and correlations. Correlation determines whether individual price movements reinforce or offset one another.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Expected return is a weighted average; portfolio standard deviation generally is not.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Two securities have a return correlation coefficient of $-0.80$. Which interpretation is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their returns tend to move in opposite directions strongly."], ["B", "Their returns must be identical."], ["C", "Both securities must generate negative expected returns."], ["D", "Their portfolio weights are automatically equal."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A strongly negative correlation indicates that the two return series tend to move in opposite directions. Correlation measures co-movement, not average return, allocation or guaranteed performance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Negative correlation does not mean negative investment returns.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 7, "question": "<p>Two assets have standard deviations of $20\\%$ and $10\\%$, respectively. They are equally weighted and perfectly positively correlated $(\\rho = +1)$. What is the portfolio standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "$10\\%$"], ["B", "$11.18\\%$"], ["C", "$15\\%$"], ["D", "$30\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With perfect positive correlation and nonnegative weights, portfolio standard deviation equals $0.5(20\\%) + 0.5(10\\%) = 15\\%$. No diversification reduction occurs relative to the weighted average in this special case.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The weighted-average risk shortcut works here because correlation equals $+1$, not because the portfolio has two assets.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Two equally weighted assets have standard deviations of $20\\%$ and $10\\%$, with zero correlation. What is the portfolio's approximate standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "$15.00\\%$"], ["B", "$5.00\\%$"], ["C", "$10.00\\%$"], ["D", "$11.18\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\sigma_{p} = \\sqrt{(0.5^{2} \\times 0.20^{2}) + (0.5^{2} \\times 0.10^{2})} = \\sqrt{0.0125} = 0.1118$, or $11.18\\%$. The covariance term is zero because the correlation coefficient equals zero.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Zero correlation does not mean zero portfolio risk.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 9, "question": "<p>Two equally weighted assets have standard deviations of $20\\%$ and $10\\%$. Their correlation is $-0.50$. What is the portfolio's approximate standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "$8.66\\%$"], ["B", "$11.18\\%$"], ["C", "$15.00\\%$"], ["D", "$5.00\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Portfolio variance} = 0.5^{2}(0.20^{2}) + 0.5^{2}(0.10^{2}) + 2(0.5)(0.5)(0.20)(0.10)(-0.5) = 0.0075$. Taking the square root gives $8.66\\%$. Negative covariance reduces risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The negative correlation term must be subtracted through the covariance component; do not simply subtract $0.50$ from a standard deviation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Two assets have $\\sigma_{A} = 20\\%$, $\\sigma_{B} = 10\\%$, and perfect negative correlation. Assuming only positive portfolio weights and full investment, what weights would eliminate portfolio variance?</p>", "options": [["A", "$50\\%$ A and $50\\%$ B"], ["B", "One-third A and two-thirds B"], ["C", "Two-thirds A and one-third B"], ["D", "$100\\%$ A and $0\\%$ B"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With $\\rho = -1$, the risk terms cancel when $w_A\\sigma_A = w_B\\sigma_B$. Thus $0.20w_{A} = 0.10w_{B}$, with $w_{A} + w_{B} = 1$, giving $w_{A} = 1 / 3$ and $w_{B} = 2 / 3$. This is the mathematical condition underlying the slide's perfect-negative-correlation principle.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Perfect negative correlation permits complete risk cancellation with appropriate weights, not necessarily with equal weights.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 11, "question": "<p>Three otherwise identical two-asset portfolios have return correlations of $+0.90$, $+0.20$ and $-0.60$. Their weights and individual asset volatilities are identical and positive. Which portfolio has the lowest standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "The portfolio with $+0.90$ correlation"], ["B", "The portfolio with $+0.20$ correlation"], ["C", "The portfolio with $-0.60$ correlation"], ["D", "All must have the same portfolio volatility"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Holding asset volatilities and positive weights constant, decreasing correlation reduces the covariance component and therefore reduces portfolio variance. The $-0.60$ portfolio has the lowest risk of the three.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare correlation while holding other variables constant; otherwise the conclusion may not follow.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 12, "question": "<p>Asset A earns $20\\%$ in a strong economy and $0\\%$ in a weak economy. Asset B earns $0\\%$ in a strong economy and $20\\%$ in a weak economy. Both states are equally likely. If the assets are equally weighted, what portfolio return occurs in each state?</p>", "options": [["A", "$20\\%$ in both states"], ["B", "$20\\%$ in the strong state and $0\\%$ in the weak state"], ["C", "$0\\%$ in both states"], ["D", "$10\\%$ in both states"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In either state, the portfolio earns $0.5(20\\%) + 0.5(0\\%) = 10\\%$. The opposing returns cancel the variation in this simplified two-state example.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An individually volatile asset can contribute to a stable portfolio when its movements offset those of another asset.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 13, "question": "<p>In the chapter's IBM-Celgene example, Celgene has a much higher individual standard deviation than IBM, yet a portfolio holding approximately $86\\%$ IBM and $14\\%$ Celgene has a lower standard deviation than IBM alone. What does this illustrate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Celgene must be risk-free."], ["B", "Adding a highly volatile asset can reduce total portfolio risk when its covariance benefits are sufficient."], ["C", "Portfolio standard deviation is always equal to the smaller asset's standard deviation."], ["D", "The higher-returning asset always eliminates all volatility."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Table 5.1 reports approximately $28.6\\%$ volatility for IBM, $58.3\\%$ for Celgene and $25.3\\%$ for their $86 / 14$ portfolio. The portfolio outcome reflects the co-movement of their returns, not just individual volatility.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Evaluate a new asset by its effect on portfolio risk, not solely by its standalone volatility.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 14, "question": "<p>In Table 5.3, Asset X returns rise from $8\\%$ to $16\\%$ while Asset Y's returns fall from $24\\%$ to $8\\%$. What is the standard deviation of a portfolio containing two-thirds X and one-third Y across the five scenarios shown?</p>", "options": [["A", "$0\\%$"], ["B", "$3.2\\%$"], ["C", "$4.2\\%$"], ["D", "$6.3\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>For every year in Table 5.3, $\\tfrac{2}{3}R_X + \\tfrac{1}{3}R_Y \\approx 13.33\\%$. Because the portfolio return is constant across the shown scenarios, its standard deviation is zero.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not assume that positive individual standard deviations necessarily imply positive portfolio standard deviation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Table 5.3 reports approximately the same average return for portfolios XY and XZ, but standard deviations of $0\\%$ and $4.2\\%$, respectively. Which portfolio dominates under the chapter's stated risk-return measures?</p>", "options": [["A", "XZ, because higher risk is always superior."], ["B", "Both, because mean returns are equal."], ["C", "XY, because it provides comparable average return with lower volatility."], ["D", "Neither can be assessed even under the chapter's two stated metrics."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>XY and XZ have average returns of approximately $13.3\\%$, but XY's standard deviation is zero in the given example while XZ has $4.2\\%$. XY offers the better measured risk-return combination.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher risk level is not itself desirable when no additional expected return is provided.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 16, "question": "<p>A Malaysian equity portfolio is concentrated in domestic industries. The investor considers adding securities from economies whose business cycles are not perfectly aligned with Malaysia's. What is the strongest justification?</p>", "options": [["A", "Foreign investments cannot experience recessions."], ["B", "Owning foreign securities guarantees higher returns."], ["C", "Currency differences eliminate all investment risks."], ["D", "Different economic-cycle exposures may provide diversification benefits."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter emphasizes that foreign markets may not move together with domestic markets, creating diversification opportunities. The benefit depends on return relationships and does not guarantee higher returns or protection from global crises.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> International diversification can reduce some exposures without eliminating common global risk.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 17, "question": "<p>A foreign stock rises $10\\%$ in its local currency, but its currency depreciates $15\\%$ against the investor's home currency. What is the most appropriate interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "The home-currency return must be $+10\\%$."], ["B", "The home-currency return is approximately $-6.5\\%$, demonstrating currency exchange risk."], ["C", "Currency changes cannot affect stock investors."], ["D", "The home-currency return must be $+25\\%$."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The combined home-currency return is $(1.10 \\times 0.85) - 1 = -0.065$, or $-6.5\\%$. The chapter identifies currency exchange risk as a disadvantage of international diversification. The numerical combination applies that concept.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Foreign investment performance must be evaluated in the investor's home currency when measuring their actual return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 18, "question": "<p>Which approach is specifically described by the chapter as typically not being considered a true international investment for diversification purposes?</p>", "options": [["A", "Purchasing shares of a U.S. multinational company with substantial overseas operations"], ["B", "Investing in a mutual fund that holds foreign company stocks"], ["C", "Purchasing American Depositary Shares"], ["D", "Directly holding foreign company equity"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes that investing in U.S. multinational companies is typically not classified as true international investing for diversification purposes, even though multinational businesses may have foreign operations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A domestic company earning revenue overseas is not structurally identical to directly holding securities of foreign issuers.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 19, "question": "<p>One company's share price falls sharply after a lawsuit against its management, while the overall stock market is relatively unaffected. What type of risk is most directly illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Systematic market risk"], ["B", "Interest-rate risk affecting all bonds"], ["C", "Unsystematic firm-specific risk"], ["D", "A guaranteed risk-free return"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter identifies lawsuits and labor strikes as examples of unsystematic risk, since they are primarily firm-specific and may be reduced through diversification.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A large loss is not automatically systematic risk; identify whether the cause affects the market broadly or the particular company.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 20, "question": "<p>An investor holds shares in 100 companies across multiple industries. An economy-wide financial shock causes most stock prices to decline together. Which conclusion best follows Chapter 5?</p>", "options": [["A", "The portfolio must be risk-free because it owns many securities."], ["B", "Only firm-specific risk can explain broad simultaneous declines."], ["C", "The portfolio was necessarily fraudulent because prices fell."], ["D", "Diversification cannot fully eliminate systematic risk arising from common market forces."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversification can reduce firm-specific unsystematic risk, but systematic risk arises from forces affecting broad groups of investments. The chapter's portfolio-risk diagram shows a residual risk floor as more securities are added.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Many holdings do not imply immunity from broad market conditions.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 21, "question": "<p>What beta value does the chapter assign to the overall market portfolio or its broad market benchmark?</p>", "options": [["A", "$0$"], ["B", "$1.0$"], ["C", "$-1.0$"], ["D", "A value that must always exceed $2.0$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>By construction in the chapter's beta framework, the overall market has beta $1.0$. Individual securities can have betas above or below one.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of $1$ represents market-level sensitivity, not an expected return of $1\\%$.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Stock A has beta $0.5$ and total return standard deviation $40\\%$. Stock B has beta $1.3$ and total standard deviation $25\\%$. Which statement is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A has greater total volatility, while B has greater market sensitivity."], ["B", "A is less volatile in every sense because its beta is smaller."], ["C", "B has greater total volatility because its beta exceeds one."], ["D", "Both securities must have identical systematic risk."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Standard deviation measures overall dispersion of returns, while beta measures exposure to market movements. A has higher standard deviation but lower beta than B.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Low beta is not synonymous with low total standard deviation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A stock has beta $1.50$. In the chapter's simplified market-sensitivity illustration, what market-related return movement corresponds to a $10\\%$ increase in the market?</p>", "options": [["A", "$+10\\%$"], ["B", "$+5\\%$"], ["C", "$+15\\%$"], ["D", "$+1.5\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The market-linked component is approximately $\\beta \\times \\text{market movement} = 1.5 \\times 10\\% = 15\\%$. This is the simplified interpretation used in the slides, not a guarantee of the stock's actual realized return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Beta is market sensitivity, not a promise that the security must produce the calculated return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 24, "question": "<p>A security has beta $0.50$. Using the chapter's simplified beta interpretation, what market-related movement corresponds to an $8\\%$ market decline?</p>", "options": [["A", "$-8\\%$"], ["B", "$-16\\%$"], ["C", "$+4\\%$"], ["D", "$-4\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The expected market-linked response is approximately $0.50 \\times (-8\\%) = -4\\%$. A lower positive beta implies lower sensitivity to the market's return fluctuations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of $0.5$ halves the market-linked movement; it does not reverse its sign.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An asset has a negative beta. Why could it be valuable in a diversified portfolio even if its expected return is relatively low?</p>", "options": [["A", "Negative beta guarantees that the investment's own price can never fall."], ["B", "The asset may provide a hedge against some market movements by tending to move oppositely to the market."], ["C", "Its return is always negative."], ["D", "It automatically eliminates all portfolio risk regardless of weight."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Negative beta means the asset's market-related return sensitivity is opposite in sign to the benchmark. This may offer a diversification or hedging benefit, making the asset valuable despite a potentially lower required return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Negative beta does not mean guaranteed profits, negative expected return or complete portfolio insurance.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 26, "question": "<p>Why does the chapter treat systematic risk, rather than total standalone volatility, as the relevant positively rewarded risk within the CAPM framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because standard deviation is mathematically meaningless."], ["B", "Because every security has zero firm-specific risk."], ["C", "Because diversified investors can reduce firm-specific risk, leaving market-related risk as the relevant priced component."], ["D", "Because higher volatility always produces a guaranteed higher realized return."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides describe only nondiversifiable risk as relevant to the positive risk-return relationship under CAPM. The reasoning assumes investors can diversify away much of the firm-specific risk of individual securities.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> CAPM's pricing relationship concerns systematic risk, not guaranteed compensation for every form of uncertainty.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 27, "question": "<p>A portfolio holds $40\\%$ in a stock with beta $1.5$ and $60\\%$ in a stock with beta $0.5$. What is the portfolio beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "$0.90$"], ["B", "$1.00$"], ["C", "$1.20$"], ["D", "$0.75$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Portfolio beta is the weighted average of individual betas: $\\beta_{p} = 0.40(1.5) + 0.60(0.5) = 0.60 + 0.30 = 0.90$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Portfolio beta is a weighted average even though portfolio standard deviation generally is not.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 28, "question": "<p>In Table 5.6, Portfolio V has weights of $10\\%$, $30\\%$, $20\\%$, $20\\%$ and $20\\%$, with corresponding betas of $1.65$, $1.00$, $1.30$, $1.10$ and $1.25$. What is Portfolio V's beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "$0.910$"], ["B", "$1.500$"], ["C", "$1.000$"], ["D", "$1.195$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\beta_V = 0.10(1.65) + 0.30(1.00) + 0.20(1.30) + 0.20(1.10) + 0.20(1.25) = 1.195$. This uses the portfolio beta formula given in the chapter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not take the unweighted mean of the constituent betas when allocation weights differ.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 29, "question": "<p>Table 5.6's Portfolio W has weights $10\\%$, $10\\%$, $20\\%$, $10\\%$ and $50\\%$ and corresponding betas $0.80$, $1.00$, $0.65$, $0.75$ and $1.05$. What is the portfolio beta, and how does it compare with Portfolio V's beta of $1.195$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$1.195$, identical to V"], ["B", "$0.910$, indicating less market sensitivity than V"], ["C", "$1.050$, indicating more sensitivity than V"], ["D", "$0.500$, meaning it eliminates all market risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\beta_W = 0.10(0.80) + 0.10(1.00) + 0.20(0.65) + 0.10(0.75) + 0.50(1.05) = 0.910$. This is lower than V's beta of $1.195$, so W has lower market sensitivity.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compare portfolio betas to assess relative market sensitivity, not necessarily total standard deviation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 30, "question": "<p>The risk-free rate is $4\\%$, expected market return is $10\\%$, and a security has beta $1.5$. What is its CAPM-required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$10\\%$"], ["B", "$15\\%$"], ["C", "$13\\%$"], ["D", "$9\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{CAPM required return} = R_{f} + \\beta(E(R_{m}) - R_{f}) = 4\\% + 1.5(10\\% - 4\\%) = 4\\% + 9\\% = 13\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Apply beta to the market risk premium, not the entire market return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 31, "question": "<p>The risk-free rate is $3\\%$, expected market return is $11\\%$, and an investment has beta $0.75$. What required return does CAPM imply?</p>", "options": [["A", "$9\\%$"], ["B", "$8.25\\%$"], ["C", "$11\\%$"], ["D", "$6\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Market risk premium} = 11\\% - 3\\% = 8\\%$. $\\text{Required return} = 3\\% + 0.75(8\\%) = 9\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A beta of $0.75$ indicates $75\\%$ of the market risk premium, not $75\\%$ of the entire market return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 32, "question": "<p>The risk-free rate is $5\\%$, the expected market return is $9\\%$, and CAPM implies that a security requires $13\\%$. What is its beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "$1.0$"], ["B", "$1.5$"], ["C", "$0.5$"], ["D", "$2.0$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$13\\% = 5\\% + \\beta(9\\% - 5\\%)$. Rearranging gives $\\beta = (13\\% - 5\\%) / 4\\% = 2.0$. Beta can be recovered from the required risk premium and the market risk premium.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> When beta is the unknown, rearrange the CAPM formula rather than guessing from the required return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 33, "question": "<p>The expected market return is $12\\%$, while the risk-free rate is $4\\%$. What is the market risk premium used in CAPM?</p>", "options": [["A", "$12\\%$"], ["B", "$8\\%$"], ["C", "$16\\%$"], ["D", "$3\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The market risk premium is the expected market return in excess of the risk-free rate: $12\\% - 4\\% = 8\\%$. It forms the slope of the Security Market Line in the standard CAPM framework.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Market risk premium is not the total market return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 34, "question": "<p>Assume the risk-free rate is $4\\%$, expected market return is $10\\%$, and a security has beta $-0.5$. What CAPM-required return follows?</p>", "options": [["A", "$7\\%$"], ["B", "$4\\%$"], ["C", "$1\\%$"], ["D", "$-1\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Required return} = 4\\% + (-0.5)(10\\% - 4\\%) = 4\\% - 3\\% = 1\\%$. Negative market exposure can carry a lower required return because of its potential hedging value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A negative beta does not automatically mean a negative CAPM-required return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 35, "question": "<p>Figure 5.6 shows a $6\\%$ risk-free rate and a $10\\%$ required return for beta $1.0$. According to this SML, what required return corresponds to beta $1.25$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$11\\%$"], ["B", "$12.5\\%$"], ["C", "$10.25\\%$"], ["D", "$13.5\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The market risk premium is $10\\% - 6\\% = 4\\%$. At beta $1.25$, the required return is $6\\% + 1.25(4\\%) = 11\\%$. This reproduces the relationship illustrated in Figure 5.6.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Read the SML using its intercept and slope rather than multiplying beta directly by the market return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 36, "question": "<p>In a standard CAPM Security Market Line, the risk-free rate is held constant while the expected market risk premium increases. What happens to the SML?</p>", "options": [["A", "It becomes horizontal regardless of beta."], ["B", "Its vertical intercept becomes zero."], ["C", "It shifts downward in parallel."], ["D", "It becomes steeper while retaining the same intercept."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The CAPM line has intercept Rf and slope $E(R_{m}) - R_{f}$. Increasing the market risk premium while keeping Rf fixed increases the slope without changing the beta-zero intercept.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish changes to the SML intercept from changes to the slope.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 37, "question": "<p>An asset has beta $1.2$. The risk-free rate is $4\\%$ and the market's expected return is $10\\%$. Your analysis estimates an expected return of $13\\%$. Where does the asset lie relative to the SML?</p>", "options": [["A", "Below the SML by $1.8$ percentage points"], ["B", "Above the SML by $1.8$ percentage points"], ["C", "Exactly on the SML"], ["D", "Below the SML by $3$ percentage points"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{CAPM-required return} = 4\\% + 1.2(6\\%) = 11.2\\%$. Expected return exceeds this by $13\\% - 11.2\\% = 1.8$ percentage points, so the asset lies above the SML on these assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A positive expected alpha is relative to the model and forecast; it is not proof that the estimate will be realized.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 38, "question": "<p>Which comparison correctly distinguishes the efficient frontier from the Security Market Line?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both plot total portfolio standard deviation on their horizontal axes."], ["B", "The efficient frontier plots beta, while the SML plots the number of securities."], ["C", "The efficient frontier considers expected return against portfolio standard deviation, while the SML relates required return to beta."], ["D", "Both graphs demonstrate that return is independent of risk."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The efficient frontier describes attainable efficient portfolio combinations in expected-return/standard-deviation space. The SML graphs CAPM-required return against systematic risk measured by beta.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A graph's horizontal-axis risk measure is often the easiest way to distinguish these two concepts.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 39, "question": "<p>Portfolio A offers $10\\%$ expected return at $15\\%$ standard deviation. Portfolio B offers $12\\%$ expected return at the same $15\\%$ standard deviation. Both are feasible under identical assumptions. What should an investor conclude under MPT?</p>", "options": [["A", "B dominates A; A is inefficient on the return-volatility comparison."], ["B", "A is necessarily efficient because its return is lower."], ["C", "Both portfolios must be on the efficient frontier."], ["D", "B must be rejected because higher return always means higher standard deviation."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>B gives a higher expected return at exactly the same standard deviation. Therefore A is dominated and cannot be efficient under the stated mean-variance opportunity set.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An investor who wants lower risk should choose a lower-risk efficient portfolio, not a dominated one.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": 40, "question": "<p>An advisor recommends a portfolio of $30$ familiar companies and claims it is well diversified solely because it holds many securities. Which improvement best reconciles traditional portfolio management with MPT as recommended by the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Choose only the stocks with the highest previous-year returns."], ["B", "Ignore the existing holdings and buy more companies from the same industry."], ["C", "Replace every asset with the lowest-beta security regardless of investor objectives."], ["D", "Evaluate expected returns, asset weights, standard deviations, correlations and portfolio beta, then select an allocation suited to the investor's risk tolerance."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's recommended reconciliation combines practical diversification across assets and industries with MPT's statistical analysis. It calls for attention to correlations, portfolio beta, investor risk tolerance and efficient risk-return trade-offs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Holding many securities is a starting point, not evidence that the resulting portfolio has been efficiently constructed.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A corporation is liquidated, and its creditors and other senior claimants have been paid in full. Which statement best describes the common shareholder's position?</p>", "options": [["A", "Common shareholders have a contractual right to a fixed interest payment before creditors."], ["B", "Common shareholders must receive the original share purchase price."], ["C", "Common shareholders hold a residual claim on what remains, subject to other senior claims."], ["D", "A share is a loan with a scheduled maturity date."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Common shareholders are residual owners: senior claims must be satisfied before any remainder is distributed to them.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Ownership is not a promise to repay the share's purchase price.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 2, "question": "<p>A share is purchased for RM40, pays RM1 in dividends and ends the year at RM43. Ignoring costs and taxes, what is the one-year holding-period return?</p>", "options": [["A", "10.0%"], ["B", "7.5%"], ["C", "2.5%"], ["D", "12.5%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>HPR = $(43-40+1)/40=4/40=10\\%$. The RM3 price gain and RM1 dividend both contribute.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Leaving out income produces 7.5%.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 3, "question": "<p>A share rises from RM25 to RM27 and pays no dividend. An investor says it therefore had no return. Which answer is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because return requires cash dividends."], ["B", "Incorrect, because the RM2 increase is a capital gain, even though there was no dividend income."], ["C", "Correct, because a price increase is always a fixed interest payment."], ["D", "Incorrect, because the firm must have distributed dividends in secret."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The gain is RM2 per share, or $2/25=8\\%$ before costs and taxes, even without dividends.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Income and price appreciation are different sources of return.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 4, "question": "<p>A chart shows that a stock index rose over several historical decades. What conclusion is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "The evidence describes that index and period; future or individual returns may differ and losses are possible."], ["B", "The historical pattern establishes a guaranteed positive return over the next five years."], ["C", "Every individual stock rose in every decade."], ["D", "Stocks contain no inflation or market risk over long periods."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Historical evidence applies to a particular index and period; it does not guarantee future or individual-stock performance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An aggregate long-run result is not a promise for a particular holding period.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 5, "question": "<p>A market index falls from 1,000 to 780. Under the chapter's simple percentage convention, which description is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A 2.2% gain"], ["B", "A 10% decline, meeting only the chapter's correction threshold"], ["C", "A 28.2% decline from the initial level"], ["D", "A 22% decline, meeting the chapter's bear-market threshold"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Decline = $(1{,}000-780)/1{,}000=22\\%$. It meets the slide's 20% bear-market convention.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the starting level as the denominator; other definitions may apply in different contexts.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 6, "question": "<p>An investor values shares because they offer liquidity and potential returns above some alternatives. Which qualification is most appropriate?</p>", "options": [["A", "All shares are equally liquid, so transaction costs can be ignored."], ["B", "Returns may be attractive, but price losses, uncertain dividends and differences in liquidity remain possible."], ["C", "Common shares promise fixed dividends and principal repayment."], ["D", "Higher expected return makes every stock suitable for every investor."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Shares offer potential returns and often liquidity, but both the amount of return and the ability to trade at a desired price can vary.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Potential and liquidity are not guarantees.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 7, "question": "<p>An existing shareholder receives an opportunity to buy newly issued shares in proportion to existing ownership. This is best described as:</p>", "options": [["A", "A stock split"], ["B", "A rights offering"], ["C", "A dividend payment date"], ["D", "A market correction"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A rights offering gives existing holders the opportunity, under its stated terms, to subscribe for new shares.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A split changes share count; it is not the same transaction as issuing shares for new funds.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 8, "question": "<p>A shareholder owns 100 of a company's 1,000 shares. The company issues 100 new shares, all bought by other investors. What happens to the shareholder's ownership percentage?</p>", "options": [["A", "It increases from 10% to 11%."], ["B", "It falls from 10% to approximately 9.09%."], ["C", "It stays at 10% because the number of shares held is unchanged."], ["D", "It becomes 9.09 shares."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Initially $100/1{,}000=10\\%$; afterward $100/1{,}100\\approx9.09\\%$. New shares bought by others dilute the fraction owned.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An unchanged share count does not imply an unchanged ownership percentage.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 9, "question": "<p>A parent company distributes shares in a subsidiary to its existing shareholders, and the subsidiary becomes independent. What has occurred?</p>", "options": [["A", "A spin-off"], ["B", "A cash dividend"], ["C", "A bond maturity"], ["D", "A rights issue necessarily requiring shareholders to pay cash"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A spin-off distributes shares in a business that becomes separately owned.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse a subsidiary becoming independent with selling subscription rights.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 10, "question": "<p>An investor holds 120 shares priced at RM30 each. The company executes a 3-for-1 split. Immediately after the split, assuming no other price change, what is the investor's position?</p>", "options": [["A", "40 shares at RM90, worth RM3,600"], ["B", "120 shares at RM10, worth RM1,200"], ["C", "360 shares at RM30, worth RM10,800"], ["D", "360 shares at RM10, worth RM3,600"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Shares become $120\\times3=360$; the adjusted price is $30/3=\\text{RM}10$. Value remains $360\\times10=\\text{RM}3{,}600$, assuming no other change.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Increase shares and adjust price in opposite directions.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 11, "question": "<p>Why does a stock split by itself not create economic wealth for existing shareholders?</p>", "options": [["A", "It makes the company cancel all future dividends."], ["B", "Shareholders' proportional ownership necessarily doubles."], ["C", "It automatically raises the company's earnings by the split ratio."], ["D", "More shares are normally offset by a proportional adjustment in per-share price, before other market effects."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With a proportional price adjustment, the value of each shareholder's holding and their ownership percentage are unchanged immediately, absent other effects.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher share count alone is not a higher economic claim.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 12, "question": "<p>A firm repurchases some of its own outstanding shares and holds them as treasury shares. What is the most direct effect at the transaction date?</p>", "options": [["A", "All repurchases guarantee an increase in intrinsic value per share."], ["B", "The number of shares held by all remaining investors must increase."], ["C", "The number of shares outstanding to investors decreases."], ["D", "The firm necessarily pays a cash dividend to every shareholder."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Repurchased shares held in treasury no longer count as outstanding. Their effect on value per remaining share depends on price paid and other circumstances.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A buyback is not automatically value-creating.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 13, "question": "<p>Class A and Class B shares belong to the same company but have different voting rights. What follows?</p>", "options": [["A", "Different classes can give holders different rights, so their terms matter."], ["B", "Each Class B share must legally have the same voting power as each Class A share."], ["C", "Both classes must be debt instruments."], ["D", "Every class must have identical dividend terms in all circumstances."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Classified shares can have different voting or other rights, depending on their actual terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Shares in one company need not confer identical privileges.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 14, "question": "<p>Under the chapter's stock-trading convention, an order for 75 shares is:</p>", "options": [["A", "A round lot"], ["B", "A stock dividend"], ["C", "An odd lot"], ["D", "A rights offering"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines less than 100 shares as an odd lot.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Lot labels do not themselves establish a particular transaction fee.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 15, "question": "<p>An investor repeatedly trades in and out of the same share while its price ends unchanged. Commissions and spreads are positive. Ignoring taxes and dividends, what is the most likely result?</p>", "options": [["A", "Each trade increases the gross return automatically."], ["B", "Trading costs reduce the investor's net return."], ["C", "The bid-ask spread is income paid to the investor on every trade."], ["D", "Costs do not matter when the ending price equals the initial price."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Each commission and spread is a trading friction that reduces net performance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A zero price change does not imply a zero net return after costs.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 16, "question": "<p>A company has RM12 million in assets, RM4 million in liabilities, RM2 million in preferred equity and 1 million common shares outstanding. What is its common book value per share under the chapter's convention?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM4"], ["B", "RM8"], ["C", "RM6"], ["D", "RM12"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Common equity = RM12m - RM4m - RM2m = RM6m; book value per common share = RM6m / 1m = <strong>RM6</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Deduct preferred claims under the stated convention before dividing by common shares.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 17, "question": "<p>A company's par value is RM1, book value per common share is RM6, market price is RM10 and an analyst estimates intrinsic investment value at RM12. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1 is the company's current market price."], ["B", "RM12 is an estimate of worth; RM10 is the current market price."], ["C", "RM6 is necessarily the price another investor will pay."], ["D", "All four measures must be equal."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Market value is the observed trading price; investment value is an estimate of what the stock is worth. Par and accounting book value answer different questions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> None of these figures must equal another.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 18, "question": "<p>Two companies each have 2 million shares outstanding. A's share price is RM5 and B's is RM20. What are their market capitalizations?</p>", "options": [["A", "A: RM2 million; B: RM20 million"], ["B", "A: RM40 million; B: RM10 million"], ["C", "A: RM5 million; B: RM20 million"], ["D", "A: RM10 million; B: RM40 million"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Market capitalization = outstanding shares x share price. A: $2m\\times5=\\text{RM}10m$; B: $2m\\times20=\\text{RM}40m$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Share price alone is not total equity market value.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A company reports net income available to common shareholders of RM9 million and has a weighted average of 3 million common shares outstanding. Its earnings per share are:</p>", "options": [["A", "RM0.33"], ["B", "RM12.00"], ["C", "RM6.00"], ["D", "RM3.00"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>EPS = RM9m / 3m shares = <strong>RM3.00 per share</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide earnings available to common shareholders by the applicable share count.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A share trades at RM24 and pays RM1.20 per share in annual dividends. What is its indicated dividend yield, assuming the dividend continues at that annual rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "2%"], ["B", "20%"], ["C", "5%"], ["D", "50%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Indicated yield = annual dividend / current price = $1.20/24=5\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is current income relative to price, not payout as a fraction of earnings or guaranteed total return.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 21, "question": "<p>A company earns RM4 per share and pays RM1.20 per share in annual dividends. What is its dividend payout ratio?</p>", "options": [["A", "3.3%"], ["B", "40%"], ["C", "30%"], ["D", "120%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Payout ratio = dividend per share / EPS = $1.20/4=30\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Dividend yield uses market price in the denominator; payout uses earnings per share.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 22, "question": "<p>A share's annual dividend is unchanged, but its market price falls by 20%. What happens to its indicated dividend yield, all else equal?</p>", "options": [["A", "It rises because the same dividend is divided by a lower price."], ["B", "It falls because the company earns a lower price."], ["C", "It remains unchanged because the dividend is unchanged."], ["D", "It becomes a guaranteed return."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>If the original price is $P$, the new yield is $D/(0.8P)$, which is 25% higher than $D/P$, assuming the dividend stays unchanged.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher yield caused by a falling price does not mean a better investment.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 23, "question": "<p>An investor observes a 12% indicated dividend yield after a steep share-price fall. Which response is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy immediately: the quoted dividend can never be reduced."], ["B", "Investigate whether the dividend is sustainable and consider the underlying risks and investor's objectives."], ["C", "Conclude that the investor has already earned 12% during the year."], ["D", "Treat the dividend yield as identical to total return regardless of price changes."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The dividend could be reduced; investigate earnings, cash flows, risk, price decline and suitability.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An indicated yield is not a guaranteed realized return.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 24, "question": "<p>What does the ex-dividend date generally identify?</p>", "options": [["A", "The date a company's stock is delisted."], ["B", "The date on which a company must issue new common shares."], ["C", "The date on which the dividend is necessarily declared."], ["D", "The date on or after which a buyer generally does not acquire the right to the declared dividend."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A buyer on or after the ex-dividend date generally does not receive the declared dividend; settlement and market rules can vary.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Declaration, ex-dividend, record and payment dates have different functions.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Which comparison between a stock dividend and a dividend reinvestment plan (DRIP) is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A stock dividend provides additional shares; a DRIP applies cash dividends to buy additional shares under its terms."], ["B", "A stock dividend pays interest on debt; a DRIP retires the underlying shares."], ["C", "Both guarantee that the investor's wealth rises immediately by the number of shares received."], ["D", "A DRIP always eliminates investment and transaction risk."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A stock dividend distributes shares, while a DRIP uses a cash dividend to purchase more shares according to plan terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> More shares do not by themselves create guaranteed wealth.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 26, "question": "<p>Which example best fits a growth-stock description?</p>", "options": [["A", "A stock whose dividends are guaranteed by the government."], ["B", "A company expected to increase operations and earnings rapidly, often retaining much of its earnings."], ["C", "A company that necessarily has no risk of disappointing investors."], ["D", "A stock that must also be classified as a bond."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Growth stocks are associated with strong expected growth, often with earnings retained for expansion.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Growth is an expectation, not a guarantee of future performance.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 27, "question": "<p>A large, established technology company has a long record of strong earnings and is still growing quickly. Which classification is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "It can simultaneously be large-cap, technology, growth, and potentially blue-chip."], ["B", "It cannot be a technology stock because it is large."], ["C", "It cannot be a growth stock if it has an established record."], ["D", "Its category labels guarantee a rising share price."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Market capitalization, sector and investment style describe different attributes and can overlap.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> These labels are not mutually exclusive.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 28, "question": "<p>An investor expects an economic downturn and compares a cyclical manufacturer with a consumer-staples company. Which assessment is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both stocks must have identical risk because they are equities."], ["B", "A defensive classification guarantees a positive return in a recession."], ["C", "The manufacturer is necessarily unaffected by economic conditions."], ["D", "The staples company may be more defensive, though its price can still fall."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Demand for staples may be more resilient in a slowdown, but company-specific and market risks still exist.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Defensive means relatively resilient, not loss-proof.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A Malaysian investor buys a foreign share for USD100 when the exchange rate is RM4.00 per USD. A year later the share is still USD100, pays no dividend, and the exchange rate is RM4.40 per USD. Ignoring costs and taxes, what is the investor's RM return?</p>", "options": [["A", "10%"], ["B", "0%"], ["C", "-10%"], ["D", "40%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Initial value = $100\\times4.00=\\text{RM}400$; final value = $100\\times4.40=\\text{RM}440$. RM return = $(440-400)/400=10\\%$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A flat foreign-currency stock price can still produce a home-currency gain or loss.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": 30, "question": "<p>An investor needs dependable spending money soon and cannot tolerate significant losses. A friend suggests concentrating all the money in a volatile speculative stock because it could double. Which response is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy it: the possibility of doubling guarantees suitability."], ["B", "Buy it because frequent trading eliminates market risk."], ["C", "Assess the investor's liquidity, loss tolerance, diversification and income needs; the concentrated speculative position conflicts with the stated constraints."], ["D", "Assume the share must distribute a fixed dividend."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A concentrated speculative position exposes urgently needed funds to significant loss and does not supply dependable income.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Potential upside alone does not establish suitability.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 1, "question": "<p>Which sequence best represents the chapter's traditional top-down security analysis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Company → industry → economy"], ["B", "Economy → industry → company"], ["C", "Portfolio beta → shareholder vote → dividend date"], ["D", "Market price → share count → broker selection"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Top-down analysis moves from the economy to industry conditions to a particular company.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Beginning with a company's accounting figures is not the traditional top-down sequence.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 2, "question": "<p>An analyst estimates a stock's intrinsic value at RM14 when its market price is RM11. What does this comparison establish?</p>", "options": [["A", "The analyst estimates the stock is undervalued, subject to assumptions and risk."], ["B", "Its future price is guaranteed to reach RM14."], ["C", "The company's accounting book value must equal RM14."], ["D", "Every investor should buy, regardless of circumstances."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The estimate exceeds the current price by RM3, but depends on forecasts, required return and risks.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Intrinsic value is an estimate, not a guaranteed future price.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 3, "question": "<p>A firm raises sales after an economic expansion that investors widely anticipated months earlier. Why might its stock still fall when the result is announced?</p>", "options": [["A", "The result may be weaker than the higher growth investors had already priced in."], ["B", "Stock prices never reflect expectations."], ["C", "Rising sales always require falling earnings."], ["D", "A positive economic result legally forces stock prices down."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Prices can respond to the difference between the result and prior expectations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Positive growth does not necessarily mean a positive surprise.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Which pair correctly identifies examples of fiscal and monetary policy, respectively?</p>", "options": [["A", "Government changes expenditure; government changes tax rates."], ["B", "Central bank changes its policy rate; a company pays a dividend."], ["C", "Government changes expenditure; central bank changes its policy interest rate."], ["D", "An individual buys shares; a company raises prices."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Government spending is fiscal policy; central-bank interest-rate policy is monetary policy.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Tax and government expenditure are both fiscal examples.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 5, "question": "<p>A central bank raises interest rates. Which company deserves particular analysis of this change?</p>", "options": [["A", "A firm cannot be affected unless it is a bank."], ["B", "Every company will necessarily experience the same change in earnings."], ["C", "A highly indebted property developer whose demand and financing costs are rate-sensitive."], ["D", "Any company with shares, because rates determine its exact stock price mechanically."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Higher rates can raise refinancing costs and affect rate-sensitive property demand; the magnitude depends on contracts and conditions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An economic driver can affect firms differently.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Which economic measure most directly tracks the market value of final goods and services produced within an economy over a period?</p>", "options": [["A", "Book value per share"], ["B", "Gross domestic product"], ["C", "Dividend payout ratio"], ["D", "Current ratio"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>GDP measures the market value of final goods and services produced domestically during a period.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> GDP is a flow, not company equity or a stock-price ratio.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 7, "question": "<p>An industry is young, products are unproven, and future market adoption is highly uncertain. Which lifecycle stage best fits the chapter's framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "Initial development"], ["B", "Mature growth"], ["C", "Stability by definition"], ["D", "Guaranteed decline"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Initial development is characterized by a new industry, uncertain adoption and substantial risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A young industry is not guaranteed to mature successfully.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Industry sales are growing rapidly, but many new firms are entering and prices are falling. What is the best conclusion?</p>", "options": [["A", "Industry growth ensures every firm's profit margin will rise."], ["B", "Company analysis is unnecessary in a growing industry."], ["C", "Competition and pricing power must be assessed alongside industry growth."], ["D", "Rapid growth guarantees every stock is cheap."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Sales expansion can coexist with intense entry, lower prices and weaker margins.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A good industry growth rate does not ensure good company profitability or a low stock price.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 9, "question": "<p>A December 31 balance sheet primarily describes:</p>", "options": [["A", "Cash sales throughout December only."], ["B", "Only next year's predicted dividends."], ["C", "Revenue and expenses over an entire financial year."], ["D", "Assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A balance sheet reports assets, liabilities and shareholders' equity as of a date.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not confuse a snapshot with an annual flow statement.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Which statement best distinguishes the income statement from the cash-flow statement?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both always show the same net amount."], ["B", "The income statement measures profit for a period; the cash-flow statement tracks cash inflows and outflows."], ["C", "An income statement is a point-in-time snapshot of assets."], ["D", "Borrowing money is revenue in every accounting context."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Accrual profit and actual cash movements answer related but different questions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Profit and cash need not coincide in amount or timing.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 11, "question": "<p>A firm records RM100,000 in credit sales at year-end. The customers have not yet paid. Which is most accurate, assuming recognition criteria are met?</p>", "options": [["A", "Revenue and receivables may increase even though cash has not yet been collected."], ["B", "Cash must increase by RM100,000 when revenue is recorded."], ["C", "Liabilities must increase by RM100,000 because sales are recorded."], ["D", "No revenue can ever be recognized before cash is received."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Recognized credit sales can increase revenue and accounts receivable while cash is unchanged until collection.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Recognized revenue does not always mean cash has arrived.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 12, "question": "<p>A company reports rising net income while cash from operations declines and receivables grow much faster than sales. Which action is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ignore receivables because reported profits eliminate liquidity risk."], ["B", "Conclude immediately that fraud is proven."], ["C", "Investigate credit terms, collection quality and the reasons cash and profit differ."], ["D", "Conclude that every receivable is uncollectible."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>These trends justify investigating working capital, cash conversion and any accounting explanations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A warning signal is not proof of fraud or of uncollectibility.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 13, "question": "<p>Current assets total RM600,000 and current liabilities total RM300,000. What is the current ratio?</p>", "options": [["A", "0.5 times"], ["B", "200,000 times"], ["C", "RM300,000"], ["D", "2.0 times"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current ratio = RM600,000 / RM300,000 = <strong>2.0 times</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A ratio is unitless, whereas working capital is a monetary amount.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 14, "question": "<p>Using the Q13 figures, net working capital is:</p>", "options": [["A", "RM300,000"], ["B", "2.0 times"], ["C", "RM900,000"], ["D", "RM180 million"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Net working capital = RM600,000 - RM300,000 = <strong>RM300,000</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Subtract; do not reuse the current ratio as a monetary figure.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 15, "question": "<p>A firm's current ratio rises from 1.5 to 2.5, mainly because slow-moving inventory has accumulated. What is the best interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Liquidity has certainly improved in every respect."], ["B", "The firm has no short-term obligations."], ["C", "The headline ratio rose, but inventory quality and cash conversion require investigation."], ["D", "The rise guarantees a higher share price."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The ratio includes inventory, but slow-moving stock may not convert to cash promptly or at its stated value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher headline ratio does not establish the quality of current assets.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 16, "question": "<p>Annual credit sales are RM1.2 million and average accounts receivable are RM200,000. Using credit sales / average receivables, what is receivables turnover?</p>", "options": [["A", "0.17 times"], ["B", "5 times"], ["C", "6 times"], ["D", "12 times"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Receivables turnover = RM1.2m / RM0.2m = <strong>6 times per year</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the stated credit-sales and average-balance convention; check the denominator.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Annual cost of goods sold is RM900,000 and average inventory is RM150,000. Using cost of goods sold / average inventory, inventory turnover is:</p>", "options": [["A", "6 times"], ["B", "0.17 times"], ["C", "15 times"], ["D", "RM750,000"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Inventory turnover = RM900,000 / RM150,000 = <strong>6 times per year</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The numerator here is cost of goods sold, not sales revenue.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 18, "question": "<p>A firm's inventory turnover jumps after it reduces inventory substantially. Which further question is most useful before calling this an unqualified improvement?</p>", "options": [["A", "Does any inventory appear on any other firm's balance sheet?"], ["B", "Is its par value identical to its share price?"], ["C", "Did it change its company name?"], ["D", "Did shortages cause missed sales or weaken service?"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Less inventory can improve turnover but can also cause shortages or lost sales.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher turnover is not universally beneficial.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 19, "question": "<p>Annual sales are RM3 million and average total assets are RM1.5 million. Total asset turnover is:</p>", "options": [["A", "0.5 times"], ["B", "2.0 times"], ["C", "1.5 times"], ["D", "4.5 times"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Asset turnover = RM3m / RM1.5m = <strong>2.0 times per year</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Sales is the numerator; assets are the denominator.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 20, "question": "<p>Using total liabilities as the question's debt measure, a company has liabilities of RM800,000 and equity of RM400,000. Its debt-to-equity ratio is:</p>", "options": [["A", "0.5 times"], ["B", "2.0 times"], ["C", "1.0 times"], ["D", "3.0 times"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Debt-to-equity = RM800,000 / RM400,000 = <strong>2.0 times</strong> using the defined debt measure.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Some sources use interest-bearing debt instead of total liabilities; follow the definition supplied.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 21, "question": "<p>A company has annual EBIT of RM500,000 and interest expense of RM100,000. Its times-interest-earned ratio is:</p>", "options": [["A", "5 times"], ["B", "4 times"], ["C", "0.2 times"], ["D", "6 times"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Times interest earned = EBIT / interest expense = RM500,000 / RM100,000 = <strong>5 times</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> EBIT, not revenue or equity, is the specified coverage numerator.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Sales are RM2 million and net profit is RM160,000 for the year. What is net profit margin?</p>", "options": [["A", "4%"], ["B", "8%"], ["C", "12.5%"], ["D", "80%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Net margin = RM160,000 / RM2,000,000 = <strong>8%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide profit by sales, then express the result as a percentage.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A company earns RM120,000 net income over a year. Average total assets are RM1 million and average equity is RM600,000. Using net income divided by each average balance, what are ROA and ROE?</p>", "options": [["A", "ROA 12%; ROE 12%"], ["B", "ROA 20%; ROE 12%"], ["C", "ROA 8%; ROE 40%"], ["D", "ROA 12%; ROE 20%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ROA = RM120,000 / RM1,000,000 = <strong>12%</strong>; ROE = RM120,000 / RM600,000 = <strong>20%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Distinguish the assets and equity denominators and use the stated average balances.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 24, "question": "<p>A firm's annual net profit margin is 5%, sales are RM2 million, and average assets are RM1 million. Using net income / average assets, what is ROA?</p>", "options": [["A", "2.5%"], ["B", "10%"], ["C", "5%"], ["D", "40%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Asset turnover = RM2m / RM1m = 2; ROA = margin x turnover = $5\\%\\times2=10\\%$. Equivalently, net income is RM100,000 and RM100,000 / RM1m = 10%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Profit margin alone is not ROA.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Two otherwise comparable companies have the same ROA, but Company B finances a larger portion of its assets with debt. What is the best inference?</p>", "options": [["A", "B's higher debt guarantees higher profits in all conditions."], ["B", "B may have higher ROE through leverage, but it may also face more financial risk."], ["C", "Both must have identical ROE regardless of their equity balances."], ["D", "Debt cannot influence returns to shareholders."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With positive equity and the stated comparable conditions, leverage can raise the equity multiplier and ROE while adding obligations and downside risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher ROE alone does not prove stronger operations or a safer firm.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 26, "question": "<p>A stock trades at RM30 and has trailing annual earnings per share of RM2. Its trailing P/E is:</p>", "options": [["A", "6 times"], ["B", "60 times"], ["C", "28 times"], ["D", "15 times"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Trailing P/E = RM30 / RM2 = <strong>15 times</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use earnings per share for the same specified basis; trailing and forward estimates differ.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 27, "question": "<p>Company A has a lower P/E than Company B. Which conclusion is best supported by that fact alone?</p>", "options": [["A", "A costs less per unit of its stated earnings, but growth, risk, accounting, and earnings quality require further analysis."], ["B", "A is certainly undervalued and will outperform B."], ["C", "A must have the lower share price in currency units."], ["D", "A's earnings are guaranteed to rise."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A lower P/E is a lower price per unit of the specified earnings, not proof of mispricing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Growth, risk and sustainability can justify different multiples.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A company has a P/E of 18 and expected annual earnings growth of 12%. If the course convention expresses growth as the whole-number percentage 12, its PEG is:</p>", "options": [["A", "0.67"], ["B", "6"], ["C", "1.5"], ["D", "150"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Using the question's convention, PEG = 18 / 12 = <strong>1.5</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the stated percentage convention; PEG is a rough comparison, not proof of fair value.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A company has common equity of RM8 million, 2 million common shares, and a share price of RM10. What is price-to-book?</p>", "options": [["A", "0.4 times"], ["B", "2.0 times"], ["C", "4.0 times"], ["D", "2.5 times"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Book value per share = RM8m / 2m = RM4; P/B = RM10 / RM4 = <strong>2.5 times</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide market price per share by book value per share; a low P/B is not automatically cheap.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": 30, "question": "<p>Company X shows growing sales, declining operating cash flow, rising receivables and a rising debt-to-equity ratio. Which investment conclusion is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sales growth alone makes X an attractive investment at any price."], ["B", "The debt-to-equity ratio establishes intrinsic value without other inputs."], ["C", "Any increase in receivables proves the company is insolvent."], ["D", "Further investigate cash collection, leverage, financing capacity and valuation before deciding."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The combination raises questions about collections, cash needs and debt service; further analysis and valuation are needed.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A single positive or negative measure does not settle a complex investment decision.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 1, "question": "<p>An investor says, \"This company had excellent profits last year, so its shares must be a good buy at any price.\" Which response is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Historical profit informs forecasts, but future cash flows, risk and the current price also matter."], ["B", "Past profits guarantee future profits and share returns."], ["C", "A high share price guarantees undervaluation."], ["D", "Historical financial data has no use in forecasting."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Past performance is evidence for a forecast, while worth depends on expected future benefits and risk relative to price.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A strong company is not automatically attractive at any price.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 2, "question": "<p>Last year's sales were RM100 million. If sales grow by 8%, what are forecast sales next year?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM92 million"], ["B", "RM108 million"], ["C", "RM100.8 million"], ["D", "RM180 million"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Forecast sales = RM100m x 1.08 = <strong>RM108m</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Add the 8% increase to the base.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 3, "question": "<p>Forecast sales are RM108 million and forecast net profit margin is 6%. What is forecast net income?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM6.00 million"], ["B", "RM64.8 million"], ["C", "RM17.28 million"], ["D", "RM6.48 million"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Forecast net income = RM108m x 0.06 = <strong>RM6.48m</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Apply the margin to forecast sales, not the old sales level.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 4, "question": "<p>Forecast net income available to common shareholders is RM6.48 million, with 2 million expected common shares. Forecast EPS is:</p>", "options": [["A", "RM2.00"], ["B", "RM6.48"], ["C", "RM3.24"], ["D", "RM12.96"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>EPS = RM6.48m / 2m shares = <strong>RM3.24 per share</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use earnings available to common shareholders and the appropriate expected share count.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 5, "question": "<p>Forecast EPS is RM3.24 and the expected dividend payout ratio is 40%. The forecast dividend per share is:</p>", "options": [["A", "RM0.81"], ["B", "RM8.10"], ["C", "RM1.944"], ["D", "RM1.296"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Dividend per share = RM3.24 x 40% = <strong>RM1.296</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Payout is the fraction distributed, not the fraction retained.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 6, "question": "<p>An investor forecasts that a company will earn RM2 per share in three years and trade then at 15 times that year's EPS. What is the forecast share price at the end of year 3?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM7.50"], ["B", "RM30"], ["C", "RM17"], ["D", "RM45"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Forecast price at year 3 = forecast EPS x forecast P/E = RM2 x 15 = <strong>RM30</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A future forecast price is not yet today's value.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 7, "question": "<p>A forecast share price of RM30 is expected at the end of year 3. What must an investor do before directly comparing it with today's price to decide whether the share offers the required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "Discount the expected RM30 and any expected interim dividends to today using a suitable required return."], ["B", "Compare RM30 directly to today's price, ignoring all dividends."], ["C", "Multiply RM30 by the share count twice."], ["D", "Assume the forecast is a certain outcome."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Bring the sale price and interim dividends to the present, accounting for timing, risk and required return.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Comparing amounts at different dates directly can produce a misleading conclusion.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 8, "question": "<p>If RM100 is received at the end of one year and the required return is 10%, what is its present value?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM111.11"], ["B", "RM100.00"], ["C", "RM110.00"], ["D", "RM90.91"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Present value = RM100 / 1.10 = <strong>RM90.91</strong>, rounded.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Discount future amounts rather than compounding them forward.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 9, "question": "<p>Using CAPM, the risk-free rate is 4%, beta is 1.5, and expected market return is 10%. What is the required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "6%"], ["B", "10%"], ["C", "13%"], ["D", "15%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>CAPM required return = 4% + 1.5(10% - 4%) = <strong>13%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Beta multiplies the market <em>risk premium</em>, not the full market return.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 10, "question": "<p>Holding forecast cash flows constant, what happens to their present value when the required return increases?</p>", "options": [["A", "It remains unchanged at every horizon."], ["B", "It rises because the cash flows increase."], ["C", "It falls because future amounts are discounted more heavily."], ["D", "It becomes identical to book value."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With unchanged future cash flows, a larger discount factor reduces their present value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher required return does not itself make forecast cash flows larger.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 11, "question": "<p>An analyst estimates today's intrinsic value at RM18 per share while the market price is RM15. What is the best interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "The share is certain to rise by RM3 tomorrow."], ["B", "The estimate suggests possible undervaluation, conditional on the model and assumptions."], ["C", "Its realized return is guaranteed to equal the required return."], ["D", "The company must distribute RM18 in dividends next year."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The estimate exceeds price by RM3, suggesting a candidate for further evaluation under the chosen assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An estimated difference is not guaranteed profit.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 12, "question": "<p>A stock is expected to pay a constant RM2 dividend annually forever, beginning in one year. If the required return is 10%, what is its zero-growth dividend-model value today?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM2"], ["B", "RM10"], ["C", "RM22"], ["D", "RM20"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Zero-growth value = $D/r=2/0.10=\\text{RM}20$, provided the first payment is one year away and the perpetuity assumptions apply.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide by the rate in decimal form.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 13, "question": "<p>A company has just paid a dividend of RM2 per share. Dividends are expected to grow at 5% forever, and the required return is 10%. What is the expected dividend one year from now?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM2.10"], ["B", "RM2.00"], ["C", "RM1.90"], ["D", "RM2.20"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$D_1=D_0(1+g)=2(1.05)=\\text{RM}2.10$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> $D_0$ has just been paid; the next dividend is $D_1$.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 14, "question": "<p>Using the assumptions in Q13, what is the constant-growth dividend-model value today?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM20"], ["B", "RM42"], ["C", "RM40"], ["D", "RM52.50"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$P_0=D_1/(r-g)=2.10/(0.10-0.05)=\\text{RM}42$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Using $D_0$ rather than $D_1$ gives RM40.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 15, "question": "<p>Which condition is required for the standard perpetual constant-growth dividend formula to produce a finite positive value under its assumptions?</p>", "options": [["A", "All dividends must be zero forever."], ["B", "Dividend growth must exceed required return."], ["C", "Required return must equal growth."], ["D", "Required return exceeds the perpetual dividend growth rate."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The perpetual dividend series converges under the standard model when $r&gt;g$, with the model's other assumptions satisfied.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The formula's algebraic appearance does not make it valid for every pair of rates.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 16, "question": "<p>A stock's expected dividend next year is RM2 and the required return is 10%. What is its constant-growth value if perpetual growth is 5% instead of 4%, holding next year's dividend fixed?</p>", "options": [["A", "It falls from RM40 to RM33.33."], ["B", "It rises from approximately RM33.33 to RM40."], ["C", "It remains RM20 in both cases."], ["D", "It becomes infinite in both cases."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>At 4% growth: $2/(0.10-0.04)=\\text{RM}33.33$; at 5%: $2/(0.10-0.05)=\\text{RM}40$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The example holds $D_1$ fixed; if $D_0$ were fixed, $D_1$ would also change.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 17, "question": "<p>For a constant-growth dividend model, the required return is 9% and assumed perpetual growth is 9%. Which response is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Divide by zero and use the result as a valid finite price."], ["B", "The standard formula is not valid under these inputs; reconsider the long-run assumptions or model."], ["C", "Apply the formula without considering assumptions."], ["D", "The correct stock value must be zero."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The denominator is zero and the standard perpetual formula has no finite solution under those assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A mathematical breakdown calls for revisiting the model, not assigning a convenient value.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 18, "question": "<p>A stock is expected to pay RM1.10 at the end of year 1 and RM1.21 at the end of year 2. It is expected to sell for RM121 at the end of year 2, immediately after the second dividend. At a 10% required return, what is its value today?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM102"], ["B", "RM100"], ["C", "RM111"], ["D", "RM123.31"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$P_0=1.10/1.10+(1.21+121)/1.10^2=1+101=\\text{RM}102$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Both the year-2 dividend and sale price occur in year 2; the price is explicitly ex-dividend.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A forecast terminal stock price is estimated at the end of year 2 using a dividend model. What should be done to use it in a valuation today?</p>", "options": [["A", "Add the terminal price directly to today's value without discounting."], ["B", "Discount the end-of-year-2 terminal price by two periods and add discounted dividends received before or at that date, without double counting."], ["C", "Discount the terminal price for one period only because it is a single amount."], ["D", "Add every future dividend again even if the terminal price already represents those dividends."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A year-2 terminal value is discounted for two years. Add separately modeled dividends only when they are not already embedded in the terminal value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Terminal value is dated, and double counting overstates value.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A company will pay RM1 at year 1 and RM1.20 at year 2. After year 2, dividends grow forever at 5%; required return is 10%. What is the terminal value at year 2?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM12.00"], ["B", "RM24.00"], ["C", "RM26.40"], ["D", "RM25.20"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$D_3=1.20(1.05)=1.26$. At year 2, $P_2=D_3/(r-g)=1.26/0.05=\\text{RM}25.20$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the dividend <em>after</em> the terminal valuation date.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 21, "question": "<p>Using Q20, what is the stock's value today, rounded to two decimals?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM20.00"], ["B", "RM25.20"], ["C", "RM22.73"], ["D", "RM26.40"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$P_0=1/1.10+(1.20+25.20)/1.10^2\\approx0.9091+21.8182=\\text{RM}22.73$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The year-2 terminal value is not today's value.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Why can a finite-horizon valuation include a future sale price even when dividends during the investor's holding period are small or zero?</p>", "options": [["A", "The sale price is a future cash flow to the investor, but its forecast must be justified and discounted."], ["B", "A future share price can never be uncertain."], ["C", "The sale price is necessarily equal to today's price."], ["D", "The sale price replaces the need to account for time and risk."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Expected sale proceeds are a future benefit, but the estimate needs support and must be discounted.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A terminal price is an assumption, not a guaranteed cash payment.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A company pays no dividend this year but is expected to begin dividends in five years. Which claim is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Expected later distributions can be valued, but their timing and the forecasts' uncertainty matter."], ["B", "A dividend valuation approach must assign zero value in all circumstances."], ["C", "The company has no assets because it pays no current dividend."], ["D", "Growth assumptions are irrelevant when dividends begin later."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Future distributions can have positive present value even if no dividend is currently paid.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A zero current dividend does not imply that all future cash flows are zero.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 24, "question": "<p>An analyst expects next year's EPS to be RM3 and selects a comparable P/E multiple of 12. What implied price is produced by this relative valuation, using consistent earnings timing?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM4"], ["B", "RM9"], ["C", "RM15"], ["D", "RM36"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Implied price = RM3 x 12 = <strong>RM36</strong> at the date corresponding to the multiple and EPS basis.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Match the multiple to the same earnings horizon; discount a future price if comparing it with today.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 25, "question": "<p>Two analysts use the same expected EPS but choose different P/E multiples. Which is the most relevant explanation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their assumptions about growth, risk or comparable companies may differ."], ["B", "P/E multiples are determined solely by the number of shares."], ["C", "Different multiples make EPS mathematically undefined."], ["D", "Both implied prices are guaranteed to occur."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Multiples can differ with expected growth, risk, comparable firms and market conditions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A multiple is an assumption, not an invariant law.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 26, "question": "<p>A firm has negative earnings, but positive sales. Which measure might remain usable as a relative comparison, provided comparability and margins are assessed?</p>", "options": [["A", "A positive P/E calculated from negative EPS without adjustment"], ["B", "Price-to-sales"], ["C", "Dividend payout ratio based on an assumed guaranteed profit"], ["D", "Par value as a substitute for all valuation work"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Price-to-sales can be calculated when earnings are negative if sales are positive, but expected margins and comparability still matter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A calculable P/S does not establish profitability or intrinsic value.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 27, "question": "<p>A stock price is RM18 and cash flow per share is RM3 under a consistently defined measure. Its price-to-cash-flow ratio is:</p>", "options": [["A", "0.17 times"], ["B", "3 times"], ["C", "6 times"], ["D", "15 times"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>P/CF = RM18 / RM3 = <strong>6 times</strong> under the stated cash-flow definition.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Cash-flow measures differ, so compare like with like.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A stock trades for RM12 and book value per common share is RM8. Its P/B is 1.5. What follows?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its expected total return is exactly 1.5%."], ["B", "It must be overpriced by exactly 50%."], ["C", "It trades at 1.5 times stated book value; this alone does not establish fair value."], ["D", "It has no intangible assets."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>P/B = 12 / 8 = <strong>1.5 times</strong>; valuation requires context about asset quality, profitability and risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A ratio above one is not proof of overvaluation.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 29, "question": "<p>Which scenario illustrates <strong>sensitivity analysis</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ignore the current price because the company is profitable."], ["B", "Choose one forecast and treat it as certain."], ["C", "Recalculate intrinsic value under reasonable alternative growth and required-return assumptions."], ["D", "Add two different dates' values without discounting."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Recomputing values across plausible assumptions shows how sensitive the conclusion is to inputs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Repeating one assumption does not test uncertainty.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": 30, "question": "<p>A model indicates RM25 intrinsic value compared with a market price of RM22, but the estimate falls below RM22 under a modest change in the growth assumption. What conclusion is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "The apparent undervaluation is sensitive to assumptions; assess scenarios, risk and an appropriate margin of safety."], ["B", "The RM3 gap is certain profit."], ["C", "The calculation eliminates forecast uncertainty."], ["D", "A higher growth estimate always changes the actual dividend immediately."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A small estimated discount that disappears under a modest input change is fragile; compare scenarios and risks before acting.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Model precision is not forecast certainty.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A company announces higher earnings, but investors expected an even larger increase. Its share price falls. What is the most defensible explanation?</p>", "options": [["A", "A decline proves all market prices are random errors."], ["B", "A positive earnings increase guarantees a positive price change."], ["C", "The market did not receive any information."], ["D", "The announcement may be disappointing relative to expectations already reflected in the price."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Prices react to news relative to expectations; good reported growth may still disappoint investors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Good news in absolute terms is not necessarily a positive surprise.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 2, "question": "<p>In an efficient market, which claim is the best interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Investors cannot lose money."], ["B", "Prices rapidly reflect the relevant information specified by the efficiency form; abnormal risk-adjusted profits are difficult to earn consistently from that information."], ["C", "Every security earns the same return."], ["D", "Prices never change when news arrives."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Efficiency concerns information reflected in price and the difficulty of consistently earning abnormal risk-adjusted returns from it.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Efficient does not mean risk-free or error-free at every instant.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 3, "question": "<p>Weak-form market efficiency concerns which information set?</p>", "options": [["A", "Only undisclosed management plans."], ["B", "Material private information only."], ["C", "Past trading information such as historical prices and volume."], ["D", "All future news before it occurs."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Weak-form efficiency concerns information in past trading data, including price histories.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Private information belongs to the stronger information set.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 4, "question": "<p>If the semi-strong form holds, which approach should not consistently earn abnormal risk-adjusted returns using its specified information alone?</p>", "options": [["A", "Trading after a public earnings announcement using only that already-public information."], ["B", "Purchasing a diversified portfolio for long-term goals."], ["C", "Collecting dividends."], ["D", "Earning a positive nominal return when markets rise."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Semi-strong efficiency implies that public information is rapidly incorporated, limiting consistent abnormal returns from trading on it alone.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Normal positive returns and dividends can still occur.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 5, "question": "<p>Which information set is included in the strong form of the efficient-market hypothesis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Public and private information."], ["B", "Public information only, excluding past prices."], ["C", "Past prices only."], ["D", "Future random events known with certainty."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Strong-form efficiency includes public and private information.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The hypothesis does not mean that unknown future events are already known.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Semi-strong efficiency is supported for a given market. What follows about the nested information sets?</p>", "options": [["A", "It automatically proves strong-form efficiency."], ["B", "Public information includes past trading data, so the semi-strong form encompasses the weak form."], ["C", "Insider information becomes public by definition."], ["D", "Every individual forecast is correct."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Historical prices are public, so semi-strong efficiency includes the weak information set; it does not prove the strong form.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Nested forms do not imply the reverse conclusion.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 7, "question": "<p>Why might stock prices exhibit unpredictable short-run changes in an efficient market?</p>", "options": [["A", "Prices must remain constant forever."], ["B", "News never changes values."], ["C", "Unexpected information, rather than a predictable pattern known in advance, can move prices."], ["D", "Firms do not publish financial statements."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>If predictable information is already incorporated, new unexpected information may drive subsequent price changes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Unpredictable changes do not mean prices are unrelated to value or news.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 8, "question": "<p>An investor identifies an apparent January return pattern in a short historical sample. What is the best next step?</p>", "options": [["A", "Assume it must persist and ignore costs."], ["B", "Assume any positive average is an abnormal risk-adjusted return."], ["C", "Conclude all markets are perfectly inefficient."], ["D", "Test its robustness out of sample and consider risk, chance, data selection and trading costs."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A short sample can reflect chance or selection; risk adjustment, realistic costs and out-of-sample testing matter.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A historical pattern is not by itself a durable opportunity.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 9, "question": "<p>Small-company stocks have historically outperformed in one sample. Which explanation must be considered before calling the difference a free profit?</p>", "options": [["A", "Small companies are risk-free by definition."], ["B", "They may have greater or differently measured risk, and sample choice may matter."], ["C", "Any past return difference is guaranteed to continue."], ["D", "Market capitalization is unrelated to company size."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Risk exposure, model choice and sample selection are potential explanations for observed outperformance.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher realized returns do not automatically mean risk-free abnormal returns.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 10, "question": "<p>After unexpectedly good earnings are announced, prices continue drifting upward over subsequent weeks. This pattern is called:</p>", "options": [["A", "Post-earnings-announcement drift."], ["B", "A stock split."], ["C", "A dividend reinvestment plan."], ["D", "An immediate bond redemption."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Continuing price adjustment after an earnings announcement is post-earnings-announcement drift.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A pattern does not establish profitability after costs and risk adjustment.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 11, "question": "<p>Which statement best describes a market anomaly?</p>", "options": [["A", "Evidence that investors can ignore transaction costs."], ["B", "A guaranteed trading profit in every future period."], ["C", "A pattern apparently inconsistent with a particular model or efficiency prediction, requiring further investigation."], ["D", "Proof that all securities have the same risk."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An anomaly is judged relative to a model or hypothesis and may have several explanations.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Observation is not proof of a reliable future trade.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 12, "question": "<p>An investor assigns a 95% chance of correctly forecasting a stock's next move despite repeated forecasting errors and takes a very large position. Which bias is most directly illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Duration matching."], ["B", "Overconfidence."], ["C", "Currency conversion."], ["D", "Loss aversion."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Overconfidence means excessive certainty about one's knowledge or forecasting ability.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Confidence is not the same as evidence of accuracy.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 13, "question": "<p>An investor credits profitable trades entirely to skill and blames every loss on bad luck or other people. Which bias is most directly illustrated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Diversification."], ["B", "Market breadth."], ["C", "Liquidity preference."], ["D", "Self-attribution bias."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Self-attribution credits oneself for successes and external factors for failures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A profitable trade alone does not isolate skill from chance.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 14, "question": "<p>A shareholder refuses to consider selling a losing stock solely because selling would make the loss feel real, despite changed fundamentals. Which bias is most relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "Efficient diversification."], ["B", "Market capitalization."], ["C", "Interest coverage."], ["D", "Loss aversion."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Loss aversion can make realizing a loss emotionally difficult; selling is not always correct, but decisions should reflect current evidence.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The original purchase price is not by itself a reason to hold.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 15, "question": "<p>A trader assumes a company is certain to keep growing because its last two quarters were excellent, ignoring the small sample and industry cycles. This is most directly:</p>", "options": [["A", "A bond call provision."], ["B", "An advance-decline measure."], ["C", "Representativeness."], ["D", "An inventory turnover ratio."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Representativeness can lead to strong conclusions from a small sample perceived as typical.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Two good quarters do not establish a permanent growth rate.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 16, "question": "<p>An investor assesses a new stock in isolation and ignores its large correlation with the rest of the portfolio. Which bias best fits?</p>", "options": [["A", "Narrow framing."], ["B", "Self-attribution."], ["C", "A stock split."], ["D", "Semi-strong efficiency."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Narrow framing ignores the broader portfolio context, including correlation and concentration.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An investment's stand-alone appeal does not fully describe its portfolio effect.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 17, "question": "<p>An investor repeatedly dismisses credible evidence against a favored thesis solely because it conflicts with the original view. Which bias is most direct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Portfolio rebalancing."], ["B", "Belief perseverance."], ["C", "Currency hedging."], ["D", "Capital budgeting."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Belief perseverance involves maintaining a prior view while discounting conflicting evidence.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A firm conviction is not a substitute for revising a thesis when facts change.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 18, "question": "<p>An employee puts nearly all savings into their employer's stock because the name feels familiar, without assessing concentration risk. This most directly illustrates:</p>", "options": [["A", "Interest-rate immunization."], ["B", "An ex-dividend adjustment."], ["C", "Market breadth."], ["D", "Familiarity bias."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Familiarity can lead an investor to overweight a known company despite concentration risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Familiarity with an employer does not make its stock safe.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 19, "question": "<p>Analysts issue matching recommendations mainly because they fear standing apart from their peers rather than because of independent evidence. This is:</p>", "options": [["A", "A record date."], ["B", "Dollar-cost averaging."], ["C", "Herding."], ["D", "A bond sinking fund."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Herding means following the group, potentially without independent assessment.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Similar recommendations can also result from shared evidence; the question specifies social pressure as the cause.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 20, "question": "<p>Which process most directly reduces the effect of overconfidence and belief perseverance?</p>", "options": [["A", "Treat all losses as proof of external manipulation."], ["B", "Document the investment thesis in advance, consider disconfirming evidence and periodically review outcomes and costs."], ["C", "Read only opinions that confirm the existing thesis."], ["D", "Trade more frequently whenever prices move."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A documented thesis, disconfirming evidence and periodic review can make errors and costs visible.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> More trading or selective evidence can amplify biases.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 21, "question": "<p>Which statement best distinguishes technical from fundamental analysis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Technical analysis studies price, volume or market patterns; fundamental analysis evaluates economic, industry and business information."], ["B", "Technical analysis can only use financial statements."], ["C", "Fundamental analysis ignores company operations by definition."], ["D", "Both methods guarantee a correct forecast."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Technical analysis uses market behavior such as prices and volume; fundamental analysis studies underlying economic and business factors.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Neither approach guarantees profitable signals.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 22, "question": "<p>On a given day 1,200 stocks advance and 800 decline. What is the day's net advance-decline figure?</p>", "options": [["A", "-400"], ["B", "1,500"], ["C", "400"], ["D", "2,000"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Net advances = 1,200 - 800 = <strong>400 issues</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is a daily difference, not the cumulative line.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A cumulative advance-decline line ended yesterday at 5,000. Today 1,200 issues advance and 800 decline. What is today's cumulative value?</p>", "options": [["A", "5,400"], ["B", "5,000"], ["C", "4,600"], ["D", "7,000"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>New cumulative line = 5,000 + (1,200 - 800) = <strong>5,400</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Add today's net advances to yesterday's cumulative figure.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 24, "question": "<p>The number of stocks reaching 52-week highs exceeds those reaching 52-week lows. Under the chapter's conventional interpretation, this can indicate:</p>", "options": [["A", "The exact future return of the index."], ["B", "Broader positive market participation, although it is not a guarantee of future gains."], ["C", "Proof that prices cannot fall tomorrow."], ["D", "Proof every stock increased today."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>More new highs than new lows can be read as a positive breadth signal in this framework, but signals can reverse or mislead.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An indicator is not a guarantee about every security.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 25, "question": "<p>There are 1,200 advancing issues, 800 declining issues, 900 million shares of advancing volume and 600 million shares of declining volume. Using TRIN = (advances/declines) / (advancing volume/declining volume), what is TRIN?</p>", "options": [["A", "0.50"], ["B", "1.00"], ["C", "1.50"], ["D", "2.25"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>TRIN = $(1{,}200/800)/(900/600)=1.5/1.5=\\mathbf{1.00}$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Compute the ratio of the two ratios, using advancing and declining volume consistently.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 26, "question": "<p>There are 1,200 advancing issues, 800 declining issues, 1,200 million shares of advancing volume and 400 million shares of declining volume. Using the Q25 definition, what is TRIN?</p>", "options": [["A", "0.50"], ["B", "1.00"], ["C", "1.50"], ["D", "2.00"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>TRIN = $(1{,}200/800)/(1{,}200/400)=1.5/3=\\mathbf{0.50}$. The slide framework treats lower readings as relatively bullish; a reading is not a certain forecast.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Do not reverse the volume ratio or treat one signal as proof.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 27, "question": "<p>A fund has RM12 million in cash and RM200 million in total assets under management. Under the chapter's cash/assets convention, what is its mutual fund cash ratio?</p>", "options": [["A", "6%"], ["B", "16.67%"], ["C", "0.6%"], ["D", "60%"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>MFCR = cash / assets = RM12m / RM200m = <strong>6%</strong>. The slides interpret higher cash as potential buying capacity, but cash may also meet withdrawals.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A high ratio alone does not predict the market reliably.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 28, "question": "<p>On-balance volume (OBV) begins at 10 million shares. The stock closes higher on volume of 3 million, then closes lower on volume of 2 million. What is the resulting OBV?</p>", "options": [["A", "5 million"], ["B", "9 million"], ["C", "15 million"], ["D", "11 million"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>OBV = 10m + 3m - 2m = <strong>11m shares</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Add or subtract the full day's volume according to the close's direction, not just the price change.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A stock's last three closing prices are RM10, RM12 and RM14. What is its simple three-day moving average?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM14"], ["B", "RM11"], ["C", "RM10"], ["D", "RM12"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Simple moving average = $(10+12+14)/3=\\text{RM}12$. It smooths historical prices but does not guarantee their future direction.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The average is not necessarily the most recent price.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": 30, "question": "<p>Short interest rises substantially. Which interpretation best reflects the chapter's competing views?</p>", "options": [["A", "It guarantees future selling by short sellers to close their positions."], ["B", "It proves the next market return must be positive."], ["C", "It can indicate pessimism, while short covering could create future buying demand; neither interpretation guarantees direction."], ["D", "It measures a company's earnings per share."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>High short interest can reflect pessimism; closing shorts generally requires buying shares, creating potential demand.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The slide's reference to future <em>sales</em> to cover short positions reverses the transaction; covering typically involves buying.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 1, "question": "<p>Which statement best describes an ordinary corporate bond?</p>", "options": [["A", "It gives the holder residual ownership identical to common stock."], ["B", "It is a debt claim with promised payments subject to its contractual terms and issuer credit risk."], ["C", "It guarantees that the market price never changes."], ["D", "It is an option that necessarily converts into equity."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A corporate bond is a contractual debt claim; the issuer may fail to make promised payments.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A bondholder is generally a creditor, not a residual owner.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 2, "question": "<p>A bond has RM1,000 par value and a 6% annual coupon rate. What is its annual coupon payment?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM6"], ["B", "RM60"], ["C", "RM600"], ["D", "RM1,060"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Annual coupon = RM1,000 x 6% = <strong>RM60</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The coupon rate is applied to par, not the current trading price.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 3, "question": "<p>The bond in Q2 sells for RM800. What is its current yield?</p>", "options": [["A", "6.0%"], ["B", "12.5%"], ["C", "8.0%"], ["D", "7.5%"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current yield = annual coupon / current price = RM60 / RM800 = <strong>7.5%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Current yield omits the maturity gain or loss and is not yield to maturity.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 4, "question": "<p>For a standard fixed-rate bond, the required market yield increases while promised cash flows remain unchanged. What generally happens to its price?</p>", "options": [["A", "Price remains at par regardless of market rates."], ["B", "Price rises because coupon payments increase automatically."], ["C", "Price falls because its fixed future cash flows are discounted more heavily."], ["D", "The coupon rate necessarily becomes the new market yield."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A higher required yield reduces the present value of fixed promised cash flows.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Fixed-rate coupon amounts do not reset whenever the market yield changes.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 5, "question": "<p>A conventional bond's coupon rate is 8%, while the market-required yield for its cash flows and risk is 6%. Under standard assumptions its price is generally:</p>", "options": [["A", "Below par."], ["B", "Above par."], ["C", "Exactly zero."], ["D", "Always equal to its annual coupon."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A coupon above the required yield makes the promised cash flows attractive enough to trade at a premium under standard assumptions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Premium means price above par; it does not change the contractual coupon.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Two otherwise comparable noncallable fixed-rate bonds have identical coupon rates and yields but different maturities. Which is generally more sensitive in percentage-price terms to a small change in yield?</p>", "options": [["A", "The longer-maturity bond."], ["B", "The shorter-maturity bond in every case."], ["C", "Neither; maturity has no role in interest-rate sensitivity."], ["D", "Both must have exactly the same duration."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>For otherwise comparable fixed-coupon bonds, longer maturity generally gives greater yield sensitivity; duration captures the timing more directly.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is a conditional comparison, not a universal ranking of all bonds.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 7, "question": "<p>An investor holds a bond promising RM60 annually. Inflation unexpectedly rises while the coupon stays fixed. Which risk does this most directly illustrate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Stock split risk."], ["B", "Shareholder voting risk."], ["C", "Purchasing-power risk."], ["D", "A guaranteed real gain."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Unexpected inflation can erode the purchasing power of fixed nominal payments.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Receiving all promised ringgit does not guarantee preserved real purchasing power.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 8, "question": "<p>A bondholder cannot sell quickly without accepting a large price concession. This is primarily:</p>", "options": [["A", "Guaranteed default."], ["B", "A stock dividend."], ["C", "Liquidity risk."], ["D", "A coupon reset."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Liquidity risk concerns the difficulty or cost of selling promptly at a reasonable price.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Illiquidity is distinct from issuer default, though both can occur.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 9, "question": "<p>A company calls a bond before maturity after market interest rates fall. What risk is most relevant to its investor?</p>", "options": [["A", "The bond must become common stock."], ["B", "The investor is guaranteed all remaining original coupons to maturity."], ["C", "The investor may have to reinvest the proceeds at a lower rate."], ["D", "Its market value was immune to interest rates before the call."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A call can end attractive coupons early and expose the investor to reinvestment at lower rates.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Callable debt does not promise every original coupon until scheduled maturity.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 10, "question": "<p>A bond has RM1,000 par and a call premium of RM40. What is the call price under these stated terms?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM40"], ["B", "RM960"], ["C", "RM1,000"], ["D", "RM1,040"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Call price = RM1,000 + RM40 = <strong>RM1,040</strong> under the given terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The premium is added to par rather than subtracted.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 11, "question": "<p>A deferred-call provision generally means:</p>", "options": [["A", "The investor can never sell the bond."], ["B", "The issuer cannot exercise the stated call option until a specified protection period has passed."], ["C", "Interest will always be deferred forever."], ["D", "The bond has no maturity date."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Deferred call protection delays the issuer's ability to call according to the contract.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Deferred call does not prevent the holder from trading the bond.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 12, "question": "<p>A bond covenant prohibits early repayment using proceeds from a lower-coupon replacement issue for a specified period. This is best described as:</p>", "options": [["A", "An increase in coupon rate."], ["B", "A conversion ratio."], ["C", "Refunding protection."], ["D", "A shareholders' voting right."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A refunding restriction limits early retirement financed by lower-rate replacement debt during its protection period.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Refunding restrictions and general call protection are related but their actual terms can differ.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 13, "question": "<p>A sinking-fund provision requires an issuer to retire a portion of a bond issue over time. What is a plausible investor consequence?</p>", "options": [["A", "It guarantees the issuer cannot default."], ["B", "Debt repayment is scheduled, but an individual bond may be retired before final maturity under the issue's terms."], ["C", "It makes the entire issue common equity."], ["D", "It guarantees that every investor receives exactly the market price at every date."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A sinking fund sets aside resources or retires portions of an issue; specific holders can face early repayment according to the indenture.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A repayment schedule does not eliminate default or reinvestment risk.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 14, "question": "<p>A bond secured by a claim on identified real estate is most accurately described as:</p>", "options": [["A", "A mortgage bond."], ["B", "An unsecured debenture."], ["C", "Common stock."], ["D", "A depositary receipt."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A mortgage bond is secured by specified real estate.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A debenture is normally unsecured; collateral does not guarantee full recovery.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 15, "question": "<p>A subordinated debenture generally differs from a senior secured bond because the debenture:</p>", "options": [["A", "Automatically owns the issuer's real estate."], ["B", "Has a guaranteed government payment."], ["C", "Always has a shorter maturity."], ["D", "Is unsecured and has a lower-priority claim than specified senior creditors."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A subordinated debenture is unsecured and has lower priority than the senior claims specified in its terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher priority affects recovery, not a guarantee of repayment.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 16, "question": "<p>An issue receives an investment-grade credit rating. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "The market price cannot fall when yields rise."], ["B", "The rating is an opinion of relative creditworthiness, not a guarantee against default or price loss."], ["C", "The bond has no liquidity risk."], ["D", "All agencies must agree and never change their assessments."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A credit rating is an agency's opinion about credit risk and can change; it does not eliminate market risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Investment grade is not risk-free.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 17, "question": "<p>Which statement best contrasts general obligation and revenue municipal bonds in the course's basic framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "Revenue bonds are common stocks, while general obligations are options."], ["B", "General obligation bonds rely on the issuer's general credit; revenue bonds rely on specified project or enterprise revenues, subject to actual terms."], ["C", "Both are necessarily free of default risk."], ["D", "Revenue bonds guarantee that project income exceeds debt service."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>These describe the common pledged-payment distinctions, which must be checked against actual bond terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Neither municipal label is a blanket guarantee.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 18, "question": "<p>An investor can earn a hypothetical tax-exempt 4% bond yield. If an otherwise comparable taxable bond's interest is taxed at a flat 20% and no other taxes apply, what taxable yield gives the same after-tax yield?</p>", "options": [["A", "4%"], ["B", "3.2%"], ["C", "5%"], ["D", "20%"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Taxable-equivalent yield = 4% / (1 - 0.20) = <strong>5%</strong>. Check: 5%(1 - 0.20) = 4%.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The tax rate and exemption are hypothetical; actual tax treatment depends on jurisdiction and circumstances.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 19, "question": "<p>Which description of inflation-indexed government securities is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their contractual adjustment can help protect against specified inflation, but their market prices can still fluctuate."], ["B", "They guarantee that their market price can never fall."], ["C", "They eliminate all taxes, liquidity risk and inflation measurement differences."], ["D", "They are identical to a fixed-price bank deposit in every respect."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Contractual inflation adjustments can protect against the referenced inflation measure, while real yields and market prices still move.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Inflation protection does not remove every investment risk.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A bond pays no periodic coupon and promises RM1,000 at maturity. What is it commonly called?</p>", "options": [["A", "An income share."], ["B", "A zero-coupon bond."], ["C", "A floating dividend."], ["D", "A common-stock split."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A zero-coupon bond pays no periodic coupon and is normally issued or traded at a discount to its maturity value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> No periodic interest does not mean zero total return.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 21, "question": "<p>What is a key risk for an investor in a mortgage-backed security when borrowers repay mortgages earlier than expected?</p>", "options": [["A", "The security necessarily has no credit or interest-rate exposure."], ["B", "Earlier repayment always guarantees a higher realized yield."], ["C", "A mortgage pool cannot repay principal before final maturity."], ["D", "Prepayment may return principal sooner and force reinvestment at then-current rates."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Mortgage prepayments can change payment timing and create reinvestment risk, especially when rates fall.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Original scheduled cash-flow timing is not assured.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 22, "question": "<p>A collateralized mortgage security is divided into tranches. Why does the division matter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Tranches remove all uncertainty from mortgage cash flows."], ["B", "Every tranche necessarily receives identical payments and risks."], ["C", "Different tranches may receive cash flows in different sequences and bear different timing or credit risks."], ["D", "The securities become common shares of every borrower."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Tranche rules allocate principal and other risks differently across classes.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Pooling and slicing cash flows do not eliminate risk.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A high-yield bond usually has which characteristic relative to an otherwise comparable investment-grade bond?</p>", "options": [["A", "A lower credit rating and generally higher compensation for credit risk, without a guaranteed higher realized return."], ["B", "A government guarantee of repayment."], ["C", "No possibility of default."], ["D", "Guaranteed positive returns if held for one year."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>High-yield debt is below investment grade and generally offers higher yield in compensation for greater credit risk.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Higher promised yield need not become a higher realized return.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 24, "question": "<p>A Malaysian investor buys a USD-denominated foreign bond. Which statement about currency risk is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The investor's RM return can change as the USD/RM exchange rate changes."], ["B", "Denomination in USD eliminates currency risk for every investor worldwide."], ["C", "The exchange rate matters only if the issuer is Malaysian."], ["D", "Currency risk makes credit and interest-rate risks disappear."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A USD payment must be converted to RM for this investor; the RM value changes with USD/RM rates.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The slides' no-currency-risk claim for USD-denominated bonds assumes a USD-based investor.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 25, "question": "<p>What does a convertible bond's conversion ratio specify?</p>", "options": [["A", "The number of years until a stock split."], ["B", "The bond's annual coupon divided by its market price."], ["C", "The issuer's income tax rate."], ["D", "The number of common shares received upon conversion under the contract."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The conversion ratio is the number of shares obtainable per bond, subject to the conversion terms.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is distinct from conversion price and current yield.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 26, "question": "<p>A convertible has RM1,000 par and a conversion price of RM40 per share. Under the stated convention, what is its conversion ratio?</p>", "options": [["A", "25 shares per bond."], ["B", "40 shares per bond."], ["C", "1,000 shares per bond."], ["D", "40,000 shares per bond."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Conversion ratio = RM1,000 par / RM40 conversion price = <strong>25 shares per bond</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use contractual par and conversion price, not market bond price.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 27, "question": "<p>A convertible bond can be exchanged for 25 shares, each worth RM36. What is its conversion value?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM900."], ["B", "RM625."], ["C", "RM1,000."], ["D", "RM1,111.11."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Conversion value = 25 shares x RM36 = <strong>RM900</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Conversion value is the shares' market value, not necessarily the convertible's own trading price.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A convertible bond trades for RM1,050 and is convertible into 25 shares. What is the conversion equivalent price per share?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,025."], ["B", "RM25."], ["C", "RM40."], ["D", "RM42."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Conversion equivalent price = RM1,050 / 25 = <strong>RM42 per share</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This uses convertible market price, unlike contractual conversion price.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 29, "question": "<p>A convertible's market price is RM1,050 and its conversion value is RM900. Under the course convention, what are its conversion premium and premium as a percentage of conversion value?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,050 and 116.67%."], ["B", "RM150 and 14.29%."], ["C", "RM900 and 85.71%."], ["D", "RM150 and approximately 16.67%."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Premium = RM1,050 - RM900 = <strong>RM150</strong>; percentage = RM150 / RM900 x 100 = <strong>16.67%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Follow the stated convention: percentage denominator is conversion value, not convertible market price.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": 30, "question": "<p>A convertible has a RM150 conversion premium and pays RM70 annual interest; the shares obtainable by conversion pay RM40 in total annual dividends. Ignoring taxes, changes and time value, what is the simple premium payback period?</p>", "options": [["A", "5 years."], ["B", "2.14 years."], ["C", "3.75 years."], ["D", "The premium is recovered immediately."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Incremental annual income = RM70 - RM40 = RM30. Simple payback = RM150 / RM30 = <strong>5 years</strong>, ignoring time value and changes. If incremental income is zero or negative, the premium is not recovered through this method.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the difference in annual income, not the full bond coupon.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 1, "question": "<p>An investor's simple required-return model includes a 2% real rate, 3% expected inflation premium and 2% risk premium. What is the approximate required nominal return under this additive model?</p>", "options": [["A", "5%"], ["B", "7%"], ["C", "6%"], ["D", "12%"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Additive required return = 2% + 3% + 2% = <strong>7%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is the stated approximation; exact compounding can differ.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 2, "question": "<p>A corporate bond yields 6% and a comparable-maturity government bond yields 4%. What is the yield spread?</p>", "options": [["A", "10 percentage points."], ["B", "0.02 basis points."], ["C", "2 percentage points, or 200 basis points."], ["D", "Exactly the probability of corporate default."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Spread = 6% - 4% = <strong>2 percentage points = 200 basis points</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A yield spread reflects multiple potential factors and is not itself a default probability.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 3, "question": "<p>Why might a callable bond offer a higher yield than an otherwise comparable noncallable bond?</p>", "options": [["A", "Callable bonds cannot be redeemed before maturity."], ["B", "A call gives the investor a guaranteed higher realized return."], ["C", "Investors may require compensation because the issuer can redeem it early under the contract."], ["D", "A call removes reinvestment risk."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An issuer's call option can deprive holders of above-market coupons; investors may demand compensation.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher quoted yield does not guarantee the bond remains outstanding to maturity.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 4, "question": "<p>A yield curve plots comparable bonds' yields against:</p>", "options": [["A", "Annual dividend payout ratios."], ["B", "Company revenue in a future year."], ["C", "The investor's original purchase price only."], ["D", "Time to maturity at a particular date."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A yield curve compares yields by maturity at a point in time, ideally for comparable credit and instrument characteristics.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is not a graph of one bond's realized returns through time.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 5, "question": "<p>A yield curve shows a 2-year yield of 3% and a 10-year yield of 5%. What is its slope between those points?</p>", "options": [["A", "Not measurable because yields are percentages."], ["B", "Inverted."], ["C", "Necessarily flat."], ["D", "Upward-sloping."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The 10-year yield is above the 2-year yield, so this portion slopes upward.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Curve shape is an observation, not a certain forecast.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 6, "question": "<p>Under a simplified pure expectations hypothesis, an upward-sloping yield curve can reflect:</p>", "options": [["A", "Proof that liquidity premiums cannot exist in real markets."], ["B", "Certainty that actual future short rates will rise exactly as predicted."], ["C", "Expectations of higher future short-term interest rates."], ["D", "A guaranteed capital gain on all long-term bonds."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In the simplified expectations view, an upward slope can indicate higher expected future short rates.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Actual future rates need not match expectations; other theories include premiums or segmentation.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 7, "question": "<p>What does liquidity-preference theory add to an interpretation of a yield curve?</p>", "options": [["A", "Maturity never affects bond yields."], ["B", "Longer-maturity bonds must have no price risk."], ["C", "Investors may demand additional compensation for holding longer-maturity bonds with greater risk or less liquidity."], ["D", "Every future rate can be observed with certainty."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Liquidity preference allows a term premium reflecting investors' preferences and risks.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A positive slope can arise without certainty that future short rates will increase.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Market-segmentation theory emphasizes:</p>", "options": [["A", "A single mandatory interest rate across all maturities."], ["B", "Supply and demand within maturity segments shaped by borrower and investor preferences."], ["C", "That every investor holds only common stock."], ["D", "Guaranteed success from predicting the next recession."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Segmentation emphasizes distinct maturity preferences and supply and demand.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A curve's shape has more than one possible interpretation.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 9, "question": "<p>A fixed-rate bond's required yield rises, with its promised cash flows unchanged. What happens to its theoretical present price?</p>", "options": [["A", "The coupon rate automatically rises to match the yield."], ["B", "It rises."], ["C", "It stays fixed at par in all circumstances."], ["D", "It falls."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Higher discount rates reduce the present value of unchanged promised cash flows.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Fixed coupons remain fixed while their market value changes.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 10, "question": "<p>A two-year bond has RM1,000 par, pays RM80 at each year-end and must yield 10% annually. What is its theoretical price today?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1,160.00."], ["B", "RM1,000.00."], ["C", "RM1,034.71."], ["D", "Approximately RM965.29."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Price = $80/1.10+1{,}080/1.10^2=72.73+892.56=\\text{RM}965.29$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include principal with the final coupon and discount each at the correct date.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 11, "question": "<p>A two-year RM1,000 par bond has an 8% annual coupon paid semiannually and a 10% nominal annual required yield compounded semiannually. Which calculation gives its price?</p>", "options": [["A", "Discount RM40 four times at 10% per half-year."], ["B", "Discount RM80 for two years at 10% per half-year."], ["C", "Discount RM40 every half-year plus RM1,000 at the fourth half-year at 5% per half-year."], ["D", "Discount RM1,000 only and omit all coupons."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Annual coupon is RM80, so each half-year payment is RM40; 10% nominal divided by two is 5% per period, with four periods.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Convert the coupon, yield and number of periods together.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 12, "question": "<p>Using the assumptions of Q11, what is the approximate bond price?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM964.54."], ["B", "RM1,000.00."], ["C", "RM1,035.46."], ["D", "RM1,080.00."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$P=\\sum_{t=1}^{4}40/(1.05)^t+1{,}000/(1.05)^4\\approx\\text{RM}964.54$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> This is a discount bond because its coupon rate is below the required yield.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 13, "question": "<p>For a standard fixed-rate bond, the coupon rate equals its market-required yield on a matching payment basis. Its theoretical price is generally:</p>", "options": [["A", "Zero."], ["B", "Above par."], ["C", "Below par."], ["D", "At par."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>When coupon rate equals the required yield on the same payment basis, the present value of the standard bond equals par.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Check compounding and payment frequency before comparing rates.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 14, "question": "<p>A bond with RM1,000 par pays RM80 annually and currently sells for RM900. Its current yield is closest to:</p>", "options": [["A", "8.00%."], ["B", "8.89%."], ["C", "9.00%."], ["D", "11.11%."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current yield = RM80 / RM900 = <strong>8.89%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Coupon rate is 8%, and YTM also incorporates the redemption amount and timing.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 15, "question": "<p>For an annual-pay noncallable bond trading below par, with positive coupon and no default, which ordering generally applies?</p>", "options": [["A", "Coupon rate &gt; current yield &gt; YTM."], ["B", "YTM &gt; current yield &gt; coupon rate."], ["C", "Current yield &gt; YTM &gt; coupon rate."], ["D", "All three are necessarily equal."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A discount makes annual coupon/current price exceed coupon/par, while convergence to par adds to YTM under the stated conditions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The ordering depends on assumptions and price relative to par.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 16, "question": "<p>Yield to maturity (YTM) is best described as:</p>", "options": [["A", "The annual coupon divided only by the current price."], ["B", "The discount rate equating the bond's current price with its promised coupon and maturity payments."], ["C", "The guaranteed return over any chosen short holding period."], ["D", "The coupon divided by par in every case."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>YTM is the internal rate of return implied by the current price and promised payments through maturity.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is not current yield and does not guarantee the investor's realized return.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 17, "question": "<p>An investor holds a fixed-rate bond to maturity and receives every promised payment. To realize its quoted YTM as a compound return over the full horizon, the standard calculation assumes interim coupons are:</p>", "options": [["A", "Reinvested at the quoted YTM on the corresponding compounding basis."], ["B", "Spent immediately while still earning the original bond's compound return."], ["C", "Reinvested at zero with no consequence."], ["D", "Replaced by stock dividends."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The usual compound-return interpretation requires coupons to be reinvested at the quoted YTM and all payments to be received.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Holding to maturity alone does not remove reinvestment or default risk.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 18, "question": "<p>When calculating yield to call for a bond callable in three years, which cash flows are used?</p>", "options": [["A", "Coupons until final maturity and a guaranteed redemption at par in three years."], ["B", "Coupons through the assumed call date and the contractual call price at that date."], ["C", "Only the current price and no future cash flows."], ["D", "The call premium alone with no coupon payments."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Yield to call assumes the specified call occurs and uses coupons through that date plus the call price.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is conditional, not proof the issuer will call.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 19, "question": "<p>A bond is purchased for RM900, pays RM60 after one year and is then sold ex-coupon for RM930. What is the one-year holding-period return before costs and taxes?</p>", "options": [["A", "3.33%."], ["B", "10%."], ["C", "6.67%."], ["D", "13.33%."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>HPR = $(930-900+60)/900=90/900=\\mathbf{10\\%}$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include both the sale-price change and the coupon exactly once.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 20, "question": "<p>A bond trades below par. Which statement about its current yield is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "It fully captures every reinvestment and call outcome."], ["B", "It necessarily equals YTM."], ["C", "It measures annual coupon income relative to price but does not by itself include the gain or loss on redemption."], ["D", "It equals the fixed coupon rate regardless of price."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current yield includes annual coupon income relative to current price, but omits any redemption gain or loss.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is not a complete measure of total return.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 21, "question": "<p>Macaulay duration is:</p>", "options": [["A", "The present-value-weighted average time to the bond's promised cash flows."], ["B", "Always equal to the time remaining until final maturity for every coupon bond."], ["C", "The annual coupon divided by market price."], ["D", "The issuer's credit rating expressed in years."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Macaulay duration weights each payment date by that payment's present value as a proportion of price.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Time to final maturity ignores the timing of intermediate coupons.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 22, "question": "<p>A two-year zero-coupon bond has a single promised payment at maturity. What is its Macaulay duration, assuming a positive price?</p>", "options": [["A", "Two years."], ["B", "Zero years."], ["C", "One year."], ["D", "It depends on coupon reinvestment."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>All promised cash arrives in year 2, so its PV weight is 100% and duration is <strong>2 years</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Yield affects price, but not the timing of the sole payment.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 23, "question": "<p>A two-year RM1,000 par bond pays RM100 at year 1 and RM1,100 at year 2 and yields 10% annually. Its price is RM1,000. What is its Macaulay duration?</p>", "options": [["A", "Approximately 1.91 years."], ["B", "1.00 year."], ["C", "2.10 years."], ["D", "10.00 years."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$D=[1(100/1.1)+2(1{,}100/1.1^2)]/1{,}000\\approx\\mathbf{1.91}$ years.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Weight times by present value, not raw cash-flow amounts.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 24, "question": "<p>Under annual compounding, a bond's Macaulay duration is 1.9091 years and yield is 10%. Its modified duration is approximately:</p>", "options": [["A", "1.74 years."], ["B", "1.00 year."], ["C", "1.91 years."], ["D", "2.10 years."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Modified duration = Macaulay duration / $(1+y)$ = 1.9091 / 1.10 ≈ <strong>1.74</strong> under annual compounding.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Use the yield per payment period when compounding frequency differs.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 25, "question": "<p>A bond has modified duration 5.0. For a small 0.20 percentage-point yield increase, the duration-only approximation to price change is:</p>", "options": [["A", "+1.0%."], ["B", "-1.0%."], ["C", "-10%."], ["D", "-0.1%."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\Delta P/P\\approx-D_{mod}\\Delta y=-5(0.002)=-0.01=\\mathbf{-1.0\\%}$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A 0.20 percentage-point move equals 0.002 in decimal, not 0.20.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 26, "question": "<p>Two otherwise comparable coupon bonds have equal maturities and yields, but Bond A pays a higher coupon. Which generally has the shorter Macaulay duration?</p>", "options": [["A", "Bond A, because a greater share of its present-value cash flows arrives earlier."], ["B", "The lower-coupon bond, in every comparable case."], ["C", "Both must have the same duration as maturity."], ["D", "Neither has duration because they pay coupons."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The higher coupon places more present-value weight on earlier cash flows, shortening duration in the stated comparison.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Duration and maturity are not generally equal.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 27, "question": "<p>What is the principal intuition behind duration-based immunization of a fixed future liability?</p>", "options": [["A", "Guarantee the issuer cannot default."], ["B", "Ensure every possible future interest-rate and credit shock has no effect without rebalancing."], ["C", "Eliminate the need to know the liability date."], ["D", "Match asset duration to the investment horizon so that, under simplifying assumptions, price and reinvestment effects can offset."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Duration matching can balance first-order price and reinvestment effects for a specified horizon under assumptions such as a parallel rate change and rebalancing.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It is not protection from every rate shape, credit or liquidity shock.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 28, "question": "<p>An investor expects yields to rise and seeks to reduce immediate interest-rate sensitivity, all else equal. Which is more consistent with that objective?</p>", "options": [["A", "Assume price cannot change before maturity."], ["B", "A longer-duration portfolio solely because maturity is later."], ["C", "The greatest possible leverage regardless of risks."], ["D", "A shorter-duration portfolio."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Lower modified duration generally means a smaller immediate percentage price response to a small rise in yields.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A rate forecast can be wrong, and other risks still matter.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 29, "question": "<p>Which best describes a bond ladder?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy only equities with increasing dividends."], ["B", "Hold one bond forever with no maturity."], ["C", "Hold bonds with staggered maturities and reinvest proceeds as bonds mature according to the strategy."], ["D", "Guarantee the highest available future yield."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A ladder staggers maturities and reinvests maturing proceeds, spreading reinvestment dates.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> It does not guarantee the highest future yield or eliminate credit risk.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": 30, "question": "<p>An investor considers selling one bond and buying a higher-yielding alternative. What is the strongest evaluation of a yield-pickup swap?</p>", "options": [["A", "Compare credit, liquidity, call terms, maturity, taxes and transaction costs as well as the stated yield."], ["B", "The higher yield is free extra return regardless of all other differences."], ["C", "Credit and call provisions are irrelevant to yield."], ["D", "A higher yield proves the new bond's price cannot fall."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A yield pickup can reflect different risks or costs; examine the complete terms and after-cost consequences.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A higher stated yield is not automatically a better risk-adjusted choice.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 1, "question": "<p>A mutual fund pools money from investors to buy securities. What does an investor normally own?</p>", "options": [["A", "A separate direct title to each underlying share in the same quantity as the fund."], ["B", "Shares or units in the fund, representing an interest in its portfolio."], ["C", "A guaranteed fixed return from every security in the portfolio."], ["D", "An obligation to manage the fund's trades personally."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The shareholder owns an interest in the fund, which owns its underlying assets.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Pooled exposure is not the same as direct ownership of every underlying share.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 2, "question": "<p>Why can a diversified fund reduce some investment risks?</p>", "options": [["A", "It guarantees a positive return each year."], ["B", "Diversification eliminates all market risk."], ["C", "Losses specific to one holding may be partly offset by other holdings, though broad market risk remains."], ["D", "Every fund is equally diversified regardless of its holdings."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversification can reduce security-specific exposure, but broad or concentrated risks remain.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> More holdings alone do not guarantee safety.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 3, "question": "<p>An open-end mutual fund has RM105 million in assets, RM5 million in liabilities and 10 million shares. What is its NAV per share?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM11.00."], ["B", "RM10.50."], ["C", "RM10.00."], ["D", "RM100.00."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>NAV per share = (RM105m - RM5m) / 10m = <strong>RM10.00</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Subtract liabilities before dividing; the source slide's simplified expression omits them.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 4, "question": "<p>What is the usual transaction mechanism for a traditional open-end mutual fund?</p>", "options": [["A", "The fund can never issue or redeem shares."], ["B", "Every share must trade continuously on a stock exchange at a negotiated price."], ["C", "Investors transact with the fund at the applicable calculated NAV, subject to its dealing and fee terms."], ["D", "Investors always receive a guaranteed price above NAV."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Traditional open-end fund dealing uses the applicable NAV and fund terms, with creation/redemption through the fund.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Intraday secondary-market trading is characteristic of ETFs and closed-end shares, not the usual open-end mechanism.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 5, "question": "<p>A closed-end fund's NAV is RM20 per share while its exchange price is RM18. What is the price relative to NAV?</p>", "options": [["A", "A 20% premium."], ["B", "A 10% premium."], ["C", "A 2% discount."], ["D", "A 10% discount."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Discount = (20 - 18) / 20 = <strong>10% of NAV</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Divide the difference by NAV, not the market price, under this convention.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 6, "question": "<p>A closed-end fund trades at RM21 while its NAV is RM20. Its premium is:</p>", "options": [["A", "20%."], ["B", "1%."], ["C", "10%."], ["D", "5%."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Premium = (21 - 20) / 20 = <strong>5% of NAV</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A premium is an observation, not an automatic guarantee of overvaluation.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 7, "question": "<p>Which description best fits a typical ETF?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its market price must equal NAV at every instant."], ["B", "Its shares trade on an exchange; an authorized participant creation/redemption mechanism can help keep price close to NAV but does not guarantee equality."], ["C", "Every ETF is actively managed."], ["D", "It cannot hold bonds or cash."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ETFs trade on exchanges and creation/redemption by authorized participants can support price-NAV alignment, though deviations can occur.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An arbitrage mechanism does not guarantee exact equality at all times.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 8, "question": "<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes a vehicle from a strategy?</p>", "options": [["A", "An ETF can never be actively managed."], ["B", "All ETFs are index funds, and all index funds are ETFs."], ["C", "An index-tracking strategy can be offered through either an ETF or an open-end mutual fund."], ["D", "An index fund must guarantee performance above its benchmark."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>ETF is a trading/vehicle structure; index tracking is a strategy that can be used in several fund structures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Vehicle and investment strategy are different dimensions.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 9, "question": "<p>A front-end load is 5% of an RM1,000 gross investment. If the remaining amount is invested at NAV, how much enters the fund?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM500."], ["B", "RM1,000."], ["C", "RM1,050."], ["D", "RM950."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Load = RM1,000 x 5% = RM50; invested amount = <strong>RM950</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The question specifies the load as a percentage of the gross contribution.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 10, "question": "<p>An investor receives RM9,800 when redeeming a fund investment worth RM10,000 before a back-end load. What is the load under these stated figures?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM20, or 0.2%."], ["B", "RM200, or 2% of the pre-fee redemption value."], ["C", "RM500, or 5%."], ["D", "There is no fee because the investor is selling."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Load = RM10,000 - RM9,800 = RM200, or <strong>2%</strong> of RM10,000.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A back-end charge is deducted on redemption under its stated terms.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 11, "question": "<p>A no-load fund is best described as:</p>", "options": [["A", "A fund without a sales load under its stated terms; other ongoing expenses may still apply."], ["B", "A fund that can never charge any operating fee."], ["C", "A fund that guarantees the market return."], ["D", "A fund with no investment risk."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>No sales load does not rule out management or other ongoing costs.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> No-load is not the same as no-fee.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 12, "question": "<p>A fund with average net assets of RM100 million charges a 1% annual management fee on those average assets. What is the approximate annual management fee?</p>", "options": [["A", "RM1 million."], ["B", "RM100,000."], ["C", "RM10 million."], ["D", "RM99 million."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>RM100m x 1% = <strong>RM1m</strong> annually, using average net assets and a constant rate.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The question concerns a fund-level charge, not 1% of the fund's gross returns.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 13, "question": "<p>Which distinction about ongoing fund fees is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "A management fee compensates investment management; a U.S. 12b-1 fee generally relates to distribution and/or shareholder-service expenses."], ["B", "A 12b-1 fee is identical to the purchase price of all securities in the portfolio."], ["C", "A no-load fund cannot have any management fee."], ["D", "Fees have no possible effect on an investor's net return."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Management and 12b-1 expenses serve distinct functions; the latter generally supports distribution and/or shareholder services.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The slide's description of 12b-1 as a general management fee is oversimplified.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 14, "question": "<p>An investor wants broad exposure to a specified stock index. Which comparison is most useful when selecting between two index funds tracking that same benchmark?</p>", "options": [["A", "Assume both must have identical net returns because their index names match."], ["B", "Select the fund with the longest name."], ["C", "Compare total costs, tracking difference, fund structure and how each fits the investor's account and needs."], ["D", "Ignore trading costs and tracking because both own securities."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Costs, tracking differences, liquidity and account suitability can create different investor outcomes even for the same benchmark.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A shared index label does not guarantee equal net performance.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 15, "question": "<p>A fund holds shares only in a single industry. What risk deserves particular attention?</p>", "options": [["A", "The fund has no exposure to stock prices."], ["B", "All market and industry risks have been diversified away."], ["C", "Sector concentration: several holdings may be exposed to the same adverse industry event."], ["D", "A sector label guarantees superior returns."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Securities in one sector can fall together when that sector experiences a shock.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Many holdings do not automatically imply broad diversification.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 16, "question": "<p>Which fund most directly aims to combine income and capital appreciation through holdings in both equities and bonds?</p>", "options": [["A", "A balanced fund, subject to its actual portfolio and mandate."], ["B", "A fund holding only one speculative stock."], ["C", "A fund that holds exclusively one issuer's short-term debt."], ["D", "A fund whose objective explicitly excludes both income and growth."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A balanced fund typically combines equities and bonds, though allocations and risk differ by fund.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A name alone does not guarantee a particular fixed allocation or safety.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 17, "question": "<p>A growth fund and a value fund can best be distinguished by:</p>", "options": [["A", "Their investment approaches: growth emphasizes growth prospects, while value seeks securities assessed as inexpensive relative to fundamentals."], ["B", "A guarantee that the growth fund always earns more."], ["C", "A rule that value stocks cannot ever pay dividends."], ["D", "A guarantee that neither fund can lose money."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Growth and value describe investment approaches or styles; neither guarantees an outcome.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Different styles can overlap and both carry risk.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 18, "question": "<p>A bond fund holds many bonds. Which claim is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is guaranteed to return a fixed principal on a particular date like a single bond held to maturity."], ["B", "It can diversify issuer exposure, but its NAV may fall when yields rise and it still faces portfolio-specific risks."], ["C", "It is immune to interest-rate movements."], ["D", "Every bond fund has identical credit and duration risk."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A bond fund can hold diversified bonds yet its share value changes with yields, credit and other risks.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A perpetual fund is not equivalent to holding one bond until a specified maturity.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 19, "question": "<p>Which statement about a money market fund is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its yield can never change with interest rates."], ["B", "Every money market fund worldwide always maintains exactly one unit of local currency per share."], ["C", "It holds short-term instruments, but its NAV and protections depend on its structure and rules; it is not universally guaranteed risk-free."], ["D", "It holds only long-term common stocks."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Money market funds generally hold short-term instruments, but stable NAV and investor protections depend on the fund and jurisdiction.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The slide's universal <span class=\"cur\">$</span>1 NAV characterization is not generally valid.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 20, "question": "<p>An internationally invested fund is held by an investor whose home currency differs from the currencies of its underlying assets. Which risk matters?</p>", "options": [["A", "Foreign-currency effects never matter inside a pooled fund."], ["B", "Exchange-rate changes can affect the investor's home-currency return in addition to the assets' local returns."], ["C", "The fund cannot own more than one security."], ["D", "A foreign investment automatically eliminates all market risk."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Exchange-rate changes can raise or lower the home-currency value of foreign assets and distributions.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Pooling does not automatically remove currency exposure.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 21, "question": "<p>What is a unit investment trust commonly designed to provide?</p>", "options": [["A", "Guaranteed active trading by the investor every hour."], ["B", "An interest in a generally fixed portfolio for a specified trust term, subject to its actual terms."], ["C", "The same legal structure as every hedge fund worldwide."], ["D", "Guaranteed return of principal."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A UIT typically holds a relatively fixed portfolio for a specified term; consult its actual rules and holdings.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A fixed portfolio does not guarantee principal.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 22, "question": "<p>Which distinction between equity and mortgage REITs is most useful?</p>", "options": [["A", "Equity REITs primarily own or operate properties; mortgage REITs primarily hold real-estate debt or related assets."], ["B", "All REITs hold only corporate common shares."], ["C", "Every REIT is necessarily a closed-end investment company."], ["D", "REIT income and prices cannot change."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Equity and mortgage REITs have different primary exposures.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> The chapter's description of every REIT as a closed-end investment company is an overgeneralization.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 23, "question": "<p>Which statement about hedge funds is most accurate in this course comparison?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their structures, strategies, fees and eligible investors vary; they should not be assumed equivalent to ordinary retail mutual funds."], ["B", "Every hedge fund worldwide has the same guaranteed return."], ["C", "All hedge funds are government-insured deposits."], ["D", "None can use leverage or short positions."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Hedge-fund structures and requirements vary by jurisdiction and fund; the course's historic eligibility and fee examples are not universal current rules.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> \"Hedge fund\" is not a guarantee of hedging or positive performance.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 24, "question": "<p>What does automatic dividend reinvestment generally do?</p>", "options": [["A", "Guarantees the investor pays no applicable tax."], ["B", "Uses distributions to acquire additional fund shares under the plan's terms."], ["C", "Prevents the fund's NAV from falling."], ["D", "Doubles the investor's wealth immediately."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A reinvestment plan applies distributions to more fund shares according to its rules.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Reinvestment does not by itself eliminate tax or investment losses.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 25, "question": "<p>An investor buys a fund share for RM20, receives RM0.60 income and RM0.40 capital-gain distribution in cash, then sells for RM21. What is holding-period return, ignoring fees and tax?</p>", "options": [["A", "5%."], ["B", "10%."], ["C", "7%."], ["D", "15%."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>HPR = $(21-20+0.60+0.40)/20=2/20=\\mathbf{10\\%}$.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Include both types of cash distribution and the price change.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 26, "question": "<p>An investor holds 100 fund shares bought at RM10 each. A RM0.50-per-share distribution is fully reinvested when shares cost RM10; the investor ends the period with 105 shares worth RM10.50 each. What is the holding-period return, ignoring fees and tax?</p>", "options": [["A", "10.25%."], ["B", "5.00%."], ["C", "15.25%."], ["D", "52.50%."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Ending value = 105 x RM10.50 = RM1,102.50; initial value = RM1,000; return = RM102.50 / RM1,000 = <strong>10.25%</strong>.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Reinvesting increases the share count used in ending value.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 27, "question": "<p>Why should the RM50 distribution in Q26 not be added again to the ending value when using the 105 shares?</p>", "options": [["A", "The share count remains 100 after reinvestment."], ["B", "Reinvested distributions have no economic value."], ["C", "Fund distributions always disappear from the calculation."], ["D", "It already purchased the five additional shares included in the ending value."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The RM50 bought five shares, already included in the 105-share ending value.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Adding the distribution again double counts it.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 28, "question": "<p>A fund's published return is 8% after its ongoing operating expenses but before a particular investor's transaction costs. What is the correct treatment of the ongoing expense ratio when using that published net return?</p>", "options": [["A", "Assume all investors experience precisely 8% after every possible cost."], ["B", "Always subtract the same ongoing fee a second time."], ["C", "Add the expense ratio to the return."], ["D", "Do not subtract those already-reflected operating expenses again; account separately for applicable investor-specific costs."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The given published return already reflects those operating expenses; other investor-specific charges may still apply.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> Subtracting the same fee twice understates return.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 29, "question": "<p>Which is generally the most suitable benchmark for assessing a diversified domestic large-cap equity index fund?</p>", "options": [["A", "Last year's best-performing single stock."], ["B", "A cash account regardless of the fund's equity exposure."], ["C", "A bond index with unrelated duration and credit risks."], ["D", "Its stated domestic large-cap equity index or another closely matched benchmark."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>An appropriately matched benchmark reflects the fund's investment universe and risk profile.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> An unrelated benchmark can make performance comparisons misleading.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": 30, "question": "<p>An investor needs accessible capital for a near-term obligation and cannot absorb a substantial loss. A friend recommends putting all of it into a volatile sector fund because last year's return was high. What is the strongest response?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ignore the fund's holdings because its name contains \"fund.\""], ["B", "Buy immediately because last year's winner must repeat."], ["C", "Sector funds eliminate all losses through pooling."], ["D", "Assess horizon, liquidity, loss tolerance, concentration and costs; a volatile concentrated stock fund may not fit the stated need."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A concentrated volatile equity fund can conflict with near-term liquidity and loss constraints; last year's performance is insufficient.</p><p class=\"exam-trap\"><strong>Exam trap:</strong> A fund structure does not make an unsuitable strategy safe.</p>"}];
window.DIFFICULT_BANK = [{"module": 1, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which definition of an investment matches the chapter exactly?</p>", "options": [["A", "Any asset into which funds can be placed with the expectation that it will generate positive income and/or increase its value."], ["B", "Any asset bought with borrowed money in the hope of a capital gain."], ["C", "Any security traded on an organised exchange."], ["D", "Any asset whose price has risen over the past year."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines an investment by the <strong>expectation of positive income and/or an increase in value</strong>. B adds borrowing and drops income; C restricts investments to exchange-traded securities, but property and tangibles can be investments too; D confuses past performance with the defining expectation.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In the chapter's classification of investors, which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Individuals are typically net demanders of funds; businesses are net suppliers."], ["B", "Governments are typically net suppliers of funds."], ["C", "All three groups are net suppliers when interest rates are high."], ["D", "Individuals are typically net suppliers of funds; businesses and governments are typically net demanders."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Individuals, as a group, save more than they borrow, while businesses and governments typically need outside funds for production and public projects. \"Net\" describes the group's typical aggregate role: individuals still borrow for houses and cars.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which pair lists the chapter's <strong>two disadvantages</strong> of short-term investments?</p>", "options": [["A", "Low liquidity and high default risk"], ["B", "Price volatility and currency risk"], ["C", "High taxes and high transaction costs"], ["D", "Low levels of return and loss of potential purchasing power from inflation"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides list advantages (high liquidity, low risk of default) and disadvantages (low return, possible loss of purchasing power from inflation). A reverses the advantages; C and B are not the chapter's listed drawbacks.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why do short-term, highly liquid, low-default-risk investments usually offer low returns?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because investors value safety and liquidity, so issuers need to offer less compensation."], ["B", "Because the government caps their interest rates."], ["C", "Because they are taxed more heavily than stocks."], ["D", "Because their prices are more volatile than those of stocks."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Desirable features (safety, liquidity) reduce the compensation investors demand. This is the general logic of the risk-return trade-off: you are paid for accepting undesirable characteristics. B and C are not the chapter's reasoning; D is false, as short-term instruments are less volatile.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An equity mutual fund and an ETF holding the same stocks differ mainly in that:</p>", "options": [["A", "the ETF is a direct investment, while the mutual fund is indirect."], ["B", "the ETF's shares trade on an exchange while the market is open, unlike conventional mutual fund shares."], ["C", "the mutual fund cannot hold more than 30 stocks."], ["D", "only the ETF provides diversification."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Both pool investors' money into a professionally managed portfolio (both are indirect and both diversify). The ETF changes the <strong>trading structure</strong>: its shares trade on exchanges during market hours, according to the slides.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does the chapter place \"meeting investment prerequisites\" before selecting investments?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because an investor without emergency funds or protection may be forced to sell investments at a bad time, whatever their quality."], ["B", "Because regulators forbid investing without insurance."], ["C", "Because prerequisites raise expected returns."], ["D", "Because selecting investments is the least important step."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The prerequisites (funds for emergencies, protection against death, illness and disability) prevent a mismatch between the investor's circumstances and the asset's characteristics. Without them, a cash need can force a sale during a market fall, the \"survive first\" logic.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A wealth manager describes a client as having \"high risk tolerance but low risk capacity\". What does this mean in practice?</p>", "options": [["A", "The client is psychologically comfortable with volatility but financially unable to absorb large losses, so the portfolio should be constrained by capacity."], ["B", "The client can afford large losses but dislikes them."], ["C", "The client should take maximum risk."], ["D", "The two terms mean the same thing."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Tolerance is psychological willingness; capacity is financial ability (horizon, income stability, buffers, obligations). Professionals generally let the <strong>lower</strong> of the two constrain the portfolio. The chapter's life-cycle logic is mainly about capacity.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A 26-year-old has stable income, a six-month emergency fund, no debt and is saving for retirement in $35$ years. Which allocation best fits the chapter's life-cycle framework?</p>", "options": [["A", "Mostly short-term deposits to preserve capital."], ["B", "Only options and futures, because young investors should maximise risk."], ["C", "Only bonds, because bond prices never fall."], ["D", "Mostly growth-oriented investments such as common stocks, because the long horizon allows time to recover from falls."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Young investors with the prerequisites met have high risk capacity and a long horizon, so growth assets are relatively suitable. A sacrifices growth needlessly; C is false (bond prices fall when rates rise); B confuses \"can take more risk\" with \"should take maximum risk\".</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor earns an $8\\%$ nominal return, pays $25\\%$ tax on it, and inflation is $4\\%$. What is the <strong>approximate</strong> real after-tax return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$4.0\\%$"], ["B", "$6.0\\%$"], ["C", "$2.0\\%$"], ["D", "$-2.0\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{After-tax return} = 8\\% \\times (1 - 0.25) = 6\\%$. $\\text{Real} \\approx 6\\% - 4\\% = \\mathbf{2\\%}$. A ignores tax; B ignores inflation; D subtracts the tax rate and inflation in the wrong way.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Classify a directly held, newly issued 5-year Malaysian government bond for a Malaysian investor.</p>", "options": [["A", "Security, indirect, equity, short-term, foreign"], ["B", "Property, direct, debt, long-term, domestic"], ["C", "Security, direct, derivative, short-term, domestic"], ["D", "Security, direct, debt, long-term, domestic"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A bond is a <strong>security</strong> (not property), held <strong>directly</strong>, it is <strong>debt</strong> (you lend to the issuer), its maturity exceeds one year (<strong>long-term</strong>), and for a Malaysian investor it is <strong>domestic</strong>.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A Malaysian bank offers a savings account at $2.5\\%$ when inflation is $3\\%$. A client says it is \"risk-free\". How should an adviser respond?</p>", "options": [["A", "Agree completely."], ["B", "It is close to free of default and volatility risk, but the expected real return is about $-0.5\\%$, so purchasing power is expected to fall."], ["C", "Say the account has high default risk."], ["D", "Say it is riskier than equities in every respect."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Real} \\approx 2.5\\% - 3\\% = -0.5\\%$. Separating nominal safety from real economic safety is the professional answer.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>Using the chapter's framework, which constraint is most binding for an investor who must pay a known $\\text{RM}200{,}000$ liability in $18$ months?</p>", "options": [["A", "Tax constraint"], ["B", "Legal constraint on owning foreign stocks"], ["C", "Time horizon and liquidity constraint"], ["D", "Unique preference for ethical investing"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A known near-term liability makes the horizon short and the liquidity need high, which dominates the choice of assets. The other constraints may matter, but they do not determine suitability here.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two investors buy the same equity fund. Investor A needs the money for a house deposit in $9$ months; Investor B is saving for retirement in $30$ years. Which statement best analyses the situation?</p>", "options": [["A", "The fund is equally suitable for both because they hold the same asset."], ["B", "Suitability differs: the same asset suits B's long horizon but exposes A to a possible large fall just before the money is needed."], ["C", "The fund is more suitable for A because A needs higher returns sooner."], ["D", "Neither should invest, because equity funds are risky."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Suitability is a function of objective, horizon, risk capacity and liquidity needs, not of the asset alone. A cannot afford a fall before the purchase; B has time to recover. D replaces analysis with a blanket rule.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An ETF can be sold within seconds on any trading day. Why can it still be <strong>unsuitable</strong> for money needed next month?</p>", "options": [["A", "ETFs are illiquid."], ["B", "ETFs cannot be sold until they mature."], ["C", "Market liquidity is not price stability: the ETF can be sold quickly, but possibly at a much lower value than was invested."], ["D", "ETFs pay no dividends."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter defines liquidity as quick conversion <strong>with little or no loss in value</strong>. An equity ETF meets the speed condition but not the price-stability one over short horizons. That distinction is what makes it unsuitable for near-term needs.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Market interest rates rise from $5\\%$ to $7\\%$. What happens to the price of an existing bond paying a fixed $5\\%$ coupon, and why?</p>", "options": [["A", "It rises, because higher rates make all bonds more valuable."], ["B", "It falls only if the issuer's credit rating changes."], ["C", "It stays at par, because coupon payments do not change."], ["D", "It falls, because its fixed $5\\%$ coupon must compete with new bonds paying $7\\%$, so buyers pay less for it."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The bond's cash flows are fixed; when new bonds offer more, the old bond's price must fall until its return is competitive. Interest rates and bond prices move in <strong>opposite</strong> directions.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>Two bonds are identical except that Bond A matures in $2$ years and Bond B in $20$ years. Market rates rise by $2$ percentage points. Which is most likely?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both fall by the same percentage."], ["B", "Neither changes, because coupons are fixed."], ["C", "Bond A's price falls by more, because it matures sooner."], ["D", "Bond B's price falls by more, because its distant cash flows are discounted over more periods."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter states rates and bond prices move oppositely. Extending its logic with TVM (Appendix 4A): the further away the cash flows, the more a rate change affects their present value, so long bonds are more rate-sensitive.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A start-up founder holds $90\\%$ of their wealth in the start-up's shares and asks whether to buy more \"because I believe in the company\". What is the strongest diagnosis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy more; conviction reduces risk."], ["B", "The founder's wealth is already concentrated: their income and wealth depend on the same company, so diversifying away from it reduces unnecessary concentration risk."], ["C", "Sell everything immediately."], ["D", "Concentration only matters for institutional investors."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Diversification is about avoiding unnecessary concentration. A founder's job and wealth are exposed to the same firm-specific risk; belief does not remove that risk. C is an overreaction; a reasoned rebalancing is the answer.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why is age only a <strong>proxy</strong> in life-cycle investing rather than a decision rule?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the real drivers are horizon, human capital (future earnings), obligations and liquidity needs, which correlate with age but differ between individuals."], ["B", "Because older investors always take more risk."], ["C", "Because life cycle stages are defined by income, not age."], ["D", "Because regulators set allocation by age."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Age correlates with time to recover and remaining earnings, but a 25-year-old with dependants and unstable income may have less capacity than a 50-year-old with secure wealth. The chapter's \"always aggressive when young\" trap rests on this.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Why might a household rationally hold short-term deposits paying below inflation instead of only higher-expected-return assets?</p>", "options": [["A", "Households are always irrational."], ["B", "Deposits have higher expected returns than equities."], ["C", "Liquidity and certainty have value: they hedge unexpected spending needs and avoid forced sales at depressed prices, a service worth paying for through a lower expected return."], ["D", "Inflation does not affect deposits."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This generalises the chapter's point that liquidity is economically valuable: the \"cost\" (a negative expected real return) is the price of insurance against liquidity shocks.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Cash is the safest investment, so a retirement fund for someone aged 30 should be held entirely in cash.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct: cash has no risk of any kind."], ["B", "Incorrect, because cash has high default risk."], ["C", "Partly right on default risk, volatility and liquidity, but wrong overall: over $30$+ years inflation can steadily erode purchasing power, so cash alone is unlikely to be suitable."], ["D", "Correct, because retirement funds must never fluctuate."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Safety is multidimensional. Cash is very safe in nominal terms but carries purchasing-power risk, which matters most over long horizons. A good answer credits what is true and rejects the \"safest in every sense\" claim.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Investment X (expected $15\\%$) is better than Investment Y (expected $7\\%$).\"</p>", "options": [["A", "True, since $15\\% &gt; 7\\%$."], ["B", "True only if X's taxes are lower."], ["C", "It cannot be judged without comparing the risks taken and the investor's objectives, horizon and liquidity needs."], ["D", "False, because low-return investments are always safer."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Return cannot be evaluated independently of risk and suitability. D swaps one blanket rule for another: a low return does not guarantee low risk.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A broker recommends a high-growth technology fund to a client in their first meeting, without asking about the client's goals or circumstances. What is the strongest criticism?</p>", "options": [["A", "Technology funds are never suitable."], ["B", "The broker skipped the steps that come first (prerequisites, goals and the plan): a product was chosen before suitability was established."], ["C", "The broker should have recommended a derivative instead."], ["D", "There is nothing wrong if the fund's past returns were high."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The seven-step process puts goals and planning before selection; the product should follow the goal. Past performance (D) does not establish suitability.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A fintech app advertises \"invest your spare change automatically into a global ETF\". For a student with no emergency fund, evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Always good: small amounts carry no risk."], ["B", "Diversified and low-cost, but the chapter's order still applies: building an emergency reserve first matters more, and the ETF's value can fall when cash is needed."], ["C", "Bad, because ETFs are illegal for students."], ["D", "Good only if the ETF tracks a single stock."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The product may be sensible, but suitability depends on prerequisites. Small amounts reduce the stakes but do not change the logic that emergency funds come first.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Evaluate the claim: \"Because institutional investors manage more money, they are always better investors than individuals.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, size guarantees skill."], ["B", "Incorrect: size brings cost advantages and professional resources, but institutions also face agency problems and constraints (benchmarks, liquidity of large trades), and size does not guarantee better risk-adjusted results."], ["C", "Correct, because they trade on exchanges."], ["D", "Incorrect, because institutions cannot diversify."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's distinction is whose objectives are being optimised, not who is better. Scale has advantages and disadvantages; D is false (institutions diversify easily).</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>A researcher finds that young investors who hold mostly cash end up with lower retirement wealth on average. Which conclusion is <strong>best supported</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "Everyone should hold only equities."], ["B", "Young people should stop saving."], ["C", "Cash causes lower income."], ["D", "On average, the long horizon favours growth assets, but the finding is about averages: individual suitability still depends on prerequisites, capacity and tolerance."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Evidence about averages does not override individual suitability. The answer combines the life-cycle logic with the chapter's insistence that \"always\" rules fail.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>The chapter's tax slides use 2012 U.S. brackets. What is the most defensible way to use them in a Malaysian course?</p>", "options": [["A", "Apply the U.S. percentages to Malaysian investors."], ["B", "Ignore taxes altogether."], ["C", "Treat the numbers as historical course material and transfer only the durable principles: after-tax return, type of income and timing of gains."], ["D", "Assume all countries tax capital gains the same way."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Concepts travel across tax systems; specific rates do not. This is exactly the chapter's own warning about its U.S.-focused, dated tax information.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A 35-year-old wants a house deposit in two years and to retire in $30$ years. Which plan best applies the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Separate the goals: liquid, low-volatility short-term investments for the deposit, and a long-term, growth-oriented portfolio for retirement."], ["B", "Keep everything in cash until the house is bought, then start investing."], ["C", "One aggressive portfolio for both goals, since retirement is the longer goal."], ["D", "Put both goals into whichever asset had the highest return last year."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>One investor can have several goals with different horizons, liquidity needs and capacities for loss, so each needs a matching strategy. C risks the deposit; B wastes the long retirement horizon; D chases past returns.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Design the order of an investment plan for a new graduate with $\\text{RM}15{,}000$ savings, unstable freelance income and no insurance, who wants to \"start investing in stocks\".</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy stocks now, then build an emergency fund from the profits."], ["B", "Build an emergency fund and basic protection first, set specific goals and target dates, then evaluate return and risk before selecting a diversified portfolio, and monitor it."], ["C", "Choose the stock with the best recent return, then decide the goal."], ["D", "Put everything in a 10-year fixed deposit."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This follows the seven steps in order: prerequisites → goals → plan → evaluate → select → diversify → manage. Unstable income makes the emergency fund especially important.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A retiree depends partly on portfolio withdrawals. Which combination of objectives should a portfolio for them emphasise, per the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Preservation of capital, a conservative portfolio and investment income."], ["B", "Capital gains and maximum growth."], ["C", "Options and futures for higher returns."], ["D", "Only foreign equities for diversification."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In the retirement stage the slides stress capital preservation, a highly conservative portfolio and income to supplement retirement income, because losses have little time to recover and current income is needed.</p>"}, {"module": 1, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Design a single question that would separate a student who memorised the seven steps from one who understands them.</p>", "options": [["A", "\"List the seven steps in order.\""], ["B", "\"What is step 4?\""], ["C", "\"A client with no emergency fund wants to buy shares tomorrow because a friend recommended them; explain which steps are being skipped, why the order matters, and what you would do first.\""], ["D", "\"Define investment.\""]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Recall questions (A, B, D) can be answered by memorisation. C requires applying and evaluating the logic of the process, the Bloom level the chapter wants for subjective answers.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement defines the <strong>secondary market</strong> correctly?</p>", "options": [["A", "The market where short-term securities are traded."], ["B", "The market in which securities are traded after they have been issued."], ["C", "The market where new issues are sold to the public."], ["D", "The market for foreign securities."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Secondary = trading after issuance. A is the money market; C is the primary market; D is not a separate category in the chapter.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is a <strong>red herring</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "The final prospectus filed after the offering."], ["B", "A preliminary prospectus made available during the waiting period."], ["C", "A public advertisement announcing a completed issue."], ["D", "An illegal promotion of a stock."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The red herring is the preliminary prospectus. The public announcement of an issue and its underwriters is the <strong>tombstone</strong> (C).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which law established the Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC)?</p>", "options": [["A", "Securities Exchange Act of 1934"], ["B", "Securities Act of 1933"], ["C", "Maloney Act of 1938"], ["D", "Sarbanes-Oxley Act of 2002"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>1933 required full disclosure for new issues; 1934 created the SEC; 1938 allowed industry self-regulation (NASD); 2002 tightened accounting and audit rules after corporate fraud.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why are secondary markets economically important even though issuers receive no money from resales?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because they set the coupon rate on new bonds."], ["B", "Because they eliminate investment risk."], ["C", "Because issuers receive a commission on each resale."], ["D", "Because they provide liquidity and continuous pricing, which make buying new issues more attractive."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides list liquidity and continuous pricing as the secondary market's role. An exit route and a market price make investors more willing to buy in the primary market.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In an underwritten offering, what does the investment bank mainly take on?</p>", "options": [["A", "The risk that the issuer's profits fall in future years."], ["B", "The legal liability for the issuer's debts."], ["C", "The risk of reselling the issue to the public at a price above what it paid."], ["D", "The risk of currency movements."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Underwriting = the bank buys the issue at an agreed price and bears the risk of reselling it. It is compensated through the discount (spread) on the offering price.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does a margin investor's <strong>percentage</strong> gain or loss exceed the stock's percentage move?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the broker adds a bonus to margin accounts."], ["B", "Because the stock price is quoted on margin."], ["C", "Because margin accounts pay higher dividends."], ["D", "Because the gain or loss is measured on the investor's equity, which is smaller than the position, while the loan stays fixed."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The same ringgit gain or loss is divided by a smaller equity base. The loan does not change with the price, so equity absorbs the full move.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Why do dealers widen their bid-ask spreads in volatile, thinly traded stocks?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their inventory risk and the difficulty of finding the other side are greater, so they need more compensation for providing liquidity."], ["B", "Regulators require it."], ["C", "Volatile stocks pay higher dividends."], ["D", "Spreads are fixed by the exchange."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The spread compensates dealers for liquidity provision and inventory risk. More risk and less trading means a wider spread, a higher transaction cost for investors.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A dealer quotes a share at $\\text{RM}4.85$ bid / $\\text{RM}4.90$ ask. You sell $2{,}000$ shares. How much do you receive, ignoring commissions?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}9{,}800$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}9{,}700$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}9{,}750$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}9{,}900$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>You sell to the dealer at the <strong>bid</strong>: $2{,}000 \\times 4.85 = \\text{RM}9{,}700$. Buying would cost the ask: $2{,}000 \\times 4.90 = \\text{RM}9{,}800$ (A).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You buy $200$ shares at $\\text{RM}40$ using $60\\%$ initial margin. The price later rises to $\\text{RM}46$. Ignoring interest and dividends, what is your return on invested equity?</p>", "options": [["A", "$15.0\\%$"], ["B", "$9.0\\%$"], ["C", "$25.0\\%$"], ["D", "$37.5\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Position} = \\text{RM}8{,}000$; equity = $0.60 \\times 8{,}000 = \\text{RM}4{,}800$. $\\text{Gain} = 200 \\times 6 = \\text{RM}1{,}200$. $\\text{Return on equity} = 1{,}200 / 4{,}800 = \\mathbf{25\\%}$. The unlevered return is $15\\%$ (A).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You short $300$ shares at $\\text{RM}25$ and cover at $\\text{RM}31$. Ignoring costs, what is the result?</p>", "options": [["A", "Profit of $\\text{RM}1{,}800$"], ["B", "Profit of $\\text{RM}9{,}300$"], ["C", "Loss of $\\text{RM}7{,}500$"], ["D", "Loss of $\\text{RM}1{,}800$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Short profit} = (P_{\\text{sell}} - P_{\\text{buy back}}) \\times N = (25 - 31) \\times 300 = \\mathbf{-\\textbf{RM}1{,}800}$. A rising price hurts the short seller.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A broker's maintenance margin is $30\\%$. A client's long margin position is worth $\\text{RM}50{,}000$ with a $\\text{RM}40{,}000$ loan. How much must the client deposit (as cash that reduces the loan) to restore $30\\%$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}15{,}000$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}5{,}000$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}10{,}000$"], ["D", "Nothing"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Current margin} = 10{,}000 / 50{,}000 = 20\\%$. Needed: $(50{,}000 - D) / 50{,}000 = 0.30$, so $D = 35{,}000$. Paying $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ off the loan restores $30\\%$.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>An investor buys $1{,}000$ shares at $\\text{RM}20$ with $50\\%$ initial margin, pays $\\text{RM}600$ interest, receives $\\text{RM}400$ dividends, and sells at $\\text{RM}23$. What is the return on invested capital?</p>", "options": [["A", "$15.0\\%$"], ["B", "$30.0\\%$"], ["C", "$28.0\\%$"], ["D", "$34.0\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Return} = \\dfrac{\\text{income} - \\text{interest} + \\text{sale value} - \\text{purchase value}}{\\text{initial equity}} = \\dfrac{400 - 600 + 23{,}000 - 20{,}000}{10{,}000} = \\dfrac{2{,}800}{10{,}000} = \\mathbf{28\\%}$. Without interest it would be $34\\%$ (D).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A margin account holds shares worth $\\text{RM}12{,}000$ with a loan of $\\text{RM}6{,}000$. The price falls $25\\%$. What happens to the margin?</p>", "options": [["A", "It stays at $50\\%$ because the loan is unchanged."], ["B", "It falls to $25\\%$."], ["C", "It rises to $66.7\\%$."], ["D", "It falls to $33.3\\%$: equity drops to $\\text{RM}3{,}000$ on a value of $\\text{RM}9{,}000$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>New $V = 9{,}000$, $D = 6{,}000$, equity = $3{,}000$. $\\text{Margin} = 3{,}000 / 9{,}000 = \\mathbf{33.3\\%}$. The stock fell $25\\%$, but equity fell $50\\%$ (from $6{,}000$ to $3{,}000$).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You buy $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}60$ with a $\\text{RM}3{,}000$ loan. The maintenance margin is $25\\%$. Below roughly what share price is a margin call triggered?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}30.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}40.00$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}45.00$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}22.50$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$V^* = D / (1 - m) = 3{,}000 / 0.75 = 4{,}000$; per share $4{,}000 / 100 = \\mathbf{\\textbf{RM}40}$. Check: at $\\text{RM}40$, equity = $1{,}000$ and margin = $1{,}000 / 4{,}000 = 25\\%$.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two pension funds trade a large block of shares directly with each other through an electronic network, with no market maker. Which market is this?</p>", "options": [["A", "Primary market"], ["B", "Fourth market"], ["C", "Third market"], ["D", "Over-the-counter dealer market"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The fourth market is institutions trading directly with each other, often via ECNs, bypassing market makers. In the third market, a non-member market maker is involved.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>In a short sale (Table 2.6 logic), the investor sold $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$ and deposited $\\text{RM}2{,}500$. At what share price does short-account margin fall to $30\\%$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}55.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}75.00$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}65.00$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}57.69$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Funds held} = 7{,}500$. $\\text{Margin} = \\dfrac{7{,}500 - 100P}{100P} = 0.30 \\Rightarrow 7{,}500 = 130P \\Rightarrow P \\approx \\mathbf{\\textbf{RM}57.69}$.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A popular stock is heavily shorted, then jumps on good news, and short sellers rush to buy shares to close their positions. What is the likely effect?</p>", "options": [["A", "The price falls, because short sellers are selling."], ["B", "No effect, because shorts are closed off-exchange."], ["C", "Their buying adds demand and can push the price up further: a \"short squeeze\" that magnifies short sellers' losses."], ["D", "Margin calls only affect long investors."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Closing a short requires buying. Forced buying from rising losses and margin calls adds demand, an extension of the chapter's point that a rising price hurts short sellers, and can feed on itself.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why might IPO shares often be priced so that they rise on the first trading day?</p>", "options": [["A", "Underwriters always overprice."], ["B", "First-day returns are guaranteed by the issuer."], ["C", "Regulators fix first-day returns."], ["D", "Leaving some \"money on the table\" can compensate investors for information asymmetry and help underwriters place a large, risky issue fully."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's Table 2.1 shows positive average first-day returns. A common explanation builds on its information-asymmetry and underwriting-risk logic: pricing slightly low attracts investors and reduces the risk of an unsold issue.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Leverage of $2$x ($50\\%$ margin) doubles the expected excess return on equity. What does it do to the standard deviation of equity returns, ignoring interest-rate changes?</p>", "options": [["A", "Roughly doubles it, so the risk-return trade-off per unit of risk is not improved."], ["B", "Leaves it unchanged."], ["C", "Halves it."], ["D", "Removes it."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Scaling a position by $2$ scales both its mean excess return and its volatility by about $2$. Leverage magnifies; it does not create a better risk-return ratio, and borrowing costs make it slightly worse.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Short selling and buying on margin carry the same risk because both involve borrowing.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Incorrect, because shorting involves no borrowing."], ["C", "Incorrect: margin borrows money and profits when prices rise, its loss limited by the price reaching zero; shorting borrows shares and profits when prices fall, with a theoretically unlimited loss as prices rise."], ["D", "Correct, because both require a broker."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Both create obligations, but different ones (owe money vs owe shares) with different payoff shapes. The unlimited-loss asymmetry of the short sale is the key point.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A U.S. stock gains $12\\%$ in dollars while the dollar falls $10\\%$ against the ringgit. Evaluate the Malaysian investor's claim: \"I made $12\\%$.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Wrong: the ringgit return is $(1.12)(0.90) - 1 \\approx 0.8\\%$."], ["B", "Correct; currency doesn't matter."], ["C", "Wrong: the ringgit return is $22\\%$."], ["D", "Wrong: the ringgit return is $-10\\%$."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Home-currency return compounds the asset return and the currency change: $1.12 \\times 0.90 = 1.008$, about $\\mathbf{+0.8\\%}$. Foreign asset return ≠ domestic investor return.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A friend says: \"I'm certain this stock will rise, so I'll use the maximum margin available.\" What is the strongest evaluation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Leverage raises the sensitivity of equity to forecast errors, adds interest cost and margin-call risk: a correct long-term view can still end in a forced sale after a short-term fall."], ["B", "Sensible, because confidence reduces risk."], ["C", "Sensible, because margin calls only happen in bear markets."], ["D", "Wrong only because margin interest is tax-deductible."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Certainty about direction does not remove uncertainty about the path. Margin turns a temporary fall into a possible permanent loss if the investor is forced out.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A start-up chooses a private placement instead of an IPO. Which trade-off is most relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is faster and less costly in disclosure and marketing, but reaches fewer investors and gives them a less liquid security."], ["B", "A private placement always raises more money."], ["C", "It removes all regulation."], ["D", "It makes the shares trade on an exchange immediately."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The three primary-market methods differ in who can buy and how. A placement sells to selected investors: less public disclosure and distribution cost, but a narrower investor base and less liquidity.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Insider trading rules only protect the people on the other side of insiders' trades.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Correct, because markets are always efficient."], ["C", "Incorrect, because insider trading is legal."], ["D", "Incomplete: they also protect the market itself, because if investors believe trading is rigged, participation, liquidity and the cost of capital for all firms suffer."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter frames regulation as preserving confidence in the market mechanism, a benefit that reaches every issuer and investor.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Some economists argue short selling helps markets. Which argument is most consistent with the chapter's view of secondary markets?</p>", "options": [["A", "Short sellers guarantee prices never fall."], ["B", "Short selling raises companies' new capital."], ["C", "Allowing pessimistic investors to trade lets negative information enter prices, improving continuous pricing (price discovery)."], ["D", "Short sellers pay dividends to issuers."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Secondary markets provide continuous pricing. If only optimists can act, prices may reflect one side of the information. This extends the chapter's price-discovery idea.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>The chapter's market statistics (NYSE share of trading, trading hours, laws) date from 2014. What is the most rigorous way to use them?</p>", "options": [["A", "Use them as the course's reference values while recognising that market structures, regulations and venues evolve; the durable lessons are the functions (liquidity, price discovery, disclosure)."], ["B", "Treat them as current facts."], ["C", "Ignore the chapter."], ["D", "Assume Bursa Malaysia works identically."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes stress separating course material from current market facts. The economic functions are stable; the institutional details are not.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A company needs $\\text{RM}800$ million quickly and fears weak demand for its first share issue. Which arrangement best addresses the risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sell the shares itself through its website."], ["B", "Issue a red herring and skip the road show."], ["C", "Use an investment bank to underwrite the issue, with a syndicate to share the risk and a selling group to widen distribution."], ["D", "List directly on the fourth market."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Underwriting transfers the resale risk to the bank; a syndicate spreads a large exposure; a selling group reaches more investors. The road show (dropped in B) helps gauge demand and set the price.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor expects a stock to fall but wants to cap the loss if it instead rises sharply. Which combination fits the chapter's tools?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy the stock on margin with a sell stop below the price."], ["B", "Short the stock and place a buy stop above the current price."], ["C", "Short the stock with no protective order."], ["D", "Buy the stock with cash and a limit order."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The short position expresses the bearish view; a buy stop above the price triggers a purchase to close the short if the price rises, limiting the otherwise unlimited loss (a Chapter 3 order type).</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You are designing a lesson to show why the <strong>secondary</strong> market matters to a firm that never sells shares there. Which example makes the point most directly?</p>", "options": [["A", "A firm pays dividends."], ["B", "Investors pay more for a firm's IPO shares when they know they can resell them easily at a visible market price later."], ["C", "A firm issues commercial paper."], ["D", "A firm's bonds have fixed coupons."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It links secondary-market liquidity and pricing directly to the terms the firm gets in the primary market: the indirect economic value of resale markets.</p>"}, {"module": 2, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Design a single scenario that tests whether a student understands \"market direction + position structure = investor outcome\".</p>", "options": [["A", "\"Define a short sale.\""], ["B", "\"What year was the SEC created?\""], ["C", "\"List three exchanges.\""], ["D", "\"A stock rises $20\\%$. Compute the returns of a cash buyer, a $50\\%$-margin buyer and a short seller, and explain why they differ.\""]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The same price move produces $+20\\%$, about $+40\\%$ and a loss, forcing the student to apply the mechanics of all three positions: analysis rather than recall.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which kind of information reports <strong>factual data on the past behaviour</strong> of the economy, a market, an industry or a company?</p>", "options": [["A", "Analytical information"], ["B", "Speculative information"], ["C", "Descriptive information"], ["D", "Insider information"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Descriptive information is factual data on past behaviour. Analytical information combines current data with projections and recommendations about potential investments.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What does Regulation FD require?</p>", "options": [["A", "That companies pay dividends every year."], ["B", "That brokers register with the SEC."], ["C", "That critical company information be disclosed to investment professionals and the public at the same time."], ["D", "That all trades be reported to an exchange within a day."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Regulation FD (fair disclosure) prevents selective disclosure of material information to a privileged group. Broker registration (B) is a different requirement.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which description of a <strong>limit order</strong> matches the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "An order to buy at or below a specified price, or to sell at or above a specified price; if the limit is not met, the order is not filled."], ["B", "An order to buy or sell at the best price available when it is placed."], ["C", "An order that becomes a market order once a trigger price is reached."], ["D", "An order that expires at the end of the day."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>B is a market order, C is a stop (stop-loss) order and D describes a day order, which is a time condition rather than a price condition.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does the chapter warn that online investing removes a \"safety net\"?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because there is no live broker to question or slow down a decision, so mistakes and impulsive trades are easier to make."], ["B", "Because online brokers are always unregulated."], ["C", "Because online trades are slower."], ["D", "Because online investing prohibits limit orders."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The convenience of acting instantly also removes useful friction: a conversation with a broker. The chapter pairs this with warnings about \"free\" advice, frequent trading and margin.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why is the S&amp;P 500 considered more representative of the overall U.S. market than the DJIA?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is calculated using a different calculator."], ["B", "It includes 500 companies from major industries, versus the DJIA's 30 stocks."], ["C", "It contains only technology stocks."], ["D", "It is published more often."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Breadth is the point: more companies across more industries. The DJIA's $30$ high-quality stocks make it popular but narrower.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is <strong>street name</strong> registration?</p>", "options": [["A", "Registering securities in the investor's home address."], ["B", "Registering securities in the brokerage firm's name while they are held in trust for the client, who remains the beneficial owner."], ["C", "A way to hide ownership from regulators."], ["D", "Ownership transferred permanently to the broker."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The broker is the registered holder for convenience, but the investor keeps the economic ownership. Registration convenience is not economic ownership.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Why might an equal-weighted index and a market-value-weighted index of the same stocks give different returns in the same year?</p>", "options": [["A", "They use different stocks."], ["B", "Value-weighted indexes ignore market values."], ["C", "Equal-weighted indexes ignore prices."], ["D", "In a value-weighted index the largest companies dominate; in an equal-weighted index every stock counts the same, so a rise in small stocks affects the two differently."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Weighting method determines which stocks drive the index. An index is not neutral: its construction methodology affects what it measures.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A stock trades near $\\text{RM}30$. You want to buy but refuse to pay more than $\\text{RM}29.50$. Which order fits?</p>", "options": [["A", "Market order"], ["B", "Sell stop order at $\\text{RM}29.50$"], ["C", "Limit buy order at $\\text{RM}29.50$"], ["D", "Fill-or-kill market order"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A limit order protects the price: it executes only at $\\text{RM}29.50$ or lower. A market order prioritises execution and could fill above $\\text{RM}29.50$. A sell stop is for selling.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor buys $1{,}000$ shares at $\\text{RM}5.00$ and sells at $\\text{RM}5.30$. Commissions are $\\text{RM}30$ on each trade. What is the net profit?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}300$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}240$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}270$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}210$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Gross profit} = 1{,}000 \\times 0.30 = \\text{RM}300$. $\\text{Total commissions} = 2 \\times 30 = \\text{RM}60$. $\\text{Net} = 300 - 60 = \\mathbf{\\textbf{RM}240}$.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You own a stock bought at $\\text{RM}20$, now $\\text{RM}25$, and place a sell stop at $\\text{RM}22$. Overnight bad news makes the stock open at $\\text{RM}18$. What most likely happens?</p>", "options": [["A", "The order sells at exactly $\\text{RM}22$."], ["B", "The order becomes a buy order."], ["C", "The order is cancelled and no sale occurs."], ["D", "The stop triggers and becomes a market order, so the sale executes near $\\text{RM}18$, below the stop price."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed price. Once triggered the order becomes a market order and fills at the best available price, which here is far lower.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A price-weighted average has three stocks priced $\\text{RM}10$, $\\text{RM}20$ and $\\text{RM}90$. Which stock dominates, and what happens if the $\\text{RM}90$ stock rises $10\\%$ while the others are unchanged?</p>", "options": [["A", "The $\\text{RM}10$ stock dominates; the average rises about $15\\%$."], ["B", "All stocks are equal; the average is unchanged."], ["C", "The $\\text{RM}90$ stock dominates; the average rises from $40$ to $43$, about $7.5\\%$."], ["D", "The $\\text{RM}20$ stock dominates; the average rises $1\\%$."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A price-weighted average is the average of prices: $(10 + 20 + 90) / 3 = 40$. After the rise: $(10 + 20 + 99) / 3 = 43$, a $7.5\\%$ increase. The highest-priced stock has the biggest influence.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A bond with a face value of $1{,}000$ and a $6\\%$ coupon trades at $950$. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The current yield equals $6\\%$ because the coupon is fixed."], ["B", "The yield is below $6\\%$."], ["C", "The yield to maturity is above $6\\%$, because the buyer receives the coupon plus a price gain to par at maturity."], ["D", "Yield cannot be compared with the coupon rate."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes bond yield is total return from buying at the current price and holding to maturity: coupon plus the gain from $950$ to $1{,}000$. Coupon ≠ yield.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why can a stop-<strong>limit</strong> order fail to protect an investor on a sharp fall?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because it becomes a market order."], ["B", "Because it becomes a limit order once triggered, and if the price falls through the limit without trading at that level, the order may not fill at all."], ["C", "Because it can only be used for buying."], ["D", "Because it executes before the stop price."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A stop-limit gives price control but gives up execution certainty. A plain stop gives execution at an uncertain price.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A broker earns a commission on every trade and recommends that a retiree \"rotate\" their portfolio monthly. What risk is most relevant?</p>", "options": [["A", "Churning: the broker's incentive (more trades, more commissions) may not align with the client's interest"], ["B", "Currency risk"], ["C", "Event risk"], ["D", "Reinvestment risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Excessive trading to generate commissions is churning. Transaction costs are a hurdle every trade must clear, so frequent trading usually reduces net returns.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A balanced fund of Malaysian small-cap stocks returned $12\\%$ while the S&amp;P 500 returned $18\\%$. Which analysis is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "The fund underperformed by $6$ percentage points."], ["B", "The comparison is unreliable: the S&amp;P 500 does not represent the fund's investment universe, so a small-cap benchmark should be used."], ["C", "The fund beat the market, because small caps are riskier."], ["D", "Benchmarks are irrelevant."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Benchmark selection must match the exposure being evaluated. Comparing a Malaysian small-cap portfolio with the S&amp;P 500 is not automatically meaningful.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>An investor places a market order to sell $10{,}000$ shares of a thinly traded stock. Which outcome is the main risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Poor execution: the order is large relative to market depth, so successive fills may occur at progressively worse prices."], ["B", "The order will never execute."], ["C", "The order becomes a limit order."], ["D", "The order expires at day end."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A market order prioritises execution, not price. In a thin market, a large order moves the price against the seller; a limit order would control price at the cost of fill certainty.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A forum user posts a strong buy case and mentions owning the stock. Which is the best first step?</p>", "options": [["A", "Treat the post as opinion from an interested party, verify its claims against filings and primary evidence, and ask what the poster gains if readers buy."], ["B", "Buy because the post is detailed."], ["C", "Ignore all online information."], ["D", "Sell the stock short immediately."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Source incentives matter (pump-and-dump). The skill is to separate evidence from narrative, not to dismiss information.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why can two broad indexes with similar long-run trends still differ materially over a two-year window, as in Figure 3.5?</p>", "options": [["A", "One of them is wrong."], ["B", "Indexes differ only in how often they are published."], ["C", "They hold different securities and weights, so shocks to particular sectors or large companies affect them by different amounts."], ["D", "One excludes dividends by law."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Same market environment ≠ identical index return. Composition and weighting create the divergence.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Behavioural finance suggests investors trade more after gains. Which chapter idea connects most directly?</p>", "options": [["A", "Mediation versus arbitration"], ["B", "The definition of the S&amp;P 500"], ["C", "The warning about frequent trading and day trading: more trades raise costs and error opportunities without necessarily improving returns."], ["D", "Street name registration"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Activity feels like control but costs money. The chapter's stance is that more trades do not automatically mean more return.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"A stop-loss order guarantees that my maximum loss is the difference between my purchase price and the stop price.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Incorrect: the stop becomes a market order when triggered, so a gap can fill well below the stop; a stop-limit may not fill at all."], ["C", "Correct, but only for day orders."], ["D", "Incorrect, because stops do not exist for long positions."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Stop price = trigger, not guaranteed sale price. This is among the chapter's most testable ideas.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"SIPC protection means my investments cannot lose money if my broker fails.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incorrect: SIPC protects against certain losses from a broker's financial failure, but not against market losses, bad advice or churning."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Correct, because SIPC insures market value without limit."], ["D", "Incorrect, because SIPC only protects the broker."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Investor protection means protection against certain institutional failures, not against ordinary investment losses. The slides cite historical limits (USD $500{,}000$ for securities, USD $250{,}000$ for cash).</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Advisors are registered with the SEC, so their advice is reliable.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incorrect: registration means meeting regulatory requirements; the slides note that no law or regulator guarantees competence, so experience and credentials still matter."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Correct, because registration tests investment skill."], ["D", "Incorrect, because advisors cannot register."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Licensed does not imply good. A good advisor also asks many questions about the client's goals and circumstances before recommending anything.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A brokerage offers zero commissions but earns money by routing orders to market makers. Evaluate \"free trading means no trading costs\".</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Incomplete: an explicit commission is only one cost; spreads and the execution price also matter, and zero commission may encourage more frequent trading."], ["C", "Incorrect, because market makers do not exist."], ["D", "Correct, because spreads are zero."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Transaction costs include commissions, bid-ask spreads and poor execution. Lower visible friction can raise trading frequency, the behavioural issue the chapter flags.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"A good advisor who charges $1\\%$ of assets a year is worth it if performance matches the market.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because matching the market is the goal."], ["B", "Correct, because fees are never relevant."], ["C", "Doubtful on returns alone: the fee reduces net return relative to the benchmark unless the advisor adds value through planning, discipline, tax and risk management that the investor could not obtain cheaply."], ["D", "Incorrect, because advisors cannot match the market."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter notes advice fees and that advice can improve process, not eliminate risk. Value must be weighed against net-of-fee results and services received.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>An analyst argues that because Regulation FD forces simultaneous disclosure, stock prices fully reflect all information instantly. Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Fully correct."], ["B", "Correct because analysts do not exist."], ["C", "Wrong because FD allows selective disclosure."], ["D", "Overreach: simultaneous disclosure improves fairness of access, but interpreting information still takes judgment and resources, so prices may adjust unevenly."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Equal access to information is not equal ability to interpret it. The chapter's descriptive-versus-analytical distinction supports this.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>The chapter's brokerage examples, SIPC limits and index lists are from 2014. What is the best research practice?</p>", "options": [["A", "Quote them as current rules."], ["B", "Assume they never change."], ["C", "Delete all numbers."], ["D", "Use them as the course's reference points and verify current specifics from primary sources before applying them."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Institutional details change; the economic functions (execution, protection, benchmarking) are durable.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Design a sound process for a student wanting to invest in a technology company after seeing an online recommendation.</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy immediately before the price moves."], ["B", "Copy a friend's portfolio."], ["C", "Ask an anonymous forum for a price target and buy the next day."], ["D", "Read the company's filings, review its financial performance, separate facts from forecasts, compare with competitors and a relevant benchmark, set an entry price, place a limit order and monitor the position."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It uses primary sources, separates descriptive from analytical information, uses a benchmark and chooses an order type that matches the price condition.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor sees a stock at $\\text{RM}28$ but is only willing to buy at $\\text{RM}25$ or below, and is happy to wait several weeks. Which order set-up fits best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Market order, day"], ["B", "Fill-or-kill market order"], ["C", "Sell stop at $\\text{RM}25$"], ["D", "Limit buy at $\\text{RM}25$, good-'til-canceled"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The price condition is \"$\\text{RM}25$ or lower\" (limit) and the time condition is \"willing to wait\" (GTC). A market order would buy at $\\text{RM}28$.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which design would best protect an investor who has shorted a stock at $\\text{RM}50$ against a sharp rise?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy stop at $\\text{RM}60$"], ["B", "Sell stop at $\\text{RM}40$"], ["C", "Limit sell at $\\text{RM}60$"], ["D", "No order"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A buy stop above the current price is activated if the price rises to $\\text{RM}60$, triggering a purchase that closes the short. The slides note stops can be used to buy stock to limit risk on short positions.</p>"}, {"module": 3, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which exam question best distinguishes a student who understands order types from one who memorised definitions?</p>", "options": [["A", "\"Define a market order.\""], ["B", "\"What does GTC stand for?\""], ["C", "\"List four order types.\""], ["D", "\"A holder of a stock bought at $\\text{RM}20$ (now $\\text{RM}25$) wants downside protection and also wants to avoid selling in a panic gap. Compare a stop and a stop-limit order and recommend one, justifying it.\""]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It requires applying the execution-certainty versus price-control trade-off to a specific objective, the Bloom Apply-Evaluate level.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement defines the time value of money?</p>", "options": [["A", "Money loses value only when prices rise."], ["B", "As long as money can earn interest, its value depends on the point in time at which it is received."], ["C", "Interest is paid only on the original deposit."], ["D", "Cash flows in the future are always larger than cash flows today."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A dollar (or ringgit) received sooner can start earning interest sooner, so it is worth more than the same amount later. A describes inflation, C simple interest, and D is not true in general.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which equation gives the <strong>required return</strong> in the chapter's model?</p>", "options": [["A", "Required return = real rate x inflation premium x risk premium"], ["B", "Required return = real rate + expected inflation premium + risk premium"], ["C", "Required return = expected return - risk premium"], ["D", "Required return = risk-free rate - risk premium"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The three components add. Because the risk-free rate equals real rate plus inflation premium, this simplifies to $R_f + RP$ (risk premium).</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What does the <strong>coefficient of variation</strong> measure?</p>", "options": [["A", "Absolute dispersion of returns"], ["B", "The sensitivity of a stock to the market"], ["C", "The slope of the security market line"], ["D", "Relative dispersion: standard deviation per unit of average return"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$CV = \\sigma / \\bar r$. Standard deviation is the absolute measure (A); beta and the SML belong to Chapter 5.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does the present value of a future payment fall when the discount rate rises?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the payment itself shrinks."], ["B", "Because inflation rises with the discount rate."], ["C", "Because a higher opportunity rate means less money today is needed to grow into the same future amount."], ["D", "Because compounding becomes simple interest."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$PV = \\dfrac{FV}{(1 + r)^n}$. A larger $r$ makes the denominator larger. Economically, if you can earn more elsewhere, you need to put less aside now.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why can holding-period return (HPR) mislead when the holding period exceeds a year?</p>", "options": [["A", "HPR does not account for the time value of money, so returns over different horizons are not comparable."], ["B", "HPR ignores capital gains."], ["C", "HPR only works for bonds."], ["D", "HPR cannot be negative."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A $40\\%$ return over one year and a $40\\%$ return over five years have the same HPR but very different annual performance. Compound annual yield (IRR) fixes this.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which pair correctly contrasts business risk and financial risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Business risk comes from interest rates; financial risk comes from inflation."], ["B", "Business risk affects bonds only; financial risk affects stocks only."], ["C", "Business risk concerns uncertainty in the firm's earnings and operations; financial risk concerns uncertainty from its debt-and-equity financing mix."], ["D", "They are the same risk described differently."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Two identical restaurants share similar business risk, but the one with heavy debt carries more financial risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A retiree holds a bond paying a fixed $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ a year while inflation rises sharply. Which risk is most directly at work?</p>", "options": [["A", "Business risk"], ["B", "Purchasing-power risk"], ["C", "Event risk"], ["D", "Financial risk"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The nominal payment is unchanged, but its real value falls. Fixed-income securities and deposits are the chapter's examples of purchasing-power risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>$\\text{RM}5{,}000$ is deposited for $4$ years at $6\\%$ compounded annually. What is the future value?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}6{,}200.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}6{,}312.38$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}6{,}000.00$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}6{,}524.77$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$FV = 5{,}000 \\times (1.06)^{4} = 5{,}000 \\times 1.262477 = \\mathbf{\\textbf{RM}6{,}312.38}$. A uses simple interest-like growth; C is simple interest at $5\\%$; D uses $7\\%$.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is the present value of $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ due in $3$ years at an $8\\%$ discount rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}7{,}938.32$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}7{,}500.00$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}8{,}000.00$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}8{,}264.46$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$PV = 10{,}000 / 1.08^{3} = 10{,}000 / 1.259712 = \\mathbf{\\textbf{RM}7{,}938.32}$. D discounts for only $2$ years.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A share is bought at $\\text{RM}80$, pays a $\\text{RM}4$ dividend, and is worth $\\text{RM}84$ at year-end. What is the HPR?</p>", "options": [["A", "$5\\%$"], ["B", "$10\\%$"], ["C", "$15\\%$"], ["D", "$4\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$HPR = \\dfrac{\\text{income} + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0} = \\dfrac{4 + 4}{80} = \\mathbf{10\\%}$. A counts only the dividend or only the gain.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A company borrows $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ at $8\\%$ and repays it in $5$ equal year-end instalments. Approximately what is each instalment?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}25{,}046$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}20{,}000$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}28{,}000$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}21{,}600$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The loan is the present value of an ordinary annuity: $PMT = 100{,}000 / [(1 - 1.08^{-5}) / 0.08] = 100{,}000 / 3.99271 \\approx \\mathbf{\\textbf{RM}25{,}046}$.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A stated rate of $8\\%$ is compounded quarterly. What is the effective annual rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "$8.00\\%$"], ["B", "$8.50\\%$"], ["C", "$8.24\\%$"], ["D", "$8.32\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$(1 + 0.08 / 4)^{4} - 1 = 1.02^{4} - 1 = 1.082432 - 1 \\approx \\mathbf{8.24\\%}$. More frequent compounding raises the true rate.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>$\\text{RM}1{,}000$ is deposited at the end of each year for $8$ years at $8\\%$ (future value $\\text{RM}10{,}636.63$). Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Interest earned is $\\text{RM}2{,}636.63$, because earlier deposits compound for longer."], ["B", "Interest earned is $\\text{RM}8{,}000$."], ["C", "The future value equals $\\text{RM}8{,}000 \\times 1.08$."], ["D", "The last deposit earns seven years of interest."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Total deposits} = \\text{RM}8{,}000$, so interest = $10{,}636.63 - 8{,}000 = \\mathbf{\\textbf{RM}2{,}636.63}$. The first deposit compounds seven years and the last (made at the valuation date) earns nothing.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Investment A has a mean return of $10\\%$ and $\\sigma$ of $20\\%$. Investment B has a mean of $20\\%$ and $\\sigma$ of $30\\%$. Which is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A is riskier on both measures."], ["B", "A has lower $\\sigma$, but B has lower relative dispersion (CV $1.5$ versus $2.0$)."], ["C", "B has lower $\\sigma$ and lower CV."], ["D", "CV cannot be computed for these data."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>CV(A) = $20 / 10 = 2.0$; CV(B) = $30 / 20 = 1.5$. Absolute and relative risk can rank investments differently.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An asset is bought for $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ and sold for $\\text{RM}1{,}400$ five years later, with no income. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its compound annual return is $8\\%$."], ["B", "Its compound annual return is $40\\%$."], ["C", "Its HPR is $40\\%$, and its compound annual return is about $6.96\\%$."], ["D", "Its HPR is $6.96\\%$."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$HPR = 400 / 1{,}000 = 40\\%$. $\\text{Annual yield} = 1.4^{1/5} - 1 \\approx 6.96\\%$. Dividing $40\\%$ by $5$ $(= 8\\%)$ ignores compounding.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>At $9\\%$ compounded annually, roughly how long does money take to double?</p>", "options": [["A", "About $4$ years"], ["B", "About $16$ years"], ["C", "About $12$ years"], ["D", "About $8$ years"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Solve $1.09^{n} = 2$: $n = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{\\ln 1.09} \\approx 8.04$ years. (The \"rule of $72$\" gives $72 / 9 = 8$.)</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>An investment costs $\\text{RM}12{,}000$ and pays $\\text{RM}4{,}000$, $\\text{RM}5{,}000$ and $\\text{RM}6{,}000$ at the ends of years $1$-$3$. At a $10\\%$ required return its NPV is about $+\\text{RM}276$. What happens at a $12\\%$ required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "NPV rises, because the payments are unchanged."], ["B", "NPV stays exactly the same."], ["C", "NPV turns negative (PV of benefits ≈ $\\text{RM}11{,}828 &lt; \\text{RM}12{,}000$), reversing the decision."], ["D", "The investment becomes risk-free."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>At $12\\%$: $4{,}000 / 1.12 + 5{,}000 / 1.12^{2} + 6{,}000 / 1.12^{3} \\approx 3{,}571 + 3{,}986 + 4{,}271 = 11{,}828$. A thin positive NPV has little room for adverse changes in assumptions.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why does using IRR to evaluate a bond implicitly assume reinvestment of coupons at the IRR itself?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the IRR solves for one single rate that discounts every cash flow, which is equivalent to compounding each interim cash flow forward at that same rate."], ["B", "Because coupons are always spent."], ["C", "Because IRR ignores coupons."], ["D", "Because reinvestment rates are fixed by law."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter flags this assumption: if actual reinvestment rates are lower, the fully compounded return falls below the IRR.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>An investment returns $+50\\%$ in year $1$ and $-50\\%$ in year $2$. The arithmetic average return is $0\\%$. What happens to wealth?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is unchanged."], ["B", "It rises by $25\\%$."], ["C", "It falls to $75\\%$ of the starting amount, a compound annual return of about $-13.4\\%$."], ["D", "It falls to $50\\%$ of the starting amount."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$1.5 \\times 0.5 = 0.75$. The compound (geometric) annual return is $\\sqrt{0.75} - 1 \\approx -13.4\\%$. This is why averages of annual returns overstate compound growth when returns are volatile.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"An investment expected to return $12\\%$ is attractive, because $12\\%$ is a good return.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incomplete: it depends on the required return for its risk; if the required return is $14\\%$, $12\\%$ is inadequate."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Correct if the investment is a stock."], ["D", "Incorrect, because only returns above $20\\%$ are attractive."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Expected return is a forecast; required return is the hurdle. No return is good or bad in isolation.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Investment B has the lower standard deviation, so it is the better investment.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incomplete: low risk is not automatically superior if the return is far lower; the decision depends on required return, risk preference and objectives."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Incorrect, because $\\sigma$ is irrelevant."], ["D", "Correct if B is a bond."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter teaches optimising the relationship between return and risk, not minimising risk.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Bank A offers $12.5\\%$ compounded annually. Bank B offers $12\\%$ compounded monthly. Which is better?</p>", "options": [["A", "A, since $12.5\\% &gt; 12\\%$."], ["B", "B, since monthly compounding always beats annual compounding."], ["C", "They are equal."], ["D", "B: its effective annual rate is $(1.01)^{12} - 1 \\approx 12.68\\%$, above A's $12.5\\%$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Compare effective annual rates, not stated rates. Option D overgeneralises: monthly compounding beats annual compounding only at the same stated rate; here the stated rates differ, so the calculation decides.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>Fund A gained $50\\%$ over $4$ years. Fund B earned $11\\%$ a year for $4$ years. Which is better on a compound annual basis?</p>", "options": [["A", "B: A's compound annual return is $1.5^{1/4} - 1 \\approx 10.67\\%$, below B's $11\\%$."], ["B", "A, because $50\\%$ is larger than $11\\%$."], ["C", "They are equal."], ["D", "A, because it earned its return faster."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Always annualise before comparing horizons.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"A 10-year historical standard deviation tells us exactly how volatile the investment will be next year.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Overstated: it is an estimate from a limited sample that can change; it is evidence for forming expectations, not a promise."], ["C", "Correct for stocks but not for bonds."], ["D", "Incorrect because standard deviation is always zero."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter says historical performance provides a basis for expectations but not a guarantee. A five-observation sample, as in the Target example, offers limited evidence.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>When is the coefficient of variation a poor measure?</p>", "options": [["A", "When returns are always positive."], ["B", "When the mean return is near zero or negative, so the ratio becomes huge, undefined or hard to interpret."], ["C", "When there are more than $10$ observations."], ["D", "When returns are in ringgit."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$CV = \\sigma / \\bar r$. A mean near zero makes CV explode; a negative mean makes its sign meaningless. Use $\\sigma$ and judgement in such cases.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Textbooks use the 3-month Treasury bill as the risk-free rate. When discounting a 25-year project, what is the main conceptual concern?</p>", "options": [["A", "T-bills pay no interest."], ["B", "No concern: the risk-free rate never changes."], ["C", "T-bills are riskier than stocks."], ["D", "A maturity mismatch: a short-term rate reflects short-term conditions, so the discount rate for long cash flows should reflect longer-horizon rates and the project's own risk."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The risk-free rate is a proxy. Required return also needs a risk premium appropriate to the project and the horizon.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You need $\\text{RM}50{,}000$ in $5$ years and can earn $8\\%$ a year. Which equal year-end contribution is required (approximately)?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}10{,}000$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}6{,}250$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}7{,}500$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}8{,}523$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{FV annuity factor} = (1.08^{5} - 1) / 0.08 = 5.8666$. $PMT = 50{,}000 / 5.8666 \\approx \\mathbf{\\textbf{RM}8{,}523}$. A ignores interest.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which sequence best builds a decision framework for choosing among three investments, per the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Choose the highest expected return."], ["B", "Choose the lowest standard deviation."], ["C", "Estimate expected returns; determine the required return for each risk; measure risk with SD and CV; compare expected with required return; choose the one consistent with the investor's risk preference."], ["D", "Choose the one with the longest history."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This is the chapter's four-step decision process, combining return, risk and investor preference.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor estimates a real return of $2.5\\%$, expected inflation of $3\\%$ and a risk premium of $4\\%$ for a particular security. What required return should the investor use as the discount rate or hurdle?</p>", "options": [["A", "$5.5\\%$"], ["B", "$7.5\\%$"], ["C", "$4.0\\%$"], ["D", "$9.5\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$R_{f} = 2.5\\% + 3\\% = 5.5\\%$; required return = $5.5\\% + 4\\% = \\mathbf{9.5\\%}$. A is the risk-free rate only.</p>"}, {"module": 4, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which exam question best separates a student who memorised formulas from one who understands TVM?</p>", "options": [["A", "\"State the PV formula.\""], ["B", "\"Define an annuity.\""], ["C", "\"What does PMT stand for?\""], ["D", "\"Two projects pay $\\text{RM}300$ a year for $5$ years starting now, versus $\\text{RM}300$ a year for $5$ years starting in one year. Without a calculator, which is worth more at a positive rate, and why? Then explain how the two values relate.\""]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It requires recognising an annuity due versus an ordinary annuity, and reasoning that every payment arrives one period earlier, so the annuity due is worth $(1 + r)$ times as much.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>How does the chapter define an <strong>efficient portfolio</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "The portfolio with the highest possible return, whatever the risk."], ["B", "The portfolio that provides the highest return for a given level of risk."], ["C", "The portfolio with the largest number of securities."], ["D", "The portfolio with zero standard deviation."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Efficiency is about the trade-off: highest return for a given risk (equivalently, lowest risk for a given return). A ignores risk, C confuses count with quality, and D is only possible in special cases.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>What is the beta of the market portfolio?</p>", "options": [["A", "$1.0$"], ["B", "$0.5$"], ["C", "$0$"], ["D", "$2.0$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta measures sensitivity to the market benchmark, so the market's own beta is $1.0$. A beta of $0$ means no linear sensitivity to the market.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which risk can be reduced or eliminated through diversification?</p>", "options": [["A", "Unsystematic (diversifiable, firm-specific) risk"], ["B", "Systematic (nondiversifiable) risk"], ["C", "Inflation risk affecting all firms"], ["D", "War and political risk"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Firm-specific events such as lawsuits and strikes can be diluted by holding many securities. Systematic risk arises from forces that affect similar investments broadly, so diversification cannot remove it.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why is the standard deviation of a portfolio generally <strong>not</strong> the weighted average of its assets' standard deviations?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because standard deviations cannot be averaged by law."], ["B", "Because standard deviation is always larger for portfolios."], ["C", "Because weights do not affect risk."], ["D", "Because portfolio risk also depends on the correlations between the assets: imperfect co-movement lets fluctuations partly offset."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The covariance term $2w_{A}w_{B}\\sigma_{A}\\sigma_{B}\\rho$ explains it. Only when $\\rho = +1$ does the portfolio $\\sigma$ equal the weighted average.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement best contrasts beta and standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "They measure exactly the same thing."], ["B", "Both measure only firm-specific risk."], ["C", "Beta measures total volatility; standard deviation measures market sensitivity."], ["D", "Standard deviation measures total volatility; beta measures sensitivity to market movements (systematic risk)."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A stock driven by company-specific news may have a high $\\sigma$ but a low $\\beta$. Neither replaces the other for every purpose.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does CAPM reward only systematic risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because unsystematic risk does not exist."], ["B", "Because CAPM was designed for bonds only."], ["C", "Because systematic risk is always smaller."], ["D", "Because investors can cheaply diversify away firm-specific risk, so the market has no reason to compensate them for holding it."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>More total risk does not automatically justify a higher CAPM return. What matters is the asset's contribution to market-related risk (an assumption-driven theoretical result).</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Why do index-fund providers emphasise holding the whole market rather than a few stocks, in terms of the chapter's risk decomposition?</p>", "options": [["A", "It eliminates all risk."], ["B", "It removes the need for beta."], ["C", "It guarantees returns above the market."], ["D", "A broad portfolio removes most firm-specific risk at low cost, leaving mainly systematic risk, the part the market compensates."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The diversification curve (Figure 5.8) flattens at the systematic-risk floor. Holding more securities beyond that gives diminishing benefit.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A portfolio is $40\\%$ in an asset with $E(R) = 10\\%$ and $60\\%$ in an asset with $E(R) = 5\\%$. What is the portfolio's expected return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$7.5\\%$"], ["B", "$6.0\\%$"], ["C", "$7.0\\%$"], ["D", "$15.0\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$E(R_{p}) = 0.40(10\\%) + 0.60(5\\%) = 4\\% + 3\\% = \\mathbf{7\\%}$. A is the simple average; D is the sum.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>$R_{f} = 5\\%$, $E(R_{m}) = 11\\%$ and $\\beta = 1.2$. What is the CAPM required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$12.2\\%$"], ["B", "$18.2\\%$"], ["C", "$13.2\\%$"], ["D", "$7.2\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{R} = 5\\% + 1.2 \\times (11\\% - 5\\%) = 5\\% + 7.2\\% = \\mathbf{12.2\\%}$. B wrongly multiplies beta by the market return; D adds only the premium part.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A portfolio holds $50\\%$ in a stock with $\\beta = 1.2$, $30\\%$ with $\\beta = 0.8$ and $20\\%$ with $\\beta = 1.5$. What is the portfolio beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "$1.14$"], ["B", "$1.00$"], ["C", "$1.17$"], ["D", "$1.50$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\beta_{p} = 0.5(1.2) + 0.3(0.8) + 0.2(1.5) = 0.60 + 0.24 + 0.30 = \\mathbf{1.14}$. Portfolio beta is the weighted average of the betas.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>$R_{f} = 4\\%$ and $E(R_{m}) = 10\\%$. What does CAPM require from an asset with $\\beta = -0.5$, and why is that sensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "$7\\%$, because all assets need more than the market."], ["B", "$4\\%$, because beta never changes required return."], ["C", "$-4\\%$, because it must lose money."], ["D", "$1\\%$: a negative-beta asset tends to rise when the market falls, so it acts as a hedge and investors accept less return for it."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$4\\% + (-0.5)(6\\%) = 1\\%$. Asset value inside a portfolio depends on how it co-moves with everything else, not only on its own expected return.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>$w_{A} = 0.6$, $w_{B} = 0.4$, $\\sigma_{A} = 15\\%$, $\\sigma_{B} = 25\\%$, $\\rho = 0.2$. What is $\\sigma_{p}$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$19.0\\%$"], ["B", "$14.7\\%$"], ["C", "$12.0\\%$"], ["D", "$21.7\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Variance} = 0.36(0.0225) + 0.16(0.0625) + 2(0.6)(0.4)(0.15)(0.25)(0.2) = 0.0081 + 0.0100 + 0.0036 = 0.0217$; $\\sigma_{p} = \\sqrt{0.0217} \\approx \\mathbf{14.7\\%}$. A is the weighted average of the $\\sigma$s.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two assets have $\\sigma = 20\\%$ and $10\\%$ and are held $50 / 50$. If they are uncorrelated $(\\rho = 0)$, what is the portfolio standard deviation?</p>", "options": [["A", "$15.00\\%$"], ["B", "$8.66\\%$"], ["C", "$11.18\\%$"], ["D", "$5.00\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Variance} = 0.25(0.04) + 0.25(0.01) = 0.0125$; $\\sigma_{p} = \\sqrt{0.0125} \\approx \\mathbf{11.18\\%}$. A is the weighted average $(\\rho = +1)$; B is $\\rho = -0.5$; D is $\\rho = -1$.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A stock has $\\sigma = 25\\%$, a correlation of $0.6$ with the market, and the market has $\\sigma = 15\\%$. What is its beta?</p>", "options": [["A", "$0.40$"], ["B", "$0.60$"], ["C", "$1.67$"], ["D", "$1.00$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\beta = \\rho(\\sigma_{i} / \\sigma_{m}) = 0.6 \\times (25 / 15) = \\mathbf{1.0}$. You cannot get beta from the stock's $\\sigma$ alone.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Stock A has $\\beta = 0.5$ and $\\sigma = 45\\%$. Stock B has $\\beta = 1.5$ and $\\sigma = 30\\%$. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "A is safer on every measure."], ["B", "B has both higher beta and higher total volatility."], ["C", "A has lower market sensitivity but higher total volatility; neither is unconditionally safer."], ["D", "Beta and $\\sigma$ must always rank assets identically."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta captures systematic exposure; $\\sigma$ captures total variation including firm-specific risk. A low beta does not make a stock low-volatility overall.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A security has $E(R) = 9\\%$, $\\beta = 0.7$, $R_{f} = 4\\%$ and $E(R_{m}) = 10\\%$. What is its expected alpha?</p>", "options": [["A", "$-0.8\\%$"], ["B", "$0\\%$"], ["C", "$+5.0\\%$"], ["D", "$+0.8\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Required} = 4\\% + 0.7(6\\%) = 8.2\\%$. $\\text{Alpha} = 9\\% - 8.2\\% = \\mathbf{+0.8\\%}$: above the SML.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>During a global crisis the correlation between two assets ($\\sigma = 20\\%$ and $10\\%$, $50 / 50$) rises from $0$ to $0.8$. Approximately what happens to $\\sigma_{p}$?</p>", "options": [["A", "It falls from $11.2\\%$ to $8\\%$."], ["B", "It becomes $5\\%$."], ["C", "It stays at $11.2\\%$."], ["D", "It rises from about $11.2\\%$ to about $14.3\\%$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Variance} = 0.0125 + 2(0.25)(0.2)(0.1)\\rho = 0.0125 + 0.01\\rho$. At $\\rho = 0.8$: $0.0205$, so $\\sigma_{p} \\approx 14.3\\%$. Historical low correlation is not guaranteed future low correlation.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Why can estimated betas be unstable?</p>", "options": [["A", "They are estimated from historical returns over a particular window and benchmark, so changes in the firm, the market or the estimation period alter the estimate."], ["B", "Betas are set by regulators each quarter."], ["C", "Beta is always exactly $1$."], ["D", "Beta depends only on dividends."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta describes a linear relationship estimated from past data; it is not a mechanical multiplier. This limits how confidently SML-based conclusions can be drawn.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>A stock's firm-specific risk doubles, but its covariance with the market is unchanged. What does CAPM predict for its required return?</p>", "options": [["A", "It doubles."], ["B", "It is unchanged: beta depends on covariance with the market, and unsystematic risk is not priced."], ["C", "It falls to the risk-free rate."], ["D", "It becomes negative."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\beta = \\dfrac{\\text{Cov}(R_i, R_m)}{\\text{Var}(R_m)}$. Unchanged covariance means unchanged beta, so the same required return. Investors could diversify the extra risk away.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A stock with $\\beta = 1.5$ is expected by an analyst to return $15\\%$. $R_{f} = 4\\%$ and $E(R_{m}) = 10\\%$. Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "It is overvalued: below the SML."], ["B", "Required return is $13\\%$, so expected alpha is $+2\\%$: above the SML, potentially undervalued, but only as reliable as the forecast."], ["C", "It is fairly priced exactly on the SML."], ["D", "CAPM cannot be applied with $\\beta$ above $1$."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{Required} = 4\\% + 1.5(6\\%) = 13\\%$. Positive alpha is not proof of a bargain; it may reflect an inaccurate forecast or an inadequate model.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"Adding more stocks to a portfolio always reduces risk.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Incorrect: a highly correlated additional stock adds little diversification; what matters is whether the new asset reduces covariance or concentration."], ["B", "Correct."], ["C", "Correct, but only for stocks above beta $1$."], ["D", "Incorrect, because adding stocks always increases risk."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Number of assets ≠ quality of diversification. Forty bank stocks are still one big bet on banking.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Evaluate: \"I'll choose a portfolio below the efficient frontier because it feels safer.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Sensible: feelings determine efficiency."], ["B", "Flawed: a dominated portfolio offers the same risk for less return; a cautious investor should pick a lower-risk point on the frontier instead."], ["C", "Correct, because the frontier is always riskier."], ["D", "Correct, because portfolios below the frontier have zero risk."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Comfort and efficiency are different things. Risk preference selects among efficient portfolios; it is not a reason to hold an inefficient one.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>Portfolios A, B and C have expected alphas of $+0.8\\%$, $+1.4\\%$ and $+1.0\\%$, and standard deviations of $12\\%$, $18\\%$ and $28\\%$. Evaluate \"Portfolio B must be chosen because it has the highest alpha.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Incomplete: the choice also depends on risk tolerance, confidence in the forecasts, feasibility and total and systematic risk."], ["C", "Incorrect, because alpha is meaningless."], ["D", "Correct, because alpha equals return."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Alpha is one input. A cautious investor may prefer A (lowest $\\sigma$ and beta) despite a smaller alpha.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Portfolio W $(\\beta = 0.91)$ is less market-sensitive than V $(\\beta = 1.195)$. Evaluate: \"W therefore has lower total standard deviation.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct."], ["B", "Correct, because beta equals $\\sigma$."], ["C", "Not implied: beta captures only market-related sensitivity; W could still have higher total volatility from firm-specific risk or concentration."], ["D", "Incorrect, because W has a higher beta."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Beta alone cannot reveal total $\\sigma$ or expected return. The chapter stresses that the two measure different dimensions.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>An optimiser built from historical estimates recommends a very concentrated \"efficient\" portfolio. What is the best critical response?</p>", "options": [["A", "Accept it; mathematics is always right."], ["B", "Recognise estimation error: small errors in expected returns and correlations can swing optimal weights, so test robustness, add constraints and compare with simple diversified alternatives."], ["C", "Reject MPT entirely."], ["D", "Invest only in the highest-return asset."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter warns that MPT depends on statistical estimates, so a mathematically optimal portfolio can perform poorly in reality if the estimates are poor.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>A manager reports positive alpha against CAPM for ten years. Which interpretation is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Consistent with skill, but also with an inadequate pricing model, omitted risk factors, mismeasured beta or luck; further tests are needed."], ["B", "Proof of superior skill."], ["C", "Proof that markets are inefficient."], ["D", "Meaningless because alpha is always zero."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes caution that positive alpha could reflect inaccurate expected-return estimates or an inadequate pricing model. It is a joint test of the manager and the model.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Asset A has $\\sigma = 30\\%$ and asset B has $\\sigma = 10\\%$, with $\\rho = -1$. Which weights make the portfolio riskless?</p>", "options": [["A", "$50\\%$ A, $50\\%$ B"], ["B", "$75\\%$ A, $25\\%$ B"], ["C", "$25\\%$ A, $75\\%$ B"], ["D", "$10\\%$ A, $90\\%$ B"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Set $w_{A}\\sigma_{A} = w_{B}\\sigma_{B}$: $w_{A} \\times 30 = (1 - w_{A}) \\times 10$, so $w_{A} = 10 / 40 = \\mathbf{25\\%}$. Check: $0.25 \\times 30 = 7.5 = 0.75 \\times 10$.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Stock X has $\\beta = 1.4$ and Stock Y has $\\beta = 0.6$. What weight in X gives a two-stock portfolio with $\\beta = 1.0$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$25\\%$"], ["B", "$60\\%$"], ["C", "$50\\%$"], ["D", "$75\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$w(1.4) + (1 - w)(0.6) = 1.0 \\Rightarrow 0.8w = 0.4 \\Rightarrow \\mathbf{w = 0.5}$. Beta is linear in the weights.</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which sequence best constructs a portfolio for an investor, per the chapter's reconciliation of traditional and modern approaches?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy the ten highest-return stocks."], ["B", "Copy the most popular fund."], ["C", "Decide how much risk you can bear, diversify across securities and industries, check correlations, use portfolio beta to hold risk at an acceptable level, and evaluate alternatives for the best return at that risk."], ["D", "Hold only familiar domestic companies for comfort."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This reproduces the chapter's policy: traditional diversification and judgment plus MPT's statistical discipline (correlations and beta).</p>"}, {"module": 5, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which exam question best separates a student who memorised the SML from one who understands what it says?</p>", "options": [["A", "\"Two stocks each expect $11\\%$. Stock P has $\\beta = 0.5$ and Stock Q has $\\beta = 1.5$; with $R_{f} = 4\\%$ and $E(R_{m}) = 10\\%$, decide for each whether it is above, on or below the SML, then explain what could make your conclusion wrong.\""], ["B", "\"State the CAPM equation.\""], ["C", "\"What does SML stand for?\""], ["D", "\"Draw the SML from memory.\""]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>P requires $7\\%$ (alpha $+4\\%$, above) and Q requires $13\\%$ (alpha $-2\\%$, below). The \"what could be wrong\" part requires evaluating forecasts, beta estimates and the model's assumptions.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Under the chapter's dividend timeline, which statement describes the <strong>ex-dividend date</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "The date on which the company closes its books and checks its shareholder register to identify every holder entitled to receive the declared dividend."], ["B", "The date from which a buyer, under the market's settlement rules, generally no longer acquires the right to the declared dividend."], ["C", "The date on which the cash dividend is actually transferred to shareholders."], ["D", "The date on which the board announces the amount of the dividend and the other key dates."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A buyer on or after the ex-date generally does not receive the declared dividend; the seller keeps it. A describes the record date, C the payment date and D the declaration date. The ex-date comes before the record date because trades take time to settle.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Under the chapter's convention, which item is subtracted from total assets, together with liabilities, before dividing by the number of common shares to obtain <strong>book value per common share</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "The cost of shares repurchased into treasury during the year"], ["B", "The company's market capitalisation at the balance-sheet date"], ["C", "The par value of the common shares outstanding"], ["D", "Preferred stock"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Book value per common share $= \\dfrac{\\text{assets} - \\text{liabilities} - \\text{preferred stock}}{\\text{common shares}}$. Preferred stock is a claim senior to common equity. Market capitalisation (B) is a market value, not an accounting deduction, and par value (C) is part of common equity, not a claim against it.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Measured from a previous peak of a major index, the slides define a <strong>correction</strong> and a <strong>bear market</strong> as declines of at least:</p>", "options": [["A", "$10\\%$ and $20\\%$"], ["B", "$5\\%$ and $10\\%$"], ["C", "$20\\%$ and $30\\%$"], ["D", "$10\\%$ and $25\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides use three thresholds: a routine decline of $5\\%$ or more, a correction of $10\\%$ or more and a bear market of $20\\%$ or more. B pairs the routine-decline and correction thresholds; C and D are invented. These are descriptive conventions, not forecasts.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does a $2$-for-$1$ stock split <strong>not by itself</strong> make a shareholder wealthier?</p>", "options": [["A", "The company must pay for the extra shares out of retained cash, and that payment exactly offsets the value of the new shares received."], ["B", "The shareholder's ownership percentage halves while the share count doubles, so the two effects cancel."], ["C", "Shares double and the price roughly halves, so the holding's value and the ownership percentage are unchanged."], ["D", "After a split the price per share is legally reset to par value."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A split cuts the same \"cake\" into more pieces: $N \\times 2$ shares at about $\\dfrac{P}{2}$ each leaves $N \\times P$ unchanged. No cash moves (A is wrong), every holder's shares double so the percentage is unchanged (B is wrong), and par value has nothing to do with the trading price (D).</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>How can a share's indicated dividend yield <strong>rise</strong> at the same time as the company's prospects <strong>deteriorate</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "The yield divides the dividend by the price; bad news can cut the price while the indicated dividend stays the same."], ["B", "A falling EPS automatically pushes the yield up, because the yield divides the annual dividend by the earnings per share for the year."], ["C", "Companies in difficulty are required to raise dividends to keep shareholders."], ["D", "A deteriorating company retains more earnings, which mechanically increases the yield."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Yield $= \\dfrac{D}{P}$. A smaller denominator raises the ratio with nothing improving in the business. B describes the payout ratio, which divides by EPS. C is false, and retaining more earnings (D) means paying less, which would lower the yield.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A European company's depositary shares trade in <strong>ringgit</strong> on a local exchange. What exposure does a Malaysian holder still have?</p>", "options": [["A", "None to the euro, because every trade is settled in ringgit."], ["B", "Only Malaysian market risk, because the shares trade on a Malaysian exchange."], ["C", "Only the depositary bank's credit risk, because the bank holds the underlying shares and the ringgit quote already removes any currency effect."], ["D", "Exposure to the European business and to the euro: the ringgit price is the euro price translated at today's rate."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The trading currency is just the unit of the quote. If the euro weakens against the ringgit, the ringgit price falls even when the euro share price is unchanged. The notes stress that a depositary share quoted in the investor's currency does not remove the underlying foreign business or currency exposure, so A and B are wrong and C names only one minor additional risk.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Why do data vendors <strong>split-adjust</strong> historical share prices?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because splits create value that must be removed from past returns."], ["B", "Because split adjustment removes the effect of dividends from the price series."], ["C", "Because a split changes the company's market capitalisation, so earlier prices must be restated to the new, larger equity value of the company."], ["D", "Otherwise a $2$-for-$1$ split shows as a false $50\\%$ fall; past prices are divided by the split ratio."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A split changes the unit, not the value, so an unadjusted chart would show a false crash. Splits neither create value (A) nor change market capitalisation (C); dividend adjustment (B) is a separate correction.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You buy $300$ shares at $\\text{RM}15.00$, receive two semiannual dividends of $\\text{RM}0.30$ a share, and sell at $\\text{RM}14.10$. Ignoring costs and taxes, what is the holding-period return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$-6.0\\%$"], ["B", "$-2.0\\%$"], ["C", "$+2.0\\%$"], ["D", "$-4.0\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Total dividends $= 2 \\times 0.30 = \\text{RM}0.60$; price change $= 14.10 - 15.00 = -\\text{RM}0.90$. $HPR = \\dfrac{0.60 - 0.90}{15.00} = \\dfrac{-0.30}{15} = -2.0\\%$ (a ringgit loss of $300 \\times 0.30 = \\text{RM}90$). A ignores the dividends, D counts only one of them, and C reverses the sign.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A company has $4{,}000{,}000$ shares and you own $200{,}000$ ($5\\%$). It offers $1$ new share for every $5$ held at $\\text{RM}2.50$. Every other holder subscribes in full; you do not. What is your ownership afterwards?</p>", "options": [["A", "$4.17\\%$"], ["B", "$5.00\\%$"], ["C", "$4.20\\%$"], ["D", "$4.00\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The others hold $3{,}800{,}000$ shares and buy $\\dfrac{3{,}800{,}000}{5} = 760{,}000$ new ones, so $4{,}760{,}000$ shares are outstanding: $\\dfrac{200{,}000}{4{,}760{,}000} \\approx 4.20\\%$. A wrongly adds your unexercised entitlement to the denominator ($4{,}800{,}000$); B assumes you subscribed; D divides by $5{,}000{,}000$.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Earnings available to common shareholders are $\\text{RM}15$ million, the weighted-average share count is $5$ million, the <strong>quarterly</strong> dividend is $\\text{RM}0.24$ and the share price is $\\text{RM}32$. What are the dividend yield and the payout ratio?</p>", "options": [["A", "$3.0\\%$ and $32\\%$"], ["B", "$0.75\\%$ and $8\\%$"], ["C", "$32\\%$ and $3.0\\%$"], ["D", "$9.4\\%$ and $32\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Annual dividend $= 4 \\times 0.24 = \\text{RM}0.96$; $EPS = \\dfrac{15}{5} = \\text{RM}3.00$. Yield $= \\dfrac{0.96}{32} = 3.0\\%$; payout $= \\dfrac{0.96}{3.00} = 32\\%$. B uses the quarterly figure, C swaps the two ratios, and D's $9.4\\%$ is $\\dfrac{EPS}{P} = \\dfrac{3}{32}$, an earnings yield, not a dividend yield.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A company has $10$ million shares at $\\text{RM}6.00$ and announces a $1$-for-$4$ rights issue at $\\text{RM}4.00$. Practitioners compute the <strong>theoretical ex-rights price (TERP)</strong>, the value per share once all rights are exercised. What is it?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}5.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}6.00$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}5.60$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}4.80$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{TERP} = \\dfrac{10\\text{m} \\times 6.00 + 2.5\\text{m} \\times 4.00}{12.5\\text{m}} = \\dfrac{60\\text{m} + 10\\text{m}}{12.5\\text{m}} = \\text{RM}5.60$. A is the simple average of the two prices, ignoring that there are four old shares per new one; B ignores the cheaper new shares. A holder who neither subscribes nor sells the rights loses $\\text{RM}0.40$ a share: the chapter's point that the economic effect depends on the price and on whether rights can be traded.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A Malaysian investor buys $200$ shares at $\\text{EUR}40$ when $\\text{EUR}1 = \\text{RM}5.00$. At year-end each share pays $\\text{EUR}1.20$ and is sold at $\\text{EUR}38$; the dividend and proceeds are converted at $\\text{RM}5.30$. What is the ringgit return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$+3.88\\%$"], ["B", "$+4.00\\%$"], ["C", "$-2.00\\%$"], ["D", "$+6.00\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Cost $= 200 \\times 40 \\times 5.00 = \\text{RM}40{,}000$. Ending $= 200 \\times (38 + 1.20) \\times 5.30 = \\text{RM}41{,}552$, so $R = \\dfrac{41{,}552}{40{,}000} - 1 = 3.88\\%$. Check: local $\\dfrac{38 + 1.20 - 40}{40} = -2\\%$, currency $\\dfrac{5.30}{5.00} - 1 = +6\\%$, and $0.98 \\times 1.06 = 1.0388$. B adds the returns, C is the local return only, D the currency return only.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Alpha trades at $\\text{RM}8$ with $50$ million shares outstanding; Beta trades at $\\text{RM}40$ with $8$ million. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Beta is the larger company in the stock market because its share price is five times Alpha's."], ["B", "The two market capitalisations are equal, because Beta's five-times-higher price is exactly offset by Alpha's larger number of shares outstanding."], ["C", "Alpha's market capitalisation exceeds Beta's by $\\text{RM}320$ million."], ["D", "Alpha's equity is worth $\\text{RM}80$ million more than Beta's, despite Beta's higher share price."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Alpha: $8 \\times 50\\text{m} = \\text{RM}400$m. Beta: $40 \\times 8\\text{m} = \\text{RM}320$m. Difference $\\text{RM}80$m. The price ratio is $5$ but the share-count ratio is $6.25$, so B is false; C confuses Beta's whole cap with the difference; A judges size by price per share.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You hold $1{,}000$ shares at $\\text{RM}50$. The company pays a $10\\%$ <strong>stock</strong> dividend and, immediately afterwards, the shares trade at $\\text{RM}46.00$ with no other news. What is the best interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "You lost $\\text{RM}4$ a share, so the stock dividend destroyed $\\text{RM}4{,}000$ of value."], ["B", "Your holding is worth $\\text{RM}50{,}600$; the stock dividend alone would have left it at $\\text{RM}50{,}000$."], ["C", "Your wealth rose by $10\\%$, because the stock dividend gave you $10\\%$ more shares and each share still represents the same claim on the company."], ["D", "Your holding is still $1{,}000$ shares, now worth $\\text{RM}46{,}000$."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>You now own $1{,}100$ shares: $1{,}100 \\times 46 = \\text{RM}50{,}600$ versus $1{,}000 \\times 50 = \\text{RM}50{,}000$. With unchanged company value the price would adjust to $\\dfrac{50}{1.10} \\approx \\text{RM}45.45$, which leaves the holding at $\\text{RM}50{,}000$. A ignores the new shares, C treats more shares as more wealth, and D forgets the shares were issued.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Year 1: dividend $\\text{RM}1.00$, EPS $\\text{RM}4.00$, price $\\text{RM}25$. Year 2: dividend $\\text{RM}1.00$, EPS $\\text{RM}2.00$, price $\\text{RM}16$. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The yield rose but the payout ratio is unchanged, because the payout depends only on the dividend, which stayed at $\\text{RM}1.00$ in both years."], ["B", "The yield fell and the payout ratio rose."], ["C", "The yield rose to $6.25\\%$ and the payout to $50\\%$, both because their denominators fell."], ["D", "Neither ratio changed, because both use the same unchanged dividend."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Yield: $\\dfrac{1}{25} = 4\\%$ to $\\dfrac{1}{16} = 6.25\\%$. Payout: $\\dfrac{1}{4} = 25\\%$ to $\\dfrac{1}{2} = 50\\%$. An unchanged numerator does not mean unchanged ratios (A, D). Both denominators fell, which is why a rising yield and a rising payout together can be a warning sign.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>Net income is $\\text{RM}24$m and preferred dividends are $\\text{RM}4$m. The firm starts the year with $8$m common shares and issues $2$m more on 1 July. What is basic EPS?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}2.40$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}2.22$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}2.67$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}2.00$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Earnings available to common $= 24 - 4 = \\text{RM}20$m. Weighted-average shares $= 8 + 2 \\times \\dfrac{6}{12} = 9$m. $EPS = \\dfrac{20}{9} \\approx \\text{RM}2.22$. A ignores the preferred dividends and uses year-end shares; C ignores the preferred dividends; D uses the year-end count of $10$m instead of the weighted average.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A firm with $40$m shares at $\\text{RM}5$ (market cap $\\text{RM}200$m) has $\\text{RM}20$m of surplus cash. It can either buy back shares at the market price or pay a special dividend of $\\text{RM}0.50$ a share. Ignoring taxes, costs and signalling, compare the outcomes for an investor holding $4{,}000$ shares.</p>", "options": [["A", "The buyback leaves the investor with about $\\text{RM}22{,}222$, because their ownership of the company rises by about $11\\%$ while the share price stays at $\\text{RM}5$."], ["B", "The dividend leaves the investor with $\\text{RM}22{,}000$: $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ of shares plus $\\text{RM}2{,}000$ of cash."], ["C", "Both leave wealth at $\\text{RM}20{,}000$: shares at $\\text{RM}5$ after the buyback, or shares at $\\text{RM}4.50$ plus $\\text{RM}2{,}000$ cash."], ["D", "The buyback is worse, because fewer shares outstanding means the company is worth less per share."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Buyback: $\\dfrac{20\\text{m}}{5} = 4$m shares retired, leaving $\\text{RM}180$m over $36$m shares $= \\text{RM}5$; holding $4{,}000 \\times 5 = \\text{RM}20{,}000$. Dividend: $4{,}000 \\times 0.50 = \\text{RM}2{,}000$ cash, and $\\dfrac{180\\text{m}}{40\\text{m}} = \\text{RM}4.50$ per share, so $18{,}000 + 2{,}000 = \\text{RM}20{,}000$. A treats a larger percentage as more wealth; B forgets the ex-dividend price fall; D ignores that the cash left too.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Empirical studies often find that a share price falls on the ex-dividend date by <strong>less</strong> than the dividend. Which explanation is most consistent with the chapter's caution that the drop is only \"approximately\" the dividend?</p>", "options": [["A", "Taxes and trading costs can make the dividend worth less than its face value, and news also moves the price."], ["B", "The price actually falls on the payment date, not on the ex-dividend date."], ["C", "The company replaces the distributed cash on the same day, so its value does not fall."], ["D", "A cash dividend is paid out of earnings rather than out of the company's assets, so in principle no adjustment to the share price is required at all."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>If dividends are taxed more heavily than capital gains, investors value one ringgit of dividend at less than one ringgit of price, so the equilibrium drop can be smaller. B gets the timing wrong (entitlement ends at the ex-date), and C and D deny that cash leaves the company.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Theory says that, with tradable rights and no frictions, the subscription price of a rights issue does not affect shareholder wealth. Use X1's issue ($\\text{RM}6.00$ share, $1$-for-$4$ at $\\text{RM}4.00$, TERP $\\text{RM}5.60$). A holder of $400$ shares <strong>sells</strong> all $400$ rights at fair value instead of subscribing. What is their position?</p>", "options": [["A", "Wealth falls to $\\text{RM}2{,}240$, a loss of $\\text{RM}160$."], ["B", "Still $\\text{RM}2{,}400$ ($\\text{RM}2{,}240$ of shares plus $\\text{RM}160$ cash), with diluted ownership."], ["C", "Wealth stays at $\\text{RM}2{,}400$ and ownership is unchanged."], ["D", "Wealth rises to $\\text{RM}2{,}560$, because the proceeds from selling the rights are an extra payment on top of the original value of the holding."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Each right is worth $\\dfrac{5.60 - 4.00}{4} = \\text{RM}0.40$, so $400$ rights fetch $\\text{RM}160$; the shares are worth $400 \\times 5.60 = \\text{RM}2{,}240$; total $\\text{RM}2{,}400 = 400 \\times 6.00$. Wealth is preserved but the holder now owns a smaller fraction (C is wrong). A is the outcome if the rights lapse unsold; D double-counts.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A company whose share price fairly reflects its value announces it will buy back $10\\%$ of its shares at a price $30\\%$ <strong>above</strong> the market price. From the viewpoint of a shareholder who does not sell, which assessment is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Their percentage rises, but overpaying transfers value to the sellers, so value per remaining share likely falls."], ["B", "Their wealth must rise, because they now own a larger percentage of the company."], ["C", "Nothing changes for them, because a buyback only exchanges cash for shares and never affects remaining holders."], ["D", "Value per share must rise, since the same total earnings are now divided among fewer shares, so EPS increases and the price follows it up."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Illustration: $100$ shares at $\\text{RM}10$ ($\\text{RM}1{,}000$); buying $10$ at $\\text{RM}13$ costs $\\text{RM}130$, leaving $\\dfrac{870}{90} \\approx \\text{RM}9.67$ a share. The notes stress that a larger percentage of a smaller company is not automatically more wealth (B) and that effects depend on the price paid (C). D's EPS argument ignores the cash that left the company.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A retiree is drawn to a share yielding $9\\%$ after its price fell $40\\%$. Its payout ratio is $140\\%$ and its debt is rising. Which conclusion is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy it: the highest yield available is the safest source of retirement income."], ["B", "Since the payout ratio is above $100\\%$, the company is certain to go bankrupt within the year, so the retiree should sell rather than buy."], ["C", "The dividend looks unsustainable: payout exceeds earnings and the yield is inflated by the price fall, so investigate further and diversify."], ["D", "Ignore the payout ratio, because only the dividend yield matters to an income investor."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The yield rose largely because the denominator fell. Paying $140\\%$ of earnings needs cash from reserves or borrowing, and debt is rising: a cut is plausible. The notes warn that a ratio above $100\\%$ warrants examination, not an automatic verdict of failure (so B overreaches), and that yield alone is not a measure of safety (A, D).</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor argues: \"This share has a par value of $\\text{RM}0.10$ and trades at $\\text{RM}5$, so it is $50$ times overvalued.\" Evaluate the argument.</p>", "options": [["A", "Valid: par value is the share's true worth and the market has inflated the price."], ["B", "Invalid: par is a stated nominal amount, unrelated to fair value; compare the price with an estimate of investment value."], ["C", "Valid, but only if the book value per share is also $\\text{RM}0.10$."], ["D", "Invalid, because in an efficient market the price always equals the share's investment value, so no share can ever be described as overvalued."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides call par value mainly an accounting term, not very useful to investors. Whether $\\text{RM}5$ is too high depends on an estimate of investment value from expected future benefits and risk. C still compares with the wrong benchmark (book value records accounting amounts), and D wrongly assumes market price and estimated value must coincide.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A dual-class IPO gives founders $10$ votes per Class B share. Founders hold $20$m B shares; the public buys $80$m one-vote Class A shares. Which assessment would a careful investor make?</p>", "options": [["A", "The founders control $20\\%$ of the votes, in line with their $20\\%$ economic stake."], ["B", "Votes must legally match economic ownership in a listed company, so the ten-vote feature changes nothing about who controls the board."], ["C", "The founders control exactly half of the votes, so neither side can outvote the other."], ["D", "Founders hold about $71.4\\%$ of the votes with $20\\%$ of the shares; the public carries most of the risk with little control."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Founder votes $= 20\\text{m} \\times 10 = 200$m; total $= 200\\text{m} + 80\\text{m} = 280$m; $\\dfrac{200}{280} \\approx 71.4\\%$. The chapter's point: ownership percentage alone does not reveal voting control when classes differ, so read the class terms (A, B, C are wrong).</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>An analyst says: \"Buybacks always raise EPS, and higher EPS always raises the share price, so buybacks always create value.\" Evaluate the claim.</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct: a higher EPS always raises the price by the same percentage."], ["B", "Wrong, because buybacks always reduce EPS by shrinking the company."], ["C", "Flawed: EPS may rise mechanically, but the cash spent cuts future earnings, and value rises only if the price paid is below value."], ["D", "Wrong, because repurchased shares held in treasury still count in the EPS denominator, so EPS is unaffected and the price cannot change."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Fewer shares can lift EPS, yet lost interest income or new interest expense reduces earnings, and greater leverage can lower the multiple investors pay. The notes stress that buyback effects depend on the price paid, financing and opportunity cost. Treasury shares are excluded from shares outstanding (D), and B's \"always\" is as wrong as A's.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Figure 6.7 reports average annual stock returns from 1900 to 2011 for markets that existed over the whole period. Why should a researcher hesitate to treat those averages as forecasts?</p>", "options": [["A", "More than a century of data from many countries is long enough for each country's average return to be treated as a reliable forecast of its future return."], ["B", "Countries with higher historical averages will keep outperforming because returns persist."], ["C", "Arithmetic and compound averages are identical over long periods, so the method is irrelevant."], ["D", "The sample includes only surviving markets, and long-run averages hide large differences between periods."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Markets that closed or were expropriated drop out of such samples, flattering the averages, and Table 6.1 shows decade returns from about $19\\%$ to about $-1\\%$. The notes insist history is period- and method-specific evidence, not a promise. Arithmetic means exceed compound means when returns vary (C), and persistence (B) is an assumption, not a fact.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>A researcher finds that in an emerging market, local share prices and the local currency tend to fall <strong>together</strong> during crises. What does this imply for a ringgit-based investor?</p>", "options": [["A", "Ringgit risk exceeds local risk: correlated stock and currency losses compound in crises."], ["B", "The currency moves offset the stock moves, so ringgit-measured risk is lower than local risk and the investor gains a natural hedge during crises."], ["C", "Currency risk becomes irrelevant over long horizons, so the finding does not matter."], ["D", "Because hedging is costly, the currency exposure can be ignored when assessing risk."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With $1 + R_{\\text{RM}} = (1 + R_{\\text{local}})(1 + R_{\\text{currency}})$, losses on both terms multiply: $0.80 \\times 0.85 = 0.68$, a $32\\%$ ringgit loss from a $20\\%$ local fall. Positive correlation raises variance (B describes negative correlation). The notes also warn that diversification benefits can weaken in stress; C and D dismiss a real exposure.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You hold a high-yield share for retirement income. Which monitoring plan best reflects the chapter?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sell automatically whenever the dividend yield falls below last year's level."], ["B", "Watch only the dividend yield, and buy more shares whenever it rises, because a higher yield means more income for every ringgit invested at today's price."], ["C", "No monitoring is needed, because the company is a well-known blue chip."], ["D", "Set review triggers: payout beyond earnings and cash flow, rising debt, a dividend cut, or an oversized position; judge total return."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A good plan defines in advance what evidence would cause a review, covering sustainability (payout, cash, debt), concentration and total return. B is the yield trap: a rising yield often means a falling price. A reacts to the wrong signal, and C treats a label as a guarantee.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You want a numerical example showing that a <strong>local gain can become a ringgit loss</strong>. Which pair of returns does it?</p>", "options": [["A", "Local return $+8\\%$, foreign currency $-10\\%$ against the ringgit"], ["B", "Local return $+8\\%$, foreign currency $-5\\%$ against the ringgit"], ["C", "Local return $-8\\%$, foreign currency $+10\\%$ against the ringgit"], ["D", "Local return $+8\\%$, foreign currency $+10\\%$ against the ringgit"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$1.08 \\times 0.90 = 0.972$, a ringgit return of $-2.8\\%$ despite the $+8\\%$ local gain. B gives $1.08 \\times 0.95 = 1.026$, still a gain of $2.6\\%$. C shows the reverse case (a local loss turned into a $1.2\\%$ gain), and D gives $+18.8\\%$.</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Investor A needs dependable cash for living expenses in six months and cannot tolerate significant losses. Which plan best fits the chapter's reasoning?</p>", "options": [["A", "Put everything in one high-yield blue-chip share, since its dividend will cover the expenses."], ["B", "Hold the six-month need in liquid, low-risk assets; use shares only for longer-horizon money, after checking dividend sustainability."], ["C", "Put everything in a diversified basket of growth stocks, because stocks have historically earned more than other assets and diversification removes the risk."], ["D", "Buy a speculative stock and protect it with a stop-loss order, which guarantees the downside."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes say even a high-quality dividend share can fall or cut its dividend at the wrong time, so equities alone may not meet A's liquidity and capital-preservation needs (A). Long-run averages do not help a six-month horizon (C), and a stop-loss is a trigger, not a guaranteed price (D).</p>"}, {"module": 6, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which research design would best test the claim that \"high-dividend-yield stocks are safer\"?</p>", "options": [["A", "Rank all listed stocks by their current dividend yield and report the average yield of the top tenth, since a higher yield means more dependable income."], ["B", "Survey investors on whether they feel safer holding high-yield shares."], ["C", "Sort stocks by yield and compare later total returns, cuts and drawdowns, separating price-driven from dividend-driven yields, with controls."], ["D", "Pick last year's highest-yield stock and check whether it paid its dividend."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>\"Safer\" must be measured by outcomes (total return, drawdowns, cuts), not by the yield itself (A). Separating the denominator effect from genuine dividend growth isolates the chapter's yield trap, and the controls address confounders. B measures perception, and D is a single anecdote.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>On the course slides, which pair of ratios uses annual <strong>sales</strong> as the numerator?</p>", "options": [["A", "The current ratio and the net working capital figure"], ["B", "Receivables turnover and inventory turnover"], ["C", "Return on assets and return on equity"], ["D", "The P/E ratio and the price-to-book ratio"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Slides 7-34 and 7-35 define receivables turnover as annual sales over accounts receivable and inventory turnover as annual sales over inventory (total asset turnover also uses sales). The common alternatives, credit sales over average receivables and cost of goods sold over average inventory, must be stated if used. ROA and ROE use net profit after taxes, and P/E and P/B use the share price.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which development is on slide 7-54's list of signs that a company could be heading for financial problems?</p>", "options": [["A", "A payout ratio that keeps rising while EPS stays stable for several years"], ["B", "A P/E ratio below the industry average"], ["C", "A rising total asset turnover"], ["D", "Cash flow from operations dropping below net income"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slide lists receivables and inventories growing faster than sales, a falling current ratio, a high and rising debt-equity ratio, operating cash flow below net income, and indecipherable off-balance-sheet items. A low P/E (B) is a valuation signal, not a listed warning sign, and a rising asset turnover (C) usually means better asset efficiency.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In which order does the chapter's industry growth cycle run?</p>", "options": [["A", "Initial development, rapid expansion, mature growth, stability or decline"], ["B", "Rapid expansion, initial development, stability, mature growth"], ["C", "Mature growth, rapid expansion, initial development, decline"], ["D", "Initial development, mature growth, rapid expansion, then stability or decline"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Slide 7-19: a new, high-risk industry (initial development) gains acceptance (rapid expansion), then grows roughly with the economy with more predictable returns (mature growth), and finally faces flat or shrinking demand (stability or decline). It is a pattern, not a timetable every industry must follow.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Apex's current ratio fell from $2.00$ to $1.56$ while its net working capital rose from $\\text{RM}40$ million to $\\text{RM}45$ million. How can both be true?</p>", "options": [["A", "Working capital is measured in \"times\", so it cannot be compared with the current ratio at all."], ["B", "Working capital excludes inventory, while the current ratio includes it."], ["C", "Current liabilities grew proportionally faster than current assets."], ["D", "It cannot be true; one of the figures must contain an error."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current assets went from $80$ to $125$ ($+56\\%$) and current liabilities from $40$ to $80$ ($+100\\%$). The difference grew ($125 - 80 = 45$), but the ratio fell ($\\dfrac{125}{80} = 1.56$). A ratio and a difference answer different questions. Working capital is in ringgit (A is backwards), and both measures include inventory (B).</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why does the chapter warn that a high ROE does not, on its own, prove that operations have improved?</p>", "options": [["A", "A bigger equity multiplier can lift ROE while margin and turnover stay flat."], ["B", "Because ROE is calculated before tax, so it overstates the profit that is actually available to shareholders."], ["C", "Because ROE uses the market value of equity, which moves with the share price rather than with operations."], ["D", "Because ROE always equals ROA, so it adds no information about operations."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The DuPont identity separates operations (margin x turnover = ROA) from financing (the equity multiplier). In the notes, two firms with the same $10\\%$ ROA show ROEs of $12.5\\%$ and $25\\%$ purely because of leverage. The course ROE uses net profit after taxes (B) and book equity (C), and ROE equals ROA only for an all-equity firm (D).</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Fuel prices jump. An airline's shares fall while an oil producer's rise. Which principle of the top-down approach does this illustrate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Economic changes affect all stocks roughly equally, so the gap between the two shares must be random noise."], ["B", "Fuel prices are a company-specific matter, so economic analysis should leave them to the company analyst."], ["C", "Once the economy has been analysed well, industry and company analysis add little to the forecast."], ["D", "The same change hits industries through different mechanisms, so link it to the industry and firm."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes stress that a macroeconomic observation becomes useful only once it is tied to a mechanism. Even two airlines can be affected differently through fuel hedging, routes and debt. A, B and C each drop a level of the economy → industry → company chain.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Current assets are $\\text{RM}5.6$ million, of which inventory is $\\text{RM}2.1$ million; current liabilities are $\\text{RM}3.5$ million. What are the current ratio and net working capital?</p>", "options": [["A", "$1.0$ times and $\\text{RM}0$"], ["B", "$1.6$ times and $\\text{RM}2.1$ million"], ["C", "$0.625$ times and $\\text{RM}2.1$ million"], ["D", "$1.6$ times and $\\text{RM}3.5$ million"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current ratio $= \\dfrac{5.6}{3.5} = 1.6$ times; net working capital $= 5.6 - 3.5 = \\text{RM}2.1$ million. A is the quick ratio, $\\dfrac{5.6 - 2.1}{3.5} = 1.0$, which removes inventory; C inverts the ratio ($\\dfrac{3.5}{5.6}$); D repeats current liabilities instead of subtracting them. That working capital happens to equal inventory here is a coincidence.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>All of a firm's $\\text{RM}14.6$ million of annual sales are on credit. Receivables were $\\text{RM}1.6$ million at the start of the year and $\\text{RM}2.4$ million at the end. Using credit sales over <strong>average</strong> receivables and a $365$-day year, what are receivables turnover and the average collection period?</p>", "options": [["A", "$6.08$ times and $60$ days"], ["B", "$9.13$ times and $40$ days"], ["C", "$7.3$ times and $50$ days"], ["D", "$0.14$ times and $2{,}664$ days"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Average receivables $= \\dfrac{1.6 + 2.4}{2} = 2.0$; turnover $= \\dfrac{14.6}{2.0} = 7.3$ times; collection period $\\approx \\dfrac{365}{7.3} = 50$ days. A uses the ending balance (the slide convention, not the one asked for), B the beginning balance, and D inverts the ratio.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Net income is $\\text{RM}42$ million, preferred dividends are $\\text{RM}6$ million, there are $15$ million weighted-average common shares, and the share price is $\\text{RM}31.20$. What are EPS and the P/E ratio?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}2.40$ and $13.0$ times"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}2.80$ and $11.1$ times"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}2.40$ and $0.077$ times"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}3.20$ and $9.75$ times"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$EPS = \\dfrac{42 - 6}{15} = \\text{RM}2.40$ and $P/E = \\dfrac{31.20}{2.40} = 13$ times. B forgets to deduct preferred dividends ($\\dfrac{42}{15} = 2.80$), C divides EPS by price (the earnings yield), and D adds the preferred dividends instead of subtracting them.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A credit analyst works quarterly. Credit sales for a $90$-day quarter were $\\text{RM}9.0$ million and ending receivables are $\\text{RM}4.5$ million. What is days sales outstanding (DSO) for the quarter?</p>", "options": [["A", "$20$ days"], ["B", "$182.5$ days"], ["C", "$45$ days"], ["D", "$2$ days"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$DSO = \\dfrac{\\text{receivables}}{\\text{credit sales}} \\times \\text{days in the period} = \\dfrac{4.5}{9.0} \\times 90 = 45$ days, equivalently $\\dfrac{90}{\\text{quarterly turnover of } 2}$. B mixes a quarter's sales with a $365$-day year; D is the turnover itself, not days.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>The five-step DuPont split is $ROE = \\dfrac{NI}{EBT} \\times \\dfrac{EBT}{EBIT} \\times \\dfrac{EBIT}{\\text{sales}} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{sales}}{\\text{assets}} \\times \\dfrac{\\text{assets}}{\\text{equity}}$. A firm has tax burden $0.75$, interest burden $0.80$, EBIT margin $12\\%$, asset turnover $1.25$ and equity multiplier $2.0$. What is its ROE?</p>", "options": [["A", "$18.0\\%$"], ["B", "$9.0\\%$"], ["C", "$7.2\\%$"], ["D", "$24.0\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$0.75 \\times 0.80 \\times 0.12 \\times 1.25 \\times 2.0 = 0.18$. The first three terms multiply to the net profit margin of the three-step version ($7.2\\%$, option C), so the three-step and five-step versions agree. B is ROA (no equity multiplier) and D leaves out the tax burden.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Year 1: net margin $8\\%$, total asset turnover $1.5$, equity multiplier $2.0$. Year 2: net margin $6\\%$, total asset turnover $1.6$, equity multiplier $3.0$. What best describes the change in ROE?</p>", "options": [["A", "ROE rose to $28.8\\%$ because the improvement in profit margins carried through to shareholders."], ["B", "ROE fell from $24\\%$ to $9.6\\%$, a sign of operating deterioration."], ["C", "ROE rose mainly because the gain in asset turnover outweighed the lower margin and the extra leverage."], ["D", "ROA fell to $9.6\\%$; ROE rose to $28.8\\%$ only because leverage rose."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Year 1: $ROA = 8\\% \\times 1.5 = 12\\%$ and $ROE = 12\\% \\times 2 = 24\\%$. Year 2: $ROA = 6\\% \\times 1.6 = 9.6\\%$ and $ROE = 9.6\\% \\times 3 = 28.8\\%$. The margin fell (A is wrong), $9.6\\%$ is the new ROA, not ROE (B), and the small rise in turnover could not offset the lower margin (C).</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Sales rose from $\\text{RM}50$ million to $\\text{RM}55$ million and net income from $\\text{RM}3.0$ million to $\\text{RM}3.3$ million. Receivables went from $\\text{RM}5$ million to $\\text{RM}8$ million; inventory from $\\text{RM}6$ million to $\\text{RM}6.6$ million. Operating cash flow fell from $\\text{RM}4$ million to $\\text{RM}1.5$ million. Which inference is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Inventory is the main problem, because stock has grown faster than sales and is absorbing the cash."], ["B", "Slower collection: slide receivables turnover fell from $10$ to about $6.9$; investigate credit terms."], ["C", "Fraud is proven, because operating cash flow fell while reported profit rose over the same year."], ["D", "The net profit margin collapsed this year, which is what explains the fall in operating cash flow."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Receivables grew $60\\%$ against sales growth of $10\\%$ ($\\dfrac{50}{5} = 10$ to $\\dfrac{55}{8} = 6.875$ times), while inventory grew exactly in line with sales ($10\\%$), so A is wrong. The margin stayed at $\\dfrac{3.0}{50} = \\dfrac{3.3}{55} = 6\\%$ (D). A warning sign is not proof of fraud (C).</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A firm's times interest earned is $6$ times, but its operating cash flow is negative and a $\\text{RM}40$ million bond matures next year. What is the best analysis?</p>", "options": [["A", "Coverage of $6$ times proves that the firm can meet all of its debt obligations, including the maturing bond."], ["B", "Times interest earned already includes scheduled principal repayments, so the maturing bond is covered too."], ["C", "It is an earnings cover for interest only; principal and cash lie outside it, so refinancing risk remains."], ["D", "Negative operating cash flow means times interest earned must also be negative."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\dfrac{EBIT}{\\text{interest}}$ is an earnings cushion for interest only. EBIT is not cash, and principal repayments are not in the ratio (the notes say so explicitly). EBIT can be positive while operating cash flow is negative (D), for example when working capital absorbs cash.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A distributor collects receivables in $50$ days, holds inventory for $70$ days and pays suppliers in $40$ days. Its main supplier cuts the credit period to $25$ days. What happens to the cash conversion cycle (receivable days + inventory days - payable days)?</p>", "options": [["A", "It falls to $65$ days, because the distributor now pays its suppliers sooner."], ["B", "It stays at $80$ days, because sales are unchanged."], ["C", "It rises to $160$ days."], ["D", "It rises from $80$ to $95$ days, tying up more working capital."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Before: $50 + 70 - 40 = 80$ days; after: $50 + 70 - 25 = 95$ days. Supplier credit funds part of the operating cycle; losing $15$ days of it means cash is tied up longer, which the firm must fund from cash, borrowing or slower growth. This is the payables line in the notes' profit-versus-cash reconciliation.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A company earns net income of $30$. Its equity was $100$ at the start of the year and $200$ at the end, because it issued new shares on the last day of the year. Which ROE best describes the year's performance?</p>", "options": [["A", "ROE on ending equity, $15\\%$, because the course slides always divide by year-end balances."], ["B", "ROE on average equity, $20\\%$, which better matches the capital used during the year."], ["C", "Both conventions give the same answer, $15\\%$."], ["D", "ROE on beginning equity, $30\\%$, because that is always the correct convention for any firm."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\dfrac{30}{(100 + 200)/2} = 20\\%$ against $\\dfrac{30}{200} = 15\\%$. Profit was earned on roughly $100$ of equity for most of the year, so the ending balance badly mismatches flow and stock. The notes say averages often approximate the resources used better, but a question's stated convention still decides the exam answer.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>Three firms in a cyclical industry: L has ROA $7.5\\%$ and equity multiplier $4$; M has ROA $16\\%$ and multiplier $1.5$; N has ROA $10\\%$ and multiplier $2.5$. A downturn cuts every firm's ROA by $5$ percentage points, and multipliers stay the same. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "M suffers most, because its high ROA has the furthest to fall: its ROE drops to $16.5\\%$."], ["B", "N suffers most, because it sits in the middle on both drivers: its ROE drops to $12.5\\%$."], ["C", "L suffers most: its ROE falls by $20$ points, from $30\\%$ to $10\\%$."], ["D", "All three ROEs fall by the same $5$ points."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$ROE = ROA \\times EM$. L: $30\\% \\to 2.5\\% \\times 4 = 10\\%$ ($-20$ points). M: $24\\% \\to 11\\% \\times 1.5 = 16.5\\%$ ($-7.5$). N: $25\\% \\to 5\\% \\times 2.5 = 12.5\\%$ ($-12.5$). The firm with the highest ROE before the downturn was also the most fragile: leverage magnifies falls as well as rises.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>A common earnings-quality measure is the accrual ratio, $\\dfrac{NI - CFO}{\\text{average total assets}}$. For the chapter's integrated case (net income $1.2$, operating cash flow $0.4$, total assets $5.5$ and $8.0$, in $\\text{RM}$ million), what is it, and what does it suggest?</p>", "options": [["A", "About $11.9\\%$: much of the profit is accruals, mostly working-capital build-up."], ["B", "$10.0\\%$, using ending total assets, which indicates unusually high earnings quality for the year."], ["C", "About $-11.9\\%$, meaning cash flow exceeded earnings."], ["D", "$66.7\\%$, using net income as the denominator, which proves that the earnings were manipulated."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\dfrac{1.2 - 0.4}{(5.5 + 8.0)/2} = \\dfrac{0.8}{6.75} \\approx 11.9\\%$. High accruals are a flag to investigate, consistent with slide 7-54's \"operating cash below net income\", not proof of manipulation (D, which also divides by net income). The sign is positive because profit exceeds cash (C).</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>A firm reports a net loss of $\\text{RM}5$ million and stockholders' equity of $-\\text{RM}20$ million. A screen computes its ROE as $25\\%$ and ranks it among the most profitable firms. What is the problem?</p>", "options": [["A", "None: a $25\\%$ ROE is strong, and the screen is right to rank the firm among the most profitable."], ["B", "The ROE should simply be reported as $-25\\%$, since the firm made a loss during the year."], ["C", "With negative equity the ratio is meaningless; exclude the firm or use other measures."], ["D", "Negative equity makes the firm safer, which the high ROE correctly reflects in the ranking."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes warn that negative or very small equity makes debt-equity misleading; the same applies to ROE and P/B. Flipping the sign (B) is arbitrary, because the ratio has no economic meaning when the base is negative. Negative equity means liabilities exceed assets: more risk, not less (D).</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An analyst writes: \"The stock's P/E is $8$ against an industry average of $16$, so it is undervalued by half. Buy.\" How should this be evaluated?</p>", "options": [["A", "Unsupported: a low P/E may reflect weak growth, high risk or inflated earnings; value must be estimated."], ["B", "Correct, because P/E ratios always revert towards the industry average, so the share price must roughly double."], ["C", "Correct, provided the company's EPS is positive."], ["D", "Wrong, because a higher P/E always means a cheaper stock relative to its earnings and growth."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes: \"a low P/E can reflect undervaluation, weak prospects or temporary earnings\". The P/E compares price with one period's earnings; it does not establish intrinsic value. Nothing guarantees reversion (B), positive EPS only makes the ratio meaningful (C), and a higher P/E means a higher price per ringgit of earnings (D).</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>After cutting stock levels, a retailer's inventory turnover rose from $5$ to $9$ times. Over the same year sales fell $12\\%$ and back-orders doubled. Management calls the higher turnover \"an unambiguous improvement\". Evaluate the claim.</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because the slides state that a higher inventory turnover ratio is better and a lower one is worse."], ["B", "Irrelevant: inventory turnover only measures how fast customers pay."], ["C", "Overstated: turnover rose partly because stock was too thin, at the cost of lost sales."], ["D", "Impossible: turnover cannot rise when sales fall."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes warn that very fast turnover can mean insufficient stock. Here the lower denominator outweighed the lower sales, so turnover rose (D is wrong), but service levels and sales suffered. \"Higher is better\" on the slide is a rule of thumb, not a verdict (A). B describes receivables turnover.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A data screen shows Firm P's debt-equity ratio as $0.4$ (calculated from long-term debt) and Firm Q's as $1.1$ (calculated from total liabilities). A student concludes that \"Q is nearly three times as leveraged as P\". Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Valid, because both ratios are divided by stockholders' equity and are therefore directly comparable."], ["B", "Not valid: the numerators differ, so recalculate both on one debt definition first."], ["C", "Valid, as long as both firms have positive equity."], ["D", "Not valid, because a debt-equity ratio can never exceed $1$."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes: \"identify the numerator before comparing\". Total liabilities include payables and other obligations that long-term debt excludes, so Q's ratio is mechanically larger. Positive equity (C) is necessary but not sufficient, and debt-equity can exceed $1$ (D), as in the practice paper's $\\dfrac{160}{80} = 2.0$.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A pitch says: \"The target trades at a P/E of $30$ with $25\\%$ consensus EPS growth, so its PEG is $1.2$: fairly priced.\" The consensus forecast covers only the next two years, after which growth is expected to settle near $8\\%$. Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Agree: a PEG close to $1$ means the share price is in line with expected growth, as the slide's rule of thumb says."], ["B", "Disagree: the PEG is really $\\dfrac{30}{0.25} = 120$, which shows that the stock is grossly overvalued."], ["C", "The P/E is irrelevant once growth is known."], ["D", "Disagree: two years of $25\\%$ then $8\\%$ is not a 3- to 5-year rate; on $8\\%$ the PEG is $3.75$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PEG is only as good as its growth input and horizon. B uses the decimal convention, which the notes warn against mixing with the percentage-point one. The slide's thresholds around $1$ are rules of thumb, not proof of fair value (A).</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>A firm with equity of $100$ and net income of $15$ (ROE $15\\%$) borrows $40$ at $5\\%$ and uses it to buy back shares. With tax at $25\\%$, net income falls by $40 \\times 5\\% \\times 0.75 = 1.5$ to $13.5$, and equity falls to $60$. ROE becomes $22.5\\%$. Evaluate the claim that \"the buyback created value because ROE rose by $7.5$ points\".</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct: a rise in ROE always means that more value has been created for shareholders, whatever caused it."], ["B", "Mostly mechanical leverage: ROE rose although net income fell, so value depends on debt cost and risk."], ["C", "Impossible: ROE must have fallen, because net income fell from $15$ to $13.5$ after the extra interest."], ["D", "Buybacks do not change equity, so ROE cannot have changed."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\dfrac{13.5}{60} = 22.5\\%$: the smaller denominator outweighs the lower numerator. The equity multiplier rose, and so did the risk borne by each ringgit of equity. This is the notes' warning that \"higher ROE alone is not proof of better operations\", applied to a financing decision.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>An \"industry average P/E\" is built from three firms with P/Es of $10$, $12$ and $200$; the last has EPS close to zero. Which benchmark is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "The arithmetic mean, $74$, because every firm in the industry should count equally in a benchmark."], ["B", "The highest value, $200$, as a conservative ceiling."], ["C", "The mean of the two lowest values, $11$, because a high P/E is always a data error."], ["D", "The harmonic mean ($\\approx 15.9$) or the median ($12$): the mean is dominated by one outlier."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\dfrac{3}{1/10 + 1/12 + 1/200} \\approx 15.9$; the harmonic mean averages earnings yields, which stay well behaved near zero EPS. The notes warn that near-zero EPS makes P/E \"unhelpful or unstable\". Dropping firms because their P/E is high (C) throws away information and is not a rule.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>A study tests whether slide 7-54's warning signs predict share returns. It forms portfolios on each firm's fiscal year-end date, using that year's financial statements, and measures returns over the next twelve months. Which flaw most threatens the result?</p>", "options": [["A", "Look-ahead bias: statements are published after the year-end, so returns must start from the filing date."], ["B", "Using too many firms in the sample makes the statistical test less reliable and the results noisier."], ["C", "The slide's ratio definitions differ from those used by some commercial data providers, biasing the ratios."], ["D", "Firms from different industries are pooled in the same sample, so industry effects drive the result."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A tradable test can only use public information. Definition differences (C) and industry mixing (D) matter and should be handled, for example with industry-adjusted ratios, but look-ahead bias can manufacture apparent predictability on its own. More firms (B) improves power.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor bought a stock on the thesis \"ROE is rising because operations are improving\". Which monitoring rule tests that thesis best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Track the share price weekly and sell after any fall of $10\\%$, since the market price already summarises everything about the firm's operations."], ["B", "Track the reported ROE every year and stay invested while it keeps rising."], ["C", "Track the dividend per share and treat any dividend increase as confirmation."], ["D", "Split ROE each year into margin, turnover and multiplier; flag rises driven only by the multiplier, and check cash conversion."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Only the DuPont split separates operating improvement from leverage, and the cash-conversion check guards against accrual profit. B is exactly the trap the thesis must avoid, A and C watch outcomes that need not reflect operations.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You want an example in which the current ratio <strong>improves</strong> while true liquidity <strong>worsens</strong>. Which set of figures ($\\text{RM}$ million) shows it?</p>", "options": [["A", "Current assets rise from $3.0$ (cash $1.0$, receivables $1.0$, inventory $1.0$) to $4.5$ (cash $0.4$, receivables $1.0$, inventory $3.1$ of slow-moving stock); current liabilities rise from $2.0$ to $2.5$."], ["B", "Current assets and current liabilities both double, from $3.0$ and $2.0$ to $6.0$ and $4.0$, as the firm grows quickly and buys more stock on credit."], ["C", "Cash rises from $1.0$ to $2.0$ while current liabilities fall from $2.0$ to $1.5$; receivables, inventory and everything else on the balance sheet stay unchanged."], ["D", "Current liabilities rise from $2.0$ to $3.0$ while current assets rise only from $3.0$ to $3.3$."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In A the current ratio rises from $\\dfrac{3.0}{2.0} = 1.5$ to $\\dfrac{4.5}{2.5} = 1.8$, but cash falls and the quick ratio falls from $\\dfrac{3.0 - 1.0}{2.0} = 1.0$ to $\\dfrac{4.5 - 3.1}{2.5} = 0.56$: the \"improvement\" is unsold stock. B leaves the ratio unchanged at $1.5$, C is a genuine improvement, and D makes the ratio fall.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Apex trades at $\\text{RM}12$; an analyst's estimate of value is $\\text{RM}15$. Its receivables and inventory are growing far faster than sales, operating cash flow is well below net income, and management plans extra borrowing. Which recommendation is best constructed?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy now: the $25\\%$ upside to the analyst's estimate is large enough to more than compensate for any working-capital or financing issues."], ["B", "Defer a firm buy: check receivables ageing, stock and loan terms; buy if collections normalise and a cautious value still exceeds $\\text{RM}12$."], ["C", "Sell immediately: receivables growth of $66.67\\%$ against sales growth of $5\\%$ proves that the firm is already in financial distress."], ["D", "Buy, because Apex's ROE of $30\\%$ shows it is a high-quality business whatever the price."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A strong answer is conditional and names the evidence that would change it, as in the model answer to Question 6. A treats an estimate as a certainty, C treats a warning sign as proof, and D ignores both leverage (Apex's equity multiplier is $2.75$) and price.</p>"}, {"module": 7, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>You are designing a composite \"trouble-brewing\" score based on slide 7-54. Which design best captures the warning signs while avoiding problems of scale and definition?</p>", "options": [["A", "Add up the raw ringgit increases in receivables and inventory for each firm, so that the largest absolute build-ups receive the highest trouble scores."], ["B", "Use the P/E ratio alone, because the market price already contains every warning sign."], ["C", "Within each industry and year, standardise receivables growth minus sales growth, inventory growth minus sales growth, the change in the current ratio, the change in debt-equity on one consistent definition, and $\\dfrac{NI - CFO}{\\text{average assets}}$; then combine the standardised scores."], ["D", "Rank firms on ROE, since the least profitable firms by that measure are the ones most likely to be heading for financial trouble."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>C turns each slide 7-54 sign into a scale-free measure, compares firms only with industry peers (the chapter's peer-comparison advice) and fixes the definitions. A rewards size, B assumes the market has already priced everything, and D would rank the highly leveraged firms (high ROE) as the safest.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In the constant-growth dividend valuation model $V_0 = \\dfrac{D}{k - g}$, which dividend goes in the numerator?</p>", "options": [["A", "The dividend just paid, $D_0$"], ["B", "The average dividend of the past five years"], ["C", "The dividend expected one year from now, $D_1$"], ["D", "Current earnings per share, because all earnings eventually belong to shareholders"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The formula is the present value of a stream that starts one period from now, so its first term is $D_1 = D_0(1+g)$. A is the classic trap: it values a stream one year too early and understates value. B and D are not dividends expected next year; EPS is not a dividend unless the payout is $100\\%$.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>The chapter projects a company's key financial variables in three steps. Which sequence is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Sales and profits, then EPS and dividends, then the stock price"], ["B", "Estimate beta, compute the CAPM required return, then discount next year's earnings at that rate to get the price"], ["C", "Compute book value per share, apply an industry P/B multiple, then compare the result with the market price"], ["D", "Forecast interest rates, then inflation, then GDP growth, because the economy determines every company's results"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Slide 8-6: step 1 sales and profits, step 2 EPS and dividends, step 3 the stock price (EPS times P/E). B describes setting the required return, not projecting the firm's variables. C is one relative-valuation shortcut. D lists economic inputs that feed a forecast but are not the chapter's three steps.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>The slides list several variables that influence the P/E ratio an analyst should assume. Which is <strong>not</strong> one of them?</p>", "options": [["A", "The growth rate in earnings"], ["B", "The amount of debt in the company's capital structure"], ["C", "Current and projected inflation"], ["D", "The par value of the common stock"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Slide 8-14 names earnings growth, the general state of the market, debt in the capital structure, current and projected inflation, and the level of dividends. Par value is a legal accounting figure with no link to how much investors pay per ringgit of earnings.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor plans to sell a share after three years. Why can the share still be valued as the present value of <strong>all</strong> its future dividends?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because shares cannot be sold before the company is wound up, so in practice every investor collects every dividend the firm ever pays."], ["B", "Because the year-$3$ sale price is itself the value of the dividends after year $3$, so discounting it is discounting them."], ["C", "Because capital gains are taxed more heavily than dividends, so the uncertain sale price is replaced by the dividends it would have produced."], ["D", "Because in a fair market the sale price must equal the original purchase price, so only the dividends are left to value."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A terminal price summarises the value of benefits after the sale. Following the chain of buyers, every price is ultimately the present value of later dividends. That is also why adding post-sale dividends on top of the sale price double counts. A and D are false; C confuses tax with valuation.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Next year's sales are forecast to rise $10\\%$, but the net profit margin is expected to fall from $8\\%$ to $6\\%$. The share count is unchanged. What happens to forecast EPS compared with last year?</p>", "options": [["A", "It rises $10\\%$: with the share count unchanged, EPS moves one-for-one with sales whatever happens to the margin."], ["B", "It is unchanged, because the higher sales and the lower margin offset each other exactly."], ["C", "It must rise, because the margin affects operating cash flow but not reported earnings per share."], ["D", "It falls $17.5\\%$: $1.10 \\times \\dfrac{0.06}{0.08} = 0.825$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Profit = sales x margin, so the EPS ratio is $1.10 \\times 0.06 / 0.08 = 0.825$, a $17.5\\%$ fall. Sales growth only becomes profit growth if the margin holds. A ignores the margin. B is wrong arithmetic: $10\\%$ growth does not offset a $25\\%$ cut in the margin. C misunderstands EPS, which is profit per share.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement correctly distinguishes <strong>expected</strong> return from <strong>required</strong> return?</p>", "options": [["A", "Expected return is a forecast of the outcome; required return is the hurdle for the risk and the wait."], ["B", "They are always equal in a competitive market, so comparing them adds nothing to a valuation exercise."], ["C", "Required return is the return the share actually delivered last year, used as a guide to next year."], ["D", "Expected return is fixed by CAPM from beta, while required return comes from the analyst's own forecasts of the dividend and the sale price."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The required return is the discount rate and benchmark; the expected return comes from forecasts of $D_1$, $P_1$ and the price paid. $E(R) &gt; k$ is the same test as $V_0 &gt; P_0$. D swaps the two: CAPM gives the required return. B and C confuse the concepts with equilibrium or with realised history.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>An analyst sets a target for a loss-making software firm using a price-to-sales multiple from peers. Why, and what is the main limitation?</p>", "options": [["A", "P/S is always more accurate than P/E, because sales cannot be managed by accounting choices the way earnings can."], ["B", "P/S measures profitability directly, so it shows how far the firm is from breaking even and when it will get there."], ["C", "P/E is meaningless with negative EPS; sales still give scale, but they do not show whether margins will ever turn positive."], ["D", "P/S automatically adjusts for the firm's debt and capital structure, so no further comparability checks are needed."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes recommend P/S when earnings are weak or negative, with the warning that sales do not reveal sustainable margins or cash flow. A overstates the case (revenue recognition can be managed too). B is false: P/S says nothing about profit. D confuses price-to-sales with enterprise-value-to-sales: neither removes the need to check capital structure.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A stock has just paid $\\text{RM}1.50$. Dividends are expected to grow $6\\%$ a year forever. Its beta is $1.2$, the risk-free rate is $4\\%$ and the expected market return is $9\\%$. What is its constant-growth value?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}37.50$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}18.07$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}39.75$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}15.90$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$k = 4\\% + 1.2(9\\% - 4\\%) = 10\\%$. $D_1 = 1.50(1.06) = 1.59$. $V_0 = \\dfrac{1.59}{0.10 - 0.06} = \\text{RM}39.75$. A uses $D_0$ ($1.50 / 0.04$). B uses the wrong CAPM, $4\\% + 1.2 \\times 9\\% = 14.8\\%$, giving $1.59 / 0.088$. D divides by $k$ alone, ignoring growth.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Last year's sales were $\\text{RM}250$ million. Forecast: sales growth $6\\%$, net margin $7\\%$, $5$ million shares, payout $30\\%$, year-end P/E $14$. The required return is $11\\%$. What is the share worth today, using next year's dividend and year-end price?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}51.94$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}47.80$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}53.05$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}46.79$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Sales $250(1.06) = 265$; profit $265 \\times 0.07 = 18.55$; $EPS = 18.55 / 5 = 3.71$; $D_1 = 3.71 \\times 0.30 = 1.113$; $P_1 = 3.71 \\times 14 = 51.94$. $V_0 = \\dfrac{1.113 + 51.94}{1.11} = \\text{RM}47.80$. A is the undiscounted future price; C adds the dividend but still does not discount; D discounts the price but forgets the dividend.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>$D_0 = \\text{RM}2.00$. Dividends grow $15\\%$ a year for three years, then $5\\%$ forever. The required return is $11\\%$. What is the value today?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}45.36$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}43.51$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}41.51$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}59.67$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$D_1 = 2.30$, $D_2 = 2.645$, $D_3 = 3.04175$, $D_4 = 3.04175(1.05) = 3.19384$. $P_3 = \\dfrac{3.19384}{0.11 - 0.05} = 53.23$. PV of $D_1$ to $D_3$ $= 6.443$; PV of $P_3 = 53.23 / 1.11^3 = 38.92$; total $\\text{RM}45.36$. B builds $P_3$ from $D_3$ instead of $D_4$. C discounts $P_3$ four years. D adds $P_3$ without discounting it.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A sell-side note forecasts next year's EPS at $\\text{RM}2.40$, a target P/E of $16$ and a dividend of $\\text{RM}0.80$. The share costs $\\text{RM}34$ and your required return is $12\\%$. Which summary is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The target is $\\text{RM}38.40$, an upside of $12.9\\%$ on today's price, which beats $12\\%$, so the dividend can be ignored in the decision."], ["B", "$V_0 = \\dfrac{0.80 + 38.40}{1.12} = \\text{RM}35.00 &gt; 34$, and $E(R) = 15.29\\% &gt; 12\\%$."], ["C", "$V_0 = \\dfrac{38.40}{1.12} = \\text{RM}34.29$, so the share is fairly priced at $\\text{RM}34$."], ["D", "The value today is $\\text{RM}39.20$, the target price plus next year's dividend."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$2.40 \\times 16 = 38.40$ is a year-end price. Adding the dividend and discounting gives $39.20 / 1.12 = 35.00$, and both tests agree. A happens to reach a similar verdict but ignores the dividend and the same-date logic; with a $13\\%$ hurdle its reasoning would mislead. C omits the dividend. D does not discount.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A firm's next-year EPS is $\\text{RM}4.00$ and its payout ratio is $40\\%$. Its return on equity is $15\\%$, so sustainable growth is $g = ROE \\times (1 - \\text{payout})$. The risk-free rate is $3\\%$, beta $0.9$ and the market risk premium $6\\%$. What does the constant-growth model give?</p>", "options": [["A", "No meaningful value: $k = 8.4\\% &lt; g = 9\\%$, so this growth cannot last forever."], ["B", "$V_0 = \\dfrac{1.60}{0.084 - 0.09} = -\\text{RM}266.67$: the model says the share is worth less than nothing, so it should be sold short."], ["C", "$V_0 = \\dfrac{1.60}{0.084} = \\text{RM}19.05$, treating the dividend as level because growth cannot be estimated reliably."], ["D", "$V_0 = \\dfrac{4.00}{0.09 - 0.084} = \\text{RM}666.67$, because all earnings ultimately belong to shareholders."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$D_1 = 4.00 \\times 0.40 = 1.60$, $k = 8.4\\%$, $g = 9\\%$. With $k &lt; g$ the perpetual series does not converge; a negative denominator does not mean a negative value (B). C drops growth; D uses EPS and reverses the denominator. The right response is to recognise that $9\\%$ growth must fade.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two analysts value the same stock with the constant-growth model and the same $D_1 = \\text{RM}3.00$. Analyst X uses $k = 12\\%$ and $g = 6\\%$; Analyst Y uses $k = 11\\%$ and $g = 7\\%$. What best explains the gap between their values?</p>", "options": [["A", "Y's value is higher only because Y's required return is lower; with $D_1$ held fixed, the growth assumption has no effect."], ["B", "Y must have used $D_0$ instead of $D_1$, which always produces the larger of two constant-growth values."], ["C", "The gap is mostly rounding: one-point changes in $k$ and $g$ move in opposite directions and largely cancel each other out."], ["D", "Together the changes narrow $k - g$ from $6\\%$ to $4\\%$, lifting value from $\\text{RM}50$ to $\\text{RM}75$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$3 / 0.06 = 50$ and $3 / 0.04 = 75$. The value depends on the spread $k - g$, so two modest changes in the same direction compound. This sensitivity is exactly why the notes warn that a precise answer is not a precise forecast. A is wrong because $g$ sits in the denominator: raising it narrows the spread just as lowering $k$ does. B contradicts the stated common $D_1$. C is wrong because the two changes push the value the same way, they do not cancel.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A share trades at $\\text{RM}40$. Next year's dividend is expected to be $\\text{RM}2$ and the required return is $11\\%$. What perpetual growth rate does the price imply, and what follows if you believe growth will be only $4\\%$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$5\\%$, the dividend yield $\\dfrac{2}{40}$; at $4\\%$ growth the share therefore looks slightly underpriced compared with the market's view."], ["B", "$6\\%$; at $4\\%$ growth the value is $\\dfrac{2}{0.07} = \\text{RM}28.57$, below the price."], ["C", "$16\\%$, the required return plus the dividend yield; the market expects explosive growth that will not last."], ["D", "$11\\%$, because in a fairly priced market the implied growth rate always equals the required return."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$g = k - \\dfrac{D_1}{P_0} = 11\\% - 5\\% = 6\\%$. The price already assumes $6\\%$ growth forever; with your $4\\%$, $V_0 = 2 / (0.11 - 0.04) = \\text{RM}28.57 &lt; \\text{RM}40$. A confuses the yield with growth. C adds instead of subtracting. D would make the denominator zero.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An analyst writes $V_0 = \\dfrac{1.00}{1.10} + \\dfrac{1.10}{1.10^2} + \\dfrac{1.155}{1.10^3} + \\dfrac{P_2}{1.10^2}$, where $P_2 = \\dfrac{D_3}{k - g}$ and $D_3 = 1.155$. What is wrong?</p>", "options": [["A", "$P_2$ should be discounted three years, because it is built from $D_3$, which is paid at the end of year $3$."], ["B", "$P_2$ should be built from $D_2$, the last dividend the analyst has written out explicitly."], ["C", "Drop the separate $D_3$ term: $P_2$ already includes it."], ["D", "Nothing is wrong, but $D_4$ should also be added separately so the stable phase is complete."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$P_2$ is the year-$2$ value of $D_3, D_4, \\dots$. Adding $\\dfrac{1.155}{1.10^3} = \\text{RM}0.87$ separately overstates value by that amount. A misdates $P_2$, which is a year-$2$ value. B would value a stream that starts a year too early. D makes the double counting worse.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>From the constant-growth model, the justified forward P/E is $\\dfrac{P_0}{E_1} = \\dfrac{\\text{payout}}{k - g}$. With payout $50\\%$, $k = 10\\%$ and $g = 5\\%$, what is the justified P/E, and what does a market P/E of $14$ imply?</p>", "options": [["A", "$14\\times$ is justified, because a P/E is set by the market and does not depend on the firm's growth or risk."], ["B", "The justified P/E is $\\dfrac{1}{0.05} = 20\\times$, so a market multiple of $14\\times$ means the share is cheap."], ["C", "The justified P/E is $\\dfrac{0.5}{0.10} = 5\\times$, so the market is paying almost three times too much for the earnings."], ["D", "$10\\times$; a $14\\times$ market multiple implies $g \\approx 6.43\\%$ or a lower $k$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$0.5 / (0.10 - 0.05) = 10$. Solving $0.5 / (0.10 - g) = 14$ gives $g \\approx 6.43\\%$. So the market multiple embeds faster growth or lower risk than the analyst's inputs. B forgets the payout ($1 / 0.05$). C divides payout by $k$ alone. A ignores the link between multiples, growth and risk.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A team's two-stage model has a present value of explicit dividends of $\\text{RM}6.20$ and a present value of the year-$5$ terminal value of $\\text{RM}31.80$, using $k = 9\\%$ and $g = 3\\%$. The price is $\\text{RM}33$. A judge asks what happens if $k$ is $10\\%$. Holding the year-$6$ dividend fixed, what happens to the terminal value's present value?</p>", "options": [["A", "It falls by one ninth, to $\\text{RM}28.27$, because a rise from $9\\%$ to $10\\%$ is a one-ninth rise in the discount rate and value is proportional to it."], ["B", "It falls to about $\\text{RM}26.04$, so total value (about $\\text{RM}32.24$) drops below the $\\text{RM}33$ price."], ["C", "It does not change, because the year-$6$ dividend is unchanged and only the explicit dividends are discounted at the new rate."], ["D", "It rises, because a higher required return means investors expect a higher return from holding the share."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Terminal value at year $5$: $31.80 \\times 1.09^5 = 48.93$, so $D_6 = 48.93 \\times 0.06 = 2.936$. At $10\\%$: $P_5 = 2.936 / 0.07 = 41.94$, worth $41.94 / 1.10^5 = 26.04$ today. Both the narrower spread and heavier discounting hit it. A treats the effect as proportional; C ignores discounting; D confuses required with expected return.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Under constant growth with $D_1 = \\text{RM}2.08$, $k = 10\\%$ and $g = 4\\%$, $P_0 = \\text{RM}34.67$. If the model holds, what is the expected price in one year and the expected one-year return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$P_1 = \\text{RM}36.05$; return $= 6\\% + 4\\% = 10\\%$."], ["B", "$P_1 = \\text{RM}34.67$, because a constant-growth share has a constant price; the expected return is the $6\\%$ dividend yield alone."], ["C", "$P_1 = \\text{RM}36.05$, and the expected return is $4\\%$, because only the change in price counts as return."], ["D", "$P_1 = \\text{RM}37.49$, and the expected return is $14\\%$, because growth compounds on both the dividend and the price."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In the model the price grows at $g$, so $E(R) = \\dfrac{D_1}{P_0} + g = 6\\% + 4\\% = 10\\%$: a correctly priced share is expected to earn exactly $k$. B forgets that dividends grow. C ignores the dividend. D compounds the growth twice and double counts.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Consider a dividend stream with $D_1 = \\text{RM}2.08$ growing at $g = 10\\%$, discounted at $k = 10\\%$. What is the present value of the first $50$ dividends, and what does it show?</p>", "options": [["A", "It converges to $\\dfrac{D_1}{k} = \\text{RM}20.80$, because once growth equals the discount rate the two simply cancel out of the formula."], ["B", "$2.08 \\times 50 = \\text{RM}104$, because when $g = k$ growth and discounting both disappear and each dividend counts at face value."], ["C", "It is negative, because the denominator $k - g$ is zero and the formula breaks down."], ["D", "About $\\text{RM}94.55$, and it grows without limit as the horizon lengthens."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Term $t$ is $\\dfrac{D_1(1+g)^{t-1}}{(1+k)^t} = \\dfrac{D_1}{1+k}$ when $g = k$. Each extra year adds $\\text{RM}1.89$, without end. This is the mechanism behind the $k &gt; g$ condition, and why a firm cannot grow dividends as fast as the discount rate forever. A and B mis-sum the series; C confuses a formula breakdown with a negative value.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>With $D_1 = \\text{RM}1.25$, $k = 10\\%$ and $g = 5\\%$, a model gives $\\text{RM}25$ against a price of $\\text{RM}22$. Which evaluation is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "Break-even growth is about $4.32\\%$, close to the base case, so the bargain is fragile: test scenarios and keep a margin of safety."], ["B", "The break-even growth rate is $10\\%$, so the share stays undervalued under any reasonable growth assumption."], ["C", "Break-even growth is $\\dfrac{1.25}{22} = 5.68\\%$; since the base case of $5\\%$ is below it, the share is already overvalued and should be sold."], ["D", "Price is below value in the base case, so any positive growth rate confirms the purchase decision."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Setting $22 = 1.25 / (0.10 - g)$ gives $g = 4.32\\%$; at $g = 4.3\\%$, $V_0 = 1.25 / 0.057 = \\text{RM}21.93$, below the price. A $\\text{RM}3$ gap that disappears under a small change is fragile. C confuses the dividend yield with the break-even growth rate. B and D ignore the sensitivity.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An analyst forecasts a price of $\\text{RM}60$ in three years, with no dividends. The share costs $\\text{RM}45$ and the required return is $12\\%$. The analyst says: \"$45 &lt; 60$, so it is undervalued.\" Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Agree: the investor gains $\\text{RM}15$, a $33\\%$ return, which is comfortably more than the $12\\%$ hurdle."], ["B", "Agree: discounted at $12\\%$ the future price is still $\\dfrac{60}{1.12} = \\text{RM}53.57$, well above $\\text{RM}45$."], ["C", "It cannot be judged until beta has been estimated and CAPM has been used to recompute the required return."], ["D", "Disagree: $\\dfrac{60}{1.12^3} = \\text{RM}42.71 &lt; 45$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The $\\text{RM}60$ arrives in three years, so it must be discounted three years. A compares a three-year total return with an annual hurdle. B discounts only one year. C: the required return is already given, so no beta is needed.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Delta's multiples give implied prices of $\\text{RM}30$ (P/E), $\\text{RM}32$ (P/CF), $\\text{RM}24$ (P/S) and $\\text{RM}25$ (P/B); the price is $\\text{RM}28$. Delta's peers have <strong>less debt and faster growth</strong> than Delta. Which evaluation is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Averaging the four estimates gives $\\text{RM}27.75$, so Delta is overvalued by exactly $\\text{RM}0.25$ and the multiples settle the question."], ["B", "The P/E and P/CF estimates probably overstate Delta's value, so $\\text{RM}28$ proves nothing yet: adjust or cross-check."], ["C", "P/CF is the most reliable multiple because cash flow cannot be manipulated, so Delta is undervalued by $\\text{RM}4$ and should be bought."], ["D", "Only P/B matters because book value is audited, so Delta is overvalued by $\\text{RM}3$ whatever the other multiples say."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Multiples carry the peers' growth and risk. With more debt and slower growth, Delta deserves lower multiples, which weakens the higher estimates. A treats an average of non-comparable estimates as precise. C and D pick one ratio by decree; no multiple is universally best.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A team pitches \"buy\" because the target trades at $8\\times$ earnings while peers trade at $12\\times$. Which judge's challenge is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "P/E below $10$ signals undervaluation in almost every market, so the pitch is sound and the judges should accept it."], ["B", "Peer multiples are irrelevant once the target's own P/E has been calculated from its audited earnings."], ["C", "A low P/E may reflect higher risk, weaker growth or one-off earnings: normalise EPS and test the peers first."], ["D", "Multiply the peers' $12\\times$ by the target's EPS to find the price the target is guaranteed to reach within a year."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes stress that a low multiple alone does not prove undervaluation; the market may be pricing risk or growth correctly, or EPS may be unrepresentative. A and D treat a multiple as a law; B discards the comparison that gives the multiple meaning.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>A firm pays only $10\\%$ of earnings as dividends but returns a lot of cash through regular share buybacks. An analyst values it with a constant-growth DVM using today's dividend. Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Appropriate: buybacks never change what a remaining shareholder receives, so the dividend is the full picture of cash returned."], ["B", "Risky: the dividend understates cash returned; model the buybacks or total payout per share."], ["C", "Appropriate, because buybacks always destroy value, so leaving them out of the model is simply conservative."], ["D", "Use P/B instead, because book value automatically captures every buyback and is therefore unaffected by payout policy."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes warn that a low current dividend is not a complete description of future shareholder benefits, and that share-count changes affect per-share figures. A and C are false generalisations. D swaps one incomplete measure for another; buybacks reduce book equity, which can distort P/B.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>A share trades at $\\text{RM}100$ with $D_1 = \\text{RM}2$ and $k = 9\\%$, so the implied perpetual growth is $g = 9\\% - 2\\% = 7\\%$. Long-run nominal economic growth is believed to be around $4\\%$ to $5\\%$. Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "The market price is always right, so $7\\%$ growth forever is the best available forecast and should be used in the analyst's own model."], ["B", "The implied growth rate is irrelevant: what matters is only whether the dividend yield is above the risk-free rate."], ["C", "Growth of $7\\%$ forever is implausible, so the price implies fast growth that fades, or a lower $k$: scrutinise it."], ["D", "The price must fall soon to the value at $4\\%$ growth, $\\dfrac{2}{0.05} = \\text{RM}40$, as the market corrects its error."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Reverse-engineering the price shows what it assumes. A firm growing faster than the economy forever would eventually outgrow it, so a two-stage reading or a different $k$ is more credible. A is uncritical; B discards the most useful output of the exercise; D treats a model value as a forecast of the timing and size of a price move.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>A working paper claims: \"Any dividend or cash-flow model in which more than $80\\%$ of value comes from the terminal value is uninformative and should be discarded.\" Evaluate.</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct: such models should be replaced by a P/E multiple, which values the firm without any terminal assumption at all."], ["B", "Partly: report sensitivities and cross-checks, but a high terminal share is normal for growing firms."], ["C", "Wrong: the terminal value comes from a formula, so it is the most reliable part of any multi-stage model."], ["D", "Wrong: if the required return comes from CAPM, the share of value in the terminal value does not matter."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In the notes' two-stage example the terminal value is about $87\\%$ of value, simply because most cash arrives later. The right response is transparency and stress testing, not rejection. A forgets that a multiple embeds the same long-run assumptions implicitly. C and D confuse a formula with certainty.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A firm pays no dividend now. It is expected to pay $\\text{RM}1.00$ at the end of year $4$, growing $5\\%$ a year forever after that. The required return is $12\\%$. Which plan and value are right?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its value is zero, because a dividend model can only value dividends paid within the next three years."], ["B", "Its value is $\\dfrac{1.00}{0.12 - 0.05} = \\text{RM}14.29$ today, because the growing-perpetuity formula already accounts for timing."], ["C", "Value the stream at year $3$: $P_3 = \\dfrac{1.00}{0.07} = 14.29$; then $V_0 = \\dfrac{14.29}{1.12^3} \\approx \\text{RM}10.17$."], ["D", "Value the stream at year $3$ but discount it four years, because the first dividend is not paid until year $4$: about $\\text{RM}9.08$."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The growing-perpetuity formula gives a value one period before the first payment. With $D_4$ first, that is year $3$. Then discount $14.2857 / 1.404928 = 10.17$. B treats a year-$3$ value as today's. D discounts one year too many. A ignores later distributions.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In your two-stage valuation, about $85\\%$ of the value comes from the terminal value. Which monitoring plan fits best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Track long-run growth and $k$; revalue when either moves past a preset trigger."], ["B", "Track next quarter's dividend closely, because it is the earliest and most certain cash flow and so carries the largest weight in present value."], ["C", "Watch only the share price and sell after any $10\\%$ fall, since the price already reflects every change in the assumptions."], ["D", "No monitoring is needed: once the formula has produced a value, the value is exact until the next annual report."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>When most of the value sits in the terminal value, the long-run growth rate and $k$ drive the answer, so those assumptions deserve explicit review triggers. B monitors the input that matters least. C reacts to price without asking whether the thesis changed. D confuses arithmetic precision with forecast certainty.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You build three scenarios for a stock with $D_1 = \\text{RM}2$ and $k = 10\\%$: growth $3\\%$ (probability $25\\%$), $4\\%$ ($50\\%$) and $5\\%$ ($25\\%$). What should you report?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}33.33$ only: the average growth rate is $4\\%$, so the scenario values average back to the base case and add nothing."], ["B", "$\\text{RM}40$, because investors are most interested in how much they could make in the bull case."], ["C", "$\\text{RM}28.57$, because a prudent valuation should always report the worst case and nothing else."], ["D", "About $\\text{RM}33.81$ probability-weighted, with a range of $\\text{RM}28.57$ to $\\text{RM}40$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Scenario values are $2 / 0.07 = 28.57$, $2 / 0.06 = 33.33$ and $2 / 0.05 = 40$. Weighted: $0.25(28.57) + 0.5(33.33) + 0.25(40) = 33.81$. Because $V$ is convex in $g$, the average of the values exceeds the value at the average growth rate, so A understates. Reporting the range shows how fragile the estimate is. B and C each report one tail only.</p>"}, {"module": 8, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>You must value a newly listed firm with negative earnings and no dividend. Which protocol is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Scenario forecasts of sales, margins and dilution, a terminal value with $g &lt; k$, a value range, and a P/S cross-check."], ["B", "Apply the peer group's average P/E to the firm's negative EPS, since a market multiple already captures growth, risk and dilution."], ["C", "Set the value to zero until the first dividend is paid, because a dividend model cannot value a firm that pays nothing."], ["D", "Take the IPO offer price as intrinsic value, since the underwriters have already done the analysis on the road show."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>It follows the chapter's chain (forecast the business → per-share benefits → timing and risk → value → compare) and adds cross-checks and sensitivity. B yields a meaningless negative price. C ignores later distributions. D confuses an offer price, set partly by demand on the road show, with an independent estimate of value.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which item belongs to the <strong>strong-form</strong> information set but <strong>not</strong> to the semi-strong set?</p>", "options": [["A", "A company's published quarterly earnings release"], ["B", "Yesterday's closing price and trading volume"], ["C", "A takeover the board has agreed but not yet announced"], ["D", "A central bank's interest-rate decision announced this morning"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Semi-strong efficiency covers all <strong>public</strong> information, which includes published earnings (A), past market data (B) and public announcements (D). Only the undisclosed takeover is private, so it enters the information set only at the strong form.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which expression is algebraically identical to $\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{A/D}{AV/DV}$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\dfrac{A \\times DV}{D \\times AV}$"], ["B", "$\\dfrac{A \\times AV}{D \\times DV}$, the issue ratio multiplied by the volume ratio"], ["C", "$\\dfrac{A - D}{AV - DV}$"], ["D", "$\\dfrac{AV/A}{DV/D}$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Dividing by $\\dfrac{AV}{DV}$ is multiplying by $\\dfrac{DV}{AV}$, so $\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{A}{D} \\times \\dfrac{DV}{AV}$. B multiplies by the volume ratio instead of dividing; C uses differences, not ratios; D is the <strong>reciprocal</strong> of TRIN (volume per advancing issue over volume per declining issue), so it would reverse the reading.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Under the slides' conventions, which pair of readings is <strong>bullish</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "A low mutual fund cash ratio, and odd-lot purchases far exceeding odd-lot sales"], ["B", "A high mutual fund cash ratio, and odd-lot sales far exceeding odd-lot purchases"], ["C", "A high TRIN, and declining issues outnumbering advancing issues"], ["D", "A confidence index falling away from $1$ as the quality spread widens, and more new lows than new highs"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides read a high MFCR as cash waiting to buy stocks, and treat odd-lot traders as contrary indicators: when small traders sell heavily, the rule reads bullish. A is the bearish version of both; C and D are both bearish under the slides' readings (high TRIN, negative breadth, a widening quality spread, more new lows).</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Walmart's quarterly revenue peaks every holiday quarter, year after year, but its share price shows no matching seasonal pattern (Figures 9.1 and 9.2). What is the best explanation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Revenue has no effect on the value of a company."], ["B", "The stock market reacts to earnings only, never to revenue."], ["C", "Share prices are so noisy from day to day that no piece of company information, predictable or not, can show up in them."], ["D", "A pattern everyone can predict is already in the price; only departures from the expected seasonal peak are news."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Prices react to surprises. A predictable peak is expected, so it moves expected cash flows before it happens and cannot move the price when it arrives. A and B are false (revenue drives cash flows); C overstates noise: prices do react, but to the unexpected part.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Why is a test of market efficiency always a <strong>joint</strong> test?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because it must be run on two markets at the same time."], ["B", "Because \"abnormal\" return is measured against a model of expected return, so a rejection could mean the model is wrong rather than the market."], ["C", "Because both a buyer and a seller are needed for every trade."], ["D", "Because the weak and strong forms must always be tested together on one sample, since the semi-strong form sits between them."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Abnormal return $= R_{\\text{actual}} - E(R)$, and $E(R)$ comes from a risk model. An unexplained return may reflect mispricing, an omitted risk, a flawed benchmark or chance. The other options use \"joint\" in a sense the chapter never means.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which statement correctly explains the \"future demand\" reading of high short interest?</p>", "options": [["A", "Every open short position must eventually be closed by buying shares, so short interest is potential future buying."], ["B", "Short sellers must sell additional shares to close their positions, so a high short interest adds a wave of future supply."], ["C", "Short interest counts shares bought on margin, which will be bought again later."], ["D", "High short interest guarantees a short squeeze within a few weeks."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A short seller borrowed and sold shares, and covers by <strong>buying</strong> them back. B repeats slide 9-27's error (\"sales\" to cover). C confuses short selling with margin buying. D overstates: shorts need not cover together or soon, so no squeeze is guaranteed.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An advance-decline line stands at $2{,}000$. Over three days: day 1, $1{,}450$ advance and $1{,}050$ decline; day 2, $900$ advance and $1{,}350$ decline; day 3, $1{,}100$ advance and $1{,}100$ decline. What is the line after day 3?</p>", "options": [["A", "$2{,}400$"], ["B", "$2{,}050$"], ["C", "$1{,}950$"], ["D", "$1{,}550$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Net breadth is $+400$, $-450$ and $0$, so $2{,}000 + 400 - 450 + 0 = \\mathbf{1{,}950}$. A counts only the positive day; D counts only the negative day; B reverses both signs ($2{,}000 - 400 + 450$).</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>On one day $1{,}500$ issues advance and $1{,}000$ decline; advancing volume is $720$ million shares and declining volume $640$ million. What is TRIN, and how do the slides read it?</p>", "options": [["A", "$0.75$: bullish, because more stocks advanced than declined and total volume was heavy"], ["B", "$1.13$: bearish, because volume rose"], ["C", "$1.50$: bullish, because the issue ratio is above $1$"], ["D", "$1.33$: bearish, even though more stocks advanced than declined"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{1{,}500/1{,}000}{720/640} = \\dfrac{1.5}{1.125} \\approx \\mathbf{1.33}$. Above $1$, the average declining stock traded more volume ($0.64$m) than the average advancing one ($0.48$m), which the slides read as bearish. A is the reciprocal; B is the volume ratio alone; C is the issue ratio alone.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>On-balance volume stands at $25$ million shares. Over five days the stock closes up on $6$m, down on $4$m, unchanged on $9$m, up on $3$m and down on $7$m. What is OBV now?</p>", "options": [["A", "$32$ million"], ["B", "$23$ million"], ["C", "$14$ million"], ["D", "$54$ million"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$25 + 6 - 4 + 0 + 3 - 7 = \\mathbf{23}$ million. An unchanged close adds nothing: A wrongly adds the $9$m, C wrongly subtracts it, and D simply adds every volume to the start.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A fund group holds $\\text{RM}45$m cash out of $\\text{RM}600$m total assets. It then pays $\\text{RM}15$m of redemptions out of cash. What is its mutual fund cash ratio afterwards?</p>", "options": [["A", "$5.00\\%$"], ["B", "$7.50\\%$"], ["C", "$5.13\\%$"], ["D", "$7.69\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Both cash and total assets fall by $\\text{RM}15$m: $\\dfrac{45 - 15}{600 - 15} = \\dfrac{30}{585} \\approx \\mathbf{5.13\\%}$. A forgets that assets fell too; B is the ratio before redemptions; D keeps the old cash over the new assets. The drop is also a reminder that cash may be held for redemptions, not purchases.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>Around an earnings announcement a stock returns $3.1\\%$, $1.0\\%$ and $0.2\\%$ on days $0$, $1$ and $2$; its risk model expected $0.4\\%$, $-0.3\\%$ and $0.3\\%$. What is the cumulative abnormal return over the three days?</p>", "options": [["A", "$3.9\\%$"], ["B", "$4.3\\%$"], ["C", "$4.0\\%$"], ["D", "$0.4\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Abnormal returns are $3.1 - 0.4 = 2.7$, $1.0 - (-0.3) = 1.3$ and $0.2 - 0.3 = -0.1$, so $CAR = 2.7 + 1.3 - 0.1 = \\mathbf{3.9\\%}$. B adds the raw returns, C drops the negative day, D adds the expected returns.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Closes of $\\text{RM}10$, $\\text{RM}11$, $\\text{RM}12$, $\\text{RM}11$ and $\\text{RM}16$ give a five-day SMA of $\\text{RM}12$. The next close is $\\text{RM}13$. Under a rule that signals \"buy\" only when the close <strong>crosses above</strong> its five-day SMA, what happens?</p>", "options": [["A", "The SMA becomes $\\text{RM}12.60$; no new signal, because the close was already above its average and stays above it."], ["B", "The SMA becomes $\\text{RM}13.00$; a buy signal fires because the close equals the average."], ["C", "The SMA becomes $\\text{RM}12.60$; a buy signal fires, because a close above the moving average is by itself the rule's buy condition."], ["D", "The SMA becomes $\\text{RM}12.17$; a sell signal fires because the price fell from $\\text{RM}16$ to $\\text{RM}13$."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Drop $\\text{RM}10$, add $\\text{RM}13$: $\\dfrac{11 + 12 + 11 + 16 + 13}{5} = \\text{RM}12.60$ (or $12 + \\tfrac{13 - 10}{5}$). Yesterday $\\text{RM}16 &gt; \\text{RM}12$ and today $\\text{RM}13 &gt; \\text{RM}12.60$, so nothing was crossed. C confuses being above with crossing; D averages six closes; B miscomputes the window.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Figure 9.5's NASDAQ column shows $1{,}093$ advancing and $1{,}366$ declining issues, with $808.5$m shares of up volume and $723.5$m of down volume. Net breadth is $-273$ but TRIN is about $0.72$. What does this combination mean?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is impossible: whenever declining issues outnumber advancing issues, TRIN must come out above $1$, so the data contain an error."], ["B", "More stocks rose than fell, and they traded on lower volume."], ["C", "Fewer stocks rose, but the typical advancing stock traded more heavily than the typical declining one."], ["D", "The volume figures must have been entered the wrong way round."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{1{,}093/1{,}366}{808.5/723.5} = \\dfrac{0.800}{1.117} \\approx 0.72$. Volume per advancing issue is about $0.74$m against $0.53$m per declining issue. Breadth and TRIN measure different things, so they can conflict (A and D are wrong); B misreads the issue counts.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Researchers find that (i) prices fully adjust within a day of public earnings news and (ii) corporate insiders trading in their own firms' shares earn abnormal returns after costs. Which conclusion fits best?</p>", "options": [["A", "All three forms of efficiency are rejected."], ["B", "Only the weak form is supported; the semi-strong form is rejected."], ["C", "The strong form is supported, because insiders are market participants too and their trades are part of how prices are formed."], ["D", "The evidence supports semi-strong (and so weak) efficiency, but challenges the strong form."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Fast adjustment to public news is consistent with semi-strong efficiency, which nests the weak form. Profits from private information contradict only the strong form. A and B ignore (i); C reverses what (ii) implies.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Fractional shares, zero-commission apps and algorithms that slice large institutional orders into small pieces are now common. Why does this weaken the odd-lot contrary rule?</p>", "options": [["A", "Odd lots are no longer allowed on exchanges."], ["B", "It makes small traders always right, so the rule simply reverses."], ["C", "It has no effect: the rule never depended on who trades odd lots, only on the total number of shares traded in lots of fewer than $100$ each day."], ["D", "Odd-lot volume no longer cleanly tracks small, supposedly mistaken traders, and the rule already assumed such traders are systematically wrong."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The rule infers small-trader sentiment from odd-lot activity. When institutions and every app user trade in odd lots, the proxy blurs. The notes add that changes in trading practices weaken historical interpretations. A and B are false; C ignores the proxy the rule depends on.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A stock with $\\beta = 1.4$ returned $13\\%$ in a year when the market returned $10\\%$ and the risk-free rate was $3\\%$. Using CAPM as the risk model, what was its abnormal return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$+3.0$ points"], ["B", "$+0.2$ point"], ["C", "$-0.2$ point"], ["D", "$+10.0$ points"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$E(R) = 3\\% + 1.4\\,(10\\% - 3\\%) = 12.8\\%$, so $AR = 13\\% - 12.8\\% = \\mathbf{+0.2}$ point. A compares with the market return and ignores beta; D compares with the risk-free rate; C reverses the sign. Beating the index is not the same as beating the risk-adjusted benchmark.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>A researcher tests $100$ different calendar rules on the same data at a $5\\%$ significance level and finds $6$ \"significant\" ones. What is the best interpretation?</p>", "options": [["A", "About $100 \\times 5\\% = 5$ false positives are expected even if no rule works; correct for multiple testing and test out of sample."], ["B", "Six rules are now proven profitable, each at $95\\%$ confidence, so the number of genuine calendar anomalies in the data set is exactly six."], ["C", "The market is $6\\%$ inefficient."], ["D", "The other $94$ rules prove the market is efficient."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Finding $6$ when chance alone would produce about $5$ is weak evidence: this is the notes' \"chance after many strategies were tested\" explanation, made numerical. B ignores the number of tests; C and D misread what a significance test measures.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>An anomaly's abnormal return vanishes once a new factor is added to the expected-return model. What can be concluded?</p>", "options": [["A", "The anomaly was always pure data-mining, because any return that a newly added factor can absorb was never a real pattern in the first place."], ["B", "Nothing decisive: the factor may be a priced risk or may simply repackage the mispricing (the joint-hypothesis problem)."], ["C", "Markets are proven efficient."], ["D", "The new factor must be a behavioral bias."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Abnormal returns are always relative to a model. A new factor that \"explains\" an anomaly could be risk (efficient interpretation) or a description of the pattern (behavioral interpretation). A, C and D each pick one reading the evidence cannot isolate.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Strategy X earned $14\\%$ against a risk-model expectation of $11\\%$, with trading costs of $2.4$ points. Strategy Y earned $9\\%$ against an expectation of $7.5\\%$, with costs of $0.6$ point. Which assessment is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "X is better: its raw return is $5$ points higher."], ["B", "Y shows the larger estimated net abnormal return, $0.9$ point against $0.6$, though both are small enough to be model error or chance."], ["C", "X is better: its gross abnormal return is $3$ points against $1.5$, and gross figures are what the risk model is built to measure."], ["D", "They are equivalent, because both beat their benchmarks."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Net abnormal return: X $= 14 - 11 - 2.4 = 0.6$; Y $= 9 - 7.5 - 0.6 = 0.9$. A ignores risk; C ignores costs; D ignores the size of the edge. Both estimates remain sensitive to the benchmark and the cost estimate.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Mutual fund cash rises from $4\\%$ to $9\\%$ of assets. A commentator says this \"guarantees a rally as funds put the cash to work\". Which evaluation is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct: the slides say a high MFCR is bullish, and a jump from $4\\%$ to $9\\%$ is a large build-up that funds must eventually spend on stocks."], ["B", "Wrong: the slides say a high MFCR is bearish."], ["C", "Possible buying power, but the cash may be held for expected redemptions or out of caution, with unknown timing; compare with history and mandates."], ["D", "Irrelevant: fund cash cannot affect stock prices."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The slides read a high MFCR as potentially bullish (so B is wrong), but \"guarantee\" is unjustified: the notes stress that cash can meet withdrawals and need not be invested soon. D overstates in the other direction.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>To \"beat loss aversion\", an investor adopts the rule: sell any stock as soon as it falls $15\\%$ below its purchase price. Which evaluation is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "It keeps the decision anchored to the purchase price; reassess prospects at today's price instead, using the fall only as a review trigger."], ["B", "It is ideal: any mechanical rule removes emotion from investing, and a fixed $15\\%$ limit also caps every possible loss at exactly $15\\%$ of the cost."], ["C", "It makes loss aversion worse, because selling realises losses."], ["D", "It is the textbook's recommendation in Table 9.1."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes: \"Selling every loser mechanically is no better: the decision should use forward-looking evidence, costs and constraints.\" Table 9.1 asks whether you would buy the stock today with the cash, which is not an automatic rule (D). A rule is not automatically rational (B), and C misstates the bias.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A manager beat a broad index by $3$ points a year for five years with a portfolio of small, illiquid, low-P/E stocks. The pitch deck calls this \"proof of skill in an inefficient market\". Which evaluation is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "It is proof of skill: five consecutive years of beating a broad index by $3$ points a year is far too long a record to be explained by luck or by risk."], ["B", "It is impossible, so the data must be wrong."], ["C", "Consistent with skill, but the excess may reward size, value and liquidity exposures the index lacks, or luck; use a benchmark with similar exposures."], ["D", "Past returns are irrelevant to any evaluation."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The chapter's small-firm and value effects are exactly the anomalies that \"may compensate for risks missing from a model\". Against an index with different risk, the $3$ points is not yet abnormal. A overstates five noisy years; B and D are extreme.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Which finding would most favour a <strong>behavioral</strong> (under-reaction) explanation of post-earnings-announcement drift over a missing-risk explanation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Drift portfolios have higher betas than the market and lose most heavily in market downturns, so their extra return looks like a reward for bearing risk."], ["B", "The drift disappears entirely once trading costs are included."], ["C", "The drift survives risk adjustment and clusters around the next earnings announcements, when investors are surprised again."], ["D", "The drift is found only in the single year that was studied."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>If investors under-react, the rest of the news arrives as a fresh surprise at the next announcements; risk compensation would not cluster there. A supports a risk story; B says the pattern is not exploitable but does not explain it; D points to chance.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>If behavioral biases create mispricing, why might it persist even when many rational traders notice it?</p>", "options": [["A", "Rational traders are legally barred from trading against mispricing."], ["B", "Mispricing always corrects itself within a single trading day, because so many rational traders are watching that no gap can survive overnight."], ["C", "Rational traders always agree with biased traders."], ["D", "Correcting it needs capital and risk: costs, short-sale limits, illiquidity, and the chance that prices first move further away."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>This links the chapter's points about costs, liquidity and short selling to why anomalies may survive. A and C are false; B contradicts the drift evidence the chapter shows.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Suppose a large review finds that published anomalies earn noticeably less after their publication date than in the original studies' samples, but not zero. Which interpretation is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Partly in-sample overfitting, partly traders exploiting real mispricing once known; published backtests overstate future returns."], ["B", "All anomalies were pure chance, found by searching through many variables, which is the only reason their returns fell after the studies were published."], ["C", "All anomalies are compensation for risk, since returns did not fall to zero."], ["D", "Publication has no effect on markets."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A partial decline fits both effects the notes list: patterns found by chance shrink on new data, and known anomalies \"disappear once known\" as traders compete. B and C each force a single cause on a partial decline; D contradicts the finding.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which design is the most credible test of a strategy that buys after positive earnings surprises?</p>", "options": [["A", "Backtest on the same sample in which the pattern was first noticed, using gross returns, since that sample is known to contain the effect and so gives the clearest estimate of its size."], ["B", "Study one well-known company whose price kept rising after good news."], ["C", "Survey investors about whether they believe in post-earnings drift."], ["D", "Fix the event time as the first public release, enter at the next executable price, measure returns against a risk-adjusted benchmark after full costs, and test on an independent later sample."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>D follows the notes' checklist: information timing, implementable prices, risk adjustment, costs, out-of-sample evidence. A invites data-mining and ignores costs; B is a single observation; C measures opinion, not returns.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor shows overconfidence, self-attribution and familiarity bias (most savings in the employer's shares). Which set of controls answers those diagnoses best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Trade more often so that feedback arrives faster, rely mainly on the analysts who already agree with the current view, and add to the positions that have worked so far."], ["B", "Record each forecast with a probability before the result, review all trades against a benchmark after costs, and cap the employer position counting salary exposure."], ["C", "Sell every losing position immediately and keep every winner."], ["D", "Move everything into the employer's shares, since the investor knows the company best."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Written forecasts and symmetric reviews target overconfidence and self-attribution; a position cap that counts salary targets familiarity and concentration. A feeds overconfidence and confirmation; C is a mechanical rule unrelated to the diagnoses; D deepens the familiarity problem.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which specification turns a breadth idea into a <strong>testable</strong> trading rule?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy the index at the next day's open when the 10-day average of new highs minus new lows turns positive and the 5-day average TRIN is below $1$; sell when the highs-lows average turns negative; charge $0.1\\%$ per trade; fit on 2000-2015 and test once on 2016-2024."], ["B", "Buy when the market feels strong and breadth looks healthy, and sell when it does not."], ["C", "Buy whenever any indicator in Figure 9.5 is bullish."], ["D", "Buy when a head-and-shoulders pattern appears, as judged by the analyst after the move."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A states the signal, timing, exit, costs and a separate test sample: everything the notes require before a rule can be tested. B is vague, C has no exit and would trade on conflicting signals, and D is judged after the fact.</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A colleague wants to put all savings, part of which is needed within a year, into a familiar technology stock that has risen for six months and appears to break resistance, citing high short interest as \"proof it must rise\". Which plan is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Invest everything now; momentum, the breakout and short covering all point the same way."], ["B", "Wait for the stock to fall back below resistance, then invest everything."], ["C", "Invest everything, but add a stop-loss order a little below the breakout level, which removes the downside risk while keeping all of the upside."], ["D", "Ring-fence the one-year money in liquid assets; size any stock position within capacity for loss; write the thesis and review triggers."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>D matches the subjective model answer: horizon and liquidity first, then evidence, diversification and written decision rules. A and B rest on familiarity, herding and a two-sided indicator; C forgets that a stop-loss is a trigger, not a guaranteed price (Chapter 3).</p>"}, {"module": 9, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which research protocol gives the most credible evidence on whether a moving-average crossover rule adds value?</p>", "options": [["A", "Try many window lengths on the full history, report only the best-performing one, and show its cumulative return against simply holding cash over the period."], ["B", "Show charts of the rule's best trades from the last decade."], ["C", "Pre-register window, universe, dates, execution price, costs and benchmark; keep a hold-out period; correct for variants tried; report everything."], ["D", "Ask experienced traders whether the rule works."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>C addresses specification, implementation, costs, risk, out-of-sample testing and multiple testing. A is classic data-mining, B is selection after the fact (the notes' \"clearly marked buy point\" problem), and D is opinion, not evidence.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which pairing of a bond term with its meaning is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Serial bond: the entire issue matures on a single date, so every bond in it is repaid on the same day."], ["B", "Refunding provision: the bondholder's right to sell the bond back to the issuer."], ["C", "Deferred call: the issuer may not call the bond until a stated protection period has passed."], ["D", "Debenture: a bond secured by specified pieces of equipment."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A deferred call gives call protection for an initial period. A describes a term bond (a serial bond matures in portions on different dates). B describes a put; a refunding provision prohibits early retirement financed by a lower-coupon refunding issue. D describes an equipment trust certificate; a debenture is unsecured.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which bond is backed by stocks or bonds held in trust by a third party?</p>", "options": [["A", "A collateral trust bond"], ["B", "An equipment trust certificate"], ["C", "A mortgage bond"], ["D", "An income bond"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Collateral trust bonds pledge securities held in trust. Equipment trust certificates pledge equipment such as railcars or aircraft, mortgage bonds pledge real estate, and an income bond is distinguished by interest that depends on earnings, not by its collateral.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In the slides, which description defines the <strong>conversion equivalent</strong> of a convertible bond?</p>", "options": [["A", "Par value divided by the conversion ratio."], ["B", "The conversion ratio multiplied by the current market price of the issuer's common stock."], ["C", "The convertible bond's current market price minus the value of the shares it converts into."], ["D", "The share price at which conversion would be worth exactly the convertible's market price."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Conversion equivalent $= P_c / N$: the price the stock would have to reach to justify today's bond price. A is the contractual conversion price, B is conversion value and C is the conversion premium in ringgit.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Market yields fall sharply. Why does a callable bond's price usually rise <strong>less</strong> than the price of an otherwise identical noncallable bond?</p>", "options": [["A", "Callable bonds are legally required to pay lower coupons, so their prices respond less to any change in market yields."], ["B", "Buyers will not pay much above the call price for a bond the issuer is now likely to redeem at that price."], ["C", "The call premium is subtracted from par once yields fall."], ["D", "Falling yields increase the issuer's default risk, which lowers the price."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Once refinancing is cheaper, a call becomes likely, and nobody pays far above the call price for a bond that will be taken back at it: the call caps price appreciation. A is not a rule, C reverses the call price (par plus premium), and falling yields do not by themselves raise default risk.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A conventional fixed-rate bond's <strong>current yield is above its coupon rate</strong>. What must be true?</p>", "options": [["A", "Its yield to maturity equals its coupon rate."], ["B", "The bond must be callable."], ["C", "The issuer has cut the coupon payment."], ["D", "The bond is trading below par."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current yield $= C / P$ and coupon rate $= C / \\text{par}$ share the same numerator, so current yield exceeds the coupon rate only when $P$ is below par. Nothing about the coupon has changed (C), a discount bond's YTM is above its coupon rate (A), and callability is irrelevant (B).</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A slide says dollar-denominated (Yankee) bonds remove currency risk. Why is a ringgit-based investor who buys one still exposed to currency risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "The claim assumes a dollar-based investor; a ringgit investor must still convert USD payments into RM."], ["B", "Yankee bonds actually pay their coupons in euros, so a second conversion is needed."], ["C", "Currency risk can only arise from the issuer's home country, which is foreign."], ["D", "Registration with the SEC requires each payment to be converted into the buyer's home currency at a fixed contractual rate."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Currency risk depends on the investor's home currency, not the bond's label. For a USD-based investor a USD bond needs no conversion; for a ringgit investor it does. B and D are false, and C confuses issuer country risk with currency risk.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Why are mortgage-backed securities said to have <strong>negative convexity</strong> when interest rates fall?</p>", "options": [["A", "Falling rates speed up prepayments, so principal returns at par just when the price would rise, capping gains like a call."], ["B", "Falling rates raise mortgage default rates sharply, which lowers the price of the security just when other bonds are rising."], ["C", "Mortgage coupons reset upward when market rates fall."], ["D", "Tranching converts the security's interest into principal."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Homeowners hold a prepayment (call) option. When rates fall they refinance, so the investor's price gains are limited by early return of principal. That is the same mechanism as call risk. B, C and D describe effects that are not the cause.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par bond with a $7.5\\%$ coupon <strong>paid semiannually</strong> trades at $\\text{RM}1{,}060$. What are the semiannual coupon payment and the current yield?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}37.50$ and $3.54\\%$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}75.00$ and $7.50\\%$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}37.50$ and $7.08\\%$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}75.00$ and $7.08\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Annual coupon $= 7.5\\% \\times 1{,}000 = \\text{RM}75$, paid as $\\text{RM}37.50$ every six months. Current yield uses the annual coupon: $75 / 1{,}060 = \\mathbf{7.08\\%}$. A divides the half-year coupon by the price, B ignores the premium price, and D gives the annual amount, not the semiannual payment.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A municipal bond yields $3.6\\%$ and is exempt from both federal and state tax. Using the slide's combined formula, what is its taxable-equivalent yield for an investor with a $30\\%$ federal and a $6\\%$ state tax rate?</p>", "options": [["A", "$5.14\\%$"], ["B", "$5.47\\%$"], ["C", "$5.63\\%$"], ["D", "$2.37\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Combined rate $= 0.30 + 0.06(1 - 0.30) = 0.342$, so $TEY = 3.6\\% / (1 - 0.342) = 3.6\\% / 0.658 = \\mathbf{5.47\\%}$. A uses the federal rate only, C simply adds the rates ($3.6\\% / 0.64$), and D multiplies by $(1 - t)$ instead of dividing.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A convertible has $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par and a conversion price of $\\text{RM}62.50$. The stock trades at $\\text{RM}55$ and the bond at $\\text{RM}960$. What is the conversion premium as a percentage of conversion value?</p>", "options": [["A", "$9.09\\%$"], ["B", "$8.33\\%$"], ["C", "$13.64\\%$"], ["D", "$4.17\\%$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Ratio $= 1{,}000 / 62.50 = 16$; conversion value $= 16 \\times 55 = \\text{RM}880$; premium $= 960 - 880 = \\text{RM}80$; percentage $= 80 / 880 = \\mathbf{9.09\\%}$. B divides by the bond price, C compares the conversion price with the share price ($7.50 / 55$), and D halves the premium for no reason.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A corporate bond yields $6.35\\%$ and a government bond of the same maturity yields $4.90\\%$. A trader quotes the credit spread in basis points. What is it?</p>", "options": [["A", "$1.45$ bp"], ["B", "$14.5$ bp"], ["C", "$1{,}450$ bp"], ["D", "$145$ bp"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$6.35\\% - 4.90\\% = 1.45\\%$, and $1$ bp $= 0.01\\%$, so the spread is $1.45 / 0.01 = \\mathbf{145}$ bp. The other answers misplace the decimal.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A $12$-year zero-coupon bond with $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par is priced to yield $5.5\\%$ a year with annual compounding. What is its price?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}525.98$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}602.41$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}947.87$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}521.48$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$P = 1{,}000 / 1.055^{12} = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}525.98}$. B uses simple interest ($1{,}000 / 1.66$), C discounts for one year only, and D uses semiannual compounding ($1{,}000 / 1.0275^{24}$), which the question does not specify.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two default-free bonds both yield $5\\%$: Bond X is a two-year $5\\%$ annual-coupon bond and Bond Y is a $25$-year zero-coupon bond. Required yields on both rise to $6\\%$. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both prices fall by about the same percentage, because both bonds are default-free and yield the same $5\\%$ at the start."], ["B", "X falls more than Y because only X pays coupons that are now below market."], ["C", "Neither price changes, because government bonds are repaid at par."], ["D", "Y falls by roughly $21\\%$ and X by roughly $2\\%$, because Y's only payment is $25$ years away."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>X: $\\text{RM}1{,}000 \\to 50 / 1.06 + 1{,}050 / 1.06^{2} = \\text{RM}981.67$, about $-1.83\\%$. Y: $1{,}000 / 1.05^{25} = \\text{RM}295.30 \\to 1{,}000 / 1.06^{25} = \\text{RM}233.00$, about $-21.1\\%$. Default-free does not mean free of interest-rate risk (A, C), and the long zero is far more sensitive than the short coupon bond (B).</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A defaulted company has $\\text{RM}150\\text{m}$ left after costs. Secured debt of $\\text{RM}100\\text{m}$ is backed by collateral worth $\\text{RM}60\\text{m}$; senior unsecured debt is $\\text{RM}120\\text{m}$; subordinated debt is $\\text{RM}50\\text{m}$. The secured shortfall ranks equally with senior unsecured debt. Under strict priority, what fraction of its claim does the <strong>senior unsecured</strong> debt recover?</p>", "options": [["A", "$75\\%$"], ["B", "$100\\%$"], ["C", "$56.25\\%$"], ["D", "$41.67\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Collateral pays the secured holders $\\text{RM}60\\text{m}$, leaving $\\text{RM}90\\text{m}$. Their $\\text{RM}40\\text{m}$ shortfall joins the $\\text{RM}120\\text{m}$ senior unsecured claims: $90 / 160 = \\mathbf{56.25\\%}$ each. A ignores the shortfall claim ($90 / 120$); D pays the secured debt in full first ($50 / 120$), which wrongly treats security as a guarantee of full recovery.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A sequential-pay CMO has tranches A (short), B and C. Rates rise sharply and mortgage prepayments slow. What is the most direct effect?</p>", "options": [["A", "Tranche A is retired sooner than expected, and tranche B starts receiving principal earlier than originally scheduled."], ["B", "Principal arrives more slowly: A takes longer to retire, and B and C wait longer for principal (extension risk)."], ["C", "Tranche C now receives principal before tranche A."], ["D", "Interest payments stop for every tranche until rates fall again."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Slower prepayments stretch the principal stream; under the sequential rule A is retired later, delaying B and C. A describes the prepayment case (rates falling), C contradicts the waterfall, and D confuses principal timing with interest.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A Malaysian insurer's USD bond returns $6\\%$ in dollars over a year, while the dollar falls $8\\%$ against the ringgit. What is the insurer's return in ringgit?</p>", "options": [["A", "$-2.00\\%$"], ["B", "$-2.48\\%$"], ["C", "$+14.00\\%$"], ["D", "$+2.48\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$(1.06)(0.92) - 1 = 0.9752 - 1 = \\mathbf{-2.48\\%}$. A simply adds the two returns and misses the cross term $0.06 \\times (-0.08) = -0.48\\%$; C adds the currency fall as if it were a gain; D has the wrong sign.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>All four bonds yield $6\\%$ and have $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par. Which has the largest <strong>percentage</strong> price fall if yields rise to $7\\%$?</p>", "options": [["A", "A five-year $8\\%$ annual-coupon bond"], ["B", "A five-year zero-coupon bond"], ["C", "A $20$-year zero-coupon bond"], ["D", "A $20$-year $10\\%$ annual-coupon bond"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Price changes: A about $-3.99\\%$, B about $-4.59\\%$, C about $-17.12\\%$, D about $-9.66\\%$. Longer maturity and no coupons both push more value into distant cash flows, so the long zero is the most sensitive; a high coupon (D) returns cash earlier and dampens the effect.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>The issuer of a convertible is downgraded, but its share price is unchanged. All else equal, what happens?</p>", "options": [["A", "The bond floor falls, so the convertible's price falls toward conversion value and the conversion premium shrinks."], ["B", "Conversion value falls because the downgrade reduces the conversion ratio."], ["C", "The conversion ratio rises to compensate bondholders automatically."], ["D", "The conversion premium rises, because a weaker issuer makes the option to switch into its shares more valuable to holders."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A downgrade raises the yield used to value the debt component, lowering the bond floor. Conversion value ($N P_s$) is unchanged because neither the ratio nor the share price moved (B, C), so the premium $P_c - CV$ narrows; nothing in the downgrade makes the option itself more valuable (D).</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>The slide's combined taxable-equivalent yield uses the total rate $t_f + t_s(1 - t_f)$ rather than $t_f + t_s$. Under which assumption is that expression exact, and what is the total rate for $t_f = 35\\%$ and $t_s = 8\\%$?</p>", "options": [["A", "Federal tax is charged on the state tax only, so the two rates simply add up; $43.0\\%$, the federal plus the state rate."], ["B", "State tax is charged on federal tax; $37.8\\%$."], ["C", "The municipal coupon is taxed twice; $43.0\\%$."], ["D", "State tax is deductible federally, so the total is $t_s + t_f(1 - t_s)$, the same thing; $40.2\\%$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Tax on $\\text{RM}1$ of taxable interest: state $t_s$, then federal $t_f(1 - t_s)$ on the remainder; total $t_s + t_f - t_f t_s$, the same as $t_f + t_s(1 - t_f)$. With the numbers: $0.35 + 0.08 \\times 0.65 = 0.402 = \\mathbf{40.2\\%}$. A and C simply add the rates ($43\\%$), which double-counts; B is not the slide's model.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A retiree in the $15\\%$ tax bracket compares a tax-exempt municipal bond yielding $3.4\\%$ with a taxable corporate bond yielding $4.2\\%$; rating, maturity, liquidity and call terms appear similar. Which conclusion is best supported?</p>", "options": [["A", "The taxable bond: its after-tax yield of $3.57\\%$ beats $3.40\\%$, provided the risks and tax treatment really are comparable."], ["B", "The municipal bond, because tax-exempt bonds are always the better choice for retirees."], ["C", "The municipal bond, because its taxable-equivalent yield of $4.00\\%$ is above its own $3.40\\%$ yield, so the exemption adds value."], ["D", "Either bond, because the $0.8$-point yield gap is exactly offset by the $15\\%$ tax."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$4.2\\% \\times 0.85 = 3.57\\% &gt; 3.40\\%$; equivalently $TEY = 3.4\\% / 0.85 = 4.00\\% &lt; 4.2\\%$. At a low bracket the exemption is worth little. C compares the TEY with the wrong yield, B ignores the bracket, and D is arithmetic that does not hold.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor says: \"My convertible's bond floor is $\\text{RM}900$, so I cannot lose more than $\\text{RM}200$ on the $\\text{RM}1{,}100$ I paid.\" Which evaluation is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct: the bond floor is a contractual minimum price written into the indenture and enforced by the trustee for every holder."], ["B", "Correct, but only if the bond is callable."], ["C", "Incorrect: the floor is only a model estimate of straight-bond value, and it falls if credit weakens or yields rise."], ["D", "Incorrect, because the conversion value is the guaranteed minimum instead."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The bond floor is the value of the debt component under current yields and credit, not a promise. If the company struggles, the shares fall and the floor falls with its credit at the same time. Conversion value is not guaranteed either (D), and callability is irrelevant (B).</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor must pay a house deposit in exactly three years and cannot afford a shortfall. Which holding best fits the need?</p>", "options": [["A", "A $30$-year AAA government bond, because its top rating removes the price risk of selling it in year $3$ and it yields more."], ["B", "A three-year BB corporate bond, because its higher yield compensates for any default."], ["C", "A mortgage-backed security with an expected three-year life, because asset backing makes it safe."], ["D", "A three-year government zero-coupon bond: one payment on the date needed, very low default risk, nothing to reinvest."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Matching the maturity to the liability removes price and reinvestment uncertainty about the nominal amount. A must be sold at an unknown price, B adds meaningful default risk, and C has uncertain timing (prepayment and extension) as well as credit risk.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A client in the $37\\%$ bracket chooses between a BBB toll-road revenue bond yielding $3.9\\%$ tax-exempt (thinly traded) and an AA taxable corporate bond yielding $5.8\\%$. Which recommendation is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "The revenue bond automatically, because once the taxable-equivalent yield exceeds the corporate yield, rating and liquidity differences no longer matter."], ["B", "Not on yield alone: the $6.19\\%$ TEY beats $5.80\\%$, but the lower rating, toll-revenue dependence and illiquidity must be weighed."], ["C", "The corporate bond, because $5.80\\%$ is higher than $3.90\\%$."], ["D", "The corporate bond, because its after-tax yield of $3.65\\%$ exceeds the revenue bond's $3.90\\%$."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$TEY = 3.9\\% / 0.63 = 6.19\\%$ and the corporate's after-tax yield is $5.8\\% \\times 0.63 = 3.65\\% &lt; 3.90\\%$, so the revenue bond wins on yield (D's claim is false). But the TEY comparison assumes equal risk, and these bonds differ in rating, payment source and liquidity (A). C compares a pre-tax with an after-tax yield.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>Convertible A has a $\\text{RM}150$ premium and $\\text{RM}50$ of extra annual income; convertible B has an $\\text{RM}80$ premium and $\\text{RM}20$ of extra income. An analyst picks A because its payback is shorter. What is the strongest critique?</p>", "options": [["A", "The analyst miscalculated: A's payback is longer than B's."], ["B", "Payback periods should be computed with the full coupon rather than the extra income, which would reverse the ranking."], ["C", "Payback ignores time value, changing dividends and share prices, and the premium at risk: A costs almost twice as much premium."], ["D", "A shorter payback always means a higher total return over the bond's life, so choosing A on this basis alone is correct."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A pays back in $150 / 50 = 3$ years and B in $80 / 20 = 4$ years, so A is not miscalculated. Payback is a crude income measure: it ignores discounting, what happens after payback, and how much is at risk if the shares fall. B is wrong because payback divides by the EXTRA income over the dividends, not the full coupon, and D treats payback as a return measure.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>A bond is downgraded from BBB- to BB+, and funds that may hold only investment-grade debt must sell it. Its yield jumps far more than the change in estimated default losses would justify. Which interpretation is best supported?</p>", "options": [["A", "The jump proves that the agencies' new estimate of default risk is exactly right and that the market agrees with it fully."], ["B", "Forced selling by rating-constrained funds adds a temporary price concession: part of the jump is liquidity, not default risk."], ["C", "Ratings have no effect on prices because they are only opinions."], ["D", "The bond's coupon must have been cut at the time of the downgrade, which is why the yield moved by so much more."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A rating is an opinion, but rules tied to it change who can hold the bond. Selling pressure at the investment-grade boundary can push the price below what credit risk alone implies. A overstates what a price reveals, C ignores rating-linked constraints, and the coupon is unchanged by a downgrade (D).</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You are writing monitoring rules for a client's portfolio of <strong>callable investment-grade corporate bonds</strong> held for income. Which set of reassessment triggers is most complete?</p>", "options": [["A", "Re-check a bond only after it misses a coupon payment."], ["B", "Reassess on a downgrade or wider spread, on rate falls that make calls likely near call dates, and on changes in the client's needs."], ["C", "Reassess only when the stock market falls by more than $20\\%$, since bond and stock risks are driven by the same events."], ["D", "Sell every bond whenever market interest rates rise, because rising rates always signal that issuers are about to default."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The triggers map to the risks that matter here: credit (downgrades, spreads), call and reinvestment risk (falling rates near call dates) and suitability (the client's needs). A reacts too late, C watches the wrong market, and D sells for interest-rate moves that a hold-to-maturity income investor may not need to act on.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An adviser needs one rule for choosing between a tax-exempt bond yielding $3.0\\%$ and an otherwise identical taxable bond yielding $4.5\\%$ for clients in different brackets. Which rule is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Choose the tax-exempt bond for any client who pays income tax at all."], ["B", "Choose whichever bond has the higher pre-tax yield."], ["C", "Choose the tax-exempt bond when the client's marginal rate exceeds $1 - 3.0\\% / 4.5\\% = 33.3\\%$; otherwise choose the taxable bond."], ["D", "Choose the tax-exempt bond when the client's marginal rate exceeds $3.0\\% / 4.5\\% = 66.7\\%$, the ratio of the two yields; otherwise the taxable one."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The bonds tie when $4.5\\%(1 - t) = 3.0\\%$, so $t^{*} = 1 - 3.0 / 4.5 = 33.3\\%$. Above it the exemption is worth more. A and B ignore the bracket; D inverts the ratio.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor will only buy a convertible if its simple payback is at most $4$ years. The bond trades at $\\text{RM}1{,}090$, converts into $25$ shares priced at $\\text{RM}40$, and each share pays $\\text{RM}0.80$ a year. What is the <strong>minimum</strong> annual coupon the bond must pay?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}42.50$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}22.50$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}20.00$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}272.50$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Conversion value $= 25 \\times 40 = \\text{RM}1{,}000$, so the premium is $\\text{RM}90$. Payback $\\le 4$ needs extra income $\\ge 90 / 4 = \\text{RM}22.50$ on top of the dividends of $25 \\times 0.80 = \\text{RM}20$: coupon $\\ge \\mathbf{\\text{RM}42.50}$. B forgets the dividends, C is the dividends alone, and D divides the whole bond price by $4$.</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A family must pay $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ in two years and another $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ in five years. Which bond portfolio best locks in both amounts?</p>", "options": [["A", "One $30$-year government bond, selling part of it in years $2$ and $5$."], ["B", "A high-yield convertible bond whose conversion value is expected to grow."], ["C", "A mortgage pass-through security with an expected average life of three and a half years, which averages out the two dates."], ["D", "Two government zero-coupon bonds maturing in years $2$ and $5$, each with $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ face value."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Each zero delivers a known nominal amount on the liability date with very low default risk and no reinvestment of coupons. A forces sales at unknown prices, B adds credit and equity risk, and C has uncertain timing (prepayment and extension).</p>"}, {"module": 10, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>You want to explain a convertible's market price with a model rather than with the slide's premium measures. Which decomposition is internally consistent?</p>", "options": [["A", "Value = conversion value + par value, because the holder receives the shares and is also repaid the par value at maturity."], ["B", "Value = conversion premium ÷ payback period, because income repays the premium."], ["C", "Value = straight-bond value at the issuer's risky yield + value of the option to exchange the bond for $N$ shares."], ["D", "Value = current yield x conversion ratio, because both measure income per share."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A convertible is a straight bond plus an exchange option; the bond component gives the floor and the option gives the upside, which is why the price curve sits above both the floor and conversion value. A double-counts (the holder gets one or the other), B and D combine measures that have no valuation meaning.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which description matches the slides' definition of a <strong>yield curve</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "A graph of one bond's yield to maturity as it changes over the bond's life, plotted from its issue date to its maturity date."], ["B", "A graph of a bond's price against its yield, showing the price-yield seesaw."], ["C", "A graph of the relationship between term to maturity and yield, for comparable bonds, at a given point in time."], ["D", "A graph of yield spreads between Treasury, municipal and corporate bonds by credit rating."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The yield curve is a cross-sectional snapshot: one date, many maturities, comparable bonds. A describes a time series of one bond, B is the price-yield curve used in pricing, and D describes sector yield spreads.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>According to the slides, which pair of statements about bond duration is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "Higher coupon rates lengthen duration; higher yields to maturity lengthen duration."], ["B", "Longer maturities shorten duration; higher coupon rates lengthen duration."], ["C", "Duration always equals maturity for coupon bonds; it is shorter only for zero-coupon bonds."], ["D", "Higher coupon rates shorten duration; higher yields to maturity shorten duration."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Higher coupons bring more present value forward, and higher yields shrink the weight of distant payments, so both shorten duration; longer maturity lengthens it. C has it backwards: a zero's duration equals its maturity, a coupon bond's is shorter.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which description matches the slides' <strong>tax swap</strong>?</p>", "options": [["A", "Selling a low-yield bond and buying a comparable-quality bond with a higher yield, so that the portfolio's income rises without adding credit risk."], ["B", "Selling a bond that has fallen in value, using the capital loss against other gains, and buying a comparable but not identical bond."], ["C", "Buying equal amounts of bonds maturing in $3$, $5$, $7$ and $10$ years."], ["D", "Matching a portfolio's duration to the investor's horizon so that price and reinvestment effects roughly offset."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>That is the tax swap, with the wash-sale warning that the replacement cannot be the identical issue. A is a yield-pickup swap, C a ladder and D immunization. Actual tax treatment depends on the jurisdiction and current rules.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Two bonds have the same maturity and yield, but Bond A pays a $10\\%$ coupon and Bond B a $4\\%$ coupon. Why does A have the shorter Macaulay duration?</p>", "options": [["A", "A larger share of A's present value arrives through coupons before maturity, so its present-value-weighted average time is earlier."], ["B", "A's price is higher, and Macaulay duration is defined as the bond's price divided by its annual coupon, so a larger coupon gives a smaller ratio."], ["C", "A's larger coupons are reinvested at a higher rate, which shortens its effective maturity and so its present-value-weighted average time."], ["D", "Duration depends only on maturity, so the difference comes from rounding."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Duration weights each payment date by that payment's share of the price. Bigger coupons move weight forward. B invents a definition, C confuses reinvestment with timing (maturity does not change), and D ignores the weights.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Ignoring default, why can a zero-coupon bond held to maturity deliver exactly its quoted YTM, while a coupon bond held to maturity may not?</p>", "options": [["A", "Zero-coupon bonds have no interest-rate risk at any time before maturity, because their price cannot move until the single payment at maturity falls due."], ["B", "Coupon bonds always trade at a premium once coupons start, and amortizing that premium pulls their realized return below the quoted YTM at maturity."], ["C", "The zero has no interim coupons to reinvest; the coupon bond's realized return depends on the rates at which its coupons are reinvested."], ["D", "The YTM of a zero-coupon bond is set by law."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>YTM's compound-return interpretation assumes coupons are reinvested at the YTM. A zero has no coupons, so only default (excluded here) can spoil the result. A is false: a zero's price swings a lot before maturity; it has the longest duration for its maturity.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Under strict <strong>market segmentation</strong>, what does an upward-sloping yield curve indicate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Investors expect short-term rates to rise, so long yields must exceed short yields to make holding either maturity equally attractive."], ["B", "Investors see long-term bonds as riskier because of their greater price volatility, and demand a term premium to hold them to maturity."], ["C", "The issuer of long-term bonds is more likely to default."], ["D", "The supply of short-term funds is high relative to borrowers' needs in that segment, so short yields are lower than long yields."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Segmentation explains each maturity's yield by supply and demand within its own segment. A is the expectations hypothesis and B is liquidity preference; the slides stress that segmentation does not read the slope as either of those.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A fund factsheet reports that the portfolio's duration was cut from $6$ years to $4$ years. If yields then rise by about $1$ percentage point across all maturities, what does the change roughly mean for the fund?</p>", "options": [["A", "The price loss is roughly $4\\%$ instead of roughly $6\\%$ (treating these as modified durations), at the cost of smaller gains if yields fall."], ["B", "The fund can no longer lose money from rate changes, because a $4$-year duration is shorter than the maturities of most of its bonds."], ["C", "The fund's credit risk has fallen by a third, since shorter-duration portfolios hold bonds closer to repayment and so face less default risk."], ["D", "The fund's income will rise by $2$ percentage points."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Duration scales the first-order price response: about $-D \\times \\Delta y$. Shortening duration reduces both the downside from rising yields and the upside from falling yields. It says nothing directly about credit risk (C) or income (D), and it does not remove rate risk (B).</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A bond has $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par, a $7\\%$ annual coupon and $3$ years to maturity. The required yield is $9\\%$. What is its price?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000.00$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}949.37$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}177.19$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}1{,}052.87$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\dfrac{70}{1.09} + \\dfrac{70}{1.09^2} + \\dfrac{1{,}070}{1.09^3} = 64.22 + 58.92 + 826.24 \\approx \\mathbf{\\text{RM}949.37}$. A discounts at the coupon rate; C forgets the par; D would be a premium price, impossible when the coupon is below the yield.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A $5$-year bond with $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par pays a $9\\%$ coupon semiannually. The market requires $8\\%$, quoted as a nominal annual yield compounded semiannually. What is its price?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}1{,}040.55$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}039.93$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}765.15$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000.00$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Coupon $\\text{RM}45$, rate $4\\%$, $10$ periods: $45\\left[\\dfrac{1 - 1.04^{-10}}{0.04}\\right] + \\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.04^{10}} = 364.99 + 675.56 \\approx \\mathbf{\\text{RM}1{,}040.55}$. B is the annual-pay answer (close, but the wrong convention); C discounts at $8\\%$ per half-year; D would need the coupon to equal the yield.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A $3$-year bond pays a $10\\%$ annual coupon on $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par and is priced at par ($y = 10\\%$). Its Macaulay duration is about $2.7355$ years. What is the duration estimate of the percentage price change if the yield rises by $50$ basis points?</p>", "options": [["A", "$-1.37\\%$"], ["B", "$-13.68\\%$"], ["C", "$-1.24\\%$"], ["D", "$+1.24\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$D_{mod} = \\dfrac{2.7355}{1.10} = 2.4869$, and $\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -2.4869 \\times 0.005 \\approx \\mathbf{-1.24\\%}$. A uses Macaulay instead of modified duration; B treats $50$ bp as $0.05$; D has the wrong sign. (Full repricing gives about $-1.23\\%$: the small gap is convexity.)</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A credit trader sees a corporate bond's yield rise by $35$ basis points after a downgrade. Its modified duration is $6.2$. What is the approximate price change?</p>", "options": [["A", "$-21.7\\%$"], ["B", "$+2.17\\%$"], ["C", "$-0.22\\%$"], ["D", "$-2.17\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\Delta y = 0.0035$, so $\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -6.2 \\times 0.0035 \\approx \\mathbf{-2.17\\%}$. A treats $35$ bp as $0.035$; C as $0.00035$; B has the wrong sign: yields up, price down.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A bond has modified duration $7.0$ and convexity $60$. Using $\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -D_{mod}\\Delta y + \\tfrac{1}{2} \\times \\text{convexity} \\times (\\Delta y)^2$, estimate the price change for a $1$ percentage-point rise in yield.</p>", "options": [["A", "$-7.30\\%$"], ["B", "$-7.00\\%$"], ["C", "$-6.70\\%$"], ["D", "$+6.70\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$-7.0 \\times 0.01 + 0.5 \\times 60 \\times 0.01^2 = -0.070 + 0.003 = \\mathbf{-6.70\\%}$. The convexity term is positive whichever way yields move, so it softens losses and adds to gains; B is the duration-only estimate the chapter warns is approximate, and A subtracts the convexity term.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Both yield $8\\%$: Bond Z is a $10$-year zero-coupon bond; Bond K is a $10$-year $8\\%$ annual-coupon bond (duration about $7.25$ years). Yields rise to $9\\%$. Which statement is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Both fall by the same percentage because they have the same maturity and start from the same $8\\%$ yield to maturity."], ["B", "K falls more because its coupons must now be reinvested, and each of its ten coupons is marked down at the higher $9\\%$ yield."], ["C", "Neither price changes, because both will repay par at maturity."], ["D", "Z falls more (about $8.8\\%$ against $6.4\\%$) because all its value sits at year $10$, giving it the longer duration."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Price sensitivity follows duration, not maturity. Z's duration is $10$ years against K's $7.25$, so Z drops about $1 - (1.08 / 1.09)^{10} \\approx 8.8\\%$ while K drops about $6.4\\%$. Reinvestment (B) helps K over time; it does not make its price fall more. C confuses the eventual repayment with today's price.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A bond with $\\text{RM}1{,}000$ par and $6$ years to maturity pays a $9\\%$ annual coupon and sells for $\\text{RM}1{,}080$. Which set of yields is consistent?</p>", "options": [["A", "Coupon rate $9\\%$, current yield $9.72\\%$, YTM $10.4\\%$"], ["B", "Coupon rate $9\\%$, current yield $8.33\\%$, YTM about $7.31\\%$"], ["C", "Coupon rate $8.33\\%$, current yield $9\\%$, YTM $9\\%$"], ["D", "Coupon rate $9\\%$, current yield $8.33\\%$, YTM about $9.6\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Current yield $= \\dfrac{90}{1{,}080} \\approx 8.33\\%$. A premium bond loses $\\text{RM}80$ on the way to par, so YTM is below the current yield: solving the price equation gives about $7.31\\%$. The premium ordering is coupon rate $&gt;$ CY $&gt;$ YTM. A and D put YTM on the wrong side; C swaps the definitions of coupon rate and current yield.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A $10$-year bond paying $\\text{RM}80$ a year trades at $\\text{RM}1{,}100$ (YTM about $6.6\\%$). It is callable in $3$ years at $\\text{RM}1{,}040$ (YTC about $5.55\\%$). Which analysis is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "The lower YTC is the more cautious planning figure: with the price above the call price and rates low, the issuer has an incentive to call, ending the high coupons early."], ["B", "YTM is always the right planning figure because a call is never certain, and the investor can simply hold the bond to its final maturity."], ["C", "YTC must exceed YTM because the call price is above par, so an early call hands the investor a premium on top of the coupons already received and returns the money sooner."], ["D", "The two yields cannot differ, because they share the same price."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A buyer paying above the call price loses the premium early if the bond is called, so the yield to the call scenario is lower. Neither yield is guaranteed, but the cautious investor plans on the less favourable plausible scenario. C fails here: the call price ($\\text{RM}1{,}040$) is below the purchase price, so an early call lowers the return.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>A semiannual-pay bond has a Macaulay duration of $4.5$ years and a YTM of $6\\%$, quoted as a nominal annual rate compounded semiannually. What is its modified duration?</p>", "options": [["A", "$4.245$"], ["B", "$4.369$"], ["C", "$4.500$"], ["D", "$4.770$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With $m = 2$, $D_{mod} = \\dfrac{D_{Mac}}{1 + y/m} = \\dfrac{4.5}{1.03} \\approx \\mathbf{4.369}$. A divides by $1.06$, the annual-pay formula applied to a semiannual bond; D multiplies instead of dividing.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>Yields fall sharply. A noncallable bond and an otherwise identical callable bond (callable now at $\\text{RM}1{,}050$) both start at $\\text{RM}1{,}000$. Which outcome is most likely?</p>", "options": [["A", "The noncallable bond rises substantially; the callable bond's price stalls near $\\text{RM}1{,}050$, because investors expect the issuer to call and refinance at the lower rates."], ["B", "Both rise by the same amount, since they have the same coupon, maturity and issuer, and the call feature affects the price only once the issuer actually exercises it and redeems the bonds."], ["C", "The callable bond rises more, because its call premium above par adds value for the holder that the otherwise identical noncallable bond does not offer."], ["D", "Both fall, because lower yields mean lower coupons."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The call option belongs to the issuer, who exercises it when rates fall. That caps the callable bond's price near the call price: the reason the slides recommend long <strong>noncallable</strong> bonds when rates are expected to fall, and why callable bonds offer higher yields.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>A perpetual bond pays a level coupon forever and is priced at a $5\\%$ yield. Using $D_{Mac} = \\dfrac{1+y}{y}$ for a level perpetuity, what is its Macaulay duration, and what does the answer show?</p>", "options": [["A", "Infinite, because it never matures and duration averages the times of an endless stream of equal payments."], ["B", "$5$ years, the yield expressed in years."], ["C", "$21$ years: although the maturity is infinite, distant payments carry little present value, so the weighted average time is finite."], ["D", "$20$ years, which equals $\\dfrac{1}{y}$, the perpetuity's price-to-coupon ratio and so the average waiting time for its payments."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\dfrac{1.05}{0.05} = \\mathbf{21}$ years. Duration and maturity diverge completely here: present-value weighting makes far-off payments almost weightless. D is the price-to-coupon ratio, close but not the duration.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>The $1$-year rate is $4\\%$ and the $2$-year rate is $5\\%$ (annual compounding). Under the pure expectations hypothesis, what one-year rate do investors expect for year $2$?</p>", "options": [["A", "$5.00\\%$"], ["B", "$6.01\\%$"], ["C", "$4.50\\%$"], ["D", "$9.00\\%$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$(1.05)^2 = (1.04)(1 + f)$, so $f = \\dfrac{1.1025}{1.04} - 1 \\approx \\mathbf{6.01\\%}$. The two-year yield is roughly the average of today's rate and the expected rate, so the expected rate must be well above $5\\%$. Under liquidity preference part of this forward rate is a term premium, so it overstates the expected rate.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor says: \"The yield curve is inverted, so long-term bonds will certainly outperform short-term bonds over the next year.\" Which evaluation is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Correct, because inverted curves always predict falling rates, and falling rates raise long-bond prices by more than short-bond prices, since long bonds have the longer durations and so gain the most."], ["B", "Correct only for corporate bonds, whose credit spreads narrow when the curve inverts and so add to the price gain from falling rates."], ["C", "Overstated: an inverted curve is consistent with expected rate falls, but expectations can be wrong and premiums or segment supply and demand also shape it; if yields rise, long bonds lose more."], ["D", "Incorrect, because an inverted curve proves that rates will rise back toward normal levels, so long bonds will lose more as their yields climb over the year."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The curve is evidence, not a certain forecast. Long bonds gain more if yields fall but, having longer durations, lose more if yields rise. D replaces one certainty claim with another.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor must pay a fixed sum in exactly $3$ years. Bond X has a duration of $3.0$ years and Bond Y a duration of $6.5$ years; both are high quality with the same yield. Which choice and reason are best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Y, because its longer duration locks in a higher realized return whatever happens to rates, since its price gains always outweigh any shortfall in the reinvested coupons at year $3$."], ["B", "X, because matching duration to the horizon lets price and reinvestment effects roughly offset for small parallel rate changes, provided it is rebalanced as duration drifts."], ["C", "Y, because reinvestment risk disappears when duration is longer than the horizon, leaving only a price effect that averages out over the $3$ years."], ["D", "Either, because duration does not affect horizon wealth."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>With Y the investor must sell a long-duration bond at year $3$, so rising yields would leave a shortfall (price risk dominates). X is the immunizing choice, with the usual conditions: small parallel shifts, no default, periodic rebalancing.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A holder of an AA-rated, noncallable $5$-year bond yielding $5.0\\%$ is offered a swap into an A-rated $5$-year bond yielding $5.6\\%$ that is callable in $2$ years. Which evaluation is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "Accept: $60$ bp of extra yield is free return, since both bonds mature in $5$ years and an A rating is still investment grade, so the added default and call risks are negligible over such a short horizon."], ["B", "Reject: swaps always generate tax losses and transaction costs that outweigh any yield pickup, whatever the two bonds' credit quality or call terms."], ["C", "Accept: a callable bond is safer because the issuer may repay early, returning the investor's principal sooner and cutting exposure to later rate rises."], ["D", "Investigate first: the pickup may only compensate for lower credit quality and the call option (which caps gains if rates fall), so compare credit, call terms, liquidity, taxes and costs."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A yield-pickup swap is worth doing only if the extra yield more than pays for the extra risks and costs. \"Comparable credit quality\" is part of the slide's definition, and here quality and call terms differ. C misreads the call: it benefits the issuer, not the holder.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>Two portfolios both have a duration of $7$ years: a \"bullet\" of $7$-year bonds, and a \"barbell\" of $1$-year and $20$-year bonds. Which evaluation is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "They are identical in every rate scenario because their durations match, and duration fully captures a portfolio's exposure to any shift in the yield curve."], ["B", "The bullet is riskier in every scenario because all its bonds sit at one maturity, whereas the barbell's short and long ends offset each other whenever the curve twists, so its value barely changes."], ["C", "The barbell has no reinvestment risk because part of it matures in one year and can be rolled over at whatever rates then prevail, locking in the target."], ["D", "For a small parallel shift they move about the same, but they differ under non-parallel shifts (a steepening hurts the barbell's long end more) and in convexity, so equal duration is not equal risk."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Duration is a first-order, parallel-shift measure. Portfolios with the same duration can respond differently to twists in the curve and to large moves. This is the chapter's warning that duration matching is a conditional approximation.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>An analyst values every bond with a single YTM. A colleague objects that each cash flow should be discounted at the spot (zero-coupon) rate for its own date. Which evaluation is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The colleague is right in principle: a YTM is a single average rate that depends on the cash flows' timing, so two bonds of the same maturity but different coupons can have different YTMs off the same spot curve."], ["B", "The analyst is right: spot rates and YTM are always identical, because a coupon bond's YTM is simply the spot rate for its maturity date, so applying it to every cash flow gives exactly the same price as discounting each flow separately."], ["C", "The colleague is wrong, because spot rates exist only for Treasury bills, so longer coupon bonds can be valued only with a single yield to maturity."], ["D", "It does not matter, because YTM ignores the time value of money, so any discounting method gives the same price for a given bond."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The notes flag the difference between a curve of coupon-bond yields and a zero-coupon spot curve. With a sloped spot curve, a high-coupon bond (more weight early) gets a YTM closer to the short spot rates. YTM is a convenient summary, not the rate for each date.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>In this chapter's horizon widget the bond's duration equals the horizon, and the realized return is lowest at the original yield, rising for any immediate parallel shift up or down. Evaluate: \"So duration matching guarantees at least the target return.\"</p>", "options": [["A", "True in all circumstances, since the curve was drawn for every possible yield and the realized return never falls below its value at the original yield, whatever happens to rates afterwards or however often they change."], ["B", "False in all circumstances: duration matching never helps, because price and reinvestment effects move in the same direction when yields change."], ["C", "True only if the investor never receives coupons, because coupons reinvested after a fall in yields would leave the realized return below the target."], ["D", "True only under the model's assumptions (one immediate parallel shift of a flat curve, no default, no calls); non-parallel shifts, repeated unrebalanced rate changes, default or calls can cause a shortfall."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The convexity result is real but conditional. The slides' \"regardless of interest rates\" claim is an idealization; real curves twist, rates move many times and duration drifts, so immunization needs rebalancing and still carries residual risk.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>Which statement about the two duration measures is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "Macaulay and modified duration are identical for every compounding convention, because both are present-value-weighted averages of the times to each of the bond's cash flows, so the compounding convention changes neither of the two measures."], ["B", "Modified duration measures time in years and Macaulay duration measures price sensitivity, which is why Macaulay duration, not modified duration, is the one multiplied by the yield change in the first-order price-change approximation."], ["C", "Modified duration equals $-\\dfrac{1}{P}\\dfrac{dP}{dy}$ under the yield's own compounding convention; dividing Macaulay duration by $1 + y/m$ turns a time measure into this sensitivity, and under continuous compounding they coincide."], ["D", "Both measures stay constant over a bond's life, because they depend only on the coupon rate and the yield, not on the time remaining to maturity."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Differentiating the price with respect to the yield gives $-\\dfrac{D_{Mac}}{1 + y/m}$ per unit of price; as $m$ grows the divisor tends to $1$. B swaps the roles; D ignores that duration drifts with time and yields, which is why immunized portfolios need rebalancing.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A fund must pay $\\text{RM}500{,}000$ in $4$ years. The flat yield is $6\\%$. It can buy Bond S (duration $2$ years) and Bond L (duration $6$ years). Which plan immunizes the liability, to a first approximation?</p>", "options": [["A", "Invest $\\text{RM}396{,}047$ (the liability's present value), half in S and half in L, so the portfolio duration is $4$ years; rebalance as time passes and yields change."], ["B", "Invest $\\text{RM}500{,}000$ (the liability's face value), all in L, because longer bonds earn more and the extra return covers any change in rates."], ["C", "Invest $\\text{RM}396{,}047$, all in S, because short bonds have no interest-rate risk, and roll the proceeds into new bonds as each one matures until the liability is due."], ["D", "Invest $\\text{RM}396{,}047$, $25\\%$ in S and $75\\%$ in L, so the portfolio duration sits comfortably above the $4$-year horizon; rebalance as time passes and yields change."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>PV of the liability $= \\dfrac{500{,}000}{1.06^4} \\approx \\text{RM}396{,}047$. The weight on S solves $2w + 6(1 - w) = 4$, so $w = 0.5$. Both the value and the duration then match the liability. D gives $0.25(2) + 0.75(6) = 5$ years, too long; B and C ignore matching.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A retiree wants cash every year for the next $5$ years and wants to avoid reinvesting all her money at one possibly bad moment. Which design best fits, using the chapter's strategies?</p>", "options": [["A", "One $20$-year callable bond with the highest available yield, selling part of the position each year to raise the cash she needs and keeping the rest invested to earn the higher coupon."], ["B", "Sell all bonds and hold cash until rates rise, then buy a single $5$-year bond at the higher yield to fund the remaining years."], ["C", "A ladder: equal amounts of high-quality bonds maturing in each of years $1$ to $5$, each maturing rung spent or reinvested at the long end, with credit and liquidity reviewed yearly."], ["D", "Lengthen duration as far as possible because rates might fall, locking in today's yields for longer and raising the bonds' prices if rates do fall."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The ladder matches the yearly cash need, spreads reinvestment dates and keeps liquidity. A concentrates call, credit and price risk; B gives up income and depends on a forecast; D is an active rate bet unrelated to her objective.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A manager immunized a $6$-year liability last year with a portfolio of duration $6$. Which monitoring rule best keeps the immunization working?</p>", "options": [["A", "Do nothing: once duration is matched, it stays matched until the liability is due, because the portfolio's duration and the remaining horizon both shrink by exactly one year each year, keeping the two in step without any trading."], ["B", "Periodically re-match the portfolio's present value and duration to the remaining horizon, because duration does not fall one-for-one with time and also moves when yields change, allowing for transaction costs."], ["C", "Double the portfolio's duration whenever rates rise, so the higher reinvestment income from the new rates arrives sooner and offsets the price loss."], ["D", "Switch to the highest-yielding bonds each year to build a cushion of extra return that covers any shortfall as the duration drifts off the horizon."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>After a year the horizon is $5$ years but the portfolio's duration has typically fallen by less than a year, and yield changes move it too, so the match drifts. Rebalancing (net of costs) restores it. C and D are rate or yield bets that break the match.</p>"}, {"module": 11, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>A researcher wants to test whether the slope of the yield curve really forecasts future short rates, as the pure expectations hypothesis implies. Which design is most informative?</p>", "options": [["A", "Compare the term spread observed at each date with the short-rate changes that actually followed, over many dates and rate cycles, and check whether the slope predicts them one-for-one or a time-varying term premium remains."], ["B", "Look at today's yield curve, report its slope, and read an upward slope as proof that short rates will rise by the amount the term spread implies."], ["C", "Survey investors once and ask whether they expect rates to rise, then compare the share expecting a rise with the current slope of the yield curve."], ["D", "Compare corporate and government bond yields on a single day across all maturities, and attribute any difference between the two curves' slopes to expected changes in future short rates rather than to credit or liquidity premiums."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A single curve (B), one survey (C) or one day's credit spread (D) cannot separate expectations from term premiums. Relating past slopes to the rates that actually followed, across many periods, is the standard way to test whether the curve's shape is a forecast or contains a premium, exactly the distinction this chapter asks you to keep.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In the organization of a mutual fund, which party <strong>physically safeguards</strong> the fund's securities?</p>", "options": [["A", "The transfer agent"], ["B", "The distributor"], ["C", "The custodian"], ["D", "The investment adviser"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The custodian holds and safeguards the assets, keeping them separate from the manager. The transfer agent keeps ownership records and processes purchases and redemptions (A), the distributor sells the fund's shares (B), and the adviser chooses the investments (D).</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>In a US mutual fund fee table, which item is a <strong>shareholder fee paid directly by the investor</strong> rather than an annual fund operating expense?</p>", "options": [["A", "The management fee"], ["B", "The distribution and service (12b-1) fee"], ["C", "Other expenses such as administration and custody"], ["D", "The maximum sales charge (load) on purchases"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Table 12.1 separates shareholder fees (sales loads, deferred loads, exchange and account fees), which come out of the investor's own money, from annual operating expenses (management, 12b-1 and other expenses), which are paid from fund assets and show up in NAV. A, B and C are all operating expenses.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Remember", "bloom_rank": 1, "ext": null, "question": "<p>According to the chapter, a mutual fund investor's return can come from which three sources?</p>", "options": [["A", "Income distributions, capital-gain distributions, and the change in the share's NAV or price"], ["B", "Management-fee rebates paid to long-term holders, sales-load refunds on redemption, and the change in the fund's NAV"], ["C", "Dividends received by the fund, the expense ratio, and the portfolio turnover rate"], ["D", "Interest on uninvested cash, the 12b-1 fee, and the bid-ask spread"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Slide 12-41 lists dividend income, capital-gains distributions and the change in price or NAV (which includes unrealized paper gains). Fees, turnover and spreads (B, C, D) reduce returns; they are not sources of return.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An open-end fund issues new shares every day. Why does this <strong>not</strong> dilute the existing shareholders?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because the fund guarantees that NAV will not fall after new money arrives."], ["B", "Because new shares are issued at NAV, so net assets and the share count rise in the same proportion."], ["C", "Because each new investor's sales load is paid into the fund and added to net assets, raising NAV for existing holders enough to offset the new shares."], ["D", "Because the number of shares in an open-end fund is fixed after the initial offering."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>If net assets are $\\text{RM}100$ million on $10$ million shares ($NAV = \\text{RM}10$) and $\\text{RM}10$ million arrives for $1$ million new shares, NAV stays $\\dfrac{110}{11} = \\text{RM}10$. No guarantee is involved (A); a load goes to the distributor, not into NAV (C); a fixed share count describes a closed-end fund (D).</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Both an ETF and a closed-end fund trade on an exchange. Why does the ETF's price usually stay much closer to NAV?</p>", "options": [["A", "ETFs are always index funds, and index funds cannot trade away from NAV because their holdings are published every day, so any gap would be spotted by ordinary investors and traded away within minutes."], ["B", "Closed-end funds publish NAV only once a year, so investors cannot compare the share price with the value of the underlying portfolio."], ["C", "Authorized participants can create or redeem ETF shares in exchange for the basket, which rewards closing a gap; a closed-end fund's share count is essentially fixed, so it has no such channel."], ["D", "Exchange rules forbid an ETF from trading more than $1\\%$ away from its NAV, and trading is halted whenever that band is breached until the price comes back."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The creation and redemption channel lets an AP profit from a premium (create and sell) or a discount (buy and redeem), which pushes the price toward NAV. A closed-end fund has no comparable mechanism, so its discount can persist. Not every ETF is an index fund (A), closed-end funds report NAV regularly (B), and no rule fixes a $1\\%$ band (D).</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": null, "question": "<p>No bond in a diversified bond fund has defaulted, yet the fund's NAV has fallen $6\\%$ this year. What is the most likely explanation?</p>", "options": [["A", "The fund must have charged a back-end load to all shareholders."], ["B", "A bond fund's NAV can only fall when a holding defaults or is downgraded, so a $6\\%$ fall with no defaults must be an error in the NAV calculation."], ["C", "The expense ratio is deducted once a year as a $6\\%$ lump sum."], ["D", "Market yields rose, lowering the market value of its bonds; unlike a single bond, the fund has no maturity date at which par is repaid."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>NAV marks the bonds at market value, and bond prices fall when yields rise, more so for long durations. The fund keeps replacing its bonds, so there is no maturity date that \"pulls\" it back to par. A back-end load affects only redeeming investors (A); default is one risk among several (B); expense ratios are far smaller than $6\\%$ and accrue gradually (C).</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X2", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Understand", "bloom_rank": 2, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>Open-end funds fill orders at the <strong>next</strong> NAV calculated after the order is received (\"forward pricing\"). Why does the industry insist on this?</p>", "options": [["A", "It guarantees that every order is filled at a higher price than the previous day's NAV, protecting the fund's remaining holders from investors who sell into a falling market."], ["B", "It stops investors from trading at a stale, already-known NAV after news has moved markets, which would transfer value from long-term shareholders to fast traders."], ["C", "It allows the fund to avoid calculating NAV on most days, since orders can be pooled and filled together at the next weekly valuation."], ["D", "It lets the investment adviser choose the price at which each investor trades, so large orders can be filled without moving the fund's published NAV for everyone else."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>If orders could be filled at an earlier NAV after prices had moved (for example after a foreign market closed), late traders could buy at yesterday's lower value and dilute existing holders. Forward pricing removes that option. It guarantees no price (A), still requires a daily NAV (C), and gives no discretion to the adviser (D).</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A fund holds securities worth $\\text{RM}180$ million and cash of $\\text{RM}12$ million. It has accrued expenses and other payables of $\\text{RM}7$ million and borrowings of $\\text{RM}5$ million. There are $16$ million shares outstanding. What is NAV per share?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}11.25$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}12.00$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}11.56$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}10.50$"]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$NAV = \\dfrac{180 + 12 - 7 - 5}{16} = \\dfrac{180}{16} = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}11.25}$. B ignores all liabilities; C subtracts only the payables and forgets the borrowings; D treats cash as a liability instead of an asset.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You pay $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ into a fund whose front-end load is $4\\%$ <strong>of the offering price</strong>. NAV is $\\text{RM}12.50$. How many shares do you receive?</p>", "options": [["A", "$1{,}600.00$"], ["B", "$1{,}536.00$"], ["C", "$1{,}538.46$"], ["D", "$1{,}476.92$"]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Amount invested $= 20{,}000 \\times (1 - 0.04) = \\text{RM}19{,}200$; shares $= \\dfrac{19{,}200}{12.50} = \\mathbf{1{,}536}$. Equivalently, offer price $= \\dfrac{12.50}{0.96} = \\text{RM}13.021$ and $\\dfrac{20{,}000}{13.021} = 1{,}536$. A ignores the load; C treats the load as $4\\%$ of NAV ($\\dfrac{20{,}000}{12.50 \\times 1.04}$), the wrong base; D divides the invested amount by $\\text{RM}13$, double counting the load.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A closed-end fund has NAV $\\text{RM}15.00$ and you buy at a $12\\%$ discount. A year later NAV is $\\text{RM}16.20$, the discount has narrowed to $5\\%$, and you received a $\\text{RM}0.40$ cash distribution. What is <strong>your</strong> holding-period return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$10.67\\%$"], ["B", "$16.59\\%$"], ["C", "$17.27\\%$"], ["D", "$19.62\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Buy price $= 15 \\times 0.88 = \\text{RM}13.20$; sell price $= 16.20 \\times 0.95 = \\text{RM}15.39$. $HPR = \\dfrac{15.39 - 13.20 + 0.40}{13.20} = \\dfrac{2.59}{13.20} = \\mathbf{19.62\\%}$. A is the NAV return $\\dfrac{1.20 + 0.40}{15}$, which ignores the narrowing discount you benefited from; B leaves out the distribution; C divides by NAV instead of the price you paid.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X1", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "Industry", "question": "<p>A fund operations team accrues the expense ratio daily. Net assets are $\\text{RM}500$ million and the expense ratio is $0.73\\%$ a year, accrued over $365$ days. What is the daily expense accrual (holding net assets constant)?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}3{,}650{,}000$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}1{,}000$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}10{,}000$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}100{,}000$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$\\dfrac{500{,}000{,}000 \\times 0.0073}{365} = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}10{,}000}$ a day. A is the whole year's expense; B and D are off by a factor of ten. Daily accrual is why expenses appear gradually as a slightly lower NAV rather than as a bill.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X3", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Apply", "bloom_rank": 3, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>An ETF trades at $\\text{RM}25.30$ while the value of its basket (indicative NAV) is $\\text{RM}25.00$. A creation unit is $50{,}000$ shares. Creating a unit costs a fixed fee of $\\text{RM}3{,}000$ plus trading costs of $0.4\\%$ of the basket's value. Ignoring price moves during the trade, what is the authorized participant's profit from creating one unit and selling it?</p>", "options": [["A", "$\\text{RM}15{,}000$"], ["B", "$\\text{RM}12{,}000$"], ["C", "$\\text{RM}10{,}000$"], ["D", "$\\text{RM}7{,}000$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Gross gap $= 50{,}000 \\times (25.30 - 25.00) = \\text{RM}15{,}000$. Basket value $= 50{,}000 \\times 25 = \\text{RM}1{,}250{,}000$, so trading costs $= 0.004 \\times 1{,}250{,}000 = \\text{RM}5{,}000$. Profit $= 15{,}000 - 3{,}000 - 5{,}000 = \\mathbf{\\text{RM}7{,}000}$. A ignores costs; B subtracts only the fee; C only the trading costs. If costs exceeded the gap, the premium could persist: arbitrage is bounded by costs.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Investors A and B each put $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ into the same fund, which earns $8\\%$ a year net of expenses for five years. A pays a $3\\%$ front-end load on the amount paid. B pays no front-end load but a $3\\%$ back-end load on the redemption value. Who ends with more?</p>", "options": [["A", "Investor A, because paying the load early means it is over and done with, and the remaining balance then compounds free of any further charge."], ["B", "Investor B, because deferring a cost always increases the ending value, since the full $\\text{RM}10{,}000$ earns $8\\%$ a year for all five years."], ["C", "They end with the same amount: both multiply the same sum by $0.97$ and by $1.08^{5}$, and the order of multiplication does not matter."], ["D", "Investor A, because the back-end load is charged on a larger, grown balance, so B gives up more ringgit at redemption than A paid at the start."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>$A = 10{,}000(0.97)(1.08)^{5} = \\text{RM}14{,}252.48$ and $B = 10{,}000(1.08)^{5}(0.97) = \\text{RM}14{,}252.48$. D is the tempting trap: B's load is larger in ringgit, but A's load also removed money that would have grown. The equality breaks if the back-end load is charged on a different base (such as the lower of cost or value) or shrinks with the holding period, which is why the stated terms matter.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A fund reports a one-year return of $6.5\\%$ after operating expenses (expense ratio $0.9\\%$); its matched benchmark returned $7.0\\%$. An investor bought at the start of the year paying a $2\\%$ front-end load on the amount paid. Which statement is correct?</p>", "options": [["A", "The fund trailed its benchmark by $1.4$ points, because the expense ratio must be subtracted from the reported return first."], ["B", "The investor earned about $4.37\\%$, and the fund trailed its benchmark by $0.5$ points."], ["C", "The investor earned $4.5\\%$, because the load is simply subtracted from the fund's return."], ["D", "The fund beat its benchmark by $0.4$ points, because expenses should be added back before comparing."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Investor: $0.98 \\times 1.065 - 1 = \\mathbf{4.37\\%}$; the load reduces the amount that earns $6.5\\%$, so it is not a simple subtraction (C). Fund: $6.5\\% - 7.0\\% = -0.5$ points; the $0.9\\%$ is already inside the $6.5\\%$, so subtracting it again (A) double counts. Adding expenses back (D) measures the portfolio before costs, which investors cannot earn.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor already owns $30$ technology shares and adds a technology-sector ETF holding $120$ companies. What does this do to the portfolio's risk?</p>", "options": [["A", "It mainly increases concentration in one sector: the ETF spreads company-specific risk within technology but adds to a sector exposure the investor already has."], ["B", "It removes market risk, because the ETF holds four times as many companies."], ["C", "It has no effect on risk, because ETFs are passive."], ["D", "It diversifies the portfolio across sectors and styles, because the ETF owns $120$ companies, most of which the investor does not already hold, so the overlap is small."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The number of holdings is not the number of risk sources. A sector shock hits the investor's own technology shares and the ETF together. Market risk cannot be diversified away (B), passive management says nothing about risk (C), and new names in the same sector do not add new sectors (D).</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X4", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "CFA", "question": "<p>An index fund with an expense ratio of $0.20\\%$ returned $9.70\\%$ in a year when its index returned $10.00\\%$. Which interpretation is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The $-0.30$-point tracking difference is larger than the expense ratio; the extra $0.10$ point likely comes from transaction costs, cash drag or sampling."], ["B", "The fund's tracking error is $0.30\\%$, which measures how volatile its deviations are."], ["C", "The fund must be actively managed, because a true index fund matches its index exactly."], ["D", "The fund underperformed by $0.50$ points, because the $0.20\\%$ expense ratio has to be subtracted from the reported $9.70\\%$ before it is compared with the index's $10.00\\%$."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Tracking difference is the return gap ($9.70\\% - 10.00\\% = -0.30$ points). Expenses explain $0.20$; the rest comes from frictions such as trading costs, uninvested cash and sampling. Tracking error (B) is the standard deviation of such gaps over time, not a single gap. Index funds never track perfectly (C), and the reported return is already after expenses (D).</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X5", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A client holds $60\\%$ of her portfolio in Fund P, which has $45\\%$ of its assets in technology, and $40\\%$ in Fund Q, a broad-market ETF with $28\\%$ in technology. What is her overall technology weight?</p>", "options": [["A", "$36.5\\%$"], ["B", "$28\\%$"], ["C", "$38.2\\%$"], ["D", "$73\\%$"]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Look through both funds and weight by the client's allocation: $0.60 \\times 45\\% + 0.40 \\times 28\\% = 27\\% + 11.2\\% = \\mathbf{38.2\\%}$. A is the simple average of the two funds' weights, ignoring the client's split; B looks only at the broad fund; D adds the two percentages. A \"diversified\" second fund can still leave a large sector concentration.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X7", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>A taxable investor buys a fund at NAV $\\text{RM}15.00$ the day before a $\\text{RM}1.20$ capital-gain distribution. NAV falls to $\\text{RM}13.80$ when the distribution is paid in cash, and the investor pays tax of $20\\%$ on it. With no other price movement, what is the investor's immediate after-tax return?</p>", "options": [["A", "$0\\%$"], ["B", "$+8.0\\%$"], ["C", "$-8.0\\%$"], ["D", "$-1.6\\%$"]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>After the distribution the investor holds a share worth $\\text{RM}13.80$ plus $\\text{RM}1.20$ cash, less tax of $0.20 \\times 1.20 = \\text{RM}0.24$: $\\dfrac{13.80 + 1.20 - 0.24 - 15}{15} = \\mathbf{-1.6\\%}$. Before tax the return is $0\\%$ (A), because a distribution only moves value out of NAV; the tax makes \"buying the distribution\" costly. B counts the distribution as new wealth; C counts only the NAV drop.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X9", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Analyze", "bloom_rank": 4, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Why can closed-end fund discounts persist for years when ETF premiums and discounts are usually small?</p>", "options": [["A", "Closed-end funds may report NAV only once a year, so traders cannot see the discount until the annual report arrives, by which time it has already changed."], ["B", "Closed-end fund investors are legally barred from short selling, so arbitrageurs cannot take the other side when the shares trade away from NAV, and gaps are left to close on their own."], ["C", "No creation or redemption channel exists; an arbitrageur who buys the fund and shorts its holdings bears costs and the risk that the discount widens, with no forced convergence date."], ["D", "Closed-end funds hold only illiquid assets, so NAV cannot be calculated reliably and the share price has no fixed, published value toward which it could converge, even over many years."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Limits to arbitrage explain persistence: the trade is costly, may require holding a widening position for a long time (noise-trader risk), and convergence is forced only if the fund is open-ended or liquidated. Sentiment-based explanations of the closed-end fund puzzle rest on exactly this risk. A, B and D are false: NAVs are reported frequently (often daily or weekly), shorting is generally possible, and many closed-end funds hold liquid listed securities.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D13", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A closed-end fund trades at a $15\\%$ discount to NAV, uses leverage and has an expense ratio of $1.8\\%$. An open-end fund in the same asset class charges $0.6\\%$. A colleague says: \"The closed-end fund is clearly better: every ringgit buys $\\text{RM}1.18$ of assets.\" Which evaluation is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "Agree: a discount is risk-free extra value that must eventually be paid out, because the fund's assets will be distributed at NAV when it winds up, so the extra $\\text{RM}0.18$ per ringgit is locked in from the day of purchase."], ["B", "Disagree: closed-end funds can never be bought below NAV, because arbitrageurs would buy the shares and redeem them, so the quoted discount must be an error."], ["C", "Agree, because leverage always raises the long-run return of a fund, and together with the discount it more than covers the extra $1.2$ points a year of expenses."], ["D", "Partly true but incomplete: $\\dfrac{1}{0.85} = 1.18$ ringgit of assets per ringgit is real, but the discount may persist or widen, the extra $1.2$ points a year of expenses erode returns, and leverage magnifies losses."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Buying at a discount does give more assets (and often more distributions) per ringgit invested, but that gain is only realized as a capital gain if the discount narrows, and ongoing higher costs and leverage risk count against it. A treats an observation as a guarantee; B is false, discounts are common; C ignores that leverage magnifies losses as well as gains.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D14", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A short-term government bond fund returned $4\\%$ in a year when a broad stock index returned $15\\%$ and a matched short-term government bond index returned $3.6\\%$. A board member says the manager \"underperformed by $11$ points.\" Which response is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Agree: a broad stock index is the standard benchmark for every fund, because it shows what investors could have earned that year by simply holding the whole stock market instead."], ["B", "Agree: a manager should always beat the best-performing asset class."], ["C", "Disagree: against a matched short-term government bond benchmark the fund is ahead by $0.4$ points; a broad stock index has different risks and is not a fair yardstick."], ["D", "Disagree: bond funds cannot be compared with any benchmark."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Relative return must use a benchmark with the same kind of assets and risk: $4\\% - 3.6\\% = +0.4$ points. Comparing a bond fund with equities (A, B) mixes up asset-class choice with manager skill. Bond funds can and should be benchmarked (D).</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D15", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": null, "question": "<p>A saver will invest $\\text{RM}100$ a month. Option 1: a no-load index mutual fund, expense ratio $0.30\\%$. Option 2: an index ETF tracking the same index, expense ratio $0.10\\%$, with a $\\text{RM}7$ commission on every purchase. Which evaluation is most accurate?</p>", "options": [["A", "The ETF is cheaper from the first month, because its expense ratio is one-third of the mutual fund's."], ["B", "The two are equal, because both track the same index."], ["C", "The commission does not matter because it is not part of the expense ratio."], ["D", "The ETF is far more expensive for this saver at first: commissions cost $12 \\times 7 = \\text{RM}84$ a year, while the $0.2$-point expense saving on a first-year average balance of about $\\text{RM}600$ is only about $\\text{RM}1.20$. The saving matches the commissions only when the balance reaches about $\\dfrac{84}{0.002} = \\text{RM}42{,}000$."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A $\\text{RM}7$ commission is $7\\%$ of each $\\text{RM}100$ purchase. The expense-ratio advantage grows with the balance, so it can win in the long run, especially if the saver buys less often. A ignores per-trade costs; B ignores all cost differences; C confuses \"not in the expense ratio\" with \"not a cost\".</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X6", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Case competition", "question": "<p>A new closed-end fund is offered to the public at $\\text{RM}10.00$ a share, but $5\\%$ of the proceeds goes to underwriting and offering costs. A client wants to buy at the offering. Which assessment is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "Buy: an initial offering price always equals NAV, so there is no premium to worry about."], ["B", "Caution: after costs the fund starts with NAV of about $\\text{RM}9.50$, so the client pays a premium of $\\dfrac{10 - 9.50}{9.50} \\approx 5.26\\%$; if the price later moves toward or below NAV, as it has for many new closed-end funds, buying later in the secondary market may be cheaper."], ["C", "Buy: the offering costs are paid by the fund sponsor, not by investors."], ["D", "Avoid all closed-end funds, because they always trade at a premium."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Offering costs come out of the money raised, so NAV per share starts below the price paid. That built-in premium disappears if the shares settle near or below NAV. A and C misstate where the costs fall; D is wrong because closed-end funds frequently trade at discounts, not always premiums.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X8", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Master's", "question": "<p>A report says: \"The average 10-year return of the funds that exist in this category today is $9\\%$ a year, versus $8.5\\%$ for the index, so active management wins.\" Which evaluation is strongest?</p>", "options": [["A", "The comparison is biased upward: funds that closed or merged after poor results are missing (survivorship bias), and risk differences and investors' actual costs are ignored; it does not show that active management wins."], ["B", "The conclusion holds: an average taken over ten years and across every fund in the category removes luck, so the half-point advantage over the index must reflect the managers' genuine stock-picking skill."], ["C", "The conclusion holds, because the index includes no costs at all, so beating it after fees is an even stronger sign of skill."], ["D", "The comparison understates active returns, because the surviving funds are the worst performers, while many of the best managers left to run private funds whose results are excluded from the average."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Poor performers are more likely to be closed or merged away, so averaging today's survivors overstates the category's historical return. Ten years still contains noise (B). The point that indexes are cost-free (C) argues for caution about the index, not for accepting a biased fund average. D reverses the direction of survivorship bias.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X10", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "PhD", "question": "<p>Studies find little persistence in mutual fund outperformance. A student concludes: \"So no fund manager has skill.\" In the Berk and Green (2004) model, why is this conclusion not justified?</p>", "options": [["A", "Because in that model past winners always continue to outperform after fees."], ["B", "Because investors move money toward managers with demonstrated skill; with decreasing returns to scale and fees set by the manager, expected alpha to investors is driven to about zero, so skill appears in fund size and fees rather than in persistent net outperformance."], ["C", "Because the model assumes fund managers have no skill at all."], ["D", "Because the model shows that index funds always underperform active funds."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>In Berk and Green, flows chase performance until the extra capital erodes the manager's edge, so investors earn about the benchmark net of fees even when the manager is skilled. Non-persistence is therefore consistent with skill. A contradicts the evidence the model explains; C reverses its key assumption; D is not a result of the model.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X11", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Evaluate", "bloom_rank": 5, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>During a period of severe stress in corporate bond markets, several large bond ETFs traded at discounts of around $5\\%$ to their published NAVs. Which interpretation is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "The ETFs malfunctioned; their market prices were simply wrong and the NAVs were accurate, because NAVs are built from the bonds' official evaluated values, which are set by independent pricing services rather than by panicked sellers on the exchange."], ["B", "The discounts prove that ETF creation and redemption never works, since arbitrageurs should have bought the ETF shares and redeemed them for bonds within the day."], ["C", "Part of the \"discount\" reflected stale NAVs based on evaluated prices for bonds that were barely trading, so the ETF price may have been the timelier signal, while dealers' limited balance sheets also constrained the arbitrage."], ["D", "The discounts show that bond ETFs should be priced only once a day like mutual funds, so that investors always trade at NAV and stress cannot open a gap."]], "answers": ["C"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>When the underlying bonds do not trade, NAV relies on evaluated or stale prices, while the ETF keeps trading and incorporates new information; and arbitrage needs dealers able to absorb bonds. Both effects widen measured discounts. A assumes the NAV was right; B overgeneralizes from stress conditions; D throws away the price discovery the exchange provided.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D16", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>Which monitoring plan for an investor's fund holdings is best designed?</p>", "options": [["A", "Review at fixed intervals and after triggers (manager or mandate change, fee rise, concentration shift, new goals), comparing after-cost returns with a matched benchmark."], ["B", "Each year, sell and switch into whichever fund in the same category had the highest return over the previous twelve months, so the portfolio always holds the most recent winner."], ["C", "Never review a fund once it has been chosen, because trading costs money."], ["D", "Review only when the NAV falls by more than $10\\%$, and then sell immediately."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>A good process is periodic plus event-driven and uses evidence on both the fund (mandate, costs, holdings, benchmark-relative results) and the investor. B chases short-term performance, which is a weak predictor and adds costs; C ignores changes in the fund and the investor; D reacts only to price and may sell after a market-wide fall that says nothing about the fund's quality.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D17", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>An investor has $\\text{RM}100{,}000$: $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ is needed for a house deposit in nine months, and the rest is for retirement in $25$ years. The investor already holds a large position in bank shares. Which plan is most defensible?</p>", "options": [["A", "Put everything into a high-return equity sector fund and withdraw the deposit when needed."], ["B", "Keep the $\\text{RM}20{,}000$ in short-term, liquid holdings; put the $\\text{RM}80{,}000$ in diversified, low-cost equity funds with little overlap with the bank shares; set review triggers."], ["C", "Put all $\\text{RM}100{,}000$ in a money market fund until retirement because it is the safest category."], ["D", "Split the money equally among ten equity funds from different fund families, because holding more funds always means more diversification, whatever each fund owns and whenever the money is needed."]], "answers": ["B"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Match each pot of money to its horizon and liquidity need, then diversify the long-term pot against the investor's existing concentration. A exposes the near-term need to a volatile, concentrated fund; C sacrifices decades of growth and is not risk-free either; D counts funds, not exposures, and ten funds can share the same holdings.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "D18", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": null, "question": "<p>You must design one number to compare two funds with different loads, expense ratios and trading costs for a particular investor. Which design is best?</p>", "options": [["A", "The projected ending value (or its annualized equivalent) after all loads, ongoing expenses and trading costs, over the investor's own holding period and contribution pattern, with the same gross return for both funds."], ["B", "The expense ratio alone, since it is the only fee charged every year and so, over any realistic holding period, it outweighs loads, trading costs and other one-off charges combined."], ["C", "The sales load alone, since it is the largest single charge an investor pays and is taken before any of the money starts earning a return."], ["D", "The sum of the load percentage and the expense ratio, ignoring the holding period, since both are stated as percentages of the amount invested and so can be added directly into one total-cost figure for each fund."]], "answers": ["A"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>Costs differ in timing (entry, annual, exit, per trade), so only a projection over the investor's own horizon and buying pattern puts them on one basis. B and C each ignore part of the cost; D adds a one-off percentage to an annual one, which overstates the load's effect for long horizons and understates the expense ratio's.</p>"}, {"module": 12, "number": "X12", "set": "difficult", "bloom": "Create", "bloom_rank": 6, "ext": "Postdoc", "question": "<p>You want to test whether an ETF's measured premiums and discounts reflect genuine mispricing or merely stale NAVs. Which research design is most informative?</p>", "options": [["A", "Count how many days the premium is positive and conclude mispricing if it is more than half."], ["B", "Compare the ETF's expense ratio with that of an index mutual fund."], ["C", "Survey retail investors about whether they believe the ETF is fairly priced."], ["D", "Regress the next period's NAV return and the next period's ETF price return on today's premium: if the premium predicts the NAV catching up, it reflects staleness; if it predicts the ETF price reverting, it reflects mispricing. Control for the liquidity of the underlying assets and the timing of the NAV strike."]], "answers": ["D"], "multiple": false, "explanation": "<p>The two explanations make opposite predictions about which price moves next, so a predictive regression separates them, and controls for underlying liquidity and timestamps address the main confounders. A counts signs without testing either mechanism; B measures costs, not pricing; C measures beliefs, not prices.</p>"}];
